window.QUESTION_BANK = {"total_vignettes": 246, "total_questions": 1288, "sources": [{"id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock"}, {"id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock"}, {"id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock"}, {"id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock"}, {"id": "uworld_equity", "label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity"}, {"id": "uworld_fsa", "label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA"}, {"id": "uworld_fixed_income", "label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income"}, {"id": "uworld_ethics", "label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics"}, {"id": "uworld_economics", "label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics"}, {"id": "uworld_derivatives", "label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives"}, {"id": "uworld_alt_inv", "label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments"}, {"id": "uworld_corp", "label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers"}, {"id": "uworld_portfolio", "label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management"}, {"id": "uworld_quant", "label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods"}], "vignettes": [{"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Chester Siggurdson, CFA, and Pamela Bowen, CFA, are both \nwith Conservative Capital.  Siggurdson is a credit analyst and \nBowen is a financial adviser, and they often work together on \nassignments.  The firm serves as an underwriter for \ngovernment bonds and manages diversified portfolios as an \nadvisor for wealthy individuals.\nTwo years ago, Conservative Capital worked on a municipality's \nsuccessful bond issue to fund the construction of a \nmultipurpose convention center complex with public recreation \nareas, a luxury hotel, and a world-class concert venue.  Bowen \nhad placed many of the municipality's bonds from prior issues \nwith her clients, and she did so again with this issue.  Often, but \nnot always, the municipality sent Bowen gifts to acknowledge \nthe placements.\nNow the center is ready to open, and as a show of \nappreciation, the municipality invites Bowen to attend the gala \nopening, to be followed the next day by a meeting with officials \nto discuss future bond underwritings.  The municipality will pay \nall expenses, including airfare, meals, and accommodation at \nthe convention center hotel.\nBowen accepts the invitation and attends the opening.  Before \nthe next day's meeting, she overhears center staff say that the \nconvention center has not received as many bookings as \nanticipated.  During the meeting, she asks the center's director \nwhether the revenue from the center will be sufficient to \nservice the bonds.  The director responds that while bookings \nhave been slower than projected, there is no risk of bond \nobligations not being paid as scheduled.\nLater that day, Bowen telephones Siggurdson from the airport \nand says, \"I don't have anything specific, but I'm not feeling \ncomfortable about the municipality's revenue forecasts.\"  \nSiggurdson replies that he has no information on the matter \nbut will check around.  Siggurdson then speaks to sources at \none of the ratings agencies.  One source tells him that some \nbookings may have been canceled, but the agency has not \nbeen able to assess whether that will negatively affect the \nbonds.  Siggurdson verifies approximately 15 cancellations \nusing public information, including concert performers' publicly \navailable websites.\n\nBased on only the information he has compiled, Siggurdson\nquickly prepares a report advising that the cancellations \nmay leave the municipality unable to pay interest on the \nbonds when due.  He concludes that investors should hold \nthe bonds until he can learn more, but several clients \nimmediately sell their holdings of the municipality's bonds.  \nThe following day, the ratings agency downgrades the \nconvention center bond issue to junk bond status, causing \nthe price to fall significantly.\nOne of Bowen's advisory clients is a wealthy individual.  \nBowen has prepared an investment policy statement (IPS) \nand reviews it with the client annually.  The client's \nobjectives and constraints, as identified in the IPS, indicate \nthat below-investment-grade bonds are not suitable for this \nclient's portfolio.  However, yields have been very low for \nseveral years, and the client has spoken to Bowen about the \nneed for the portfolio to generate higher returns from its \nbond holdings.\nThe client has researched the recently downgraded \nmunicipal bonds and is attracted by their high yields.  \nBowen believes that the bonds' riskiness makes them \nunsuitable, but she has had difficulty with this client in the \npast and Bowen worries that she will lose his business if she \nvoices her concerns, so she remains silent.  The client \nplaces an order for a significant amount of the convention \ncenter bonds, which Bowen instructs Conservative Capital's \ntrading desk to execute.\nWithout informing Conservative Capital, Siggurdson has \naccepted an offer to work full time as an employee for one \nof the firm's competitors.  Siggurdson was previously an \nemployee of Conservative Capital, but several years ago he \nand the firm mutually agreed to change his status to an \nindependent contractor.  As an independent contractor, \nSiggurdson:\nworks far fewer hours than analysts who are full-time \nemployees;\nis free to accept assignments from Conservative Capital's \ncompetitors, although he has not done so; and\n\nworks primarily from his home but sometimes visits the \nfirm's office, working at any empty desk when he is there.\nSiggurdson has posted his new position on his personal \nsocial media account, which is visible to some of \nConservative Capital's clients.  He has also obtained \ninformation about these clients from sources unrelated to \nConservative Capital.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Luca Albrecht and Katrin Sauer are quantitative analysts at a \nhedge fund specializing in credit strategies.  They are \ndeveloping machine learning (ML) models to predict the credit \nratings for new bond issues and/or changes in ratings for any \nbond.\nAlbrecht plans to use an unsupervised clustering model to \npredict credit ratings and is deciding between k-means \nclustering and hierarchical clustering models.  Sauer helps \nAlbrecht review the data being analyzed and states:\nStatement 1:\nSince you know the number of clusters you plan \nto use beforehand, this bypasses a limitation of k-means \nclustering.\nStatement 2:\nSince you are looking at the global structure of \nthe data and identifying large clusters, agglomerative clustering \nwould be better suited than divisive clustering.\nSauer has built a deep neural network (DNN) to analyze bonds \nto estimate when there might be changes to an issuer's credit \nrating.  After going through several iterations of the model and \nchanging the hyperparameters, Sauer settles on a model with:\n8 fundamental features\n11 industry dummy variables\n3 hidden layers with 80 nodes each\nAn output of the estimated credit rating over the next quarter\nAlbrecht suggests introducing a term to shrink the number of \nnodes with nonzero weighting in the DNN to reduce overfitting \nrisk.\nThen, Albrecht asks Sauer for an update on the ML algorithm \ndeveloped at the beginning of the year to predict market \nsentiment based on social media posts.  Sauer explains that:\nthe data set was split into training, cross-validation (CV), and \ntest data sets,\na logistic regression model was used to classify texts and \npredict sentiments,\npositive sentiments were classified as 1 and negative \nsentiments as 0, and\nthe model was tested based on different threshold p-values.\nSauer tells Albrecht that she compared the model performance \non the training and CV data sets using three different threshold \np-values.  The analysis included receiver operating \ncharacteristic (ROC) plots and the area under the curve (AUC) \nfor each ROC curve.  The conclusions of the analysis are \npresented in Exhibit 1:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Ciara Amutenya is the head of research at Brandberg Asset \nManagement.  She is currently reviewing the valuation and \ngrowth outlook of two stocks in the firm's portfolio: Kunene \nSemiconductors and Swakop Oil.  She directs Linus \nNangombe, a junior analyst at Brandberg, to analyze both \ncompanies.\nAmutenya asks Nangombe to calculate Kunene's justified \ntrailing P/E based on the Gordon growth model and to \ndetermine whether the stock is currently overvalued, fairly \nvalued, or undervalued.  Nangombe gathers information \nfrom Kunene, summarized in Exhibit 1:\nNangombe explains that the expected dividend growth rate \nreflects Kunene's performance in the previous five years.  \nAmutenya knows that Kunene's competitors are making \nsubstantial investments in innovative technologies and \nexpects these technologies to be market-ready in the \nmedium term.  Therefore, she believes that Kunene will not \nbe able to maintain a dividend growth rate of 8.0% over \ntime.  She asks Nangombe to estimate the value of \nKunene's stock using the H-model and assuming that the \ndividend growth rate:\nis initially 8.0%,\ndeclines linearly during a 10-year period, and\nstabilizes at a perpetual growth rate of 5.0%.\nAmutenya then turns her attention to Swakop Oil, one of \nthe major operators of retail gas stations in the region.  She \nasks Nangombe to estimate the company's sustainable \ngrowth rate.  Nangombe decides to use DuPont analysis \nand summarizes the information in Exhibit 2:\n\nAmutenya is concerned about Swakop's ability to maintain \nits current level of returns.  Nangombe explains that he \nconducted an industry and competitive analysis for Swakop\nand the oil distribution industry.  He lists the following \nobservations:\nObservation 1:Swakop changed its gasoline suppliers \nseveral times due to intense competition among the \nsuppliers to offer the best prices and terms.\nObservation 2:Swakop is the lowest-cost producer; its \nproducts are similar to competitors' and are priced slightly \nbelow average market price.\nObservation 3:Swakop has achieved above-average returns \nin the previous five years, and the barriers to entry in its \nindustry are low.\nAfter listening to Nangombe, Amutenya concludes that \nSwakop's current level of returns may not be sustainable \nand decides to reduce long-term growth rate forecasts.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Antti Laine is a portfolio manager for a large pension fund \nand is responsible for the day-to-day management of the \nfund's alternative investments.  The fund has exposure to \ncommodities through numerous futures positions.\nSix months ago, the fund took a fully collateralized position \nin a gold futures contract at $1,750/ounce.  Since then, the \ncollateral has earned a 1.50% annualized rate.  Because the \ncontract was nearing expiration, Laine rolled the entire \nposition into the current 6-month gold futures contract.  At \nthe time of the roll, the near-term contract was \n$1,770/ounce and the farther-term contract was \n$1,785/ounce.\nLaine turns his attention to Alpha Fund, a private equity \nfund with a 20X0 vintage.  Alpha Fund has a 2% \nmanagement fee on paid-in capital and carried interest of \n20% when NAV after distributions is greater than the \ncommitted capital of $200 million.  Laine notes that all the \ncapital calls occur at the beginning of the year, while all \nother cash flows and fees occur at year-end.  Information \non Alpha Fund is shown in Exhibit 1:\nLaine is comparing Alpha Fund's performance to other \nfunds of the same vintage, including Beta Fund, which has a \nnet IRR of 6.80%, and Gamma Fund, which has a net IRR of \n11.80%.\nLaine intends to increase the pension fund's exposure to \nreal estate.  He is considering both REITs and direct real \nestate investment.  When evaluating Tower Block, an \napartment REIT, Laine identifies raw land on the company's\n\nbalance sheet that was acquired 5 years ago.  The land \nremains undeveloped; given the market's growth, the land \nvalue has appreciated significantly since it was purchased.\nLaine then reviews a direct investment in a real estate \nproperty, Casa Grande Apartments.  Laine intends to hold \nthe investment for 3 years and wants to compare the \nproperty's estimated value using the direct capitalization \nmethod versus the discounted cash flow (DCF) method.  \nLaine collects information on the property, shown in Exhibit \n2:\nLaine expects Casa Grande's net operating income to grow \nat a constant 2% rate into perpetuity.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Robert Lopez is a newly hired risk analyst at MultiStrat\nCapital, a multi-strategy hedge fund.  Paola Reyes is a senior \nrisk analyst at MultiStrat and Lopez's supervisor.  Reyes \nexplains to Lopez that the firm has two hedge fund \nstrategies, both consisting of numerous independent sub-\nportfolios.  Each sub-portfolio has a different investment \nstrategy, portfolio manager, and risk budget.  Due to the \nvaried nature of the investment strategies, the correlations \namong strategies are relatively low.\nReyes explains that the firm's risk management and risk \nbudgeting process is based on parametric value at risk (VaR) \nat the fund level and marginal value at risk (MVaR) at the \nsub-portfolio level.  Reyes states that the VaR for the Alpha \nFund is $100 million and provides Lopez with information \non the sub-portfolios, shown in Exhibit 1:\nLopez makes the following comments:\nComment 1:\nSince the portfolios are not highly correlated, \nthe firm is underutilizing the fund's full risk appetite.\nComment 2:\nMVaR is useful as a pre-trade evaluation tool \non the potential impact of a trade on total VaR.\nReyes explains that as part of the firm's risk management \nprocess, the risk team conducts scenario risk analysis for \nthe fund and on a sub-portfolio basis each month.  The \nteam begins the analysis by identifying each portfolio's five \nmost significant exposures and then assesses the potential \nfor unforeseen vulnerabilities.  The analysis stresses \ncorrelations and co-movement of assets.\nReyes then provides Lopez with VaR input assumptions for \nthe Sigma Fund and asks that he calculate VaR using the \nparametric approach.\n\nMultiStrat's business model calls for it to identify new \ninvestment talent that complements existing strategies and \nthen provide capital, along with integrated technology \nsolutions, back office, and risk management services, to \nimplement those strategies.\nThe risk group at MultiStrat is one of the first to evaluate \nnew investment strategies presented by outside managers.  \nMost new strategies are quantitative factor strategies that \nhave been backtested but without real-world \nimplementation.\nA recent manager submission included the following \ninformation and attributes:\nHistorical investment simulation\nInvestment universe defined as benchmark securities\n5, 10, and 15 years of historical data analyzed\nRolling windows approach analysis\nPoint-in-time data\nUp to 10 factor portfolios\nAfter backtesting dozens of strategies based on this \ninformation, the outside manager presented MultiStrat\nwith a strategy for consideration.  Based on 5 years of \nhistorical data, a monthly rebalance, and 7 equally \nweighted factors, the strategy produced a Sharpe ratio of \n2.5 with realized volatility of 3%.  This strategy also had the \nhighest t-statistic of 2.7 and lowest p-value of 1.4% of any \nstrategy tested.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Luca Girardi is a fixed income portfolio manager at Bled \nCapital.  Girardi works closely with Eva Novak, Bled's chief \neconomist.  The domestic economy has been in a sustained \nexpansion for the past few years.  Recently, inflation has \naccelerated well above the central bank's inflation target.\nNovak's base case economic forecast is for the economic \nexpansion to continue in the coming year and for inflation \nto continue rising over the next few quarters.  Novak \nforecasts that the central bank will respond to these \ndevelopments by aggressively raising its policy rate over the \nnext six months, resulting in a mild recession in the \nfollowing year.\nThe current government bond yield curve is the steepest it \nhas been in years, as the yield on the 10-year bond is \ncurrently 300 basis points above the yield on the 2-year \nbond.  Girardi compares the government bond yield curve \nwith the interest rate swap curve to get a relative measure \nof the credit and liquidity risks reflected in the corporate \nbond market, as shown in Exhibit 1:\nGirardi is evaluating the relative attractiveness of a BBB-\nrated industrial bond.  The bond matures in 5 years and is \ncontinuously callable beginning in 2 years.  Girardi will \ndetermine the bond's attractiveness relative to similar \nbonds based on its option-adjusted spread (OAS).", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Arianna Varela is the head of research at an asset \nmanagement firm.  She directs Theron Misko, a senior \nanalyst who covers the technology industry, to analyze \nColossus Robotics and Acheron Semiconductors.  Varela is \nparticularly interested in evaluating the quality of both \ncompanies' financial reports and earnings.\nMisko explains that Colossus Robotics has been operating in \nthe industrial robotics segment for over two decades, but \nthe company recently started manufacturing drones for \nconsumer and commercial uses.  Investors see great growth \npotential in this market and have been valuing drone \nmanufacturers at higher multiples; as a result, Colossus's \nstock price has increased substantially.  Misko reached the \nfollowing conclusions after analyzing the company's \nfinancial reports:\nConclusion 1: Colossus's receivables turnover increased \nsignificantly in recent quarters.\nConclusion 2: Colossus's drone segment had excellent \nperformance in the last two years, but the industrial \nrobotics segment performed significantly worse than \ncompetitors.\nConclusion 3: Colossus has a Beneish's M-score of −2.70, \nwhich is below the industry average.\nVarela asks Misko to compare Colossus with its competitors.  \nMisko explains that Colossus adopted financial reporting \nchoices that overstate its assets, affecting the comparability \nof the firm's financial statements.\nVarela then asks Misko about Acheron Semiconductors.  \nAfter analyzing the company's financial statements, he \nbelieves that Acheron has good reporting quality, but there \nare warning signs suggesting that the company has low-\nquality earnings.  The analysis is summarized in Exhibit 1:\n\nVarela is concerned that a new accounting rule may create \nmore restrictions on capitalizing installation and assembly \ncosts relative to capital projects.  Currently, Acheron treats \nsuch costs as capital expenditures and records them as part \nof PPE.  This change would decrease the portion of costs \nthat Acheron can capitalize and, consequently, increase the \nportion that is expensed.  Varela does not expect any other \nfactor to impact the firm's financial statements.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Paula Kostas is a junior analyst at InvestUp, a multi-strategy \nasset management company.  Kostas is analyzing a \nconglomerate planning to divest one of its businesses.  She \nasks her supervisor, Vasso Tzortzi, about underlying \nmotivations that drive divestment actions and Tzortzi lists \nthe following:\nMotivation 1: Raising cash to reduce financial leverage\nMotivation 2: Selling undervalued businesses to reduce \nconglomerate discount\nMotivation 3: Complying with court decisions in antitrust \ncases\nInvestUp is considering a significant investment in \nChattergram, which has large cash reserves from its online \nsales of social media applications.  Although Chattergram\nhas never paid dividends, its shareholders have recently \nurged the company's management to begin doing so.  \nHowever, management does not believe dividends are \nwarranted since it has identified several projects that would \nrequire large cash outlays.\nOne area that InvestUp considers is a company's corporate \ngovernance.  Kostas is asked for information concerning \nChattergram's ownership structure.  She provides the \nfollowing:\n30% of Chattergram's common stock is owned by the \nfounder's family.\nNo other shareholder owns more than 1% of the stock.\nNone of the family members participate in Chattergram's\nmanagement, nor do they serve on the board of directors.\nThere is a dual class share structure:\nFamily members own Class A shares, with 1.6 votes per            \nshare.\nNonfamily members own Class B shares, with 1 vote per \nshare.\nKostas turns her attention to Balaskas Group, a large \nsoftware developer that recently restructured its mix of \ndebt and equity through a dividend recapitalization.  \nBalaskas is a mature company with stable revenues and \ncash flows.  Kostas analyzes the company's financial \nstatements to understand the effects of this restructuring \naction.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Angelika Frueh is a senior analyst at Forschung Global Asset \nResearch.  She is estimating the intrinsic value of Wohnung\nGmbH, using a two-stage free cash flow valuation model.  \nFrueh uses Wohnung's cash flow statement for the most \nrecently ended fiscal year, shown in Exhibit 1:\nShe also makes the following assumptions when valuing \nWohnung:\nWACC is 12%.\nFCFF will grow at 10% annually for the next 5 years and \nthen grow indefinitely at 4% annually.\nMarket value of debt is €100 million.\nThere are 40 million common shares outstanding.\nFrueh then meets with Laura Schriner, an intern, to review \nthe model.  Schriner, who has little experience in valuing \ncompanies using free cash flow, points out that Frueh has \nnot factored future corporate actions into this model and \nasks how share buybacks, dividend payments, and debt \nissuance affect free cash flows.\n\nFrueh responds with the following statements:\nStatement 1: Share repurchases will affect FCFE but not \nFCFF since FCFE represents cash flows after accounting for \nshare buybacks.\nStatement 2: Changes in the target debt ratio will impact \nFCFE.  For example, borrowing additional debt this year will \nincrease FCFE.\nStatement 3: Paying dividends will decrease FCFE since \nFCFE is calculated net of dividend distributions.\nOne month later, Frueh begins to cover Spielspas GmbH, \nwhich is currently privately held, and meets with Spielspas's\nfounder and CEO, Dieter Kuster.  During the meeting, Kuster\nreveals near-term plans to take the company public and \nasks Frueh to estimate the company's intrinsic value.  When \nFrueh states that she will do so by discounting FCFE, Kuster\nasks why it is better to use FCFE instead of net income or \nEBITDA.  She notes three differences between them:\nDifference 1: Unlike EBITDA, free cash flow is net of \ndepreciation and amortization.\nDifference 2: Unlike net income, free cash flow is net of \ndividends paid to shareholders.\nDifference 3: Unlike net income and EBITDA, free cash \nflow deducts investments in fixed capital.\nThree years later, Spielspas is trading on Euronext for €48 \nper share with an estimated EPS of €2.  To assess whether \nSpielspas is fairly valued, Frueh gathers three similar \ncompetitors' forward P/E ratios to compare with Spielspas's\nP/E.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Harold Harrison, a CFA Level I Candidate, works as an \ninvestment analyst at Igneous Wealth.  Igneous provides \nfinancial and estate planning to high-net-worth and retail \ninvestors.  Igneous's investment process relies on numerous \nthird-party research providers.  All third-party research \nmust be vetted and approved by Igneous's investment \ncommittee.\nPumice Research publishes weekly macroeconomic \nanalyses and forecasts and is one of the firm's approved \nresearch providers.  Harrison often disagrees with Pumice's \nconclusions regarding the expected impact on financial \nmarkets but still finds its work interesting and thought-\nprovoking.\nAnnually, Igneous forecasts asset class returns for the next \nfive years and uses this information in constructing client \nportfolios.  Harrison is responsible for these forecasts and \nuses many of Pumice's economic estimates to create them.\nHarrison's entire personal investment portfolio is invested \nin Igneous's most aggressive model portfolio, which consists \nprimarily of individual stocks.  Harrison's account is at the \nsame brokerage firm as clients' accounts.  The firm's trading \ndesk executes all model portfolio changes, including \nHarrison’s.\nHarrison's CFA Level I exam date is quickly approaching.  He \nhas been working unusually long hours and is also studying \nfor a mandatory financial regulatory exam scheduled just a \nweek after the CFA exam.  During the CFA exam, he notifies \nthe proctor that he needs to use the washroom.  As he is \nwalking through the testing center, he glances at one of the \nscreens of another CFA Level I test taker.  Harrison \nrecognizes the question and sees the other Candidate \nselect a different answer than he did.  All of this goes \nunnoticed by the proctor.  Once Harrison resumes the \nexam, he returns to that specific question, realizes he did \nnot read the question carefully, and confidently selects a \ndifferent answer.  Harrison's new answer choice is not the \nsame answer that the other Candidate selected.  Harrison \ncompletes the remainder of the exam without incident.\n\nExhausted by work and by studying for the CFA exam, \nHarrison is not confident in his ability to pass the regulatory \nexam, which contains many country-specific questions on \nrules, regulations, and taxes.  Harrison's coworker Natalie \nGrimes, who is also a CFA Level I Candidate, is taking the \nsame regulatory exam the day before Harrison is.\nThe morning that Harrison is scheduled to take the exam, \nGrimes and Harrison are discussing Grimes's exam \nexperience in general, but nothing specific regarding the \ntest or question content.  Grimes notices that Harrison has \na small piece of paper with what appear to be exam-related \nnotes.  Harrison takes these notes into the exam room and \nuses them during the exam to answer questions.  After the \ntest, Harrison confides to Grimes that he would not have \npassed the exam without using his notes.  Grimes reassures \nHarrison that his secret is safe and that she will not mention \nthis to anyone.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Claris Wentz is an interest rate derivatives trader for an \ninternational hedge fund.  She works with Todd Martens, an \nanalyst at the firm.  Wentz believes EUR interest rates are \nlikely to increase over the next several months and enters \ninto an advanced set, advanced settled, €1 million notional, \n3% pay-fixed/receive-floating, 2 × 5 forward rate agreement \n(FRA).  The market reference rates (MRRs) at the expiration \nof the FRA are shown in Exhibit 1.\nWentz is also considering taking a position in a EUR fixed-\nfor-floating interest-rate swap.  She asks Martens to price \nan at-market, 3-year, annual-payment swap.  Martens \ngathers the necessary information on EUR interest rates, \nwhich is shown in Exhibit 2.\nWentz also expects GBP interest rates to rise.  However, she \nwants to limit the firm's risk exposure on the GBP rate \nspeculation.  She decides the best strategy is to buy an \ninterest rate call and asks Martens to develop a binomial \ninterest rate tree to value a £5 million notional, 2-year, 3% \nEuropean call.  Exhibit 3 contains the binomial tree \nconstructed by Martens, which uses 50% as the probability \nof an interest rate up move.\n\nWentz wants to understand the difference in approaches \nused in binomial models compared with other methods for \npricing options.  She asks Martens to contrast the Black \nmodel with the binomial approach for pricing European \ninterest rate call options.  In response, Martens makes the \nfollowing statements:\nStatement 1: Both approaches value options based on the \nPV of the expected payoff at expiration.\nStatement 2: The Black model uses a single risk-free rate \nand the binomial model uses different discount rates to \nobtain values for each node prior to expiration.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s1_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Senn Dekker, CFA, is the CEO of Dommel, a US asset \nmanagement firm.  Dommel is launching a small-cap ETF.  \nDekker believes this ETF will be Dommel's main product in five \nyears; however, he is concerned that low trading volume in the \ninitial months could jeopardize the future of the product.  He \norders his sales team to contact Dommel's clients and propose \na temporary reduction in commissions for those clients who \ncommit to purchase a certain amount of the ETF during the \nfollowing six months.  He discloses these agreements to clients \nand potential investors in all public materials related to the ETF.\nDekker supervises Gwen Visser, CFA, head of portfolio \nmanagement at Dommel.  He learns that Visser has been \nreceiving additional compensation from clients without \ndisclosing the arrangements to the firm.  Dekker summons her \nto a meeting, in which she explains the extent of each \narrangement, provides all pertinent information and \ndocumentation, and assures they will be terminated.  Dekker \ninforms Visser that her annual bonus will be cut by half and \nthat if she repeats this behavior, she will be fired.\nBack at her desk, Visser turns her attention to the IPO of Waal \nIndustries.  She reviews the suitability of the investment for her \nclients, including her mother, who has a fee-paying portfolio at \nDommel.  She submits a bid for 5,000 Waal shares, but since \nthe IPO is oversubscribed, she is able to secure only 4,000 \nshares and needs to decide how to allocate them among her \nclients' portfolios.  She does not want to give Dekker any \nreasons to suspect a conflict of interest and decides to cut her \nmother's allocation by half of the amount originally planned, \nincreasing the amount allocated to the other clients.\nLater, Dommel's buy-side research team completes an extensive \nanalysis of Vecht Robotics.  The analysts recommend buying the \nstock, given Vecht's solid recent performance and future \ngrowth prospects due to the firm's groundbreaking technology.  \nVisser reads the report and decides to purchase a substantial \namount of Vecht's shares for the clients' managed portfolios.  \nOne week later, Visser sells part of her personal holdings, which \ninclude a certain amount of Vecht's shares, to make a \nprescheduled down payment for a new home.  Although \nDommel does not have reporting procedures for employees, \nshe provides duplicate trade confirmations after the sale is \ncompleted.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Matilde Ricci is the chief financial officer of Mille Torri \nTelecom (MTT), an Italian mobile network operator.  MTT \nacquired an interest in Crescita, a smaller competitor that \ndeveloped a promising mobile communication technology.  \nRicci asks Tyler Cooper, a financial analyst who reports to \nher, to calculate the goodwill from this transaction using \nconsolidation with full goodwill.  Cooper gathers the \nfollowing information:\nInterest acquired in Crescita: 85%\nPrice paid for interest: €17 million\nIdentifiable net assets: €18 million\nAfter calculating goodwill, Cooper tells Ricci he does not \nhave much experience with business consolidations under \nIFRS since he has spent most of his career in the United \nStates.  He asks if she can list some differences in the \nacquisition method between US GAAP and IFRS.\nLater, Ricci turns her attention to MTT's employee \ncompensation plans.  The company offers a defined benefit \npension plan, which promises retirees a defined periodic \npayment for life.  This amount depends on each employee's \nfinal compensation and total years of service.  Ricci has \nrecently updated some of the assumptions used to \ndetermine the pension obligation; as a result, the obligation \nhas decreased.  The previous and updated values of each \nassumption are presented in Exhibit 1:\nMTT also uses assumptions to value stock options offered \nto its executives as share-based compensation.  Ricci \nrecently updated one of these assumptions; she increased \nthe expected volatility of MTT stock from 28% to 33% \nbefore recording the stock options granted in the current \nyear.  This change impacted the fair value of stock options \nand had effects on MTT's financial statements.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Mila Keller is a currency trader at Eiger Bank, a large \ninternational bank.  Keller is reviewing currency exchange \nrate quotes in the interbank market and looking for \narbitrage opportunities.  Keller compiles the quotes from \nthe interbank market and compares them with a dealer \nquote from F/X Traders:\nKeller is also monitoring the currencies and interest rates in \nthe North American markets and has noticed that \nuncovered interest rate parity has not held.  After reviewing \nthe following data, she executes a carry trade by borrowing \nCAD 1,000,000 to invest in MXN-denominated bonds.\nKeller has lunch with Karl Yoder, an economist at Eiger\nBank.  The two discuss international parity conditions, and \nYoder states:\nStatement 1: Of the key international parity conditions, \nonly covered interest rate parity is enforced by arbitrage.\nStatement 2: If covered and uncovered interest rate parity \nhold, then by definition the forward exchange rate will be \nan accurate predictor of the future spot exchange rate.\n\nYoder then comments about how different currencies may \nmove based on their countries' respective monetary and \nfiscal policies.  For example, Susian and Gutium are both \nemerging market countries that have placed restrictions on \ncapital flows.  Both countries experienced a recession \nseveral years ago but have had strong GDP growth since, \nand in both countries, the central bank has increased its \npolicy rate to prevent the economy from overheating.  \nHowever, the countries differ when it comes to fiscal policy:\nIn Susian, government spending is steady, but taxes have \nbeen increased to lower the budget deficit.\nIn Gutium, government spending has increased, but the \ncurrent tax regime has been maintained.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Yuni Torimoto is a senior fixed-income analyst at a global \nbank supporting the portfolio managers of several bond \nfunds.  She is assisted by Darcy Stuponski, a junior analyst \nat the firm.  A portfolio manager asks Torimoto to evaluate \nthe interest rate risk of several government bonds.  Exhibit \n1 contains information on three fixed-rate sovereign bonds, \nall of which mature in exactly 5 years, on Oct 1 20X9.  The \ngovernment par bond yield curve is currently flat at 3.00%.  \nBond 1 is currently priced at 100% of its par value.\nTorimoto obtains an interest rate forecast from the bank's \neconomists to use in evaluating the bonds.  The economists \nforecast two potential interest rate environments:\nScenario 1:\nThe government bond yield curve remains \nflat at 3.00%, but there is an increase in interest rate \nvolatility.\nScenario 2:\nInterest rate volatility remains constant, but \nthere is a significant steepening of the yield curve, with \nshort-term rates staying at about the same level while long-\nterm rates significantly increase.\nTorimoto directs Stuponski to assess the impact of these \nscenarios on the values of Bonds 1, 2, and 3.\nOne of the bank's portfolio managers asks Torimoto to \nevaluate Bond 4, a noncallable convertible bond issued one \nyear ago by Quantan Corporation.  Exhibit 2 contains \nselected data about Bond 4.  Bond 5 is an otherwise \nidentical nonconvertible Quantan Corporation bond \ncurrently priced at 93.0000 per 100 of par value.\nAssume all bonds pay coupons once per year and all \ninterest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Amir Hassan is an analyst at Luxor Advisors, a wealth \nmanagement firm that caters to high-net-worth individuals.  \nHassan is developing a macroeconomic factor model to \nestimate the returns on the Intermediate Investment Grade \nBond ETF (IIG), an index-based ETF that tracks the domestic \nbond market.\nHassan creates a three-factor model using GDP growth, \ncorporate credit spreads, and inflation.  The portion of the \nreturn not explained by the model is 0.3%.  The details of \nthe model are presented in Exhibit 1, and the previous \nyear's (20X7) model inputs are in Exhibit 2:\nHassan is discussing the investment merits of IIG with \nfellow Luxor analyst Dina Omar.  Omar makes the following \nobservations:\nObservation 1:IIG's annual expense ratio is 0.20%.\nObservation 2:Relative to a traditional mutual fund, IIG's \nETF structure results in a higher tax burden for investors.\nObservation 3:IIG uses a representative sampling portfolio \nconstruction approach.\nHassan and Omar then compare IIG to the Suez Bond ETF \n(SBF) and the Nile Income ETF (NIF).  Both funds are actively \nmanaged, long-only ETFs that are benchmarked to the \nsame index as IIG.  Omar compiles the following \ninformation net of fund fees and expenses:\n\nDuring the discussion of the funds' investment merits, \nHassan states that the information ratio may be useful in \nthe investment manager selection process.  Hassan \ncontinues by stating:\nStatement 1: The expected information ratio is the single \nbest criterion for assessing active performance among \nvarious actively managed funds with the same benchmark.\nStatement 2: Investing with the highest information ratio \nmanager will produce the highest Sharpe ratio for the \ninvestor's portfolio.\nStatement 3: The optimal unconstrained portfolio is the \none with the highest Sharpe ratio.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Antero Virtanen is a senior portfolio manager at an asset \nmanagement firm.  Virtanen has decided to increase the \nfirm's exposure to the technology sector and is choosing \nbetween Morava Tech and Bosona Industries.  He explains \nto Louise Herrera, a junior equity analyst on his team, that \nMorava and Bosona are similar companies operating in the \nsame industry.  He asks Herrera to compare the companies \nusing market-based valuation.  She gathers the financial \ninformation shown in Exhibit 1:\nHerrera adds that she also calculated multiples based on \nenterprise value (EV) since EV multiples address some \nweaknesses of price multiples.  Virtanen asks Herrera to be \nmore specific and provide examples of the EV/EBITDA \nmultiple's advantages.  She explains that, in contrast to P/E, \nthe EV/EBITDA multiple:\nAdvantage 1: Adequately accounts for changes in working \ncapital\nAdvantage 2: Is suitable to compare capital-intensive \nbusinesses\nAdvantage 3: Directly reflects a company's capital \nexpenditures\nVirtanen asks Herrera whether Bosona's 20X6 EPS of €10.20 \ndeviated from market expectations.  Herrera replies that \nthe mean consensus forecast was €9.90 with a standard \ndeviation of €1.50.  She adds that the historical standard \ndeviation of past unexpected earnings is €2.00.  Virtanen \nbelieves that momentum indicators may provide insights on \nfuture trends and asks Herrera to calculate Bosona's\nstandardized unexpected earnings (SUE) for 20X6.\n\nVirtanen then turns his attention to a client's portfolio that \nconsists of three stocks with a market value of €200 million.  \nHe gives Herrera the position and P/E of each stock, \nsummarized in Exhibit 2, and asks her to estimate the \nportfolio's P/E.  Herrera explains that there are different \nmeasures of central tendency and that she needs to use the \nmost appropriate measure for this situation.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Jason Kim is a senior derivatives trader for a UK-based \nhedge fund.  Hannah Bardot is a recently hired junior trader \nworking with Kim.  Kim wants to assess Bardot's familiarity \nwith the pricing and valuation of currency swaps.  Kim \nprovides Bardot with information (shown in Exhibit 1) \nrelated to a 4-year, GBP/EUR, pay-fixed, receive-floating \ncurrency swap initiated one year earlier.\nKim asks Bardot to describe a method of determining the \nGBP value of the swap and to identify a portfolio of fixed-\nincome securities that would replicate the cash flows of the \nhedge fund's swap position.  She responds:\nResponse 1:\nThe GBP value of the swap is the PV of the \ndifference between 3% and the coupon rate for 4-year, at-\nmarket, annual pay, GBP fixed rate for floating rate in EUR, \nhaving the same notional amounts and the same floating-\nleg reference interest rate.\nResponse 2:\nA replicating portfolio of the original swap is \na long position and a short position in two annual payment \nbonds that mature in exactly three years:\nShort a GBP 40 million par value, 3% fixed-coupon bond\nLong a EUR 50 million floating-rate coupon based on the \nsame reference interest rate as the floating leg of the swap.\nKim then asks Bardot to use a two-period binomial model \nto calculate the value of a 2-year European call on Gondorf\nCorporation common stock, based on the following \ninformation:\n\nThe current stock price is 48 and the call option has an \nexercise price of 50.\nThe up factor (u) is 1.221, and the down factor (d) is 0.8187.\nThe risk-free rate is 3% per period, and the company does \nnot pay a common dividend.\nKim is considering using the Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) \nmodel to value equity put options.  However, Kim is more \nfamiliar with binomial models than with BSM, so he asks \nBardot to describe how the BSM model values options.\nKim and Bardot discuss the use of the option Greeks for \nmeasuring and managing the risks of option positions.  \nDuring the discussion, Kim describes two interpretations of \ndeltas:\nInterpretation 1:\nThe hedge ratio\nInterpretation 2:\nThe fraction of one share that creates \nmarket exposure equal to one option", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Ismail Tang is the treasurer at Vela Corp., a retailer \nspecializing in sailboat equipment and accessories.  Vela, a \nmature company that has historically distributed a \nsubstantial portion of its profits as dividends, is facing \nincreasing competition along with declining revenues and \nearnings.\nVela's dividend policy consists of gradually adjusting \ndividends to a target payout ratio.  Tang provides the \nfollowing information to Freya Rochester, a junior analyst \non his team, and asks her to estimate this year's dividends:\nIn 20X7, Vela had an EPS of $3.20 and paid dividends per \nshare of $1.80.\nThe expected EPS for 20X8 is $2.00.\nThe target payout ratio is 60%, with a four-year period to \nadjust dividends.\nRochester concludes that, according to the dividend policy, \nVela would pay less in dividends in 20X8 than in the \nprevious year.  She asks Tang how investors and analysts \ncould interpret such a decrease.  Tang says:\nStatement 1: Dividend reductions typically send negative \nsignals to investors, assuming perfect capital markets.\nStatement 2: Companies with poor earnings can increase \ndividends to attract investors and increase share price.\nStatement 3: Cutting dividends may send a positive signal \nin some situations.\nTang wants to consider the implications of repurchasing \nVela's shares instead of paying dividends.  He is specifically \nconcerned about the impact of a share repurchase on \nVela's book value per share.  He asks Rochester to gather \ninformation, summarized in Exhibit 1:\nTang then asks Rochester to estimate Vela's coverage ratios \nfor 20X8 and assess how these ratios would be affected by \nchanges in the company's payout policy.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Buena Comida is a Mexico-based food manufacturing \ncompany that operates in Central and South America and \nhas facilities in Brazil, Peru, and Argentina.  Buena Comida's\nreporting currency is MXN.  On Jan 1 20X7 Buena Comida \ncreated a European subsidiary, Euro Comida.  All PPE for \nEuro Comida was purchased on Jan 1 20X7.  At the end of \n20X7, Euro Comida reported the data shown in Exhibit 1:\nManagement has elected to use EUR as Euro Comida's\nfunctional currency.  Relevant exchange rates (using the \nprice/base currency convention) are shown in Exhibit 2:\nMaría Vargas, Buena Comida's CEO, asks the CFO, Rafael \nMendoza, what would happen to the subsidiary's return on \nassets ratio in 20X7 if Euro Comida used MXN as its \nfunctional currency instead of EUR.\nVargas also asks about the foreign exchange risks to Buena \nComida over the next year.  Mendoza explains that Buena \nComida hedges most, but not all, of its foreign exchange \nexposure.  He compiles information on cash flows exposed\n\nto foreign currency fluctuations and expected exchange \nrates over the next year, shown in Exhibit 3:\nIn the fourth quarter of 20X7, Buena Comida has the \nfollowing transactions with a Brazilian company that will be \nsettled in BRL:\nPurchases of inventory worth BRL 50,000\nSales of finished products worth BRL 75,000\nBoth transactions will settle in 20X8.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Alamo Trust is a large wealth manager that caters to high-\nnet-worth clients.  Tara Lawson, an analyst at Alamo, is \nresponsible for creating model portfolios that meet certain \nrisk and return objectives.\nThe Goliad Equity Fund is included in several of Alamo's \nmore aggressive model portfolios.  This fund invests in \nlarge-cap growth stocks and has a historical realized active \nrisk of 4%.  The fund has numerous portfolio construction \nconstraints, such as long-only construction, a minimum \nmarket cap, position limits, and a portfolio turnover of no \nmore than 40%.\nDuring a regularly scheduled quarterly call with David \nPeterson, the fund's portfolio manager of the Goliad Fund, \nLawson asks if it is possible to increase the fund's targeted \nactive risk to 5% in an attempt to increase returns.  \nPeterson responds by stating that according to the \nfundamental law, if the active-risk target is increased, there \nwill be three results:\nStatement 1: The transfer coefficient will decrease.\nStatement 2: The information ratio will increase.\nStatement 3: The portfolio's expected active returns will \nincrease.\nLater that day, Lawson evaluates the investment merits of a \nhedge fund that is based on a low-latency trading strategy.  \nInterested in the strategy, Lawson stops by the firm's \ntrading desk to discuss the strategy's merits with the head \nequity trader, Joe Davis.\nShe asks Davis to briefly describe the ways low-latency \ntraders gain a competitive edge.  Davis has just received a \nlarge order to buy 1 million shares of TNT Corp., so he \nsimply replies with:\nTaking\nMaking\nCanceling\n\nDavis quickly refocuses his attention on the TNT trade.  \nDavis is speaking to a sell-side trader who offers to provide \nan immediate fill on 50,000 shares of TNT at $120.45.  Davis \nagrees to the offer, and the trade is done.\nHowever, Davis is sensitive to the potential market impact \nof an order this large.  Therefore, he instructs the sell-side \ntrader to use one of the brokerage firm's trading algorithms \nto minimize the market impact, and to buy the remaining \n950,000 shares on the condition that the trade must be \ncompleted by the end of the day.\nAfter the market close, the trader reports the details of the \ncompleted trade, presented in Exhibit 1:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock1_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 1 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Hondo Billups manages a portfolio for a fixed-income asset \nmanagement company.  Billups asks Mary Ling, an analyst \nat the firm, to construct binomial interest trees based on a \nspot rate curve derived from the government par bonds \n(shown in Exhibit 1) for use in evaluating fixed-income \nsecurities.\nBillups has Ling construct one tree using the historical \ninterest rate volatility of 12% and another tree using the \nimplied volatility of 20%, obtained from interest rate \nderivatives.  Billups asks Ling about her approach to \nconstructing the trees.  In response, Ling makes the \nfollowing statements:\nStatement 1: For a given benchmark yield curve, a \nbinomial tree can be constructed for any desired interest \nrate volatility.\nStatement 2: A tree is calibrated by solving for the 1-\nperiod forward rates that replicate the price of the par \nbonds used in constructing the tree.\nStatement 3: A properly calibrated tree has volatilities at \neach period that equal the values from the term structure \nof volatility for that maturity.\nWhile reviewing Ling's work, Billups calculates the \narbitrage-free value of an off-the-run 3-year government \ncoupon bond.  The resulting value based on the \ngovernment zero-coupon curve is greater than the value \ndetermined from the binomial tree constructed using 20% \nvolatility.\nBillups then gives Ling two tasks.  First, he asks her to \nevaluate the interest rate risk of a government bond \nportfolio.  The firm's economists use a yield curve factor \nmodel to forecast interest rates.  They forecast that the \nlevel and slope of the yield curve will remain essentially\n\nunchanged but that there will be a significant change in the \ncurvature of the yield curve.  Second, Billups asks Ling to \nsummarize the implications of the usual shape of the \nvolatility curve for bond portfolio risk", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock1_s2_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Daniel Nguyen is a CFA Level II Candidate who was recently \nhired as a junior analyst at Broadscope & Company.  \nBroadscope is an investment bank based in the United States \nwhose clients include large multinational entities.  Broadscope\noffers investment banking and investment advisory services, \nand it conducts sales and trading in many countries around the \nworld.  In some countries, securities laws and regulations are \nless strict than those in the US, while in other countries, the \nlaws are more strict.\nWhen underwriting securities outside the United States, \nBroadscope enters into a syndication agreement with \ninvestment banks located in the countries where the securities \nwill be sold.  The syndication agreement explicitly states that \nthe local investment banks in a given country are responsible \nfor ensuring that sales of all securities within that country \ncomply with local laws and regulations.\nNguyen is assigned to a group that is underwriting an issue of \ncallable bonds by Stone Industries in several countries, \nincluding the United States and Country A.  Country A permits \nonly accredited investors to invest in derivatives, and Country \nA's laws treat callable bonds as derivative securities.  Nguyen's \nsupervisor, Farah Baghdadi, CFA, is aware of this restriction, but \nNguyen is not.  The local investment bank in Country A sold \nsome of the bonds to nonaccredited investors.\nDuring the underwriting process, Nguyen spoke frequently with \nPaul Marchesse, a senior analyst at Stone Industries, about \nissues relating to the underwriting.  During one of these \nconversations, Marchesse told Nguyen, \"I've heard from a \ncouple of sources at Barstow Conglomerates that they really like \nwhat Broadscope has been able to do for Stone Industries and \nthey are considering giving Broadscope some underwriting \nbusiness.\"  Nguyen relayed this information to Baghdadi, who \naccordingly advised Broadscope's business development team.\nVincent Pardew, CFA, is a senior vice president with Broadscope\nwho leads the sales team in the United States for the Stone \nIndustries bonds.  Twenty institutional clients expressed \ninterest in purchasing the bonds, but based on their investment \npolicy statements, Pardew concluded that the bonds would be \nsuitable for only five of them.  In addition, three of those five \nclients had discretionary accounts with Broadscope; the other \ntwo were nondiscretionary.\n\nThe Stone Industries issue was greatly oversubscribed.  In \ntotal, the clients for whom Pardew believed the bonds \nwould be suitable placed orders for $10 million in face \nvalue for the bonds, but Pardew received only $4 million in \nface value.  Pardew allocated the bonds only to the \ndiscretionary clients on a pro-rata basis.\nNguyen wants to take a second job to generate more \nincome.  He contacts Cleaner Air Products, for whom he \nworked as an inventory clerk during college.  Cleaner Air \nProducts offers him his former position, and he arranges \nthe schedule so that it does not conflict with his duties at \nBroadscope.  Before accepting, however, he asks Baghdadi \nto approve this arrangement.  Baghdadi replies, \"Yeah, I \nguess so.  Just make sure it doesn't interfere with what \nyou're doing for us.\"", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Modesto Gallardo is an equity analyst at an asset \nmanagement firm.  One of the firm's portfolio managers \nasks Gallardo to analyze Legado Bank, a local commercial \nbank.  Gallardo decides to start his analysis using the \nCAMELS approach and Legado's key performance ratios, \nwhich are shown in Exhibit 1:\nGallardo is concerned about the quality of Legado Bank's \nearnings.  He analyzes the bank's income statement, \nlooking for signs of potential problems.  Summarized data is \npresented in Exhibit 2:\nGallardo knows that Legado Bank offers a defined benefit \npension plan to all its employees.  In 20X2, the bank \nreported a net pension asset in its balance sheet.  Gallardo \nwants to better understand the plan's pension obligation \nand funding status and asks his colleague, Irina Belda, for \nsupport on this issue.  Belda makes the following \nstatements:\nStatement 1: The pension obligation is the difference \nbetween the PV of future obligations and the fair value of \nthe plan's assets.\nStatement 2: The discount rate is based on the plan \nsponsor's cost of debt.\n\nStatement 3: The plan surplus may be greater than the \nreported net pension asset.\nBelda informs Gallardo that companies typically report \nimportant information about their pension plans in the \nnotes to the financial statements.  For example, Legado \nBank presents the effects that potential changes in pension \nplan assumptions would have on pension obligations.  \nGallardo uses Exhibit 3 to estimate how a 1 percentage \npoint increase in the discount rate would affect Legado \nBank's debt-to-equity ratio.  Currently, the bank's \nshareholders' equity is €47 billion and total liabilities are \n€517 billion.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Romeo Bulan, an equity analyst at Quezon Capital, \nconsiders a stock to be over- or undervalued in the market \nif the stock's price differs by more than 5% from his \nvaluation.  Bulan is evaluating the investment prospect of \nCandon, a packaged food producer.  On the first day of \n20X2, Candon's current share price is $55.50.  Selected \nforecasted financial data are presented in Exhibit 1:\nBulan also forecasts the following specific financial line \nitems and assumes growth in free cash flow to equity \n(FCFE) will match the long-term earnings growth rate \nbeginning in 20X5:\nNext, Bulan focuses on Tangub, a retail drugstore chain that \ncurrently has a market price of $60 per common share.  \nBulan compiles the following selected 20X1 financial \ninformation in Exhibit 3 to conduct a relative valuation \nanalysis:\n\nBulan also collects drugstore industry data for his relative \nvaluation analysis, as presented in Exhibit 4:\nLater that day, Bulan discusses the merits of various \nvaluation methods with a colleague.  Bulan states that, \nrelative to a FCF model, residual income (RI) models:\nStatement 1: Require minimal accounting data input \nadjustments\nStatement 2: Are less reliant on terminal value estimates\nStatement 3: Provide a more accurate intrinsic value \nestimate", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Kayla Gharib is a macro strategist at a university \nendowment.  Nora Jabari is an investment analyst at the \nendowment and works closely with Gharib.  Gharib and \nJabari are discussing key macro assumptions that affect the \nendowment's investment portfolio.\nJabari provides a recap of current macro interest rate \nconditions:\nOver the past year, inflation has averaged 3%.\nThe real yield on the one-year inflation-protected \ngovernment bond is currently −2%.\nThe current break-even inflation rate is 2%.\nThe market's consensus is that the business cycle appears \nto have peaked, the yield curve has inverted, and an \neconomic contraction seems imminent.  The central bank \nhas indicated a policy bias toward easing monetary policy, \nand current market expectations are for the policy rate to \nbe lowered by 1% over the coming year, with an associated \nparallel shift in the yield curve.\nBased on this macroeconomic backdrop, Gharib and Jabari \ndiscuss the upcoming investment prospects for several \nbond indexes, all of which have a five-year average maturity \nand four-year duration profile.\nJabari makes the following statement regarding the one-\nyear return outlook for the indexes:\nStatement 1: Since the interest rate risk is the same for all \nthree indexes, all three should benefit equally from falling \ninterest rates and have similar performance.\n\nGharib counters by stating:\nStatement 2: The investment grade corporate index \nshould outperform the government bond index.\nStatement 3: The high yield index is likely to be the best \nperformer as it has an inherent yield advantage.\nShifting the focus of the conversation to corporate earnings \ngrowth expectations in relation to the business cycle, \nGharib makes the following observations:\nObservation 1:A leading indicator of the business cycle is \nthe trend in corporate profit margins.\nObservation 2:A downturn in the business cycle will \ngenerally result in an equal decline in profit margins across \ndurable and nondurable industries.\nObservation 3:A sharp decline in real earnings nearly \nalways coincides with a recession.\nWith the potential for a downturn in the business cycle, the \nconversation shifts once again to the consumption hedging \nproperties of bonds and equities.  Jabari makes the \nfollowing comments:\nComment 1:\nThe equity risk premium can be thought of \nas a spread relative to credit-risky bonds.\nComment 2:\nThe consumption hedging properties of \nequities are a fundamental driver for investors to demand \nan equity risk premium.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Domenic Guimond is a principal at Mintaka Partners, a \nventure capital (VC) firm.  In 20X7, Mintaka invested in \nCardShield, a cybersecurity start-up that uses artificial \nintelligence to prevent credit card fraud.  Due to \nCardShield's impressive performance in predicting and \npreventing fraud, its client base and growth prospects \nincreased substantially in 20X8, attracting more investors \nfor a Series B financing.\nGuimond asks Tatiana Goyette, a junior associate, to \ncalculate the firm's implied ROI after this second round of \nfinancing.  Goyette prepares data (shown in Exhibit 1) \nrelated to CardShield's financing by Mintaka and other \ninvestors in 20X7 (Series A) and by other VC funds in 20X8 \n(Series B).\nGuimond adds that the estimated return reflected in the \nimplied ROI is based on the current valuation level, but the \nactual return will depend on other factors, such as the \nchosen exit route and the exit timing.  He asks about the \ndifferences between alternative exit routes, and Goyette \nmakes the following statements:\nStatement 1: An IPO is a more common exit alternative \nthan a secondary market sale.\nStatement 2: A secondary market sale typically results in a \ngreater valuation than a management buyout (MBO).\nStatement 3: An IPO aligns the interests of management \nand investors more efficiently than an MBO.\nGuimond then focuses on Mintaka Capital 3 (MC3), the VC \nfund that holds the investment in CardShield, among other \nstart-ups.  He asks Goyette to gather information about the \ncapital already called down, operating results, fees, and \ndistributions to limited partners.  Goyette prepares Exhibit\n\n2 with the accumulated amounts since the fund's inception \nin 20X4:\nGoyette tells Guimond that she would like to better \nunderstand the structure and life cycle of private equity \n(PE) funds.  Guimond lists the following characteristics of PE \nfunds:\nCharacteristic 1:\nThe liability of limited partners is \nlimited to their proportional share of the fund's liabilities.\nCharacteristic 2:\nProceeds from sales of investments \nare returned to limited partners when the fund is dissolved.\nCharacteristic 3:\nPE funds typically have a duration of \n10 to 12 years plus an extension of 2 to 3 years.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Aditya Lanka manages a bond fund for an asset \nmanagement company, and Arina Skya is a fixed-income \nanalyst on his team.  Lanka and Skya discuss the use of \nbinomial interest rate trees for valuing straight debt \nsecurities and bonds with embedded options.  During the \ndiscussion, Lanka asks Skya to explain pathwise valuation \nfor a single interest rate path.  Skya describes two \nalternative approaches to pathwise valuation that result in \nthe same value:\nApproach 1:\nUse period-by-period discounting along a \nsingle interest rate path, from maturity to Time 0.\nApproach 2:\nSum the PV of all periods' cash flows, with \neach discounted by the appropriate term spot rates implied \nby that interest rate path.\nOn Aug 15 20X3, Lanka asks Skya to develop a binomial \ninterest rate tree to evaluate three 3-year bonds of Tiptri\nCorporation being issued that day.  Information on the \nbonds is shown in Exhibit 1:\nExhibit 2 contains the binomial tree developed by Skya:\n\nLanka and Skya then discuss the arbitrage-free valuation of \nthe Tiptri convertible callable bond.  Skya makes the \nfollowing comments about the possible range and \nlimitations of the bond's no-arbitrage price:\nComment 1:\nWhen the market price of Tiptri's common \nstock is less than the bond's conversion price, the bond will \ntrade at a discount to its conversion value.\nComment 2:\nOnce the bond becomes callable, the bond \nshould not trade at more than a slight premium to its call \nprice.\nComment 3:\nWhen the bond is considered a busted \nconvertible, its minimum value is the value of an otherwise \nequivalent nonconvertible bond.\nAssume all interest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Sommay Siwong is the senior portfolio manager of an asset \nmanagement firm.  Siwong is considering increasing the \nfirm's exposure to Seginus Stores, a large chain of home \nfurnishings stores.  Seginus's management recently issued a \npress release announcing its intention to acquire 100% of \nHamal Home, a smaller company that operates in the same \nsegment.\nSiwong directs Janjira Tay, an equity analyst who reports to \nhim, to perform an initial evaluation of the acquisition.  He \nmakes the following statements to guide her analysis:\nStatement 1: The acquisition costs 35% of Seginus's EV but \ndoes not change Seginus's strategy or focus, so it is \nconsidered immaterial.\nStatement 2: The greater the size and complexity of the \ntransaction, the longer the timeline from announcement to \nclosing.\nStatement 3: Comparable company analysis is used to \nunderstand the acquisition and its motivations.\nAfter analyzing Seginus's and Hamal's 20X9 forecasted \nincome statements, Tay creates a combined income \nstatement considering the acquisition.  She explains to \nSiwong that the combined statement incorporates \nassumptions of synergies and acquired intangible assets \nand that cash costs related to the combination are \ninsignificant.  She presents the statements in Exhibit 1:\n\nTay also gathers the following information based on \nSeginus's press release and on its financial statements:\nBefore the acquisition, Seginus had annual interest \nexpenses of $50 million and 200 million outstanding shares.  \nHamal does not have any debt.\nTo finance the acquisition, Seginus expects to issue $500 \nmillion in additional debt (at an annual cost of 6%) and 50 \nmillion new shares.\nSeginus's effective tax rate is expected to remain stable at \n25%.\nSiwong turns his attention to Bubbles Bev, a manufacturer \nof soft drinks.  Recently, Bubbles entered the energy drinks \nmarket, which has been growing faster than the soft drinks \nsegment.  Bubbles' management believes that the company \nis currently trading with a conglomerate discount and \nstudies a potential sale or spin-off of its soft drinks \nsegment.\nSiwong requests the most recent data about Bubbles's\ncapitalization and EV multiples in the beverage industry.  \nTay gathers this market data, summarized as follows:\nBubbles has 200 million shares outstanding at a market \nprice of $35.00 per share.\nBubbles' debt has a market value of $4.0 billion.\nPeers in the soft drinks segment have a median EV-to-\nEBITDA multiple of 8.0.\nPeers in the energy drinks segment have a median EV-to-\nsales (EV/S) multiple of 12.0.\nTay also presents Bubbles' most recent financial data by \nsegment in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Mariel Olidan manages an option trading desk for Bearing \nBrothers (BB), a global investment bank where Jare Fento is \na trader.  During a discussion of option pricing models, \nOlidan asks Fento to explain how the Black-Scholes-Merton \n(BSM) model values European equity options.\nThen Olidan asks Fento to explain the differences between \nthe BSM model for valuing equity options and the Black \nmodel for valuing equity futures options.  In particular, \nOlidan wants to understand the reasons why the two \nmodels typically calculate different no-arbitrage values for \nseemingly similar equity and futures options (ie, both \noptions at the money with the same time to expiration).  \nFento makes two statements to explain the differences in \nvalue between the two types of options:\nStatement 1: The primary difference between the values \nof similar equity options and equity futures options is due \nto differences in carry costs, which are significantly higher \nfor equities than for a futures contract.\nStatement 2: The BSM model equity option values reflect \nthe difference between the PV of the equity price and the \nPV of the option exercise price; the Black model equity \nfutures option values reflect the PV of the difference \nbetween the futures price and option exercise price.\nThe BB trading desk is currently short 1,000 1-month \nPontus Corporation calls with a 120 strike price.  Olidan\nwants to hedge the position before the current trading \nsession ends since Pontus will announce earnings shortly \nafter the market closes.  Olidan asks Fento to identify \npossible strategies to make the short call position delta \nneutral.  Exhibit 1 contains information on Pontus stock and \n1-month options Fento is considering for the delta-neutral \nstrategy.\n\nOlidan and Fento notice that implied volatility (vol) has \ngenerally increased for options on most equities.  During \nthe discussion of the general rise in implied vols, Fento\nprovides two possible explanations:\nExplanation 1: Higher implied vols indicate the price of risk \nhas increased, which means a greater cost to hedge risk \nwith long options.\nExplanation 2: Higher implied vol can be driven by an \nimbalance between the supply and demand for options in \nwhich, on balance, there are more speculators and hedgers \nseeking to sell options than the number of such traders \nseeking to buy options.\nAll options discussed by Olidan and Fento are European \noptions, and each option contract is on one share of the \nunderlying stock.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Pierre LeBlanc is an emerging markets strategist at a buy-\nside firm.  LeBlanc is discussing the investment prospects of \nvarious emerging market countries with Noelle Roche, the \nportfolio manager for the firm's emerging market fund.  \nRoche is particularly concerned about Nazca, an emerging \nmarket country in South America where the fund has \nseveral investments.\nNazca's currency is pegged to the US dollar (USD).  Nazca \nhas embarked on aggressive expansionary fiscal and \nmonetary policies over the past several years, resulting in a \nsizeable trade and current account deficit.  This deficit was \nfunded primarily with short-term dollar-denominated debt \nsold to foreigners.\nNazca recently signed a free trade agreement with the \nUnited States that the two countries had been working on \nfor several years.  Over the past few years, in anticipation of \nthe deal closing, there has been a surge of capital inflows, \nand local companies have significantly increased debt levels \nto build the infrastructure necessary for taking advantage of \nthe free trade environment.  The net effect of these \ndevelopments has put significant upward pressure on \nNazca's currency.\nRecently, capital flows appeared to hit a key inflection point \nand have reversed dramatically over a short period of time.  \nSpeculators and other leveraged market participants have \nbegun aggressively selling the local currency.  To maintain \nthe currency peg, the country's central bank has been \nbuying the local currency, but the cost of issuing new debt \nhas become prohibitive and the central bank's reserves are \nalmost exhausted.  In response to these developments, \nNazca's central bank has announced capital controls that \nprevent foreign investors from repatriating funds.\nBased on these events, LeBlanc believes the following \noutcomes are likely:\nOutcome 1:\nMost investors will view the capital controls \nfavorably, easing selling pressure on the local currency.\nOutcome 2:\nNazca's currency will appreciate, and the \ncentral bank will reset the peg with the USD to a higher \nlevel.\nRoche is also concerned about Nazca's geographic neighbor \nAtacama, which has taken a completely different path\n\ntoward trade.  Over the past several decades, Atacama \ndeveloped inward-oriented policies allowing domestic \nindustries to develop locally produced substitutes for goods \npreviously imported.\nAtacama's GDP per capita remains low, and growth has \nstagnated.  There is also a large informal economy and \nworkforce.  The country is now considering policy changes \nin an attempt to increase its GDP growth and standards of \nliving.\nLeBlanc lists Atacama's proposed new policies:\nPolicy 1:\nLower the income tax rates.\nPolicy 2:\nEncourage foreign direct investments to \nbuild factories that focus on exports.\nPolicy 3:\nImpose higher tariffs on imports of goods \nproduced by certain protected domestic industries.\nThe conversation then shifts to a discussion on the \ncharacteristics of developed versus developing markets.  \nLeBlanc argues that numerous factors limit growth in \ndeveloping countries relative to developed countries.  For \nexample, he cites antitrust regulation as a key protection \nfor investors in developed markets.\nLeBlanc continues by stating that, relative to developed \ncountries, economic growth in developing countries is \nlimited by the following key factors:\nFactor 1:\nHigh savings rates that result in an inefficient \nallocation of capital, reducing investment\nFactor 2:\nRegulatory policies that discourage \nentrepreneurship\nFactor 3:\nPoorly developed financial markets, which \ninhibit capital formation", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Globetechz is a multinational corporation headquartered in \nGermany with subsidiaries and investments across the \nworld.  Globetechz adheres to IFRS reporting standards.\nOn Jan 1 20X1 Globetechz acquired 30% of Floridex, a \nGerman manufacturer, for €3,600,000.  Globetechz will use \nthe equity method to account for its investment in Floridex.\nOn Jan 1 20X1, the book value of net assets was \n€10,000,000.\nDuring 20X1, Floridex sold €120,000 of inventory upstream \nto Globetechz for €150,000.  On Dec 31 20X1, €30,000 of \nthat inventory remained unsold by Globetechz.\nFloridex reported net income of €800,000 for 20X1 and paid \n€100,000 in dividends.\nHeidi Holmon is Globetechz's new chief strategy officer.  \nShe recently came to the company from a large US-based \ncompetitor that reports under US GAAP.  Holmon is \nunfamiliar with IFRS rules for intercorporate investments \nand asks Gretchen Goodman, the chief accounting officer, \nto explain some of the differences.  Goodman cites the \nfollowing differences:\nDifference 1: Under US GAAP, the fair value option is \navailable for all equity method investments.  Under IFRS, \nthe fair value option is available only to certain \norganizations, such as venture capital firms and unit trusts.\nDifference 2: US GAAP require objective evidence of one \nor more events that resulted in a loss in order to record \nimpairment on an investment in an associate, whereas with \nIFRS, impairment is recorded when the fair value of the \ninvestment falls below its carrying value.\nDifference 3: Under the acquisition method, both US \nGAAP and IFRS allow for recognition of partial goodwill.\nGoodman then focuses on the effective tax rate for \nGlobetechz over the past three years.  All of Globetechz's\nforeign subsidiaries operate in countries with higher \ncorporate tax rates than Germany.  She notices that the rate \nhas been increasing (as shown in Exhibit 1) and wants to \nfind the cause.\n\nFinally, Holman and Goodman assess several operating \nsegments for sales performance, as shown in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s1", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 1", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Simba Sefu is the Chief Strategy Officer at Safri Co., a large \nfinancial institution.  Safri has achieved remarkable organic \ngrowth but plans to expand through acquisitions in the near \nfuture.  Sefu meets with Nuru Narla, the corporate \ndevelopment manager at the firm, to discuss potential \nacquisition targets.\nThey first review several technology companies in distinct \nverticals.  Narla plans to value each company using the \nmarket-based valuation approach.  She gathers a \ncomparable market value of invested capital-to-EBITDA \n(MVIC-to-EBITDA) reference for each potential target \ncompany (shown in Exhibit 1).  Sefu asks which transaction \nwould warrant a valuation premium adjustment with \nrespect to its MVIC-to-EBITDA reference.\nNext, they assess Ranko Tech, a fintech software startup \nwith a unique proprietary platform that is rapidly capturing \na growing customer base.  No other competition exists in \nthis space.  Although Ranko has experienced rapid growth, \nit has reported negative earnings in the past year due to \nsignificant trade-show promotional expenses.  Normalized \nearnings would have been significantly positive after \nadjusting for these expenses and are expected to \ncontinually grow for the foreseeable future.  Based on this \nprofile, Sefu tries to determine the appropriate valuation \nmethodology.\nThey then focus on Jengo LLC, a luxury goods lending \ncompany.  To value Jengo, they need to determine the \nappropriate discount rate.  Due to Jengo's small size, its \ndebt capacity is very low and its financial statements are\n\nunaudited; the company has historically experienced \nchallenges in accurately forecasting financial results.  Narla\ncomments on several factors to consider when estimating \nJengo's discount rate:\nComment 1:\nUsing expanded CAPM to calculate Jengo's \ndiscount rate requires adding an industry premium.\nComment 2:\nDue to its lower debt capacity and reliance \non other sources of capital, Jengo would have a lower \ndiscount rate compared with otherwise similar public \ncompetitors.\nComment 3:\nThe discount rate should be adjusted upward \nfor the lack of reliable financial forecasts.\nFinally, they consider Lasco, a wealth management firm.  \nNarla gathers the following information in Exhibit 2 to value \n100% of Lasco using the Guideline Public Company Method \n(GPCM):", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s1_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Marie Anouilh, CFA, is a fund manager at Navigator Investment \nManagement, managing the firm's High Tech, Biotech, and \nFintech Disruptors (HTBFD) Fund.  She is planning to meet with \na potential client later that day and prepares by reviewing one \nof the firm's marketing pamphlets.  The pamphlet states:\nStatement 1: Our firm employs only CFA charterholders to \nmanage our funds since they have enhanced their financial \nskills through a well-rounded education in many asset classes \nand portfolio management.\nStatement 2: Our funds have outperformed their benchmarks \nover the past five years.  Given our managers' enhanced skill \nsets due to their CFA education, the funds should continue to \ndo so.\nOne of the fund managers, Henry Paradis, recently passed the \nCFA Level III exam.  His application was accepted by CFA \nInstitute, and he has signed the Professional Conduct \nStatement, but he is deciding which society to join and has not \nyet paid his fees.  The other fund managers have active CFA \ndesignations.\nAnouilh typically trades through Insight Brokerage, which \nprovides research to Navigator through a soft dollar agreement.  \nAnouilh often requests specific reports and shares these reports \nwith her analysts to help them generate ideas for the fund; the \nanalysts also perform all checks on the reports' sources and \naccuracy.  Anouilh uses the reports herself in conjunction with \nthe internal reports generated by Navigator.  Since the reports \nare used regularly in the research process, she does not \ndisclose the Insight agreement to clients.\nLater that day, Anouilh meets with a client, Nicolas Chastain, to \ndiscuss Navigator's performance and various funds.  Chastain \nhas a long time horizon and does not plan to withdraw funds \nfor several years.  Anouilh presents Navigator's marketing \nmaterials, which include the performance of each fund and \nstrategy.  She suggests Chastain review the performance of the \nHTBFD Fund.  Chastain notes that the HTBFD Fund is the only \nfund without a benchmark listed.  Anouilh explains that the \nHTBFD Fund's strategy is unique, so there are no compatible \nbenchmarks.\n\nChastain notices that Navigator does not have any small-cap \nfunds and asks Anouilh about putting money into small \ncaps.  Anouilh responds that \"Navigator can invest in small-\ncap strategies but only does so upon a client's request.\"  \nShe does not mention that Navigator uses external \nmanagers to execute client-requested small-cap strategies.\nChastain then asks about two other investment funds: the \nEmerging Market Bond (EMB) Fund and the Macro Fund.  \nAnouilh explains that the EMB Fund tends to be volatile but \nusually outperforms its benchmark.  Since Chastain does \nnot plan to withdraw any money for years, Anouilh does \nnot discuss the EMB Fund's strategic use of derivatives, \nwhich gives it limited liquidity.\nAnouilh explains that the Macro Fund forecasts returns \nusing a complex statistical model, the details of which are \navailable upon request.  She adds that the model has been \nbacktested and the firm believes, based on the model, that \nemerging markets will outperform over the next year.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Sylvia Merced is the performance attribution analyst at \nSound Financial, a mutual fund firm, where she works with \nDan Chang, portfolio manager of the Sound Equity Fund.  \nChang and Merced are discussing the outlook for the \neconomy and the markets, as well as the implications for \nthe fund.\nChang predicts the economy will weaken, with a deep \nrecession beginning before the end of the year.  He tells \nMerced that the equity markets will price in a recession \nwell ahead of time, as he believes equities are overvalued \nand susceptible to a decline.  Based on his predictions, \nChang plans to implement changes to mitigate the negative \nimpact to the fund's return.\nThe Sound Equity Fund consists of 25 equally weighted, \nlarge-cap stocks that are rebalanced at the end of each \nyear.  After reviewing last year's relative performance, \nChang hypothesizes that more frequent rebalancing would \nhave improved the fund's information ratio, and he asks \nMerced to examine how quarterly rebalancing would have \nchanged this metric.  Merced gathers the information \nshown in Exhibit 1:\nWith respect to the information in Exhibit 1:\nChang revises each stock's expected return at rebalancing.\nChang assumes his active returns are uncorrelated over \ntime.\nTC and IC remain the same irrespective of rebalancing \nscheme.\nChang also discusses with Merced what investors, in \ngeneral, might expect to happen to market valuation \nmeasures if there is a recession, as he forecasts.  They \ncreate a macroeconomic factor model to help them \nforecast the fund's return for the full year.  Merced\n\nleads this project, and after analysis and testing against the \nfund's prior returns, she derives a macroeconomic model \nwith the following factors:\nUnemployment rate (FUNEM)\nConsumer spending growth (FCONS)\nGDP growth (FGDP)\nIn the model, Merced uses a sampling of consensus \npredictions from Wall Street strategists as the baseline \nvalues (ie, predicted values) for the chosen factors.  Merced \nnotes that the consensus view is more optimistic than her \nown expectations for the new year.  Based on her \ndiscussions with Chang, her model predictions (ie, actual \nvalues) represent what she believes will happen by the end \nof the current year.  The model's estimated intercept is \n+4.50% and the error term is +0.63%; the predictions and \nfactor sensitivities are shown in Exhibit 2:\nTo reduce some of the negative impact of an adverse \nmarket environment, Chang decides to allocate 25% of the \nSound Equity Fund to one equity ETF.  Merced screens the \nETF universe and arrives at three choices for Chang's \nconsideration:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "The Horizon Global Real Estate Fund is a Luxembourg-based \ninstitutional fund that invests in publicly listed real estate \nsecurities (eg, REITS) across the world, as well as direct real \nestate properties within the European Union (EU).\nMia Vogel, a lead analyst, is conducting due diligence on De \nLuxe, an existing mixed-use (eg, housing/shopping) \ndevelopment that the fund plans to purchase for its EU \ndirect real estate allocation.  During her due diligence \nreview, she finds one item of concern: improvement \nallowances that were given to several major tenants for \nleased space upgrades may have been excessive.  Horizon's \nportfolio manager, Florian Sommer, comments on Vogel's \nconcern:\nComment 1:\nA cash allowance for tenant improvements is \nnot expensed by the landlord but instead capitalized and \namortized over the lease term.\nComment 2:\nAn examination of the most recent \nunaudited financial statements will help determine \nwhether these incentives are in line with local real estate \nconditions.\nVogel then shifts to the financing of Horizon's purchase of \nDe Luxe.  She wants to know the maximum loan Horizon \ncan expect under current market conditions.  Vogel \nestimates De Luxe's net operating income (NOI) at €12.6 \nmillion.  Based on this estimate and a purchase price of \n€152 million, Norstad Commercial Bank offers the \nfollowing:\nIn addition to the De Luxe assignment, Sommer asks Vogel \nto calculate the net asset value per share (NAVPS) of a \nprospective holding, Nexbit Realty Trust, a US data center \nREIT.  Sommer believes Nexbit's stock price is trading at a \npremium to its net asset value.\n\nVogel calculates Nexbit's NAVPS by capitalizing its income \nstreams, and after reviewing its financials, she estimates a \nnext-12-month NOI growth rate of 2.50% and a 5.80% \ncapitalization rate (cap rate).  Nexbit has 255 million shares \noutstanding.\nThe next day, Vogel meets with her colleague Emil Kaiser to \nreview a draft of a new marketing brochure Kaiser created \nfor Horizon.  Vogel asks for clarity on a couple of statements \nKaiser included in the brochure concerning the advantages \nof public and private real estate investing:\nStatement 1: Listed public real estate tracks real estate \nfundamentals over the long term.\nStatement 2: Private real estate investors have the ability \nto pursue merchant development, which is restricted for \npublic REITs.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Yiannis Costa is a fixed-income analyst at a buy-side firm.  \nMaria Andino is a summer intern assigned to Costa's team.  \nBefore giving Andino a project, Costa wants to assess her \nconceptual knowledge of effective duration.  Costa provides \nAndino with Exhibit 1:\nTo gauge her knowledge of effective convexity and various \nforms of duration, Costa then presents Andino with an \ninterest rate scenario:\nA 100 basis points (bps) parallel downward shift of the \ngovernment par curve\nNo change in Acme Industries bond's credit spread\nInterest rate volatility is unchanged\nLastly, Costa tests Andino's knowledge of convertible bonds \nand provides her with Exhibit 2:\nAssume all bonds pay coupons once per year and all \ninterest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Stefanie Jager, a senior analyst covering the retail sector, \nwants to integrate quantitative forecasts into her analysis.  \nShe asks Hans Obermann, a newly hired associate, to run \nseveral time series regressions.  First, Jager asks Obermann\nto forecast retail sales for a number of developed countries \nthat have been growing steadily over the past decade.  \nObermann creates a 1-period autoregressive model on the \nmonthly retail sales data, not adjusted for seasonality, using \nthe equation xt = b0 + b1xt − 1 + εt for each country.\nJager covers several companies that do business in the \nUnited States and wants to forecast the USD/EUR exchange \nrate.  Obermann uses an autoregressive model on the \nweekly historical exchange rate with a 1-period lag to \nforecast USD/EUR.  Reviewing the data, Jager indicates that \nthe model may have a unit root and asks Obermann to test \nfor this.  Jager adds that if the model indeed has a unit root \nand therefore is a random walk and non-covariance \nstationary, it can still be used for autoregressions, claiming \nthat:\nClaim 1:\nConverting the time series using first-\ndifferencing will create a covariance-stationary time series.\nClaim 2:\nThe newly converted time series will provide \nmeaningful forecasts for currency exchange rates.\nJager then asks Obermann to run a regression on two \nrelated time series.  After analysis, Obermann finds that \nboth time series have unit roots.  Jager asks whether the \ntwo time series are cointegrated.  Obermann runs an \n(Engle–Granger) Dickey–Fuller test on the two time series \nand fails to reject the null hypothesis that the error term \nhas a unit root.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Melvin Walsh is a junior equity analyst at a sell-side firm.  \nRose Kelly, Walsh's supervisor, asks him to analyze Durian \nShops, a chain of apparel stores that complies with IFRS.  \nShe wants to understand whether the company will be able \nto sustain its profitability going forward.  Walsh gathers the \nfollowing information about Durian:\nIn the last three years, average ROE was 23%, which is \nabove industry average.\nDurian's operating cash flow (OCF) accounted for 55% of its \nnet income during the period.\nDurian's main competitor reported a similar ROE in the \nsame period, but OCF was 75% of net income.\nKelly asks Walsh to analyze Marang Clothing, a traditional \napparel manufacturer that reports under US GAAP.  Kelly \nsuspects that net income may have been affected by \nunusual charges in the last three years.  She believes it is \nimportant to normalize Marang's earnings before \nforecasting the company's results.  She asks Walsh to \nanalyze how the earnings normalization would affect \nfinancial statements and margins.  Walsh gathers the \nfollowing financial information:\nKelly turns her attention to Longan Footwear, another \napparel manufacturer that reports under US GAAP.  Longan \nhas announced its financial results for 20X6: Although \nearnings were in line with Kelly's expectations, OCF was \ngreater than anticipated.  She asks Walsh to look for signs \nof potential cash flow manipulation that could explain the \ndivergence.  Walsh investigates Longan's financial \nstatements and makes the following statements:\n\nStatement 1: Days sales outstanding (DSO) increased from \n45 to 60 in 20X6.\nStatement 2: Dividends received were reported in OCF, \nwhile dividends paid were reported as financing activities.\nStatement 3: In 20X6, inventory levels decreased 25% over \nthe year, although revenues were relatively stable.\nWalsh tells Kelly that, according to Longan's notes to the \nfinancial statements, the company implemented a change \nin the depreciation method of some assets in 20X6.  Since \nmost of the equipment used in the manufacturing process \nremained in use longer than expected, management \ndecided to use the straight-line method instead of an \naccelerated method.  This resulted in a prospective change \nthat reduced depreciation expenses in Longan's 20X6 \nfinancial statements, with no restatement of previous \nperiods.  Walsh explains to Kelly that the change in method \nseems to be a reasonable and appropriate decision.  Kelly \nasks Walsh how this change impacted Longan's 20X6 \nfinancial ratios.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Pacotter Investments is a US-based asset manager.  It \ncurrently sponsors a number of ETFs that mimic domestic \nand international equity indexes.  One of the funds, the \nEnhanced Opportunities ETF, is a leveraged fund that offers \n1.5 times the exposure to the FTSE 100 Index.  The fund is \nactively managed and uses exchange-traded futures to \nmagnify its exposure to the underlying index.  A Pacotter\nrepresentative, Bryan Parsons, makes the following \nstatements to prospective investors concerning potential \nrisks of investing in the Enhanced Opportunities ETF:\nStatement 1: Since the fund uses exchange-traded futures, \ninvestors can be exposed to counterparty risk.\nStatement 2: If the FTSE 100 Index has a 10% gain over the \nnext year, it is unlikely that the fund's return (before fees) \nwill equal 15%.\nStatement 3: The fund lends its securities, which means \nthat investors are exposed to the risk that the fund will \nexperience a \"soft\" closure.\nBefore launching the Enhanced Opportunities fund, \nPacotter performed various backtests to determine how the \nfund would have performed in different economic \nconditions.  These test periods ranged from 10 to 30 years.  \nAll of the studies included only point-in-time data.  Pacotter\nretested the data many times, changing the assumptions \nabout the variables in each test to obtain an optimal \noutcome.  All backtests were conducted using in-sample \ndata.\nPacotter also offers funds that implement different \ninvestment strategies and is considering a new fund that \nwill invest only in emerging market equities.  Pacotter is \nevaluating whether it will utilize a long-only or long-short \nstrategy.  Parsons has backtested both strategies using \neconomic data over the past 15 years.  This time horizon \nincludes periods of both high and low volatility as well as \nhigh and low growth.  The Treynor ratios for each strategy \nare shown in Exhibit 1:\n\nPacotter also conducted Monte Carlo simulations on each \nstrategy.  The distributions from the simulations are shown \nin Exhibit 2:\nParsons draws the following conclusions from Exhibit 2:\nConclusion 1: Strategy 2 will lose at most 5%.\nConclusion 2: When Strategy 1's losses are more than 4%, \nthe average expected loss is 9%.\nConclusion 3: Over a specified time period, Strategy 1 will \nhave more losses than Strategy 2.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Noel Rollins and Beatriz Lane are sell-side analysts at a \nbrokerage firm.  They are currently initiating coverage on \nChefTaste Foods, a large food processing company.  Rollins \nis concerned about the effectiveness of ChefTaste's\ncorporate governance, so he compares the firm's main \npolicies and practices with those of two similar-size peers, \nas shown in Exhibit 1:\nLane cautions that ChefTaste may have material ESG risks \nthat require adjustments when valuing the company.  \nRollins asks how these risks and adjustments impact their \nfinancial model.  Lane replies:\nStatement 1: Corporate governance risks are typically \nadjusted for by increasing estimated operating costs.\nStatement 2: Excessive product recalls and quality issues \nare typically adjusted for by increasing the risk premium.\nStatement 3: Excessive greenhouse gas emissions and \nwater pollution may affect both revenues and costs.\nChefTaste has recently issued a press release announcing \nthe acquisition of Allmeat, its major supplier of beef and \npoultry.  The press release states that the acquisition \n\"secures vital resources that will allow ChefTaste to \ncontinue growing in the next decade\" and \"reduces the risk \nof disruption in the company's supply chain.\"  Rollins \nreviews the press release to understand the motivation \nbehind the acquisition.\napproximately 70% and is expected to remain stable.\n\nAllmeat is a foreign company that operates under a split-\nrate tax system.  Lane wants to understand how that system \naffects corporate and shareholder taxes.  Lane examines \nAllmeat's notes to the financial statements and gathers the \nfollowing information:\nRetained earnings are taxed at 40%.\nEarnings allocated to dividends are taxed at 20%.\nDividends are taxed at 35% at the shareholder level.\nAllmeat's dividend payout ratio in recent years was", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Petra Goldsmith is a fixed-income portfolio manager for a \nlarge bank.  Harrison Jenkins, a credit analyst who is \nworking with Goldsmith, is evaluating the investment \nprospects for a group of corporate bonds.  He gathers \nselected data on the group, shown in Exhibit 1:\nGoldsmith asks Jenkins to calculate the credit spread for \nBond A, assuming that the bond is currently trading at fair \nvalue.  Exhibit 2 contains selected data for the government \npar bonds Jenkins is using in the analysis:\nGoldsmith and Jenkins then discuss the investment outlook \nfor Bond B.  Goldsmith believes there will be a change in \nBond B's credit rating and asks Jenkins to estimate the one-\nyear expected return on Bond B, should a credit rating \nchange occur.  The bond's current YTM is 4.85%.  Exhibit 3 \ncontains the credit transition matrix Jenkins will use for \nevaluating Bond B:\nGoldsmith and Jenkins next decide to evaluate the credit \ndefault swap (CDS) market.  Goldsmith believes that a slight \nstrengthening of economic growth will result in a relative \nimprovement in credit quality in the high-yield sector, \nwhere credit spreads are a bit wider than their historic\n\naverage.  She believes high-yield spreads will narrow \nrelative to investment-grade sector spreads, which are \ncurrently trading (above Treasuries) well below their \nhistoric average.  However, Goldsmith and Jenkins agree \nthat the intermediate- to long-term outlook for corporate \nspreads to Treasuries is uncertain.  Goldsmith decides to \nexecute a long-short CDS index trade to profit from the \nexpected relative improvement in the high-yield sector.\nJenkins tells Goldsmith there is a discrepancy in the pricing \nof credit risk for Bond C in the fixed-income market and CDS \nmarket.  Exhibit 4 contains selected data on Bond C and \nsingle-name CDS on the bond's issuer.\nJenkins makes the following statements regarding the \nstructure of a basis trade to profit from differences in the \npricing of Bond C's credit risk in the two markets:\nStatement 1: Profiting from the relative mispricing of \ncredit requires buying Bond C and buying a single-name \nCDS on Bond C's issuer.\nStatement 2: Since Bond C is the cheapest-to-deliver bond \non the CDS, the bond and CDS are duration matched, which \nmeans the position is hedged against a general rise or fall in \ninterest rates not related to credit spread changes.\nAssume all bonds and CDS pay annual coupons and all \ninterest rates are expressed on an annual compound basis.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Stan Fields, CFA, previously worked for a very successful \nhedge fund, Apex Hedge, for 10 years.  Apex's Alpha Fund is \ncomprised of 10 unrelated (eg, quant, macro, long/short) \ninvestment sub-strategies (ie, sleeves).  Fields joined Apex \nas a quantitative analyst working on one of the quant-based \ntrading sleeves.  During his time at Apex, Fields developed a \nproprietary quantitative trading strategy, and he was \npromoted to portfolio manager five years ago, managing \none of the 10 investment sleeves.\nFields left Apex six months ago to set up his own hedge \nfund, Candle Capital.  Fields developed a new investment \napproach similar to the trading strategy that he built while \nat Apex.  Fields's new trading strategy focuses on \ncompanies that:\nhave just released new information to the markets,\nhave reported a positive EPS surprise, and\ntheir stocks are trading higher in reaction to the news.\nFields then:\nbuys aggressively, pushing up the price in an attempt to \ndraw in momentum buyers, and\nsells into the momentum traders at higher prices for a \nprofit.\nIn his sales and marketing literature to prospective \ninvestors, Fields includes the following performance \ninformation:\nInformation 1: Past six months' performance at the new \nfirm\nInformation 2: Track record of Apex's Alpha Fund during the \n10 years that he worked there\nInformation 3: Five-year track record of his trading strategy \nat Apex, indicating he was the sole portfolio manager\nFields has requested and received written permission from \nApex to use the historical performance information.\n\nSimon Swank, CFA, also left Apex to join Fields as Candle's \nhead trader.  Swank is a trusted employee, having worked \nwith Fields for more than eight years.  In addition to \nexecuting the orders received from Fields, Swank has broad \ndiscretionary trading authority, not to exceed 1% of the \nfund, to take advantage of short-term trading \nopportunities.\nSwank's discretionary short-term trades are typically closed \nout by the end of the day, and he is authorized to trade \ncommon stocks, ETFs, and equity options.  Swank's most \ncommon trade is a broad market index ETF.  Three months \nago, to reduce transaction costs, Swank began speculatively \ntrading index futures on the same index as the ETF.\nFields just now learns of Swank's futures trading after \noverhearing Swank talking on the phone with a futures \nbroker.  As Swank's supervisor, Fields confronts Swank \nabout the trades.  Fields states that the firm's written \npolicies and procedures allow trading futures only in \nhedging transactions.\nSwank responds by stating that he is an experienced trader \nwho has traded futures contracts for more than 10 years; \nfutures are more cost-effective than buying the ETF and \ndeliver the same basic exposure for the fund.  Swank argues \nthat positions are closed out by the end of the day, thus the \nfund is not at risk, and these trades could be thought of as \nintraday hedges.  Fields has Swank draft new policy and \nprocedure language for speculative futures trading, which is \nthen added to the compliance manual.\nFields and Swank both personally invest in the hedge fund.  \nThe hedge fund is set up as a limited partnership, with \nFields acting as general partner.  When trades are executed \nfor the fund, both Fields's and Swank's accounts are \nallocated shares at the same time and price as clients.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_mock2_s2", "source_label": "UWorld Mock 2 – Session 2", "topic": "Mock", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Letteria Meloni is a senior equity analyst at Vergani\nInvestments, an asset management firm, where Faustino \nGargano is a junior analyst.  Meloni and Gargano are \nanalyzing JKP Industries, a well-established chemical \nproducer.  Gargano uses a single-stage residual income (RI) \nmodel to estimate JKP's intrinsic value.  Based on the model \nassumptions, he concludes that net income should remain \ngreater than the equity charge over the years.  Meloni asks \nhim to use the same assumptions to calculate the \ncompany's justified price-to-book (P/B) ratio.\nMeloni believes that the assumptions used in the forecast \nhorizon may not persist in the long term.  She asks Gargano \nabout JKP's prospects: He says that the firm is planning to \nincrease its dividend payout ratio (p), but it is not clear \nwhen that change will take place.  Meloni asks him to \nreevaluate JKP using a multi-stage RI model and assuming \nan increase in p after the forecast horizon.\nMeloni then asks Gargano to evaluate JKP using a two-stage \nRI model and the following assumptions:\nBook value per share (BVPS) = €20.\nCost of equity (r) = 12%.\nROE (= EPS / BVPS) will remain at 20% for the next three \nyears.\nDividend payout rate is 50% each year.\nAt the end of three years, JKP's market share price will be \n2.0x BVPS.\nMeloni notices that the previous year's net income was \naffected by nonrecurring expenses and tells Gargano that \nhe may need to adjust the RI model.  Gargano asks for more \ndetails, and Meloni explains that the nonrecurring \nexpenses:\nStatement 1: Are a violation of the clean surplus relation\nStatement 2: Should be added back to net income and to \nthe book value of equity", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_mock2_s2_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Jonna Jaworski is an equity research analyst at Baltic \nInvestment Partners, a long-only equity manager.  She is \npreparing an equity valuation analysis for three companies: \nSalt Mine Corp., Steel Mill Inc., and Central Tire.  During her \npreliminary work on these companies, she makes the \nfollowing observations regarding Salt Mine, a major salt \nproducer in the country.\nObservation 1:Last year, Salt Mine reported a relatively \nlarge nonrecurring restructuring charge.\nObservation 2:Salt Mine has consistently reported \nsignificant amounts of other comprehensive income.\nJaworski creates a residual income model for Salt Mine that \nassumes a 9% cost of equity and a terminal value of zero \nafter 20 years.  Salt mine's stock price is currently $80, and \nJaworski's condensed forecast is presented in Exhibit 1:\nJacek Chopin, an analyst and colleague, questions the \nassumption of zero terminal value for Salt Mine.  Chopin \nproposes that the mine could operate indefinitely and \nsuggests calculating the terminal value as a perpetuity.\nJaworski then reviews the financial statements of Steel Mill \nand creates the following per-share financial forecasts:\n\nJaworski turns her attention to Central Tire and notes that \nthe capital structure is 60% debt and 40% equity and \ninterest expense is tax-deductible.  She gathers the \nfollowing data:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Vadim Vasiliy is a corporate development analyst at Boleslav\nJSC, an international conglomerate.  Vasiliy has recently \nidentified several acquisition opportunities and asks the \nfirm's new junior analyst, Boris Botschenko, to assist him \nwith the valuation process.\nBotschenko is unsure of when to use FCFF versus FCFE \nwhen valuing companies and seeks guidance from Vasiliy.  \nVasiliy answers with the following statements:\nStatement 1: Instead of discounting FCFE at the required \nreturn on equity to find equity value, one can theoretically \nderive the same equity value by discounting FCFF at WACC \nand subtracting the book value of debt and preferred \ndividends.\nStatement 2: It is more appropriate to use FCFF when a \nfirm has negative FCFE and no debt in its current and target \ncapital structure.\nStatement 3: It is more appropriate to use FCFF when a \nfirm has a volatile capital structure.\nBoleslav's management is considering acquiring a 90% stake \nin Agro JSC, an agricultural equipment manufacturer.  Agro\nhas historically paid dividends, and will continue to do so in \nthe foreseeable future, at a consistent dividend payout \nratio.  Vasiliy tells Botschenko that FCFE should be used to \nvalue Agro.  Botschenko asks why it is more suitable to \nvalue Agro using FCFE in place of dividends.  Vasiliy\nresponds:\nResponse 1:\nFCFE better aligns to the short-term \nprofitability of the firm.\nResponse 2:\nUse of FCFE implies control and discretion \nover dividend distribution.\nResponse 3:\nFCFE reflects uses of free cash flow, whereas \ndividends reflect sources of free cash flow.\n\nBoleslav's board believes it can create substantial cross-\nselling synergies from its existing distribution network by \nacquiring Freezky JSC, a top home appliance company.  \nFreezky stock is currently trading at RUB 843.28.  \nBotschenko gathers the following data (shown in Exhibit 1) \nand calculates the intrinsic equity value for Freezky using a \none-stage FCFF model:\nBotschenko then develops a sensitivity analysis for another \npotential investment, Trucksky JSC, a trucking company, \nbased on sales growth, gross margin, and capital \nexpenditures (CAPEX) to gauge how these variables impact \nhis valuation estimate.  This analysis is shown in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Bruno Martins is a senior sell-side analyst covering the \ntechnology, media, and telecom sectors.  He is meeting \nwith Mariana Dias, one of his junior analysts, to discuss \nvaluing companies using their forecasted dividends.  All \ndividends are assumed to be paid annually at the end of the \nyear.\nDias is currently analyzing Terra Manufacturing and Oceano \nTechnologies.  Both companies have a stable ROE of 12%, a \nstable capital structure, no changes to their common stock \noutstanding, and a constant dividend payout ratio.  \nHowever:\nTerra's payout ratio is 60% and\nOceano's payout ratio is 45%.\nIt is the beginning of 20X3, and Dias has the following \nforecast for Oceano's dividends:\nMartins is valuing Oceano based on a three-year holding \nperiod and given a required rate of return of 8%.  Dias uses \ntwo methods:\nMethod 1:\nUsing the Gordon growth model to estimate \nthe terminal value, Dias expects Oceano's dividend to grow \nat a constant 5% rate starting in 20X6.\nMethod 2:\nUsing P/E to estimate the terminal value, \nDias expects Oceano's justified forward P/E ratio at the end \nof 20X5 to be 11x.\n\nMartins is analyzing three other companies and asks Dias to \ngenerate dividend expectations for each.  Dias provides the \nfollowing:\nMartins estimates the risk-free rate at 3% and equity risk \npremium at 5%.  He believes a stock would be over- or \nundervalued if it's more than 8% from its calculated \nintrinsic value to allow for uncertainty in the model.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Vu Nguyen is a junior analyst at Mekong Investment \nPartners (Mekong), a long-only buy-side firm.  Dep Tran, \nNguyen's supervisor, asks Nguyen about his knowledge of \nresidual income models.  Nguyen replies, \"Assuming a \nconstant growth residual income model and clean surplus \naccounting, these fundamental assumptions are always \ntrue\":\nStatement 1: Residual income is earnings in excess of the \ncost of equity.\nStatement 2: If accounting net income is positive, residual \nincome will be positive.\nStatement 3: If ROE is less than the cost of equity, residual \nincome can be created.\nTran then asks Nguyen to value three stocks based on \ninformation for fiscal year 20X5: Megawatt Inc., a \ngovernment-regulated electric utility company; Acme Cola, \nthe country's dominant market share soda brand; and \nSemiComm, a fast-growing and high-return-earning niche \nsemiconductor manufacturer for the communications \nindustry.  Specific information for each company is shown in \nExhibits 1, 2, and 3.\n\nTran tells Nguyen to be prepared to discuss the justified \nprice-to-book ratio, the residual income equity charge, \neconomic value added (EVA), and market value added \n(MVA) for these stocks.  Tran adds that Nguyen should be \nprepared to discuss an estimate of continuing residual \nincome persistence at the end of the forecast horizon for \neach of the three companies, based on company-specific \ncharacteristics and industry factors.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Hakim Rashid is a credit analyst at an investment \nmanagement firm.  He is evaluating his free cash flow (FCF) \nforecasts of multiple companies.  Rashid first evaluates \nVelocità Automotive, an Italian car parts manufacturer.  \nVelocità reports under IFRS and has a capital structure that \nincludes common stock, preferred stock, and debt.  Rashid \ncompiles information on Velocità's 20X0 financials to use as \na basis for his forecasts and notes several items that require \nspecial attention:\nRashid notes that Velocità:\npurchased an additional €40 million of equipment in \nexchange for Velocità common stock; this purchase was to \nreplace required operating equipment.\nclassifies its interest paid and dividends paid as financing \ncash flows.\nwill reverse its deferred tax asset in the near future.\nwill maintain a persistent level of stock-based \ncompensation expense.\nKarima Yusuf has developed EPS and FCFE forecasts for EU \nTelecom and Kamera1 Solutions, and she provides them to \nRashid.  For EU Telecom, a European wireless telecom \ncarrier, Rashid wishes to convert Yusuf's estimates to FCFF\n\nto use in his credit analysis.  Yusuf's 20X1 estimates for EU \nTelecom are shown in Exhibit 2:\nRashid then reviews the forecast for Kamera1, a German \nsoftware security firm that is experiencing slowing growth.  \nKamera1 reported EPS of €3.00 in 20X0, which is the most \nrecently reported period.  Rashid reviews Yusuf's estimates:\nAfter 20X5, Kamera1 will grow at a 4% growth rate \nindefinitely.  Based on current market conditions for \nKamera1 and using the CAPM, Yusuf assumes a:\nRisk-free rate of 3%\nEquity risk premium of 5%\nBeta of 1.4", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "It is December 20X1, and Ananya Gupta, CFA, is analyzing \nMerlion Singapore, Inc. (MSI).  After 20 years of rapid \ngrowth, MSI has announced that it will initiate a dividend, \nbut growth and value creation are expected to continue for \nmany years.  The company reports under IFRS, and Gupta \nhas analyzed its most recent financial statements, press \nreleases, and current market data.  Based on these sources, \nshe compiles the following (in SGD):\nGupta values MSI using two different applications of the \nresidual income (RI) valuation model.\nShe applies a single-stage RI valuation model, assuming a \nconstant ROE and constant dividend growth.  Based on the \nCAPM, Gupta estimates that the company's required rate of \nreturn is 8%.\nIn addition, she applies a multistage, 15-year RI valuation \nmodel, assuming terminal value of zero, and estimates the \ncompany's intrinsic value per share as SGD 49.58.\nGupta's supervisor questions the assumption of a terminal \nvalue of zero, so Gupta also runs the multistage model \nusing two alternative assumptions:\nAssumption 1: Year 15 residual income of SGD 1.73 \ncontinues in perpetuity.\nAssumption 2: ROE will revert to the required return, and \nresidual income  will eventually be zero.  The persistence \nfactor is 0.7 and Year 15 residual income will be SGD 1.73.\n\nAnother analyst observes that Gupta could just use a \ndividend discount model (DDM), which would be easier to \napply.  Then, a colleague from the accounting department, \nChan Ding Hao, joins the discussion and suggests Gupta \nverify that MSI has not violated clean surplus accounting", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Lukas Dietrich is a portfolio manager for Yama Financial.  \nDietrich has a large holding of Himmel Manufacturing stock \nin his portfolio and is reviewing the company after it \nreported annual earnings.  Himmel's earnings growth rate is \nabove the country's GDP growth rate but has begun to \ndecline.  In addition, its capital requirements have been \ndeclining and its payout ratio has been increasing.\nGiven the above-average yet decreasing rate of growth, \nDietrich uses the H-model to value Himmel's stock.  After \nthe earnings report, Dietrich notes that Himmel's most \nrecent dividend per share was €1.00 and that shareholders' \nrequired rate of return is 7.50%.\nDietrich expects dividends to:\ntransition linearly from the current 12% rate of growth over \nthe next 15 years, then\nsettle at 4% growth indefinitely.\nDietrich is also researching three companies, Rot, Blau, and \nGrün, for possible inclusion in his portfolio.  He forecasts \ntheir earnings and dividends for the next six years, as \nshown in Exhibit 1.\nHe also collects pertinent information about these \ncompanies, as shown in Exhibit 2.\n\nFor a fourth stock, Wolke Telekom, Dietrich expects \ndividend growth to remain steady but wants to compare his \nexpectations for growth against the market's.  He compiles \nthe following data based on his expectations:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Anna Fortier, head of acquisitions at a major food \ndistributor, is analyzing Modern Farm Markets, a privately \nheld produce retailer, as a possible target for acquisition.  \nFortier discusses the target company valuation with her \nteam, and she states:\nStatement 1: The market approach is often considered \npreferable to the income- and asset-based approaches \nsince it relies on actual transaction data, as recommended \nby accounting standards.\nStatement 2: Price volatility of comparable public \ncompany shares is irrelevant to valuations made for \nacquiring a target private company.\nFortier directs the team to apply the guideline public \ncompany method (GPCM), using an average of the market \nvalue to invested capital (MVIC)-to-EBITDA multiples of the \ncomparables.  She has chosen three comparable \ncompanies: Urban Foods Inc. (UFI), Republic Company Ltd. \n(RCL), and The West Hills Company (WHC).  The team \ngathers selected data, shown in Exhibit 1:\nFortier forecasts Modern Farm Markets' EBITDA at CAD 18 \nmillion, applies a 10% downward relative risk adjustment, \nand determines no control premium is needed.\nIn addition to Modern Farm Markets, Fortier recently \nevaluated Montagne's Fresh Market, a three-store \nsupermarket chain.  Montagne is 100%-owned by its \nfounder, who wishes to retire and sell the company.  Fortier \nviews this company as having a moderate risk profile with a \nstable growth rate.  She forecasted EBITDA at CAD 2.4 \nmillion.\n\nDue to the company's much smaller size and ownership \nstructure, Fortier used the guideline transaction method \n(GTM) to value Montagne.  Her team found two \ntransactions very similar to the proposed acquisition of \nMontagne: acquisitions of 100% of two privately held small \nsupermarket chains.  Exhibit 2 shows the targets' MVIC-to-\nEBITDA multiples, after the acquirer applied a relative risk \nadjustment:\nWhile Fortier considered the Canada East Foods transaction \nthe most relevant for valuing Montagne, she decided to use \nboth guideline transactions in the valuation calculation.  In \naddition, her team determined that Montagne warranted a \n5% downward adjustment for the relative risk.  After \nevaluating the results, an analyst on Fortier's team \ncommented on the GTM:\nComment 1:\nA control premium for Montagne is already \nincorporated into the guideline transaction multiple.\nComment 2:\nWhen using the GTM, the difference \nbetween the transaction date and the valuation date does \nnot factor in a downward relative risk adjustment.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Josue Hernandez and Maria Perez are newly hired \nassociates at BLL Bank.  Francisco Carrera, a senior analyst \nat BLL, is testing them on their knowledge of market-based \nvaluation, starting with price multiples.  Carrera asks \nHernandez about rationales for using multiples other than \nP/E.  Hernandez responds with:\nRationale 1:\nSales is much more difficult to manipulate \nthan earnings, and therefore P/S can be used without fear \nof manipulation.\nRationale 2:\nSince dividends are a less risky component of \nreturn than price appreciation, dividend yield is more \noptimal than P/E.\nRationale 3:\nSince book value is cumulative, it is often \npositive even when earnings are negative and P/E cannot \nbe used.\nCarrera then asks Perez to identify some drawbacks to using \nmultiples other than P/E.  Perez responds:\nDrawback 1:\nDue to cost control measures, sales and P/S \nare often more volatile than EPS and P/E.\nDrawback 2:\nWhen cash flow is defined as earnings plus \nnoncash charges, certain items affecting actual cash flow \nare ignored.\nDrawback 3:\nP/B is typically viewed as inappropriate for \nvaluing companies with largely liquid assets, such as banks.\nCarrera then asks about their familiarity with enterprise-\nbased multiples.  He provides the two associates with the \nfinancial information for retailer Molina Primero, shown in \nExhibit 1:\n\nMolina Primero has 10 million shares outstanding, currently \npriced at MXN 4,500 per share.\nCarrera mentions to Hernandez and Perez that investors \nuse the earnings surprise from sell-side analysts' earnings \nestimates as an indicator of future stock price movement.  \nSome investors use the accuracy of past forecasts to \nnormalize surprises for a given period.  Carrera provides the \ntwo associates recent earnings information on three stocks, \nshown in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Alvin Blake and Amber Cazares, sell-side analysts covering \nthe pulp and paper industry for a brokerage firm, are \nanalyzing Promenade Paper's stock.  Blake has decided to \nvalue the stock by comparing current price multiples, based \non market price, with justified multiples based on a \nconstant growth model.  He summarizes the information in \nExhibit 1:\nCazares tells Blake that she prefers to use forecasted \nearnings to value Promenade.  She explains that, before \nmodeling future earnings, she estimates Promenade's \nunderlying earnings by adjusting the company's reported \nearnings.  Cazares then explains to Blake how to calculate a \ncompany's underlying earnings.\nBlake turns his attention to Papiro Industries.  He uses \nprice-to-sales (P/S) multiples to evaluate whether Papiro's\nstock is relatively under- or overvalued and estimates a \njustified P/S multiple of 0.91 based on a constant growth \nmodel.  Cazares separately estimates a justified P/S multiple \nof 1.12 using a similar calculation.  She compares her \nassumptions with those used by Blake to understand the \ndifferences in the justified multiples.\nCazares says that she also uses multiples based on the \nconcept of earnings plus noncash charges, or cash flow (CF), \nto evaluate Papiro's stock.  Blake agrees that cash flow \nmultiples based on CF are a widely used metric but adds \nthat he prefers to use cash flow from operations (CFO), \nsourced directly from the statement of cash flows.  Blake \nthen lists to Cazares some of the reasons for his preference.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Lei Fan, CFA and Ju Zhang, CFA, two portfolio managers at \nSunrise Capital, are discussing potential investments.  Their \ndiscussion begins with the construction equipment industry \nand Falcon Manufacturing's competitive position.  Fan \nstates that during his initial analysis, he observed that \nFalcon has been able to improve on the most popular \nproducts sold across the industry.  The improvements have \nallowed Falcon to maintain selling prices higher than its \ncompetitors.\nZhang mentions that she has analyzed Falcon's revenue \ngrowth and profitability, compared to its peers, using ratios \nbased on data from its financial statements for the last five \nyears.  She finds that Falcon has outperformed its peers for \nthe first four years of her analysis, and then performed on \naverage with peers in the last year.\nDuring their conversation, they also discuss their latest \nvaluations for Falcon and make the following comments:\nFan:  Investors can only estimate Falcon's intrinsic value \nsince knowledge of a company's investment characteristics \nwill always be incomplete.\nZhang:  My value estimate is above the current market \nprice, and if I am right, convergence will make the price \nincrease in the near-term.\nFalcon's stock currently trades at ¥780 and Zhang's forecast \nmodel estimates the stock's intrinsic value at ¥870.  Zhang \nexpects Falcon to maintain profitability for the foreseeable \nfuture.  Despite Falcon's consistent positive free cash flow \ngeneration and profitability, Fan suggests that Zhang also \nlook at Falcon's liquidation value and compare it against the \ngoing-concern value derived in her forecast.\nFan and Zhang have a meeting with Falcon's CFO scheduled \nfor later in the week.  Zhang tells Fan that she will send the \nvaluation model she created to the CFO to facilitate \ncommunication with her.  Zhang plans to ask the CFO about \na rumored merger between Falcon and one of its \ncompetitors and to use the model to analyze the effects of \nthe merger on Falcon's growth and profitability.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Rudra Dhawan, CFA, is a corporate development analyst at \nPrime Technologies.  He is currently analyzing two large, \npublicly traded companies identified by his manager, Aditi \nMadan, as potential acquisition targets.  Madan suggests \nstarting the analysis with each company's stock price, based \non the following statements:\nStatement 1: Since markets are fully efficient and other \nanalysts have done the research, each stock's price will \naccurately represent its intrinsic value.\nStatement 2: The rational efficient markets formulation \nsuggests that investors will not incur the expense of equity \nanalysis without the expectation of greater returns.\nStatement 3: It does not matter which valuation model \nyou choose; all of them will provide valid estimates of \nintrinsic value.\nDhawan begins his analysis by examining each company's \ncompetitive positioning and financials, pulling together the \nfollowing data:\n\nAfter conducing this analysis, Dhawan begins forecasting \neach company's financials and running valuations based on \nhis estimates.  Madan provides Dhawan with the potential \nsynergies that Prime can expect to achieve by buying and \nintegrating each potential acquisition target.\nMadan is curious about Target 1 since it is a conglomerate \nand asks Dhawan what issues she should consider.  Dhawan \nmakes two comments in response:\nComment 1:\nA conglomerate discount should be applied \nto any company with multiple business segments.\nComment 2:\nThe fact that Target 1 is a conglomerate may \nsignal that its primary business unit has underperformed.\nMadan tells Dhawan that Prime will acquire one of these \ncompanies by taking a majority stake and then taking the \nacquired company private.  Madan is concerned about a \nscenario in which the acquisition target is purchased, the \nintegration fails, and Prime must then resell the target to \nanother buyer.  Madan asks Dhawan what situational \nadjustments must be made in this scenario to properly \nvalue the company to be acquired.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c12_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c12_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Martha Hayes is a sell-side analyst covering Envision Inc., \nwhich is planning an IPO.  Given that this will be Hayes's \nfirst IPO, she asks her senior analyst, Benjamin Reed, CFA, \nfor guidance.\nReed states, \"Completing an equity valuation model will \nmake it easier to communicate with company management \nand investor relations.  It will enable us to infer the market's \nexpectations about the company before its IPO.  In terms of \nprocess, creating a fundamental model for a private \ncompany is largely the same as creating a fundamental \nmodel for a public company.\"\nHayes begins her analysis by collecting data on Envision's\ncompetitive position:\nHayes then collects data on Envision's financial position \nfrom its reported financial statements:\n\nHayes believes Envision is well positioned to survive and \ngrow for the foreseeable future based on this analysis.  She \nasks Reed which measure of value would be the most \nsuitable basis for their investment recommendation once \nthe company begins trading on the secondary market.\nReed is more concerned about how Envision will compare \nwith its peers once it is priced.  Some of Reed's clients are \nasking about whether Envision is likely to be overpriced, \nfairly priced, or underpriced.  Reed asks Hayes to pull \ntogether relevant data on Envision and its publicly traded \npeers with identical investment characteristics, shown in \nExhibit 3:\nHayes questions whether a relative valuation is the best \nway to model Envision.  Regarding model selection, she \nsuggests that the valuation models be consistent with:\nFactor 1:  the analyst's purpose of the valuation.\nFactor 2:  the models used for the company's competitors.\nFactor 3:  the way other analysts are modeling the company \nfor comparison.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Nathan Lavigne works as a senior analyst for a brokerage \nfirm.  His supervisor, Agathe Castillon, asks him to update \nthe research report of Trottier Auto Parts, Vertou Retailers, \nand Chaumont Chemical Industries as of January 1, 20X5, \nusing valuations based on price multiples.\nLavigne gathers information about Trottier and the auto \nparts industry, summarized in Exhibit 1, and calculates the \ncompany's justified trailing P/E ratio.  He assumes a \nconstant growth rate for dividends and earnings, resulting \nin a constant dividend payout ratio.\nCastillon asks Lavigne about the assumptions underpinning \nhis estimates.  She wants to understand how isolated \nchanges in the model's fundamental factors will affect \nestimates of the trailing P/E and other price multiples, such \nas price-to-book value.  She asks specifically about the \neffects of changes in the required rate of return and the \nearnings growth rate.  She also asks Lavigne to use the \nforward P/E-to-growth (PEG) ratio to evaluate whether \nTrottier's stock is undervalued or overvalued relative to the \nauto parts industry.\nCastillon then turns her attention to Vertou Group.  Lavigne \nexplains that he projected earnings for three years and \ngathered the following information and estimates:\nVertou's required rate of return = 11%\nVertou's expected earnings for Year 3 (E3) = €10.00\nRetail industry average dividend payout ratio = 40%\n\nRetail industry average ROE = 13%\nRetail industry average trailing P/E = 11.0\nCastillon asks Lavigne to estimate Vertou's terminal value at \nthe end of Year 3 based on comparables and based on \nfundamentals, using the Gordon growth model.\nLavigne switches his focus to Chaumont Chemical and \ninforms Castillon that the company's:\nCurrent stock price is €57.00\n20X4 EPS was €5.00\n20X5 EPS is estimated to be €6.00\nIn addition, Lavigne has estimated the forward P/E \nmultiples of Chaumont's competitors in the chemical \nindustry, summarized in Exhibit 2:\nLavigne believes that Peer 2's forward P/E may be \nabnormally high and asks Castillon to compare Chaumont's \nmultiple with the harmonic mean of the peer group's \nmultiples.\nCastillon wants to expand the comparison to include \ncompetitors from other countries, including the United \nStates.  Lavigne warns Castillon that cross-border valuation \ncomparisons may show significant differences and makes \nthe following observations:\nObservation 1: \nStudies show that companies \ntypically report lower ROE under IFRS than they would have \nreported under US GAAP.\nObservation 2: \nGoodwill and pension are among the \nmost relevant categories of reconciliation between IFRS and \nUS GAAP.\n\nObservation 3:Multiples based on cash flow are typically \nmore affected by accounting differences than multiples \nbased on EBITDA and book value.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Davish Ikram is an associate in the acquisitions department \nat Stark, Inc., a publicly listed steel producer.  Ikram and \nKyung Sonam, an intern at the company, are discussing \nprivate firm valuation.  Ikram advises Sonam to consider the \nfollowing:\nConsideration 1:\nPrivate firms' management teams \ntend to have a controlling stake in the firm.  Therefore, \nagency issues are less likely to arise in private firms than in \npublic companies.\nConsideration 2:\nPrivate firms are usually smaller than \npublic firms within the same line of business.  Thus, \nmanagement teams in private firms generally provide \nbetter oversight and more management depth compared \nwith those in public firms.\nIkram analyzes potential investment opportunities in two \nprivate firms: Firm X and Firm Y.\nFirm X is a lumber mill with a significant operating history.  \nIt is expected to receive a purchase offer from a publicly \nlisted competitor in the same industry.  Ikram anticipates \nthat the deal will not go through due to antitrust laws and is \nconsidering recommending that Stark's management make \nan offer to acquire Firm X.  Ikram asks Sonam to value Firm \nX using the market approach.\nSonam notes that information is not available on private \nmarket transactions of Firm X's shares.  She presents \navailable data in Exhibit 1:\n\nIkram asks Sonam to compare the firm's operating expenses \nwith those of publicly listed firms.  Sonam states that \noperating cost synergies from closing redundant facilities \nare expected to amount to CAD 400,000 annually.  In \naddition, some of Firm X's offices are being used by \nshareholders for noncore activities, resulting in additional \nrent expense of CAD 70,000 each year.  Sonam also notes \nthat the annual compensation of the firm's management is \nCAD 5 million, 30% above market average, and that \nmanagement will most likely reject an offer to sell.\nIkram states that Stark would then make a purchase offer to \nFirm Y, which is expected to grow at a stable rate.  Sonam \ngathers information on Firm Y, based on the current year, in \nExhibit 2:\nIkram then asks about the method used to estimate the \nrequired return on equity.  Sonam states that the expanded \nCAPM and the build-up approach incorporate company-\nspecific risks and therefore provide a better estimate of \nrequired return than CAPM.  She then comments:\nComment 1:\nThe build-up approach is more appropriate \nto use than the expanded CAPM when guideline public \ncompanies are not available.\nComment 2:\nThe build-up approach considers the \nsensitivity of company-specific factors to the different risk \npremiums.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c15_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c15_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Kaito Fujino, a senior analyst covering the apparel industry, \nasks Nori Hagino, an associate analyst, to value several \ncompanies.  The first company, Haiku Apparel, is a mature \ncompany that distributes most of its earnings as dividends.  \nIts dividend growth has been constant for a considerable \nperiod of time, and Hagino expects this to continue.  Fujino\nsuggests modeling Haiku using either the dividend discount \nmodel or residual income model.  Hagino collects \ninformation on Haiku's fundamentals in Exhibit 1 and uses \nthe single-stage residual income model.  Haiku's market \nprice is ¥3,600 per common share.\nFujino asks what assumptions Hagino used for estimating \ncontinuing residual income.  Hagino replies that he added a \npersistence factor to the model to account for a decline in \nROE over time.  He adds:\nComment 1:\nThe persistence factor accounts for how \nquickly a company's ROE will revert to its long-term growth \nrate.\nComment 2:\nIncreasing the persistence factor results in a \nslower decay of residual income and higher overall \nvaluation.\nComment 3:\nExtreme levels of accounting accruals and a \nlow dividend payout are characteristics of greater residual \nincome persistence.\nFujino then asks Hagino to review Himalayan Outfitters, a \nforeign retailer that has expanded into Japan.  Himalayan is \ntrading at a P/B ratio of 2.0x, a premium to peers.  Fujino\ncalculates Himalayan's ROE at 10% and required rate of \nreturn at 7%.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Heinrich Schultz is CFO of C-Store Corp., a private regional \nconvenience store chain.  Schultz is evaluating C-Store's \npotential minority investment in or complete acquisition of \nGiraffe Fuel Distribution Inc.  Schultz begins valuation work \non Giraffe by determining an appropriate discount rate and \ngathers the following information.\nUsing the most recently reported financial data, Schultz \nestimates the WACC, a long-term growth rate, and \nnormalized FCFF.\nGiraffe's core business is very stable and predictable, while \na Giraffe subsidiary, Calf Tanker Trucks, has a history of \nvolatile growth of free cash flow.  Calf has acquired \nnumerous small trucking firms over the past several years \nand carries a significant amount of intangible assets on its \nbalance sheet.\nEmma Hoffman, a financial analyst at C-Store, is assisting \nSchultz in his analysis.  Hoffman prepares the following \ninformation on Calf.\n\nSchultz reviews the information and states that to complete \nhis excess earnings model analysis for Calf, he needs to \nknow Hoffman's required return estimates for:\nItem 1:  Working capital\nItem 2:  Fixed assets\nItem 3:  Intangible assets\nUsing the guideline transactions method, Hoffman then \nestimates the value of equity in the event C-Store takes a \nminority equity interest in Giraffe.  She uses the following \nestimates:\nValue of equity = $150 million\nMinority equity ownership stake = 10%\nControl premium = 15%\nDiscount for lack of marketability = 20%", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c17_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c17_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 18, "vignette": "Grace Collins and Brandon Reid are investors discussing \ntheir investments at a conference at the beginning of 20X6.  \nThey are discussing Kosciuszko Gas and Electric, a utility \ncompany located in Australia.  Collins has used the Gordon \ngrowth model to value Kosciuszko's stock and gives three \nreasons why she used this model:\nReason 1:\nThe company will return to a long-term level \nof growth after a period of above-average growth\nReason 2:\nThe company's long-term growth should be \nclose to Australia's nominal GDP growth\nReason 3:\nThe long-term growth is equal to the \nrequired rate of return\nReid mentions that he uses the Gordon growth model only \nin certain situations due to its limitations, particularly the \nsignificant effect that small changes to the required rate of \nreturn and long-term growth estimates may have on the \nestimated value.  Collins replies that Reid should consider \nusing the Gordon growth model more broadly in his \nresearch due to its strengths.  She states that the model can \ncalculate the growth rate or required rate of return priced \ninto a stock or index.\nCollins plans to use the Gordon growth model to estimate \nKosciuszko's P/E ratio and compare Kosciuszko with its \npeers based on this valuation.  She provides data on \nKosciuszko, shown in Exhibit 1:\n\nReid wishes to use a dividend discount model to value High \nTide Bank (HTB), but he believes HTB's dividend is growing \nfaster than its sustainable level.  He asks Collins for her \nopinion and provides the following data:\nCollins believes HTB is in a period of above-average \ndividend growth and that this growth will slow as follows:\nDividends will grow at 10% in 20X6, 20X7, and 20X8.\nDividends will then transition to a long-term growth rate \nover a 10-year period.\nDividends will grow at 3% in the long term.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c18_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c18_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c18_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c18_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c18_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c18_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 19, "vignette": "Zebra Windows and Doors is a family-run, privately held \nwindow and door manufacturer.  Zebra has a long history of \nstable operating profit growth of 3%, which is also next \nyear's forecast.  The owners have decided to put Zebra up \nfor sale, through either an IPO or outright sale.\nTamika Williams is an investment banking analyst at a sell-\nside firm that has been approached by Zebra.  Williams is \nconducting an initial valuation due diligence for a possible \nIPO of Zebra.  Two companies have expressed an interest in \nbuying Zebra outright: Strategic Buyer Inc., a competing \nwindow and door manufacturer, and Financial Buyer Corp., \na private equity firm.\nWilliams's forecasted financial information for 20X1 is \npresented in Exhibit 1:\nIn addition, for 20X1, Williams forecasts the following:\n15% cost of equity\n£500,000 increase in capital expenditures (from reported \n20X0 capex)\n£50,000 in additional working capital\nWilliams has been unable to identify any comparable \nguideline public companies and is concerned the company \nmay be too small for a successful IPO.  Given the \ncircumstances surrounding Zebra, Williams believes the \ncapitalized cash flow method (CCM) will provide a good \nstarting point for her initial IPO valuation assessment, \nalthough this approach is not typical.\n\nWilliams has also done some preliminary analysis for the \ncontingency that Zebra may be acquired by Financial Buyer \nin a private transaction.  The resulting forecast is presented \nin Exhibit 2:\nZebra recently raised equity capital in a private placement, \nselling a 5% stake that valued the entire firm at £24 million, \nor 6x market value of invested capital (MVIC)-to-EBITDA.  \nRecent acquisitions by nonstrategic buyers of comparable \nprivate companies in the same industry have occurred at 8x \nMVIC-to-EBITDA.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c19_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c19_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c19_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c19_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c19_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c19_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 20, "vignette": "Ellis Howard is a junior equity analyst for a university \nendowment fund.  The fund's policy for evaluating equity \ninvestments is to use free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) and \nfree cash flow to equity (FCFE) to estimate a company's \nintrinsic value.  Howard's supervisor asks about her \nmethods for forecasting FCFF and FCFE from a company's \nreported net income.  Howard replies:\nMethod 1:\nWhen determining changes in working \ncapital, I do not include short-term notes payable as part of \ncurrent liabilities.\nMethod 2:\nWhen using net income to derive FCFF or \nFCFE, I do not normally add back deferred tax liabilities to \nnet income.\nHoward is asked to evaluate Horizon ElectroCar as a \npotential investment.  The company's selected financial \ninformation is shown in Exhibit 1:\nThe notes to the company's financial statements disclose \nthe following information:\nThe tax rate is 40%.\nIn 20X1, Horizon spent SGD 1.0 million to repurchase \ncommon equity shares and borrowed SGD 1.3 million.\nThere are no notes payable, and all debt is long term.\nThe company classifies interest paid as cash from \noperations (CFO).\nHoward estimated Horizon ElectroCar's per-share FCFE for \n20X0 to be SGD 2.50.  The applicable risk-free interest rate\n\nis 2%.  Exhibit 2 shows Howard's estimates for the ranges of \ninputs used to value Horizon ElectroCar with a constant-\ngrowth FCFE model:\nHoward is also evaluating Innovation Resources, which is \nlocated in Chad, where there has been high inflation in \nrecent years.  Relevant information concerning Chad and \nInnovation Resources is shown in Exhibit 3:\nFinally, although Howard understands the endowment \nfund's rationale for valuing equity investments based on the \ncompany's free cash flow (FCF), she believes that other \nmetrics can be adequate substitutes for FCF.  She makes the \nfollowing statements to support her position:\nStatement 1: If a company makes no investment in fixed \ncapital, then EBITDA can be used as a proxy for FCFF.\nStatement 2: CFO is the same as FCFE if the company's \ninvestment in PPE is equal to its net amount of borrowing.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c20_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c20_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c20_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c20_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c20_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c20_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 21, "vignette": "Hector Diakos is a financial analyst at an asset management \nfirm.  His supervisor, Penelope Panagos, asks him to \nresearch three companies from different industries—\nOthonos Retailers, Sagitta Health, and Galatas Airlines—\nand update their valuations.\nFirst, Diakos analyzes Othonos, whose stock is currently \ntrading at €45.50.  He decides to value the stock by \ncomparing the forward P/E ratio, based on current market \nprice, with a justified forward P/E ratio.  Diakos:\nestimates an EPS of €3.50 for the next year (ie, the next \nfour quarters),\nbuilds a FCFE model that values the stock at €49.00, and\ncalculates a justified forward P/E ratio.\nPanagos adds that the retail industry currently has an \naverage forward P/E of 12.0.\nDiakos tells Panagos that he valued the shares of Sagitta \nHealth and Galatas Airlines as of 1 January 20X9.  The data \nused in his analysis are summarized in Exhibit 1:\nDiakos evaluated Sagitta's stock using three different price \nmultiples: forward P/E, price-to-sales (P/S), and price-to-\nbook value (P/B).  He explains the rationales supporting the \nuse of each approach to Panagos:\n\nStatement 1: P/E ratios do not require adjustments if EPS \nis positive.\nStatement 2: P/S ratios are less subject to manipulation \nthan P/E or P/B.\nStatement 3: P/B ratios are deemed appropriate to value \nmature and cyclical companies.\nPanagos then asks about Galatas Airlines.  Diakos explains \nthat the company's business is extremely sensitive to \nbusiness-cycle influences, which results in large variations \nin its annual earnings from one year to another.  Therefore, \nDiakos calculates a normalized P/E ratio using the average \nreturn on equity (ROE) method.  The 20X4–20X8 period in \nExhibit 2 represents a full business cycle for Galatas.\nPanagos wants to compare Galatas with competitors in the \nairline industry.  She is concerned, however, that it would \nnot be possible to rank the stocks according to multiples \nsince several competitors frequently experience negative \nearnings.  Diakos proposes a solution for ranking the stocks.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c21_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c21_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c21_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c21_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c21_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c21_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 22, "vignette": "Quality Roofing, Inc. is a successful, privately owned \nregional roofing company.  CEO Amanda Gagnon has \napproval from Quality's board to pursue acquiring its main \nsupplier of roofing materials, privately held RamCo, Inc.  \nGagnon asks CFO Brady Schmidt to analyze RamCo's\nfinancials.  Schmidt reviews the income statement, shown \nin Exhibit 1:\nSchmidt notes that RamCo has minimal debt and stable \nlong-term revenue growth of 4%, but he has concerns \nabout two SG&A items:\nExecutive compensation of $1.25 million\nNon-business-related CEO personal expenses of $0.10 \nmillion\nSchmidt observes that the market rate for executive \ncompensation is $0.75 million.  He also sees that RamCo\nhas $1.60 million of debt with an interest rate of 8.75%.  \nGagnon confirms that Quality Roofing would prefer not to \nchange RamCo's debt load.\nSchmidt consults with an independent appraiser to help \nhim estimate an optimal value for RamCo.  The appraiser \nsuggests that Quality Roofing value RamCo using the \ncapitalized cash flow method (CCM).  He indicates to \nSchmidt that although no public company comparables are \navailable, an adjustment for industry risk is warranted for \nthis transaction.  He also makes additional comments \nrelated to the challenges of estimating the required rate of \nreturn for a private company:\n\nComment 1:\nCompared with a similar public company, the \nlack of information concerning a private company's \noperations introduces greater uncertainty and may lead to \na higher required rate of return.\nComment 2:\nIn an evaluation of an acquisition, finance \ntheory indicates that the cost of capital should be based on \nthe buyer's capital structure and the riskiness of its cash \nflows.\nFinally, the appraiser and Schmidt gather additional data on \nRamCo, shown in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c22_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c22_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c22_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c22_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 23, "vignette": "Marina Slevin, CFA, is an equities analyst for a hedge fund.  \nShe is analyzing three companies as investment candidates \nfor the fund.\nStarlight Industries\nStarlight Industries is a publicly traded company that \nreports financial results under IFRS.  Slevin's research on \nStarlight produces the following information:\nOne family owns 60% of the common shares and occupies \n12 of the 20 seats on Starlight's board of directors.\nStarlight is capitalized with 30% long-term debt and 70% \ncommon equity.  The average interest rate on its debt is 8%.\nStarlight recently began paying dividends, and Slevin\nbelieves the company has the ability to greatly increase its \ndividend payout since the cash reported on its balance \nsheet has increased each year.\nStarlight's tax rate is 25%.\nExhibit 1 shows relevant financial information for Starlight:\nSlevin's supervisor asks her to estimate an intrinsic value for \nStarlight using either free cash flow to the firm (FCFF) or \nfree cash flow to equity (FCFE).  Slevin makes the following \nstatements in response:\n\nStatement 1: When a company pays dividends, using a \ndividend discount model is more appropriate than using a \nfree cash flow model.\nStatement 2: For Starlight, a dividend discount model \nwould be more appropriate than a free cash flow model \nsince the company is family controlled.\nHer supervisor insists, however, so Slevin is determining \nwhether to use FCFF or FCFE to value Starlight.  She offers \nthe following rationales to her supervisor for using FCFE\nRationale 1:\nFCFF is not useful for valuing a firm's equity \nwhen the firm uses debt in its capital structure.\nRationale 2:\nIf Starlight's capital structure remains stable, \nit is simpler to use FCFE to value its equity than FCFF.\nSlevin's supervisor tells her that she could also use cash \nflow from operations (CFO) as a starting point to derive \nStarlight's FCFF and asks what adjustments to reported CFO \nwould be needed.  Slevin makes the following observations \nin response:\nObservation 1:One adjustment to CFO is to subtract \nchanges in the company's working capital.\nObservation 2:Another adjustment to CFO is to add back \ndepreciation expense.\nObservation 3:If Starlight treats interest paid as financing \ncash flow, then the after-tax interest paid would be added \nback to CFO.\nDeejon Enterprises\nSlevin is also evaluating Deejon Enterprises, which is a \nprivately owned foreign company.  Slevin's assumptions \nabout and estimates for Deejon are shown in Exhibit 2:\n\nHorn Trading Corp.\nFinally, Slevin analyzes Horn Trading Corp., a publicly traded \ncompany based in the United States.  She estimates that \nHorn Trading's FCFF will grow at a constant 2% each year.  \nRelevant information concerning Horn Trading is shown in \nExhibit 3:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c23_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c23_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c23_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c23_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c23_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c23_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 24, "vignette": "Sofia Almado is a research analyst with a large European \ninvestment bank.  The bank's management is considering \nthree companies for recommendation to its clients: \nSnowcap Solutions, Broadview Analytics, and Evermore Inc.  \nAlmado is charged with preparing valuations for each.\nSelected data from the financial statements for Snowcap is \nshown in Exhibit 1, which Almado will use to derive FCFF.  \nSnowcap's applicable tax rate is 30%.\nOne of Almado's colleagues asked her why she used FCFF \ninstead of FCFE to value Snowcap.  She responded with \nseveral ideas.\nBroadview plans to repurchase common stock.  The most \nrecent annual FCFE for Broadview is €375 million, but \nAlmado wishes to understand how the FCFE would be \naffected by planned stock repurchases totaling €30 million \nduring the same period.  Exhibit 2 shows selected financial \ndata for Broadview:\n\nAlmado believes FCF models are the best option for \nestimating Broadview's value.  Her manager, however, \nstates that since Broadview has paid steady dividends for \nseveral years, a better option would be to use dividend \ndiscount models (DDM).  Almado counters that she would \nnormally agree, but Broadview has characteristics that led \nher to choose FCF valuation.  Among them, she states that \nBroadview has:\nReason 1:\nLow dividends relative to its positive FCFE\nReason 2:\nA diverse investor base without concentrated \nstock positions\nReason 3:\nProfitability that does not align with FCF over \nthe forecast period\nFinally, Almado is reviewing Evermore's financials as part of \nher analysis.  She is interested in calculating the company's \nFCFF from its reported net income.  She needs to determine \nthe proper treatment of the following noncash items:\nItem 1:An impairment charge for an intangible asset\nItem 2:A noncash gain related to nonoperating activities\nItem 3:Amortization of discounts related to the purchase of \nlong-term bond holdings", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c24_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c24_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c24_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c24_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c24_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c24_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 25, "vignette": "Mei Tong, an equity fund manager at Pearl Investments, is \nanalyzing several companies during a market downturn, \nstarting with Sapphire Electronics.  Sapphire's earnings are \nhighly cyclical and are the lowest they've been in years.  \nGiven the current stage of the business cycle, Tong decides \nto calculate P/E based on normalized earnings using the \nmethod of historical average EPS and the method of \naverage return on equity.  She collects the following data \nfrom the last full business cycle:\nTong plans to compare Sapphire with competitors based on \ntheir P/E ratios but estimates significantly different growth \nrates between the companies.  She decides to use the P/E-\nto-growth (PEG) ratio instead of P/E.  Bao Lam, a colleague \nat Pearl, reminds her about several considerations when \nusing the PEG ratio:\nConsideration 1:\nThe PEG ratio does not account for \ndifferences in risk between companies.\nConsideration 2:\nCompany-specific growth should be \nnormalized for the growth of the overall economy.\nConsideration 3:\nThe PEG ratio assumes a nonlinear \nrelationship between P/E and growth and must be adjusted \nfor linearity.\nTong and Lam discuss how to evaluate the relative value of \nGranite Manufacturing compared with peers and then \nappropriately rank the companies according to a price ratio.  \nGranite has experienced losses in recent years, and\n\nearnings are expected to remain negative for the \nforeseeable future.  Granite uses a unique, asset-light \nmanufacturing process that differs from the asset-centric \nprocess its peers use, giving it a smaller asset base and \nlower COGS than its peers.\nTong turns her attention to Sunrise Telecommunications.  \nShe wants to compare its value with industry averages \nbased on several ratios.  Using this comparison, she will \ndetermine whether the company is undervalued or \novervalued.  She collects the following information on \nSunrise and the industry:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c25_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c25_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c25_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c25_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 26, "vignette": "Tomás Montero is a financial analyst at an independent \nequity research firm.  He is updating, as of January 1, 20X6, \nthe research reports on Industrias Melendez and Tiendas \nGuadalupe.  On that date, the stock prices of Melendez are \nMXN 150 (per share of common) and MXN 80 (per share of \npreferred).\nMontero summarizes Melendez's financial statements in \nExhibit 1:\n\nMontero calculates Melendez's enterprise value (EV) and its \nEV/EBITDA ratio.  He also calculates the price-to-book (P/B) \nratio and compares it with the industry multiple of 1.15.\nMontero presents his conclusions to his supervisor, Yolanda \nBarcenas, who asks him to calculate price multiples based \non cash flow measures.  Montero explains that there are \nalternative definitions of cash flow, and each has specific \nlimitations:\nLimitation 1:\nCash flow from operations (CFO) is not a free \ncash flow concept.\nLimitation 2:\nCF (earnings plus noncash charges) is more \nvolatile than FCFE.\nLimitation 3:\nEBITDA will exceed CFO if working capital is \nconsistently decreasing.\nBarcenas asks Montero to include a momentum valuation \nindicator in his analysis.  She recommends using an \nindicator that compares Melendez's stock performance \nwith the performance of a group of similar stocks.\nMontero turns his attention to Guadalupe.  He gathers \ninformation about the company and a competitor, \nComercial Arana, summarized in Exhibit 2:\nMontero notices a significant difference in the trailing price-\nto-sales (P/S) multiples of both companies.  He analyzes \nthree fundamentals—beta, profit margin, and earnings \ngrowth rate—to understand whether any of those factors \ncould help explain, at least in part, the difference between \nthe multiples.  In this analysis, Montero assumes that both \ncompanies' dividend payout ratio and ROE will not change \nin the long run.\n\nMontero also analyzes a large number of stocks in the \nindustry and builds a cross-sectional regression model to \npredict Guadalupe's P/E.  He compares the predicted price-\nto-earnings (P/E) ratio with Guadalupe's current trailing P/E \nto evaluate the stock.  The model is:\nPredicted P/E = 10.7 + (1.1 × Dividend payout ratio) − (0.2 ×\nBeta) + (65.6 × Earnings growth rate)", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c26_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c26_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c26_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c26_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c26_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c26_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 27, "vignette": "uigi Pellegrini is a portfolio manager at an asset \nmanagement firm.  He asks Gianna Veronese, a senior \nanalyst at the firm, to perform an analysis of Grivola Group, \nAdamello Industries, and Secchia Systems.  He directs \nVeronese to calculate the companies' market-based \nmultiples and compare them with an appropriate \nbenchmark.\nVeronese explains that there are alternative choices of \ncomparison assets when the method of comparables is \nused, including a peer group, the firm's industry, or the \nfirm's historical multiples.  After reviewing Grivola Group, a \nholding company that operates in different business \nsegments, she states:\nStatement 1: Grivola's peer group must consist of \ncompanies from the same industry that have no significant \ndifferences from Grivola.\nStatement 2: Since Grivola operates in different business \nsegments, it may be classified in more than one industry or \nsector.\nStatement 3: The comparison of current and historical P/E \nmay be affected by changes in Grivola's business mix or \nleverage.\nVeronese adds that a multiple of a representative equity \nindex may also be used as a benchmark.  However, since \nthe overall market may be over- or undervalued, she uses \ntwo models—the Fed model and the Yardeni model—to \nvalue the equity index multiple.\nPellegrini would like to compare Grivola with international \npeers, but he expresses concern about differences in \ninflation rates.  Veronese answers that a justified forward \nP/E can be calculated based on forecasted fundamentals \nusing the information in Exhibit 1.  She uses a constant \ngrowth model that assumes earnings growth for the three \ncompanies results only from inflation and that earnings are \ndistributed entirely as dividends.\n\nVeronese adds that, in the analysis, she defined cash flow \nas earnings plus noncash charges and considered that \nAdamello's industry had a P/CF of 9.0.  Adamello's income \nstatement is summarized in Exhibit 2:\nExhibit 3 presents additional financial information on \nAdamello gathered by Veronese:\nFinally, Veronese switches her focus to Secchia Systems.  \nSince Secchia has been paying dividends regularly, she \ndecides to calculate the company's trailing dividend yield \nand compare it with a justified multiple based on a constant \ngrowth model.  She gathers information on the company, \npresented in Exhibit 4:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c27_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c27_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c27_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c27_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c27_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c27_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 28, "vignette": "Dimitri Mazur was recently hired as a junior research \nanalyst at Nivki Partners, a sell-side brokerage firm.  Oksana \nKoval is Mazur's supervisor and is responsible for covering \nconsumer cyclical stocks.\nKoval asks Mazur what he knows about the use of residual \nincome models.  Mazur responds that, although there are \nother discounted cash flow models, residual income models \nare most suitable for companies that are:\nType 1:Non-dividend paying\nType 2: \nEarly-stage start-ups\nType 3: \nHave terminal values that can be estimated \nwith high confidence\nKoval agrees that other discounted cash flow models are \navailable and believes they can be useful in certain \nsituations.  However, she states that for estimating a \ncompany's intrinsic value:\nStatement 1: An FCF approach is preferred because it \nprovides a more accurate estimate than a residual income \napproach.\nStatement 2: Dividend discount models are too simplistic \nand provide a less accurate intrinsic valuation assessment.\nKoval then provides Mazur with the most recent annual \nfinancial information on Work Shoe, Inc.  She asks him to \nreview this information and be prepared to calculate an \nintrinsic value:\n\nKoval then turns her attention to Auto Parts, Inc. (AP), a \nleading after-market auto part retailer in the country.  AP \nhas grown steadily over the past few years, and Koval\nexpects that trend to continue.\nKoval adds the following assumptions for AP:\nEarnings per share will grow at 10% of beginning book value \nfor each of the next three years.\nThe present value of residual income per share for the next \nthree years is €3.74.\nEnding book value per share in three years' time is €30.15.\nIn three years' time, AP will trade at a price-to-book ratio of \n1.6.\nKoval mentions that several years ago, AP acquired an \nexclusive 10-year license to sell a particular brand of \nautomotive battery for a nominal fee.  This battery brand is \nnow the most popular in the country.\nAP is also rumored to be the target of a takeover by an \nequal-size direct competitor.  Koval asks Mazur to consider \nthe possible implications for the estimated residual income \nintrinsic value of the combined company if AP were \nacquired in a fair value cash transaction that produced no \ngoodwill.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c28_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c28_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c28_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c28_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c28_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c28_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 29, "vignette": "Ilse Hauser is an investor performing valuations on several \ncompanies.  She read about Turbo Mechanical in an online \narticle and wants to follow up with her own residual income \nvaluation.  Turbo is a rapidly growing auto parts \nmanufacturer with a 6% WACC and 20% tax rate.  Hauser \ncompiles Turbo's latest financials:\nDuring her research, Hauser notices that an industry leader, \nStrasse Industries, plans to acquire a specialty \nmanufacturer, Rennen Auto.  Both are mature companies, \nwith no expected future growth, that pay out all of their \nincome as dividends and have required rates of return of \n10%.  This will remain true after the acquisition.  Strasse is \nacquiring Rennen for €2 billion in an all-cash deal with no \nplanned synergies.  Hauser collects financial information on \nboth companies and calculates that Rennen's fair value is \n€2 billion, including its customer list.\nHauser notices that Strasse has been reporting foreign \ncurrency translation losses in other comprehensive income \nover the past two quarters.  Based on her macroeconomic \nforecast, she expects the PV of future foreign currency \ntranslation gains/losses to net to zero.\n\nReiner Eskelson, another investor Hauser collaborates with, \nasks Hauser why she uses the residual income model over \nother discounted cash flow models.  Hauser provides the \nfollowing reasons:\nReason 1:\nResidual income can be used even when \ncash flows are unpredictable.\nReason 2:\nBook value is less susceptible to \nmanipulation than income statement metrics.\nReason 3:\nThe model does not assume the cost of debt \nis appropriately reflected by interest expense.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c29_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c29_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c29_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c29_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 30, "vignette": "Jeremy Collins and Bridget Parker manage an equity fund of \ndividend-paying stocks and are conducting a quarterly \nevaluation of the fund's constituents.  They are also \nassessing several companies that have recently initiated \ndividends for inclusion in the fund.  Collins collects \ninformation (shown in Exhibit 1) about shares of Federal \nMills, which initiated a dividend last year.\nCollins and Parker agree to use the two-stage dividend \ndiscount model for Federal Mills, given a short-term boost \nto earnings growth from a new product.  They are \ndiscussing the best methods to calculate the terminal value, \nusing the estimated final dividend and earnings values \ncalculated by the model.  Parker suggests three alternative \nmethods:\nMethod 1:\nCalculate the terminal year stock price using \na price ratio.\nMethod 2:\nForecast the value of all future dividends \nusing the Gordon growth model.\nMethod 3:\nCalculate the terminal value as the dividend \ndiscounted by the long-term growth rate.\nFor another stock recently added to the fund, Garrison \nMedia, Parker begins calculating the rate of return required \nby investors based on its current stock price of $15.  She \nexpects Garrison's dividend growth rate to decline linearly \nduring a transition period before reaching a constant \ngrowth rate.  Parker's estimates are provided in Exhibit 2:\n\nCollins then evaluates Traverse Technologies, which \ninitiated a dividend last year.  Collins expects Traverse's \ngrowth to slow in phases from its current pace, but he also \nbelieves the company will pay a special dividend.  The \nspecial dividend does not affect the base dividend used to \ncalculate payments in future years.  Collins presents his \nestimates in Exhibit 3:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c30_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c30_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c30_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c30_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_equity", "source_label": "UWorld – Equity", "topic": "Equity", "case_no": 31, "vignette": "Axelrod Partners, an investment banking firm, has been \nretained by two firms, HR-Pay Corp. and Potterville \nFinancial, as each is preparing for an IPO.\nHR-Pay is an online payroll and human resource software \nprovider that is rapidly growing market share and revenue \nbut is not yet profitable.  HR-Pay is currently in the process \nof raising equity capital to strengthen its financial position \nby selling a 5% stake through a restricted stock private \nplacement.  This sale will be in advance of the IPO, which is \nexpected to occur in the coming months.  No individual \nshareholder owns more than 25% of HR-Pay.\nCatherine Evans, an investment banking analyst at Axelrod, \nis creating a financial model for HR-Pay while also \nconsidering an appropriate valuation for the restricted stock \nsale.  Evans is contemplating whether a discount for lack of \nmarketability (DLOM) for HR-Pay's restricted stock is \nappropriate.  Evans believes that using the guideline public \ncompany method (GPCM) based on market value of \ninvested capital (MVIC) to EBITDA multiple is an appropriate \napproach for the IPO valuation.  She compiles the following:\nIn addition to researching comparable private sales of stock \nprior to a subsequent IPO, Evans is also considering a DLOM \nbased on an option pricing model, which estimates the \nDLOM as the value of the option as a percent of HR-Pay's \nprice.  Evans narrows her choices down to the following \nthree option models, based on:\n\nOption model 1:  At-the-money put option\nOption model 2:  In-the-money call option\nOption model 3:  Out-of-the-money call option\nEvans turns her attention to Potterville, a private financial \nholding company with affiliated companies that provide \ntraditional banking, investment management, and real \nestate development.  Potterville is wholly owned by a single \nfamily, except for a 15% minority equity interest owned by \nJim Landry, a private investor.  Family members hold all \nsignificant management positions in the entire \norganization.\nPotterville needs growth funding to expand the business \nand intends to sell a 10% equity stake in the IPO.  Evans \nbelieves that, in this situation, an asset-based valuation \napproach using the GPCM is appropriate.  Based on this \ninformation and her own estimates, Evans's initial valuation \nestimate for Potterville is £100 million.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_equity_c31_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c31_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c31_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c31_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c31_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_equity_c31_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Malee Sangthong, CEO of Thai Electronics Public Company \nLimited (Thaitronics), is preparing a presentation on two of \nthe company's post-employment retirement benefits plans \nto be included in the upcoming board meeting. Plan A \nprovides life and health care insurance, and Plan B promises \nyearly payments for life. Sangthong calls Somchai Nguyen, \nan investment analyst, and requests additional information \nabout Plan B.\nNguyen explains the following:\nThe present value (PV) of Plan B obligations is THB 20 \nbillion, the fair value (FV) of assets is THB 25 billion, and the \nPV of future economic benefits is THB 3.5 billion.\nPlan B's yearly payments to retirees are based on this \nformula: 1.5% × Years of service × Final salary.\nThaitronics measures its pension obligations using the \nprojected unit credit method since the company reports \nunder IFRS.\nSangthong asks Nguyen to provide an example of how the \nmethod works. In Exhibit 1, Nguyen presents information \ngathered from both a participant and the plan assumptions:\nNguyen then demonstrates the calculation of the annual \nunit credit.\nIn the current period, Thaitronics reported an operating \nincome of THB 1.5 billion and profit before taxes of THB 1.2 \nbillion. Sangthong is concerned about Thaitronics's\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\noperating performance and asks Nguyen how benefits \nexpenses are reported in the profit and loss (P&L) \nstatement. Nguyen presents the components of post- \nretirement benefits expenses in Exhibit 2:\nNguyen explains that all benefits are classified as operating \nexpenses, but he could make adjustments to address that \nconcern. Nguyen then reclassifies benefits as operating and \nnon-operating expenses to recalculate profit before \ntaxation.\nThaitronics also grants options in the company's stock to its \nmanagers as part of their compensation. Sangthong is \nconcerned about the negative effect of the stock option \nplan on the company's net income. Although the number \nof stock options granted to Thaitronics's managers did not \nchange materially in recent years, reported compensation \nexpense increased significantly in the current year. Nguyen \nexplains that these variations may be due to assumptions \non which the FV of options is based, including volatility, \ndividend yield, and estimated life. He adds that using \nshare-based compensation—such as stock options, stock \ngrants, and stock appreciation rights (SAR)—has the \nfollowing disadvantages:\nDisadvantage 1: \nStock options may lead managers to \nseek higher returns and undertake riskier projects.\nDisadvantage 2: \nStock grants and SAR increase the \nnumber of outstanding shares and dilute shareholders' \nownership.\nDisadvantage 3: \nShare-based compensation is less \nefficient than cash bonuses for aligning the interests of \nmanagers and shareholders.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Sicherheit Insurance Group is a German property and \ncasualty insurance company. Otto Krause, CFO of \nSicherheit, is reviewing the company's investments and \nacquisitions. He first meets with Rebekka Boesch, portfolio \nmanager at Sicherheit. Boesch explains that there have \nbeen significant changes to three of the company's \ninvestments:\nInvestment 1: A debt instrument was purchased to \ngenerate cash flows through the receipt of interest \npayments, but Boesch decided to realize profit through a \nsale.\nInvestment 2: A debt instrument was purchased to realize \nprofit through a sale, but after the issuer missed an interest \npayment, Boesch is holding the asset to minimize realized \nlosses.\nInvestment 3: An equity investment was purchased to \nrealize profit through trading, but after dividends were \ninitiated, Boesch decided to hold the asset to collect \ndividends.\nKrause asks about the equity investment in Hamburg Bank's \nstock; Sicherheit owns 5% of the stock. For reporting \npurposes, Sicherheit had classified the investment as fair \nvalue through other comprehensive income (FVOCI).\nBoesch provides Krause data on the investment:\nBoesch notes that companies Sicherheit invests in are often \nacquirers themselves. However, since some report under \nIFRS but others use US GAAP, their financials are not always \ncomparable.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nKrause then reviews Sicherheit's recent acquisitions. \nSicherheit plans to close its purchase of 80% of Rhine \nReinsurance stock for €56 million in the coming days.\nSicherheit agreed to purchase Rhine using 1 million shares \nof its €1 par stock, priced at €56 to fund the purchase. The \ncompanies' financials are provided in Exhibit 2:\nKrause also reviews Westen Insurers, a company that \nSicherheit acquired three years ago. During the most \nrecent review, it was determined that Westen has:\na carrying value of €76 million, \nrecoverable amount of €70 million,\nidentifiable net assets of €69.5 million, and \na fair value of €69 million.\nSicherheit carries €2 million of goodwill from this\nacquisition.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Leilani Kalela, CFA, is a recently hired junior equity analyst \nat Corona Investment Management, a buy-side firm. Kalela \nhas been tasked with becoming a subject matter expert on \ncompanies and stocks in the financial sector. Her coverage \nuniverse consists mostly of banks and insurance companies.\nKalela reports to Kai Oshiro, lead financial sector analyst at \nCorona. To test Kalela's knowledge of the insurance \nindustry, Oshiro asks Kalela to describe the combined ratio.\nKalela makes three observations about the combined ratio:\nObservation 1:It represents underwriting profitability\nadjusted for global risk-based capital requirements.\nObservation 2:It includes investment returns.\nObservation 3:A combined ratio greater than 100% is \ndesirable.\nOshiro then asks Kalela to compare property and casualty \ninsurers (P&C) to life and health insurers (L&H). Kalela \nnotes the following differences:\nDifference 1: \nrequirements.\nDifference 2:\nDifference 3:\nL&H insurers generally have higher capital\nL&H insurers' claims are less predictable. \nThe combined ratio is more appropriate for\nP&C insurers than for life insurers.\nOshiro then shifts the discussion to the banking sector and \nprovides Kalela with an earnings breakdown for three \nbanks, shown in Exhibit 1:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nOshiro notes that Bank C's net interest income has been \nunusually volatile over the past few years, as shown in \nExhibit 2:\nIn a final note, Oshiro observes that the central bank is \nexpected to cut its policy rate by 25 basis points at the \nbeginning of the next quarter. In anticipation of this \nchange, Bank A has exactly matched its assets and liabilities \nin terms of interest rates, maturities, and repricing \nfrequencies, and its Level 3 assets have increased \nsignificantly over the past six months.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Miguel Reyes, a senior portfolio manager, is considering \nadding two stocks—Platinum Solar Energy (PSE) and Hill \nBrothers Stores (HBS)—to the mutual fund he manages. \nBoth companies report under US GAAP and have \nconsiderable pension obligations. Reyes is concerned about \nhow these pension plans may affect the companies' \nfinancial statements and asks Patricia Stewart, a senior \nanalyst, to provide a deeper analysis.\nStewart gathers information from PSE's financial statements \nin Exhibit 1:\nStewart explains that part of periodic pension costs is \nrecognized in the profit and loss statement (P&L) and \nclassified as pension expenses, while the other part is \nreported in other comprehensive income (OCI.) She also \nreminds Reyes that PSE recently disclosed that the company \nwill launch a share-based compensation program. PSE is \ncurrently analyzing three alternatives: stock appreciation \nrights (SAR), performance shares, and stock options.\nStewart explains the alternatives and makes the following \nstatements:\nStatement 1: Stock appreciation rights do not dilute the \nownership interests of the company's shareholders since \nthey are based on the performance of hypothetical stock.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nStatement 2: Performance shares must be returned to the \ncompany if the employee resigns or does not achieve \ncertain performance goals.\nStatement 3: In a stock option plan, compensation expense \nis typically measured on the date options are granted and \nrecognized over the service period.\nStewart then gathers information from HBS's financial \nstatements in Exhibit 2:\nStewart explains that HBS pension plan's net unrecognized \nlosses reported in OCI at the beginning of the year are \namortized to the P&L under the corridor approach. She \nalso explains that, under US GAAP, all pension contributions \nare treated as operating activities in the cash flow \nstatement. However, since the difference between \ncontributions and pension cost could be interpreted as a \nsource of financing, she proposes an adjustment to HBS's \noperating cash flow in her analysis.\nReyes believes that the expected return on plan assets is \nhigh given the current market scenario. Stewart says that \nthis is the company's assumption and explains how changes \nin that assumption would affect HBS's financial statements.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Lightspeed Industries is a British company that \nmanufactures specialty computer hardware which it sells \nglobally. It reports financial results under IFRS and uses \nGBP as its functional and reporting currency. On October \n15, 20X2, Lightspeed made two transactions:\nTransaction 1: Sold goods to an American company for USD \n2 million and will receive this amount on January 20, 20X3.\nTransaction 2: Purchased inventory from a German supplier \nfor EUR 1.6 million and will pay this amount on January 20, \n20X3.\nExhibit 1 shows the relevant exchange rates for these \ntransactions:\nLightspeed has two wholly owned subsidiaries, Impala \nSemiconductors, which it created in 20X1, and Sarallen \nSwitches, which it created many years before it created \nImpala. Sarallen and Impala sell different products, so they \ndo not compete for customers. All three companies use the \ncalendar year as their fiscal year.\nImpala reports financial results under IFRS and is required \nto use the Peruvian sol (PEN) as its reporting currency.\nAdditional information on Impala is:\n50% of its sales are to companies in Argentina. \nApproximately 30% of its sales in Argentina are\ndenominated in Argentine pesos (ARS), with the remainder\ndenominated in PEN.\nAll sales are fulfilled from inventory in Peru.\nImpala's board of directors has 15 members; Lightspeed \ncan appoint three members to the board.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nSarallen is in Argentina and uses ARS as its reporting \ncurrency. It hires its own employees without input from \nLightspeed, maintains its own sales force, and most of its \nsales are denominated in ARS. On January 1, 20X1, it \npurchased undeveloped land in Argentina for ARS 4 million \nto build a factory/warehouse facility. Exhibit 2 contains \ninformation concerning exchange rates and economic \ninformation for Sarallen:\nExhibit 3 shows data from Sarallen's 20X2 income \nstatement and balance sheet:\nSarallen's common stock was issued when the Argentinean \nprice index was 100; its long-term debt is denominated in \nARS.\nJarvis Fung, CFA, is a junior financial analyst with Lightspeed \nwho has been assigned to analyze several aspects of\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nSarallen's results to determine their impact on Lightspeed's \nconsolidated financial statements. Specifically, Fung's \nsupervisor wants him to answer three questions:\nWhat value should Lightspeed report on its 20X2 \nconsolidated financial statements for the land purchased by \nSarallen?\nAssuming that the ARS is expected to continue to weaken \nagainst the GBP, how could Sarallen increase its purchasing \npower?\nOn its consolidated statements, where does Lightspeed \nreport Sarallen's purchasing power gain or loss?\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Edvin Sundberg, head of research at Kalix Brokers, meets \nwith Mona Lundin, the firm's analyst covering the \nmachinery manufacturing industry. Sundberg wants to \ndiscuss Lundin's findings about three companies that she \nhas been researching: Caph Corporation, Enif Group, and \nRigel Industries, all reporting under IFRS.\nLundin tells Sundberg that, in an initial analysis, she noted \nthat the ROE of the three companies are similar and above \nindustry average. She applied the mean reversion principle \nin her forecast of each company's future ROE, breaking \nearnings down into a cash flow component, expressed by \noperating cash flow (OCF), and an accruals component.\nLundin's forecasts are based on the companies' 20X2 net \nincome and OCF, shown in Exhibit 1:\nLundin also assessed the likelihood of misreporting. She \nused a quantitative tool, the Beneish model, to calculate \nthe probability of manipulation, expressed by the M-scores \nin Exhibit 2:\nSundberg asks Lundin if she noticed any warning signs of \ndeterioration in Caph's OCF. Lundin explains that, in the \nlast month of 20X2, Caph:\nSold products on credit in a one-off transaction to a special \npurpose entity\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nReceived an early payment discount to pay a supplier 90 \ndays before maturity\nStarted to capitalize certain costs that are typically treated \nas expenses by the industry\nSundberg says that Enif's long-term contracts to purchase \ncomponents have been structured as take-or-pay \nagreements. These obligations are normally recorded on \nEnif's financial statements only when the purchase takes \nplace and the components are received. Sundberg is \nconcerned that this may affect the quality of Enif's balance \nsheet and asks Lundin to estimate the present value of the \nobligations and related assets.\nLundin tells Sundberg that she completed the risk \nevaluation of Rigel, using three main sources of information \nin her analysis:\nnews about Rigel in the financial press,\nRigel's management commentary (ie, management \ndiscussion and analysis [MD&A]), and\nthe auditor's opinion on Rigel's financial statements.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Xia Munashe, a bank examiner, and Terry Meztli, an analyst, \nare assigned to review Bank HIJ. They incorporate the \nCAMELS rating system in their evaluation. Bank HIJ is a \ncommunity bank with investments heavily allocated in the \nfarming industry, specifically real estate financing. Xia tells \nTerry: \"The CAMELS rating system is not a thorough \napproach to assessing banks and should be combined with \na separate analysis of factors that CAMELS does not \nconsider.\"\nXia explains how the bank's capital is categorized in \ndifferent tiers under the CAMELS rating system. Terry notes \nthat the bank has changed its capital structure and issued \nnoncumulative perpetual preferred stock to pay down \nsubordinated debt maturing in 7 years.\nTerry then analyzes the second component of the CAMELS \nrating system, asset quality, and states that Bank HIJ has not \nrecorded adequate provisioning for expected loan losses in \nthe coming year. Terry gathers information, shown in \nExhibits 1 and 2, to evaluate the bank.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nXia then makes the following comments:\nComment 1: The CAMELS approach uses the net stable \nfunding ratio (NSFR) when assessing the liquidity of a bank.\nComment 2: All else equal, increasing the maturity of \nloans versus deposits will improve both the NSFR and \nliquidity coverage ratio (LCR).\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Emily Gibson is a senior analyst covering the manufacturing \nsector for an Australian asset manager. She has been \ndirected by her supervisor, Noah Murphy, to assess the \nquality of the financial reports of three companies: \nWoodroffe Industries, Brumlow Group, and Meharry \nManufacturing.\nAfter analyzing Woodroffe Industries' financial reports, \nGibson noted that:\nthe reports comply with IFRS;\nearnings derive primarily from recurring activities and \nreturns are greater than the cost of capital; and\nmanagement uses reserves and allowances to reduce \nearnings variation and to increase predictability.\nGibson learned that Woodroffe acquired a manufacturing \nplant on the last day of 20X6, generating a substantial \namount of goodwill. After a detailed analysis, Gibson \nconcluded that Woodroffe's management likely overstated \ngoodwill by understating the fair value of intangible assets \nat the time of the acquisition. The company did not report \nany goodwill impairment charges in 20X7.\nAfterward, Gibson tells Murphy that she has arrived at two \nconclusions about Brumlow Group:\nConclusion 1: The CEO and CFO reported fictitious \ntransactions with service providers and misappropriated \nthe funds paid by Brumlow.\nConclusion 2: Brumlow sold a manufacturing plant at a \ngain but did not deduct this gain from net income when \nconstructing the indirect cash flow statement.\nMurphy asks Gibson about Meharry Manufacturing. He is \nconcerned that Meharry may have been overstating its \nrevenues, and he gives Gibson two suggestions:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nSuggestion 1: Calculate revenue metrics, like revenue per \noutput or area, and compare those metrics with peers.\nSuggestion 2: Find out if Meharry's officers or shareholders \nown companies that are also Meharry's clients.\nGibson adds that Meharry's cost capitalization policies are \nsignificantly more aggressive than those of its competitors. \nShe believes that the company is improperly capitalizing \ncosts that should have been expensed. She has analyzed \nMeharry's financial statements, investigating trends that \ncould indicate an improper capitalization of costs.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c8_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c8_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Manor Furnishings (Manor) is a publicly traded British \ncompany that manufactures home furniture. Sleepwell \nBedding (Sleepwell) is a privately held British company that \nmakes mattresses. Sleepwell's CEO, Jacques Penrose, owns \n100% of Sleepwell's common shares. Manor and Penrose \nhave agreed that Manor will purchase 50% of Sleepwell's \ncommon shares for GBP 17 million on January 1, 20X1.\nManor will purchase its interest in Sleepwell entirely from \nits cash reserves. Both companies report results under IFRS \nand each uses the calendar year as its fiscal year. Sleepwell \nwill be a fully integrated subsidiary of Manor; Manor will \nmake all decisions pertaining to Sleepwell.\nExhibit 1 shows selected information for the two \ncompanies' balance sheets on December 31, 20X0, \nimmediately prior to the transaction:\nSleepwell's PPE, patents, and trademarks have a remaining \nuseful life of 20 years. Manor uses the straight-line method \nto depreciate noncurrent assets.\nExhibit 2 shows each company's abbreviated income \nstatement for the year ending December 31, 20X0, and its \nprojected income statement for the period ending \nDecember 31, 20X1:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Mallory Mill is an accredited investor who focuses on \ninvesting in financial stocks. Mill is currently reviewing the \nfinancial performance of her holdings with her cousin Sarah \nSkinner, another financial stock investor. They first review \nTreehouse Bank.\nMill assesses Treehouse's liquidity performance by \nexamining information related to the bank's liquidity \ncoverage under a 30-day stress scenario, shown in Exhibit 1:\nSkinner mentions that, in addition to the liquidity coverage \nratio (LCR), the net stable funds ratio (NSFR) is also a metric \nfor analyzing liquidity. Treehouse's NSFR ratio is low \ncompared with the industry. Mill asks about changes in \nbalance sheet items that can increase the NSFR.\nThey turn their attention to Springfeel Group, a property \nand casualty (P&C) insurance company. Mill understands \nthat managing loss reserves is critical to running a P&C \nbusiness and gathers the loss reserve information shown in \nExhibit 2:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nFinally, they assess Shelbyhill Group, a life and health \ninsurance company. Mill and Skinner analyze the sources of \nrevenue information shown in Exhibit 3:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Ada Richter, an equity analyst for a European brokerage \nfirm, is currently researching three companies in the \nautomotive industry: Nordstern Auto, Horizont Industries, \nand Ausdauer Motors.\nInitially, Richter applies a sequence of steps to evaluate the \nquality of Nordstern's financial reports. After analyzing the \ncompany's financial statements, she concludes that \nNordstern complies with IFRS and provides decision-useful \ninformation. She determines with confidence that return \non investment was consistently above the cost of capital \nduring the previous five years. Richter believes that \nNordstern's current challenge is to find new sources of \nrevenue since a substantial part of its earnings comes from \na special project that is expected to end in the following \nyear.\nRichter receives a call from Gertrude Scholz, her supervisor, \nwho asks if she has finished her analysis of Horizont \nIndustries and Ausdauer Motors. Richter informs Scholz \nthat she has reached the following conclusions about \nHorizont:\nConclusion 1: Horizont issues invoices to its clients and \nrecognizes revenues for goods that remain on the \ncompany's premises until a later date. These sales \narrangements are unusual but allowed under specific \nconditions.\nConclusion 2:   Horizont's allowance for doubtful accounts, \nas a percentage of accounts receivable, is significantly lower \nthan the allowance estimated by its main competitors.\nRichter then presents to Scholz a table with information \nabout Ausdauer's revenues and accounts receivable for the \nlast three years (Exhibit 1):\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nRichter also advises Scholz that, according to Note 6 of \nAusdauer's 20X5 financial statements:\nIn 20X5, the Company entered into an agreement to sell \ncertain trade receivables to a third-party financial \ninstitution. On December 30, 20X5, the Company received\n€15.0 million from the sale of trade accounts receivables.\nThese proceeds are included in cash from operating \nactivities, and those receivables were not included in 20X5 \nbalance sheet.\nScholz wants to better understand Ausdauer's earnings \nquality and asks Richter to build a pro forma table excluding \nthis transaction.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nLang & Bauer Group (LBG) is a German company that \nreports using IFRS. LBG has two retirement benefit plans: a \ndefined contribution plan, open to all employees, and a \ndefined benefit plan, closed to new participants.\nLBG's chief executive officer, Emma Schuster, asks the chief \nfinancial officer, Jan Berger, to outline the current situation \nof the company's retirement benefit plans. Berger explains \nthat the plans have distinct features, and although the \naccounting treatment of the defined contribution plan is \nrelatively simple, the defined benefit plan requires a more \ndetailed analysis. Berger presents the following \ninformation from the defined benefit plan:\nBerger warns Schuster that this information relies on some \nkey assumptions, and that changes in these assumptions \nwould have material effects on the estimates of benefit \nobligations and periodic pension costs. Berger lists the \ndiscount rate, the annual compensation growth, and life \nexpectancy as some of these key assumptions.\nSchuster asks Berger to prepare a comparison with defined \nbenefit plans of LBG's peers in Europe and in the United \nStates. Berger explains that, since US companies comply \nwith US GAAP, there are several differences in the way that \npension costs are recognized in the profit and loss (P&L)\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nand other comprehensive income (OCI) statements. \nSchuster asks Berger to provide examples of how the \naccounting treatment under US GAAP differs from the \ntreatment under IFRS. Berger states that under US GAAP:\nExample 1: Actuarial losses (gains) recognized in OCI are \nnot subsequently amortized to P&L.\nExample 2: The effects of plan amendments on obligations \nfor prior services are recognized in P&L.\nExample 3: Differences between actual and expected \nreturn on assets result in an actuarial loss or gain.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Element Pharmaceuticals is a European-based biotech \ncompany. Management is analyzing its position in 20X9. At \nthe end of 20X3, Element purchased a 40% stake in Spire\nPharmaceuticals for €70 million. Element was deemed to\nhave significant influence over Spire but not control. \nExhibit 1 shows Spire's balance sheet at the time of \npurchase:\nSpire depreciates its laboratory equipment over 10 years \nand its office buildings over 20 years, using the straight-line \nmethod and assuming no salvage value.\nExhibit 2 shows Spire's net income and dividends paid over \nthe next five years:\nThere were no related-party transactions between Element \nand Spire until 20X7. Then the following transactions \noccurred:\nIn 20X7, Spire sold goods with a carrying value of €3 million \nto Element and realized a €1 million profit on this \ntransaction. Element sold these goods at cost in 20X9.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nIn 20X8, Element sold goods with a carrying value of €6.5 \nmillion to Spire for €10 million. Spire sold €3 million of \nthese goods in 20X8 and sold the remainder in 20X9.\nTranscontinental, a US-based biotech company, is in talks \nwith Element and Accord Bank, hoping they will provide \nTranscontinental with short-term capital. Transcontinental \nis considering two scenarios:\nScenario 1: \nTranscontinental will create a variable \ninterest entity (VIE). Element will invest $30 million in \nreturn for equity in the VIE. The VIE will then borrow $170 \nmillion from Accord Bank and purchase $200 million of \nTranscontinental's accounts receivable. Element will \nreceive the majority of the VIE's residual returns; \nTranscontinental will absorb the majority of any losses.\nScenario 2: Transcontinental will borrow $200 million \nfrom Accord Bank, with the accounts receivable used as \ncollateral.\nExhibit 3 shows items from Transcontinental's balance \nsheet:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Nishant Nayar is a senior financial sector analyst at an asset \nresearch firm. Nayar meets with Grish Gupta, a junior \nanalyst at the firm, to review several banks. Nayar tells \nGupta that the Basel Committee establishes the global \nbanking regulatory framework and that the latest guidelines \nare provided under Basel III. Gupta asks Nayar to further \nelaborate on Basel III. Nayar responds by stating that Basel \nIII requires banks to:\nStatement 1: fund a minimum percentage of their total \nasset value with equity capital\nStatement 2: hold enough highly liquid assets to cover \ntheir liquidity needs for 90 days in a stress test scenario\nStatement 3: maintain a minimum level of stable funding\nrelative to their liquidity needs over a 1-year horizon\nNayar then asks Gupta to research Samriddhi Bank and \nreport back his findings. Gupta collects information related \nto capital adequacy, shown in Exhibit 1:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nSubsequently, Gupta gathers information related to the \nbank's allowance for loan losses, shown in Exhibit 2:\nGupta finishes the analysis and presents his findings to \nNayar. Nayar asks whether, in addition to analyzing \ninformation from the bank's financial statements, Gupta \nhas assessed any off-balance-sheet considerations. Gupta \nconfirms that he has done so and reports the following \nobservations regarding off-balance-sheet activity:\nObservation 1:The bank has experienced a significant \ndecline in assets under management (AUM) year over year.\nObservation 2:The bank has started consolidating assets \nand liabilities from variable interest entities (VIEs) that were \npreviously unconsolidated.\nObservation 3:Interest rate increases this year have \nchanged the defined benefit plan's funded status.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Felipe Hernandez is a portfolio manager at Eclipsar \nFinanciero. He recently read an article about Sycamore \nTherapeutics that indicates that the company's stock is \novervalued based on its elevated risk profile. Hernandez \nasks Jada Carter, an analyst at Eclipsar, to conduct \nadditional research on the stock.\nCarter compiles the following information on Sycamore:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nHernandez asks Carter to adjust Sycamore's financials to \nbest reflect the company's ongoing operations. Carter \nplans to make the following modifications to calculate \nadjusted income:\nRemoving unusual items\nRemoving income from associates\nCarter notes that removing financial information related to \nassociates will not only change Sycamore's financial profile \nbut also affect its market metrics. Sycamore's sole \ninvestment in associates is its noncontrolling ownership of \n10 million shares of Acceleration Pharmaceuticals.\nHernandez asks Carter to compare Sycamore with its peers \nwithout the contribution from Acceleration. Sycamore's \nclosest peers have an average trailing P/E ratio of 10.4 in\n20X4. Carter observes that Sycamore's stock price is $24 \nand Acceleration's stock price is $150.\nHernandez is also concerned about Sycamore's earnings \nquality. He asks Carter to:\nReview Sycamore's accruals, noting that anything more \nthan a 10% increase in a given year or an increasing trend \nshould be a cause of concern. Hernandez asks Carter to \nremove all unusual items for this exercise.\nMeasure Sycamore's cash generation and measure whether \nselect cash flow ratios are improving or deteriorating\nHernandez reviews all the data and concludes that the \narticle's report of Sycamore's risk is exaggerated. Carter \nnotes that there is a change in accounting standards \nplanned to force companies to report more liabilities that \nare currently \"off balance sheet.\" Carter observes that \nSycamore's debt and assets would increase by $1 million \nunder the new accounting standards, but nothing else \nwould change.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c15_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c15_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Tamiko Sato, Osamu Ishida, and Chiyo Harada, a team of \nanalysts, are assigned to create a forecast model for \nNeutron Cable Corp. (NCC). Sato suggests forecasting \nrevenue by estimating customer growth and price growth \nseparately:\ncustomer growth estimates would be based on expected \nmarket growth and market share, and\nprice growth would be estimated using a time series \nforecast based on the company's historical price growth \nrates.\nThe portfolio manager asks the team to compare revenue \nforecasts using the \"market growth and market share\" and \n\"growth relative to GDP growth\" approaches. The team \ncompiles the following data and assumptions:\nAfter discussion, the team settles on estimating NCC's \nrevenue growth at 3% per year and begins to model \noperating expenses. Ishida makes the following \nassumptions for NCC's COGS:\nMaterials represent 40% of NCC's current year COGS.\nLabor costs represent 60% of NCC's current year COGS. \nMaterial costs will increase 6% in Year 1 and 4% in Year 2.\nLabor costs will decrease as a percentage of revenue by 100 \nbps each year.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nHarada then creates the following assumptions for NCC's \nselling, general, and administrative (SG&A) expenses:\nBased on the forecast, Harada states that NCC is \ndemonstrating economies of scale, as indicated by three \nfactors:\nFactor 1: Increasing sales\nFactor 2: Expanding EBITDA margin \nFactor 3: Expanding free cash flow margin\nAfter finishing the model, the portfolio manager asks how it \nwould be affected by expected inflation or deflation. The \nteam gives the following responses:\nSato: \"Since NCC's services are price inelastic, passing \ninflation costs on to customers through increased prices will \nlikely result in lower revenue.\"\nIshida: \"In general, strong brand names allow companies to \nbetter maintain profitability during periods of inflationary \ncost pressures without a significant reduction in sales \nvolumes.\"\nHarada: \"Vertical integration would likely result in less \nstable margins during periods of input cost volatility.\"\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c16_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c16_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Ellende Enterprises (\"Ellende\") is a publicly traded \nmultinational entity based in the United States that reports \nits financial results under US GAAP. On January 1, 20X1, it \nestablished a wholly owned subsidiary, Sable Leisure Goods \n(\"Sable\"), in the country of Slandsden.\nSable operates primarily as a sales arm for Ellende. Ellende \nestablishes the sales prices for Sable's finished goods, and \nSable is required to purchase most of its inventory from \nEllende. Ellende also funds Sable's payroll and other \noperating expenses and will provide additional capital to \nSable if needed. Ellende uses the temporal method to\ntranslate Sable’s financial results.\nExhibit 1 shows Sable's condensed income statement and \nbalance sheet for 20X1:\nThe accompanying notes to Sable's financial statements \nprovide the following information:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nSable uses FIFO as its inventory cost method.\nInventory was purchased and sold regularly throughout \n20X1.\nThe long-term assets were all acquired on July 1, 20X1. \nThey have no salvage value and are depreciated on a \nstraight-line basis over 15 years.\nThe long-term debt is a 10-year, non-amortizing loan that \ncarries a 4% annual coupon.\nEllende has determined that Sable's results will be \ntranslated into USD using the temporal method. Exhibit 2 \nshows the relevant exchange rates between USD and SLK \nthroughout 20X1:\nGarson Landers, Ellende's chief financial officer, presents \nEllende's consolidated results at a board of directors \nmeeting. In the presentation, Landers makes the following \nremarks about possibly changing from the temporal \nmethod to the current rate method for translating Sable's \nresults:\n\"While we presently use the temporal method, I am \nconcerned that Slandsden will become a hyperinflationary \neconomy soon, perhaps as early as next year. Also, we \nanticipate that Sable will become more independent from \nEllende, including having the ability to establish its own \nsales prices.\"\nAt the conclusion of the meeting, the directors asked \nLanders three questions:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nQuestion 1: \nWhat, if any, is the translation adjustment \nthat Ellende reports on its consolidated financial \nstatements from Sable's results?\nQuestion 2: \nWhat would the impact on Ellende's \nconsolidated results have been if the SLK had been stronger \nagainst the USD on December 31, 20X1, than shown by the \nrate in Exhibit 2?\nQuestion 3: \nHow would Ellende's consolidated results \nhave been affected if Ellende used the SLK as Sable's \nfunctional currency?\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c17_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c17_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 18, "vignette": "On January 1, 20X1, Ace Auto Parts (Ace) paid the market \nprice, EUR 300,000, for a 15% equity interest in Europacar, a \nBritish company that makes high-end accessories for \nvintage sports cars. Ace made this investment to determine \nif Europacar could broaden Ace's customer base and did \nnot anticipate selling it. Both Ace and Europacar report \nfinancial results under IFRS. The fair value of Ace's \ninvestment in Europacar at the end of 20X1 was EUR \n340,000. Exhibit 1 shows Europacar's income and dividends \npaid (in EUR) for 20X1:\nIn mid-20X1, Ace considered making an additional \ninvestment in Europacar by purchasing its convertible debt. \nThis debt could convert at any time into 15% of Europacar's \ncommon stock. Ace's chief financial officer (CFO) made the \nfollowing statements concerning this potential investment \nto a member of Ace's board of directors:\nStatement 1: If Ace makes this investment, we will account \nfor it as an investment in a financial asset and not as an \ninvestment in an associate, so we would not be required to \nuse the equity accounting method.\nStatement 2: IFRS permit debt to be classified either as \nmeasured at amortized cost or at fair value.\nStatement 3 \nOnce we classify the debt, it cannot be \nreclassified.\nUltimately, Ace chose not to make this additional \ninvestment.\nOn January 1, 20X2, Ace paid EUR 700,000 for 30% equity \ninterest in Vintage Wheels, PLC, a privately held company \nthat refurbishes sports cars. Ace received two seats on \nVintage Wheels' board and began to integrate the \ncompany's systems and technology into its operations.\nSelected financial information for Vintage Wheels is shown \nin Exhibit 2:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nIn 20X2, Vintage Wheels had EUR 325,000 in net income \nand paid dividends of EUR 200,000.\nThe PPE and the intangible assets have a useful life of 10 \nyears and Vintage Wheels uses straight-line depreciation for \nall noncurrent assets.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c18_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c18_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c18_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c18_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c18_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c18_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 19, "vignette": "Common Cotton Corporation (CCC) is a Canadian-based \ntextile manufacturer that operates throughout the \nAmericas through foreign subsidiaries.  Noah Wilson and \nAnna Bouchard, two of CCC's board members, are \ndiscussing the growth prospects of the company's South \nAmerican subsidiaries. Wilson presents the following \ninformation:\nCCC's US subsidiary, USsub, sells its goods primarily in the \nUS in USD but mainly purchases supplies from India (75% of \nCOGS) with payments in INR. CCC helps USsub's \nmanagement make strategic decisions and provides CAD- \nbased debt financing for the majority of USsub's borrowing \nneeds.\nWilson and Bouchard begin discussing Mexisub, CCC's \nsubsidiary in Mexico. Mexisub began operations this year \nand uses MXN as its functional currency. Wilson compiles \nthe following information on Mexisub:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nWilson and Bouchard are deciding whether Mexisub's \nfunctional currency should be changed and, in turn, \nwhether the translation method should also be changed. \nWilson compiles exchange rates related to Mexisub:\nThey then discuss the potential for CCC exiting its foreign \nsubsidiary in Brazil, Brazisub. CCC started Brazisub three \nyears ago with an equity investment. Brazisub took out a \nloan from a local bank and purchased inventory, but the \nsubsidiary never began operations. Bouchard compiles the \nfollowing information on Brazisub:\nBouchard suggests exiting the Brazisub business by\nliquidating the inventory and:\nusing its cash on hand to pay its liabilities.\nhaving CCC send enough CAD to cover any remaining \nliabilities.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c19_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c19_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c19_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c19_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c19_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c19_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 20, "vignette": "Baltasar Olmedo, a portfolio manager at an asset \nmanagement firm, is considering stocks of two retail \ncompanies: Jarama Stores and Tiendas Henares. Both \nreport under IFRS and have defined benefit pension plans. \nOlmedo is concerned about the companies' pension \nobligations and asks Piedad Cardona, the firm's analyst \nspecializing in the retail industry, to provide more details.\nCardona notes that she assumes a discount rate of 6% and \nexpects a return on assets of 7% for Jarama's pension plan. \nShe presents additional information about Jarama's pension \nplan, shown in Exhibit 1:\nCardona then explains to Olmedo how to calculate two \nimportant measures of defined benefit pension plans: \nfunded status and periodic pension cost. Cardona adds that \nJarama is planning to introduce a defined contribution \npension plan for new hires. Olmedo asks Cardona about \nthe differences between defined contribution and defined \nbenefit plans, and she lists the following advantages of \ndefined contribution plans:\nAdvantage 1: The accounting treatment for the employer \nis much simpler.\nAdvantage 2: Employees do not bear risks if the invested \nassets underperform.\nAdvantage 3: The company recognizes only a small \namount of future obligations as a liability.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nOlmedo then asks about Tiendas Henares. Cardona \npresents information about changes to the defined benefit \nplan's obligation and assets and explains the calculation of \nthe fund's periodic pension cost based on information from \nExhibit 2:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c20_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c20_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c20_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c20_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 21, "vignette": "Rosa Perez, CFA, is a portfolio manager researching \nChallenger Watches (Challenger), a manufacturer of \nmidrange watches. The industry has been coming under \npressure from the advent of smartwatches. On its latest \nearnings call, Challenger's CEO announced that the \ncompany will enter the smartwatch market. Perez believes \nthis move will cannibalize some sales of its traditional \nwatches.\nPerez receives the following industry forecast from a third- \nparty research firm. She believes that Challenger's \ntraditional watch and smartwatch breakdown matches that \nof the industry:\nIn January 20X7, after her own analysis, Perez creates a pre- \ncannibalization forecast for Challenger (Exhibit 2), assuming \nthat no cannibalization has occurred prior to 20X7.\nHowever, she believes 80,000 of the company's smartwatch \nsales will cannibalize sales of its traditional watches. She \nexpects forecasted variable costs to rise in line with \nrevenue growth, while fixed costs will grow at 75% of the \nvariable cost growth rate.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nPerez wants to know how well Challenger will be able to \nprotect its profits as the industry transitions to \nsmartwatches. She prepares an analysis based on Porter's \nfive forces:\nWhen deciding on a time frame to construct the long-term \ncash flow model for her fund, Perez considers the following \nfactors:\nFactor 1: Challenger is one year into a 3-year restructuring \nproject.\nFactor 2: Based on the growth trajectories of past watch \ntechnologies, smartwatches are expected to grow at a rapid \nrate for 4 years, then normalize.\nFactor 3: The average annual turnover for the stock \nportfolio managed by Perez is 16%.\nPerez uses a discounted cash flow approach to estimate a \nterminal value for the model. She identifies three tasks to \ncomplete to ensure that the model's terminal value is \naccurate:\nTask A: Normalize the terminal cash flow by ensuring it \naccurately represents an average cash flow during the \nmodel period.\nTask B: Ensure that the long-term growth rate used \nmatches Challenger's historical growth rate.\nTask C: Identify the inflection points at which Challenger's \nfuture will diverge significantly from its recent past.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c21_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c21_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c21_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c21_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c21_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c21_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 22, "vignette": "Parquin Industries reports financial results under US GAAP. \nThe company offers a defined benefit (DB) plan to its \nemployees. Relevant information concerning the plan is \nshown in Exhibit 1:\nParquin's board of directors ask the company's CFO to \nassess the impact of a change in the DB plan's assumptions \non the company's financial performance. The CFO made \nthe following statements in response:\nStatement 1: Changing the expected return on the plan's\nassets will not affect the reported pension expense.\nStatement 2: All else equal, decreasing the assumed rate \nof compensation growth will result in decreased net \nincome.\nThe CFO makes additional observations about Parquin's \nfinancial reporting related to its DB plan:\nObservation 1:When we amend the DB plan, past service \ncosts due to the amendment are expensed immediately.\nObservation 2:US GAAP permits us to report actuarial gains \nand losses each period either on the income statement or \non the balance sheet under accumulated other \ncomprehensive income.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c22_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c22_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c22_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c22_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 23, "vignette": "Eckhard Fabian is a senior equity analyst at a sell-side firm \nresearching a recently launched IPO, Shield Networks, \nbefore initiating coverage. Shield is a technology company \nthat has been expanding in multiple segments. Fabian \ncompiles information from Shield's financial statements for \nthe past three years:\nBased on the notes to Shield's financial statements, Fabian \nalso notices that Shield makes its payments to suppliers on \nthe last day before payments are due.\nFabian is most curious about Shield's: \nReturn on equity\nLiquidity\nFinancial instruments\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nManagement skill\nAfter reviewing Shield's main financial statements, Fabian \ndecides to analyze Shield's segment breakout. Fabian \ncompiles information on the company's three product \nsegments:\nAfter review, Fabian concludes that the data centers \nsegment is the most profitable for Shield.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c23_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c23_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c23_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c23_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c23_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c23_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 24, "vignette": "Zoe Stepan is an analyst at All Fork, Inc., an asset \nmanagement company. Stepan meets with a manager, \nSandro Reima, to discuss fund allocations to firms with high \nmarket risk exposures, specifically banks and insurance \ncompanies. Reima makes the following statement:\n\"Banks are more heavily regulated than nonfinancial \ncompanies. Bank examiners ensure that banks abide by \nBasel III's minimum capital adequacy ratios and liquidity \nrequirements.\"\nReima explains the six components of the CAMELS rating \nsystem used to analyze banks: capital adequacy, asset \nquality, management capabilities, earnings, liquidity, and \nsensitivity to market risk.\nReima tells Stepan:\nComment 1: The value-at-risk (VaR) measure is used to \nmonitor the long-term effects of market shocks on a \nportfolio using simulations.\nComment 2: The VaR presented in a bank's financial \nstatements is typically used for intracompany trend \nanalysis, not intercompany comparisons.\nStepan discusses the differences between banks and \ninsurance firms, stating that unlike banks, insurance firms \ndo not have an international standard on minimum risk- \nbased capital requirements. Stepan analyzes the capital \nadequacy of two insurance firms, Firm X and Firm Y, and  \nnotes that Firm X factors in interest rate risk when \ncalculating its risk-based capital, whereas Firm Y does not.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nStepan then analyzes changes in the loss reserve balance \nfor Firm Y and comments that the firm has been \nconservative in its historical claims estimates and will most \nlikely have to make revisions for prior years.\nReima asks Stepan to assess the revenue sources and \nprofitability of Firm Y and shares the data shown in Exhibit \n1:\nStepan analyzes the change in the combined ratio across \nthe industry and states that Firm Y's peers have \nexperienced a similar trend.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c24_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c24_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c24_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c24_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c24_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c24_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 25, "vignette": "SolarWorks offers a defined contribution (DC) pension plan \nto most of its employees while offering a defined benefit \n(DB) pension plan and another post-retirement benefit \n(OPB) plan to the company's management.\nThe board of directors of SolarWorks is considering a stock- \nbased compensation plan as an additional benefit for the \ncompany's executives. The plan could include employee \nstock options or outright grants of SolarWorks stock. The \nboard asks Paul Franco, SolarWorks CFO, how a stock-based \ncompensation plan using either approach would affect the \ncompany's financial results. Franco replies:\nStatement 1: Adding a stock option plan will have no\nimpact on SolarWorks's ROE.\nStatement 2: All else equal, a higher assumed risk-free \nrate will increase stock option expense and therefore \ndecrease net income.\nStatement 3: Assuming equal market value, outright stock \ngrants will result in greater net income than restricted \nstock.\nSolarWorks extends an employment offer to a new \ncontroller, Marley Beezer. Her benefits include\nparticipation in the DB plan. The DB plan currently provides \nfor a lump-sum retirement payout that is the product of:\n2.0% of the participant's final annual salary, and\nthe number of years the participant was employed by \nSolarWorks.\nExhibit 1 shows the assumptions relevant to calculating \nBeezer's retirement payment:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nSolarWorks is considering an amendment to the plan that \nwould provide annual payments for the greater of 20 years \nor the employee's remaining lifetime.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c25_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c25_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c25_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c25_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 26, "vignette": "Uncorked Distribution is an Australian wine distributor that \npurchases and sells wine to several countries. Uncorked \nalso has several European subsidiaries, including Verre \nDistribution, a recently established foreign subsidiary based \nin France. Shane Wood, the director of supply chain at \nUncorked, recently made the following transactions on \ncredit:\nPurchased NZD 20,000 of wine from a New Zealand winery \non Dec 1, 20X4\nSold NZD 10,000 of wine to a New Zealand grocery store \nchain on Dec 15, 20X4\nBoth transactions were paid in NZD on Jan 15, 20X5, and \nwere the only purchases and sales in NZD for Dec 20X4. \nWood observed the following exchange rates:\nGrant Lewis, Uncorked's head of accounting, notes that \nunder both GAAP and IFRS, Uncorked must report its \nforeign currency transaction gains and losses on the income \nstatement, but it can choose how to report them.\nKatherine Bennett, CFO of Uncorked, is reviewing the \ncompany's financial statements with Lewis. Bennett notes \nthat Uncorked's effective income tax rate has been below \nthe statutory tax rate for the last few years and wants to \nknow how the profits from foreign subsidiaries are \ncontributing. Lewis presents the following data:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nBennett also asks Lewis to review the financials for Verre.  \nVerre was established on Jan 1, 20X4 and purchased all of \nits PPE on that date.  Verre locally reports its financial \nstatements in EUR; its most recent balance sheet is shown \nin Exhibit 3.\nVerre currently uses AUD as its functional currency since it \nmakes most of its purchases from Uncorked. Lewis believes \nthat EUR would be a more appropriate functional currency \nsince most of Verre's customers are based in Europe and \npay in EUR. Lewis also compiles the following information:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c26_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c26_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c26_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c26_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c26_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c26_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 27, "vignette": "Ethan Reed is a portfolio manager at an asset management \ncompany. He directs Kayla Ortiz, a senior analyst covering \nthe chemical industry, to analyze three companies reporting \nunder US GAAP: Legacy Chem, Bayside Industries, and Atria \nPlastics.\nOrtiz builds a regression model to assess Legacy's earnings \npersistence. In this model, earnings are split into two \ncomponents (cash flow and accruals), which are used to \npredict the next period's earnings. After validating the \nmodel and reviewing Legacy's financial statements, Ortiz \nreaches the following conclusions:\nConclusion 1: The cash component is larger than the \naccruals component.\nConclusion 2: A significant portion of the accruals\ncomponent consists of discretionary accruals.\nReed asks Ortiz if she found any indication of manipulation \nin Legacy's cash flow. Ortiz replies:\nStatement 1: In the last few years, Legacy's accounts \nreceivable increased as a percentage of sales, while \naccounts payable decreased substantially.\nStatement 2: In the previous year, Legacy acquired patents \nand capitalized their costs, which will be amortized over ten \nyears.\nReed then asks about Bayside Industries, and Ortiz presents \nthe selected financial information shown in Exhibit 1:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nOrtiz explains that she will compare Bayside's operating \ncash flow with other profit and loss (P&L) items to \ninvestigate potential reporting problems, and then she will \napply the Beneish model. Reed requests more details \nabout this model, and Ortiz tells him that:\nThe model is a quantitative tool that estimates the \nprobability of earnings manipulation.\nFinancial statement items (eg, sales) are the variables used \nto calculate an M-score.\nThe higher the value of the M-score, the higher the \nprobability of misreporting.\nReed tells Ortiz that Bayside is acquiring Atria Plastics, a \nsmaller competitor in the chemical industry. The \ncombination will be accounted for using the acquisition \nmethod and will be reported on a consolidated basis. Reed \nis concerned that Bayside's management could take \nadvantage of the acquisition to overstate earnings and asks \nOrtiz to investigate the impact of the transaction when \nBayside releases its financial reports after consolidating \nwith Atria.\nReed also requests Atria's financial information, and Ortiz \npresents Exhibit 2:\nReed wants to forecast Atria's earnings in the next years but \nnotices the pretax earnings in 20X8 appear to be influenced \nby nonrecurring items. He asks Ortiz to calculate the core \npretax earnings in 20X8, excluding all one-time events.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c27_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c27_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c27_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c27_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c27_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c27_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fsa", "source_label": "UWorld – Financial Statement Analysis", "topic": "FSA", "case_no": 28, "vignette": "Bob Smith is an equity analyst who covers the banking \nsector at Excel Partners, a sell-side research firm. Smith is \nconsidering adding Zervos Bank to his coverage universe \nand is in the process of making a preliminary assessment.\nSmith first looks at Zervos' capital adequacy, which is \ndetailed in Exhibits 1 and 2:\nWhile examining the bank's balance sheet, Smith notices an \nasset line item for nontrading reverse repurchase \nagreements (ie, reverse repos). Smith is also aware that the \nbank is a very active participant in the 30-day reverse repo \nmarket.\nSmith then reviews selected data from Zervos Bank's \ncorporate loan portfolio and asks fellow analyst Amita Suri \nfor her observations.\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet\n\nObservation 1:The bank increased its general reserve \nagainst bad debts in 20X5.\nObservation 2:Overall, credit quality of the loan portfolio \nimproved in 20X5.\nObservation 3:Fewer loan losses are expected in the future.\nSuri continues by stating that a more complete credit \nanalysis should also include an evaluation of the allowance \nfor loan losses, the provision for loan losses, and net \ncharge-offs.\nSuri also suggests that Smith look at Zervos' off-balance- \nsheet exposure to better understand the bank's market risk. \nSmith reviews Zervos' credit and interest rate derivative \nexposure and notes that freestanding derivatives increased \nsubstantially year over year in 20X5, while hedged \nderivative exposure decreased.\nFinally, Suri says a market risk analysis would not be \ncomplete without evaluating Zervos' value at risk (VaR), and \nshe provides Smith with the following information:\nFinancial Statement Analysis – Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c28_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c28_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c28_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c28_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c28_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fsa_c28_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Chee Tan is a newly hired fixed income analyst at a large \ninvestment management firm.  Mei Chong is a senior \ngovernment bond analyst at the firm and has been assigned \nto mentor Tan for the first few weeks of employment.\nChong explains that Tan will initially work on the firm's \nshort- to intermediate-term government bond fund.  She \nasks him to describe his familiarity with YTM calculations \nand short-term interest rate measures.\nTan responds by saying the YTM calculation for a coupon-\npaying bond is based on the following:\nAssumption 1: The YTM is a realistic estimate of return only \nif the yield curve is flat.\nAssumption 2: Future interest rate volatility is irrelevant \nonce the YTM is determined.\nTan then elaborates on short-term yield measures by \nstating:\nStatement 1: The overnight indexed swap (OIS) rate is a \ntype of interest rate swap in which the swap's periodic \nfloating rate equals the geometric average of the overnight \nindex rate.\nStatement 2: The secured overnight funding rate (SOFR) is \nan indicator of the overnight US Treasury repurchase (repo) \nrate.\nStatement 3: SOFR is a survey-based rate calculated by \naveraging the information submitted by leading global \nbanks.\nChong then provides Tan with Exhibit 1:\n\nChong then asks Tan to construct a maturity spread carry \ntrade based on Exhibit 1 and to provide an I-spread \nestimate for the following bond:\nTan asks Chong why the swap rate curve would be used to \nvalue bonds instead of a government bond curve.  In \nresponse, Chong makes the following observations:\nObservation 1: Swap rates are like the YTM on a \ngovernment bond.\nObservation 2:The swap curve is derived using short-term \nlending rates; thus, its usefulness as a benchmark is limited \nto short-term securities.\nObservation 3:Some institutions prefer the swap curve as a \nbenchmark since they regularly hedge their balance sheet \nwith swaps.\nChong then quizzes Tan on his knowledge of spot and \nforward rates.  Chong asks Tan to evaluate the relative \nattractiveness of purchasing a three-year zero-coupon \ngovernment bond based on the following forecasted spot \nrates:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Tomas Alum and Hernan Sovo are fixed-income analysts at \nan asset management company.  They are evaluating \nseveral bonds currently held in one of the firm's portfolios.  \nInformation about the bonds is shown in Exhibit 1:\nAlum and Sovo discuss the credit valuation adjustment they \nmust factor in to estimate the corporate bonds' fair values.  \nAlum uses the government par bond yield curve to develop \na binomial tree to determine the value of Bond II assuming \nno default, as shown in Exhibit 2:\nThe two analysts discuss the potential impact of credit \nrating changes on bond values and expected returns and \nthe use of credit models for estimating the default risk of \nbonds issued by publicly traded companies.  Sovo makes\n\nthe following statements regarding the advantages of \nstructural models compared with reduced-form models.\nStructural models:\nStatement 1: Are easier to implement since they rely \nprimarily on publicly available information, such as \ncoverage ratios and debt-to-equity ratios\nStatement 2: Attempt to directly value the option-like \naspects of a straight bond since a company can elect to not \nrepay debt, effectively selling the company to the \nbondholders for the value of the debt.\nStatement 3: Treat default as an exogenous random \nvariable and use statistical methods to estimate when \ndefault is likely to occur.\nWhile discussing the models, the analysts discuss the \nfactors reflected in corporate bond yield spreads.\nAlum and Sovo are also evaluating several structured debt \nsecurities in the portfolio.  They discuss whether the same \napproaches can be used for evaluating the different types \nof asset-backed securities (ABS) held in the portfolio.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Jenda Harrar manages fixed-income portfolios for an \ninvestment company.  Harrar directs Dag Kennon, an \nanalyst at the firm, to construct a binomial interest rate tree \nto evaluate bond values.  Harrar and Kennon discuss how to \nproject interest rates over time and Kennon makes the \nfollowing statements about the benefits of using a \nlognormal random walk for modeling interest rate changes.\nStatement 1: Interest rates on a binomial tree are \napproximately lognormally distributed around the forward \nrates derived from the benchmark yield curve.\nStatement 2: A lognormal random walk allows for the \npossibility of negative interest rates as are sometimes \nobserved in market rates.\nStatement 3: A lognormal model of interest rates results in \nperiod-to-period rate changes that are inversely \nproportional to the level of interest rates.\nExhibit 1 contains a partially completed binomial interest \nrate tree.  Kennon constructed the tree from spot rates \nderived from the 1-year, 2-year, and 3-year option-free \ngovernment par bonds, using a lognormal interest rate \nmodel, and an assumed interest rate volatility of 12%.\nExhibit 2 contains the binomial valuation for a 3-year 3.5% \ncoupon bond based on the information in Exhibit 1.\n\nHarrar is concerned about rising inflation and uncertainty \nabout central bank monetary policy, which she believes will \ncause interest rate volatility to increase.  Harrar directs \nKennon to create a second binomial tree based on 20% \nvolatility, shown in Exhibit 3.  She then asks Kennan to use \nthis tree (Exhibit 3) to value a 3-year, 3.0% coupon option-\nfree government bond.\nHarrar and Kennon discuss using a Monte Carlo simulation \nas an alternative method for valuing bonds.  Kennon \nidentifies several advantages of using a Monte Carlo \nsimulation rather than a binomial interest rate tree.\n\nStatement 4: Monte Carlo simulations allow for more \ncomplex interest rate behavior, such as mean reversion, \nthat better represents observed interest rate movements.\nStatement 5: Monte Carlo simulations allow analysts to \nassign different probability weightings to specific interest \nrate paths to better align with their expectations regarding \nfuture interest rates.\nAssume all bonds pay coupons annually and that all interest \nrates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Darien Trading Ltd. is a fixed-income asset management \nfirm that runs several domestic bond funds, two of which \nare an investment-grade corporate bond fund and a high-\nyield corporate bond fund.\nJay Zendo, CFA, manages the investment-grade bond \nportfolio, which is an actively managed fund.  Zendo \nfrequently uses single-name and index credit default swaps \n(CDS) both to manage the credit risk of portfolio securities \nand to make speculative bets on company or sector credit \npricing.  Zendo holds two Marsh Manufacturing, Inc. bonds:\nBond 1:  $1 million par value of 3-year subordinated bonds\nBond 2:  $1 million par value of 4-year senior unsecured \nbonds\nZendo buys credit protection on the Marsh bonds.  Exhibit 1 \ncontains information on the purchased CDS.\nSeveral months after the CDS purchase, Marsh declares \nbankruptcy.  Exhibit 2 shows the post-bankruptcy bond \nprices and the CDS recovery rate.\n\nZendo sometimes uses basis trades to profit from the \ndifference in pricing of credit risk by the bond and CDS \nmarkets.  He evaluates Quant Corp. bonds and CDS to \ndetermine the relative pricing of credit in the two markets.  \nExhibit 3 contains the pertinent information for Quant \nbonds and CDS.\nLeti Xena, CFA, manages the domestic high-yield fund.  She \nbelieves general credit conditions are likely to worsen and \nbuys $50 million notional of the on-the-run high-yield index \nCDS (CDX) to hedge credit risk in the fund.  The CDX \nincludes 100 reference entities.  The CDX has an upfront \npremium of 2%.\nXena anticipates improving creditworthiness for several \nissuers included in the index that are not held in the fund.  \nShe sells credit protection on two of the companies, Casha, \nInc. and Donbo Corp.  Exhibit 4 contains information related \nto the CDS sold by Xena.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Ethan Lavigne is a fixed income analyst at Seine Financial, \nan investment management firm.  Lavigne assists fund \nmanager Sofia Pascal with managing a bond portfolio.  \nPascal asks Lavigne to assume the primary responsibility for \nmanaging several credit default swap (CDS) positions, but \nfirst she asks Lavigne what he knows about CDS, starting \nwith their risk profile.\nSeine is the credit protection buyer for a CDS of Montage \nIndustries.  Montage recently experienced a credit event \nthat triggered a settlement on the company's CDS.  \nMontage has three bonds outstanding; the reference bond \nfor the CDS is Bond 1.  Pascal collects information on each \nof the three bonds, shown in Exhibit 1, and asks Lavigne \nwhich bond is the cheapest to deliver:\nLavigne wishes to practice estimating probabilities of \nsurvival and default and asks to see the hazard rates of a \ncompany's senior debt that are used in pricing its CDS.  \nPascal provides the hazard rates for Riposte Technologies \nover the next five years, shown in Exhibit 2:\nLavigne and Pascal then discuss index CDS.  Seine is \ncurrently the credit protection buyer in two index CDS \ninvestments:\n\nThe North American CDX with €100 million notional \nprincipal.  Lavigne has tracked default probabilities between \nthe index's constituent companies and notes that the \ncorrelation in the likelihood of defaults among the \ncompanies has increased over the past year.\nThe iTraxx Main CDS with €250 million notional principal.  \nThe iTraxx Main has 125 constituents, one of which is \nCompany A.  Company A accounts for 1% of the combined \nmarket cap of the constituents within the index.  Lavigne \nwishes to unwind the credit protection in Company A only.  \nPascal asks Lavigne to sell a single-name CDS in Company A \nwith a €1.2 million notional principal.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Jonathon Paster is an analyst at a fixed-income trading firm.  \nPaster has been assigned to evaluate a developed \neconomy's sovereign debt issues.  Exhibit 1 contains \ninformation on five sovereign bonds; all the bonds mature \nin exactly three years, on Jun 15 20X9.  Three of the bonds \nhave a fixed coupon rate and two of the bonds are floating \nrate notes (FRNs, or floaters).\nRecent interest rate volatility has been minimal, and the \nsovereign yield curve is currently flat at 2.00%.  Siena \nMastro, a portfolio manager at the firm, asks Paster to \nevaluate the five bonds (Exhibit 1) under several different \nscenarios:\nScenario 1:\nAssume interest rates remain unchanged and \ninterest rate volatility is 0%.\nScenario 2:\nAssume an upward parallel yield curve shift \nof 0.50%, resulting in a 2.50% flat sovereign yield curve, and \ninterest rate volatility is 0%.\nScenario 3:\nAssume the yield curve steepens, as shown \nin Exhibit 2, and interest rate volatility is 0%.\nScenario 4:\nAssume the yield curve has already \nsteepened, as shown in Exhibit 2, and then interest rate \nvolatility increases to 30%.\n\nMastro asks Paster to describe the effective convexities of \nthe callable and putable bonds.  In response, Paster makes \nthe following statements:\nStatement 1: Both callable and putable bonds exhibit \npositive convexity when the embedded options are out of \nthe money since, in that circumstance, the bonds will \nbehave similarly to otherwise equivalent straight bonds.\nStatement 2: Both callable and putable bonds exhibit \nnegative convexity when the embedded options are in the \nmoney since, in that circumstance, the value of the option \nwill offset the effect of interest rates on the straight bond \ncomponent of the securities.\nMastro directs Paster to construct a binomial interest rate \ntree based on the yields in Exhibit 2.  The tree constructed \nby Paster is shown in Exhibit 3:\nAssume all bonds pay coupons once per year and all \ninterest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Jethro Timkin is a portfolio manager at a fixed-income asset \nmanagement firm.  Timkin wants to evaluate the \ninvestment characteristics of three new bonds being issued \nby Moontite Corporation on 31 January 20X4.  Each bond \nhas a maturity date of 31 January 20X7.  Details of the \nbonds are shown in Exhibit 1.\nTo better understand the risk and relative value of the \ncallable bond, Timkin seeks to determine Bond 2's effective \nduration.  The related information is shown in Exhibit 2.\nTimkin also performs an option-adjusted spread analysis of \nBond 2 versus a similar bond from Beta Alpha Corporation \n(Bond 4).  Selected information regarding the bonds is \nshown in Exhibit 3.\nTimkin wants to evaluate the relative value of a previously \nissued callable Beta Alpha Corporation bond currently \ntrading at 105% of par.  The bond pays a 6% coupon rate \nand has three years until maturity.  The bond is callable at \n103% of par on either of two dates, exactly one year or two\n\nyears prior to maturity, respectively.  The binomial interest \nrate tree Timkin developed to value the bond is shown in \nExhibit 4.\nAssume all the bonds pay coupons annually and that all \ninterest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Sophie Ortega is a fixed-income portfolio manager for a \nhedge fund, where Manuelo Ruiz also works as an analyst.  \nOrtega asks Ruiz to evaluate the potential for earning \narbitrage profits by stripping or reconstituting off-the-run \ngovernment coupon bonds.  Exhibit 1 contains selected \ndata for three off-the-run government securities:\nExhibit 2 contains information on zero-coupon government \nbonds:\nOrtega and Ruiz discuss creating a binomial interest rate \ntree to evaluate the bonds.  During the discussion, Ruiz \nmakes the following statements regarding the construction \nof a binomial tree using a lognormal interest rate model.\nStatement 1: The time steps on a binomial tree must \nmatch the number and frequency of cash flows to the \nmaturity of the bond being valued.\nStatement 2: The average of the 1-period forward rates on \nthe tree at a given time step is approximately equal to the \n1-period forward rate derived from the spot rate curve.\nStatement 3: The adjacent 1-period forward rates at a \ngiven time step beyond Time 0 differ by one standard \ndeviation, based on the volatility used in constructing the \ntree.\n\nAn increase in the central bank's policy rate results in a \nbearish flattening of the government spot rate curve.  \nOrtega asks Ruiz to develop a binomial interest rate tree for \nvaluing government bonds in the new interest rate \nenvironment.  Exhibit 3 contains the binomial rate tree \ncreated by Ruiz.  The tree is accurately calibrated to the \ncurrent government par bond curve based on 12% \nvolatility:\nOrtega has Ruiz perform a binomial valuation of Bond C \n(from Exhibit 1) using the 12% volatility tree.  The binomial \ntree value is slightly lower than the current market price of \nthe bond.  Ortega asks Ruiz to estimate Bond C's value using \nthe current government spot rate curve.\nAssume all bonds pay coupons once per annum and all \ninterest rates are expressed as annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Pella Aswami and Gurt Pachen manage fixed-income \nportfolios for an international bank.  They are assessing the \nvalues of several securities held in a multicurrency \ncorporate bond portfolio.  Exhibit 1 contains information \nabout Bonds I, II, III, and IV.\nAswami asks Pachen to perform in-depth analyses of the \nbonds.  Pachen discovers that the eurozone benchmark \nyield curve is flat at a risk-free rate of 2.50% and gathers \ninformation on par bond rates (contained in Exhibit 2) to \nconstruct a GBP benchmark yield curve.\nExhibit 3 contains information generated by Pachen to \nestimate Bond III's fair value.\n\nAfter reviewing a similar analysis of Bond IV, Pachen\nconcludes that the appropriate credit valuation adjustment \nfor that bond is 3.2289.\nAswami asks Pachen to gather information about the yield \nspreads of the bonds being evaluated by calculating the \nyield spreads using the swap curve rather than the \ngovernment bond yield curve.  Pachen objects, identifying \ntwo reasons why government bonds are preferable to swap \nrates for calculating yield spreads.\nReason 1:\nThe liquidity of government bonds is more \nuniform across maturities, compared with various maturity \nswaps.\nReason 2:\nFor any given corporate bond, the likelihood \nis greater of finding a government bond with a matching \nmaturity.\nAfter assessing Pachen's research on Bond III, Aswami\ncomments that the yield curve constructed from the \ncompany's senior unsecured bonds is less steeply sloped \nthan the GBP benchmark government bond curve.\nAssume that all bonds pay coupons once annually and that \nall interest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Kentaro Okada is a fixed income trader at Sensei \nInvestments, an asset management firm.  Okada is \nanalyzing Sensei's credit default swap (CDS) positions, \nstarting with Crane Pharmaceuticals.  Sensei owns ¥10 \nbillion of Crane's 10-year, senior unsecured bonds and is \nalso a credit protection buyer of ¥10 billion in notional \nvalue of CDS on Crane.  Crane just announced that due to \nfinancial difficulties, future interest and principal payments \non its 10-year bonds will be reduced by 50%.  The company \nhas not made any other announcements or changes.  Based \non this credit event, an auction has determined that the \nultimate recovery rate for the bond will be 65%.\nOkada then collects information on a CDS for which Sensei \nis a credit protection seller.  Okada updates the gain or loss \nfor Sensei if it unwinds the position, based on the \ninformation in Exhibit 1:\nOkada is discussing company risk expectations with Chiyo \nHirano, an analyst at Sensei.  Hirano provides Okada with \nher expectations for several companies that are all peers in \nthe life insurance industry, presented in Exhibit 2.  Okada \nuses these expectations to create long-short trades.\nHirano mentions that she has noticed some mispricing \nbetween the bond market and CDS market, allowing for \nbasis trades.  She states that these mispricings do not last \nlong and suggests taking advantage of a likely convergence.  \nHirano provides Okada data on Tokyo Industries, one \ncompany with a mispricing:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c10_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c10_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Macon Quark manages a proprietary trading desk at a \nmultinational financial institution.  Quark directs Jemma \nMuon, CFA, an analyst at the firm, to evaluate the US \nmarket for profitable trading opportunities.  Muon collects \nthe US par bond rates shown in Exhibit 1:\nDuring a discussion of bond valuation in the current market \nenvironment, Muon describes the following approaches to \nthe arbitrage-free valuation of a coupon bond:\nApproach 1:\nValue the bond as a portfolio of zero-coupon \nbonds that replicate the bond's cash flows.\nApproach 2:\nValue the bond as the sum of the individual \ncash flows' present values, discounting each cash flow by \nthe spot rate with a matching term.\nApproach 3:\nValue the bond as the sum of the individual \ncash flows' present values, discounting each cash flow by \nthe YTM of a benchmark bond with the same time to \nmaturity as the bond.\nAfter the discussion, Muon is directed to evaluate several 3-\nyear bonds, shown in Exhibit 2:\n\nQuark had begun developing binomial interest rate trees for \nboth the Swiss and eurozone fixed-income markets, based \non equal probabilities of rates being higher or lower in \nfuture periods.  Quark instructs Muon to complete these \ninterest rate trees (Exhibits 3 and 4):\nOnce the trees are completed, Quark uses the eurozone \nbinomial tree to calculate the arbitrage-free value of a 3-\nyear, 6% coupon, option-free bond.  Afterward, Quark and \nMuon discuss the implications of using different volatility \nassumptions and the pathwise valuation of bonds using the \nbinomial interest rate trees.\nAssume that all bonds pay coupons annually and that all \ninterest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Dante Cardoso runs a bond portfolio for an asset \nmanagement company.  Melissa Yang is a fixed-income \nanalyst on his team.  Cardoso asks Yang to analyze recent \ntrends in the spread between the 3-month market \nreference rate (MRR) and the 3-month government T-bill \nrate.  After reviewing the data, Yang reports back that, \nfollowing a prolonged period of stability, the spread has \nrecently widened to twice its historical average.\nCardoso then asks Yang to analyze the value and expected \nreturns of two corporate bonds held by the portfolio.  \nExhibit 1 contains selected information on the bonds, which \nhave no embedded options:\nExhibit 2 contains selected rates and discount factors from \nthe benchmark spot curve that Yang is using to analyze the \nbonds:\nCardoso believes interest rates are likely to increase over \nthe next several years.  He directs Yang to analyze the \nreturn on Bond B if it is held to maturity based on this \nexpectation.\nAssume the coupon bonds and swaps in this scenario are \nannual-pay instruments and that all interest rates are \nannual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Ling Wang, a portfolio manager at Universal Bond \nManagement, is training Ishi Devi, a recently hired junior \nanalyst.  Wang begins by testing Devi's knowledge of \narbitrage-free valuation concepts for fixed income \nsecurities by making the following statements:\nIgnoring transaction costs, the arbitrage-free valuation \napproach assumes:\nStatement 1: Arbitrage profits are possible when value \nadditivity does not hold\nStatement 2: Market participants can earn an arbitrage \nprofit through stripping and reconstitution\nStatement 3: A financial asset with a risk-free payoff in the \nfuture must have a positive price today\nWang then provides Devi with the par and spot rates, \nshown in Exhibit 1, that are used to build the interest rate \ntrees in Exhibits 2 and 3.  Wang states that both binomial \ntrees have been calibrated and to assume that all bonds pay \ncoupons annually and all interest rates are annual \ncompound rates.\n\nDevi asks about the types of term structure models.  Wang \nreplies:\nReply 1:\nArbitrage-free models are parameterized to \nmatch current market prices\nReply 2:\nEquilibrium models use fundamental \neconomic variables to forecast many possible interest rate \npaths.  This often results in a term structure that is \ninconsistent with current market data\nReply 3:\nEquilibrium models such as the Cox-\nIngersoll-Ross (CIR) and Vasicek models are multifactor \nmodels that incorporate short-term, intermediate, and \nlong-term rates", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Jesse Mahoud is a fixed-income analyst for an international \nbank.  Mahoud is tasked with analyzing the relative values \nof five new issue bonds from Peachco Corporation.  All the \nbonds are to be issued on 15 May 20X6 and mature on 15 \nMay 20X9.  All bonds make annual coupon payments on 15 \nMay.  Exhibit 1 contains additional information on the \nbonds:\nMahoud derives a binomial interest rate tree to evaluate \nthe Peachco bonds, shown in Exhibit 2:\nMahoud continues to follow the Peachco convertible bonds \nafter issuance, periodically reassessing their value.  Exhibit \n3 contains selected data regarding Bond 5 at issuance and \nat several later dates when Mahoud is reevaluating the \nsecurities:\n\nAssume all interest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Pablo Serrano is an analyst supporting Jana Lanka, a bond \nportfolio manager at a global investment company.  Lanka \nasks Serrano to assess the value of a sinking fund bond \n(sinker) held in the portfolio.  The sinker has call, \nacceleration, and delivery option provisions.  Lanka wants a \nsummary of the effect the embedded options have on the \nbond's value.  Serrano makes the following statements:\nStatement 1: The issuer is long both the call and \nacceleration options since they give the issuer a right to \nretire bonds more rapidly than reflected in the sinking fund \nschedule.\nStatement 2: Investors are long the delivery option since \nthis feature tends to result in sinking fund bonds trading at \nhigher values than similar bonds without a sinking fund \nprovision.\nAnother holding, Quad Incorporated's convertible bonds, \nwere purchased at issuance and are still held in the \nportfolio managed by Lanka.  Selected data on the bonds is \nshown in Exhibit 1:\nLanka directs Serrano to assess the value of another \nportfolio holding, Terapolis Corporation's convertible \nbonds.  Terapolis is a money-losing smartphone\n\nmanufacturer.  However, investors believe that Terapolis's\nportfolio of technology patents makes it a potential \ntakeover target.  During the morning of Aug 15 20X3, \nSerrano gathers information to evaluate the bond, which is \ncontained in Exhibit 2:\nLater that day, the stock price increases to £45 per share \nbased on rumors of a takeover bid.  At the same time, \nTerapolis's straight debt yields increase to 8.50% due to \nrising risk-free rates and widening credit spreads.\nAssume all bonds pay coupons once per year and all \ninterest rates are annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c15_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c15_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Silvie Fischer, a fixed-income analyst at an international \nbank, has been assigned to work with a bond fund \nmanager, Ramona Escalada, in evaluating floating-rate \nnotes (FRNs).  Details concerning several FRNs being \nconsidered for investment are shown in Exhibit 1.  Each \npays a coupon once per year, with rates for the next period \nreset on the most recent coupon date.\nEscalada asks Fischer to construct a binomial interest rate \ntree assuming 12% volatility using the USD benchmark par \nbonds shown in Exhibit 2.\nThe binomial tree constructed by Fischer is shown in Exhibit \n3:\nEscalada also asks Fischer to construct a EUR benchmark \nrate tree and then calculate the value of Bond B, assuming \nno default.  Exhibit 4 contains a completed tree.\n\nBelieving that Bond C's credit quality is likely to decline, \nFischer evaluates Bond C assuming the probability of \ndefault increases over time, as follows:\nYear 1: 1.00%\nYear 2: 1.50%\nYear 3: 2.00%\nEscalada asks Fischer to perform additional analyses on \nBonds A and D.  Fischer produces estimates of various risk \nparameters for Bond A, which are shown in Exhibit 5.\n\nFischer constructs a binomial tree to estimate Bond D's \nvalue assuming no default, shown in Exhibit 6:\nFischer also produces estimates of various risk parameters \nfor Bond D, which are shown in Exhibit 7:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c16_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c16_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Mary Stanzik, a portfolio manager, and Rolf Rohm, an \nanalyst supporting her, are doing credit analyses of several \nbonds in the portfolio.  Information about Bonds A, B, C, \nand D is shown in Exhibit 1.\nStanzik and Rohm discuss the effects of credit rating \ndowngrades and defaults on the bonds' values.  Stanzik\nbelieves the credit spreads in Exhibit 2 accurately reflect \nthe pricing of relative credit risk.  She asks Rohm to \nestimate the expected return over the next year on Bond B, \nwhich has a modified duration of 2.77 and a YTM of 3.66%.\nStanzik also asks Rohm to evaluate the value of Bond C \nusing the credit spreads and the government par bond \ncurve based on the securities shown in Exhibit 3.\nAssume all corporate and government bonds pay coupons \nonce annually and that all their interest rates are annual \ncompound rates.\n\nStanzik has Rohm evaluate a covered bond held in the \nportfolio.  Rohm makes two statements regarding the \ndifference between covered bonds and asset-backed \nsecurities (ABS):\nStatement 1: Both covered bonds and ABS are backed by a \ncollateral pool that consists of a portfolio of specified \nassets, such as residential mortgages and government \nobligations.\nStatement 2: Unlike ABS, which give investors recourse \nonly to the securitized assets held by a special purpose \nentity, covered bonds give investors recourse to the issuing \nfinancial institution as well as the underlying asset pool", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c17_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c17_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 18, "vignette": "Marina Bearing, a senior fixed-income analyst at a wealth \nmanagement firm, is responsible for evaluating corporate \nbonds in a new client's account.  Information about the \nbonds is shown in Exhibit 1:\nBearing is concerned about the effect of default on the \nclient's bond holdings and directs Silviano Pastor, the junior \nanalyst assisting her, to estimate the exposure related to \nseveral of the bonds.  Although none of the bonds seems \nlikely to default soon, Bearing is concerned about the \nimpact of rating downgrades and whether the bonds' credit \nrisks are priced accurately.  Pastor collects information to \nconduct the credit analysis.  Exhibit 2 contains a partial \ncredit rating transition matrix:\nExhibit 3 provides information on government par bonds \nfor constructing a benchmark yield curve:\n\nBearing and Pastor discuss various approaches to \ncalculating yield spreads for Bond III.  Pastor notes that \nthere are no on-the-run government bonds with the same \nmaturity as Bond III.  They discuss the trade-offs between \ntwo options: using an off-the-run government bond with a \n4-year maturity, which results in a smaller spread, or \ninterpolating a 4-year rate from on-the-run 3-year and 5-\nyear government bonds.\nBearing decides to evaluate several structured debt \nsecurities that may be added to client portfolios.  Bearing \nand Pastor discuss how to assess the credit enhancement \nused in different types of asset-backed securities (ABS).  \nBearing directs Pastor to identify automotive and credit \ncard ABS that have larger-than-average excess spreads.\nAssume all corporate and government bonds pay coupons \nonce annually and that all the bonds' interest rates are \nannual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c18_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c18_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c18_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c18_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c18_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c18_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 19, "vignette": "Darla Esquallo is a fixed-income analyst for an asset \nmanagement company that specializes in managing \ngovernment employee pension funds.  Carlos Fiero is a \nretirement plan administrator for one of the company's \nclients.  Fiero contacts Esquallo regarding the valuation of \nsecurities held in one of the plan's portfolios.  Exhibit 1 \ncontains selected data on government par bond yields and \nspot rates:\nFiero has only recently been given responsibility for \nsupporting the plan's fixed-income investments, after \nhaving spent most of his career supporting equity \nportfolios.  He asks Esquallo several questions regarding the \nrelationship between spot rates, forward rates, and YTM.  \nEsquallo describes spot rates and forward rates and \nexplains how spot rates can be bootstrapped from par bond \nyields.  To explain YTM to Fiero, Esquallo makes the \nfollowing statements:\nStatement 1: YTM is the realized rate of return on bonds \nheld to maturity, so long as the issuer does not default.\nStatement 2: YTM is the weighted average of spot rates \nover the time until the bond's maturity.\nStatement 3: YTM is the single periodic discount rate that \nresults in the PV of the bond's coupon and principal being \nequal to the dirty price of the bond.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c19_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c19_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c19_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c19_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 20, "vignette": "Eleanor Green is a portfolio manager at Guadalupe Credit \nGroup and oversees the actively managed Seguin Bond \nFund.  Nena Chowdhury is an analyst who assists Green in \nmanaging the fund.  The two are discussing the domestic \neconomic outlook and the potential implications for the \nportfolio.\nThe economy is currently in a mild but prolonged recession.  \nAfter initially lowering interest rates to very low levels at \nthe onset of the recession, the country's central bank \ninstituted a quantitative easing (QE) policy of selling short-\nterm bills to purchase long-term bonds (20–30 years) in an \nattempt to further stimulate the economy.  As a result of \nthis policy, the very long end of the yield curve is now \ninverted since the 30-year maturity is now yielding less than \nthe 15-year maturity.  In response to this development, \nGreen has sold the fund's positions in 20- to 30-year bonds \nand has repositioned the proceeds into the 15-year \nmaturity segment.\nGreen states that based on the current macro environment, \nshe believes inflation and GDP growth are poised to \naccelerate, which will increase the bond risk premium for \nlong-term bonds and negatively impact the portfolio since it \nis currently comprised.  Chowdhury disagrees, stating that \ninflation and GDP growth impact short-term rates more \nthan long-term rates.\nThe portfolio is currently positioned in investment-grade \nsecurities as follows:\n\nThe two then continue to discuss this situation:\nGreen: \"My base forecast is for a rapid economic recovery \nover the next year, and I anticipate an end to the central \nbank's QE policy in the coming months.\"\nChowdhury: \"I question these assumptions.  It is more likely \nthat the recession will continue and the QE policy will be \nmaintained.\"\nGreen: \"I am concerned that the portfolio is not \nappropriately positioned for a continuation of the recession \nand the associated QE policy.\"\nGreen then asks Chowdhury to construct a multi-factor \nyield curve model to assess the fund's sensitivity to \ndifferent macro scenarios.  The model is presented in \nExhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c20_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c20_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c20_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c20_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c20_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c20_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 21, "vignette": "Alpha Bond Investors (ABI) is a global fixed-income fund.  \nGemmy Marsico, CFA, is an ABI portfolio manager and \nMarget Redithy is an analyst who assists Marsico by \nperforming valuation and risk analysis.\nMarsico intends to increase her portfolio's allocation to \nshort-term German bunds.  To evaluate relative values in \nthe sector, Marsico directs Redithy to obtain the spot rates \nfor short-dated Bunds.  Annual compound par bond yields \non Bunds are shown in Exhibit 1:\nMarsico asks Redithy for assistance in evaluating two \nstrategies with 4-year investment horizons:\nStrategy 1:\nBuy a 4-year zero-coupon bond and hold it to \nmaturity.\nStrategy 2:\nBuy a 1-year zero-coupon bond and \nsimultaneously enter into a forward agreement to buy a 3-\nyear zero-coupon bond 1 year in the future.\nExhibit 2 contains current information on annual compound \nzero-coupon bond (ie, spot) rates and forward rates.\n\nWhile reviewing Redithy's analysis of the two strategies, \nMarsico comments that their relative attractiveness \ndepends on future interest rates.  During the discussion, \nRedithy expresses the view that \"the term structure of \ninterest rates is not determined by economy-wide \navailability and demand for credit.  Instead, due to the \nconstraints facing most issuers and investors, the shape and \nlevel of the yield curve is determined solely by the expected \nsupply and demand for credit at each maturity.\"\nMarsico wants to evaluate values in the eurozone corporate \nbond market and asks Redithy to gather information on the \nswap rate curve and swap spreads, which is shown in \nExhibit 3.  All rates are expressed on an annual compound \nbasis.\nIn the discussion with Marsico, Redithy identifies two \nadvantages of using the swap curve for valuing corporate \nbonds rather than using the government bond yield curve \nof the issuer's home country.\nAdvantage 1: Yields on government bonds are sometimes \ndistorted due to differences in liquidity that favor on-the-\nrun versus off-the-run issues.  However, swap rates are not \nsubject to the same distortions due to generally uniform \nliquidity across maturities.\nAdvantage 2: When analysts are valuing corporate bonds \nof a given maturity (eg, 10 years), a swap rate that reflects \nexactly 10 years to maturity is always available.  \nUnfortunately, even if it is the government's benchmark \nsecurity, the quoted government 10-year bond will rarely \nhave exactly 10 years to maturity.\n\nMarsico asks Redithy if she has considered interest rate \nvolatility in her analysis and what factor has the biggest \nimpact on short-term interest rate volatility.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c21_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c21_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c21_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c21_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c21_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c21_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 22, "vignette": "Gerard Marleau and Giles Toussaint are fixed income \nanalysts at Brittany Capital Advisors.  The two are having a \ngeneral discussion on the binomial interest tree framework \nsince their supervisor, Anne Callaise, has asked a series of \nquestions on the topic.\nToussaint comments that when valuing an option-free bond \nwith an arbitrage-free binomial lattice, he prefers to \nconstruct the binomial tree rates based on the spot rate \ncurve since:\nStatement 1: using par rates will result in an artificially \nhigh valuation\nStatement 2: using forward rates will result in an \nartificially low valuation\nMarleau then brings up the topic of pathwise valuation and \nstates that when valuing a 4-year, arbitrage-free, default-\nrisk-free bond, the bond's value:\nComponent 1: is probability-weighted\nComponent 2: follows only a single specific interest rate \npath\nComponent 3: results in the same value as a backward \ninduction process only if the path begins at the highest \nprobability node in Year 4.\nThe two analysts begin the process of calibrating a binomial \ninterest rate tree to match a specific term structure.  \nCallaise has asked them to work on this model together and \ndetermine the most appropriate interest rate at time i1,L.\n\nThe conversation then shifts to various term structure \nmodels.  Callaise has asked Marleau and Toussaint to \nidentify the term structure models that can accommodate \nthe current yield curve, which is inverted and has negative \nyields.  Toussaint adds that they also need to consider the \nappropriate volatility and drift assumptions of the model.\nFinally, Marleau asks Toussaint about the use of a Monte \nCarlo simulation.  Toussaint replies that it depends on the \nsecurities being evaluated and lists three types of securities \nthat would justify using a Monte Carlo simulation, with its \nadded complexity:\nSecurity 1:\nMortgage-backed security\nSecurity 2:\nZero-coupon bond with a European call\nSecurity 3:\nA-rated corporate straight bond", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c22_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c22_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c22_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c22_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c22_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c22_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 23, "vignette": "Imelda Jimenez is a derivatives analyst with a large \ninvestment bank.  She is talking with Sara Gutierrez, who \nmanages a large diversified bond portfolio.  Gutierrez is \nconcerned about a bond in the portfolio issued by SKF Inc., \nwhich has reported declining earnings and may face a \nratings downgrade within the next year.  Gutierrez would \nlike to determine the upfront premium that would be \nexpected for credit default swap (CDS) protection on the \nSKF bond, given the information shown in Exhibit 1:\nGutierrez is unsure what type of CDS would be the best \noption to fully hedge a downgrade in SKF's rating, and she \nasks Jimenez for her recommendation.  She also asks \nJimenez what kind of trade might be used if there are \ndifferences between bond and CDS spreads for Avron\nCorporation, another company in the portfolio.  Exhibit 2 \nhighlights the relevant company information:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c23_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c23_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c23_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c23_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 24, "vignette": "Stefan Bagby is a portfolio manager at Mulderberg\nInvestments, a firm that specializes in fixed income \nmanagement, and Jonathan Landa is a newly hired analyst \nwho reports to Bagby.  Bagby is interested in assessing \nLanda's knowledge of the relationships between spot rates, \nforward rates, and bond pricing.\nBagby provides Landa with Exhibit 1 and asks him to \ncalculate:\nthe forward price of a 2-year bond to be issued in 1 year, \nusing the forward pricing model\nthe interest rate on a 2-year loan that begins in 1 year, using \nthe forward rate model.\nHe then asks Landa to provide an interpretation of the 3-\nyear forward rate 1 year from today.  Landa replies that, \nassuming no arbitrage, the 3-year forward rate 1 year from \ntoday is equivalent to the return from buying either:\nReply 1:\nA 1-year zero-coupon bond today and then \nreinvesting the proceeds a year later in a 3-year zero-\ncoupon bond\nReply 2:\nA 4-year zero-coupon bond today\nBagby then turns his attention to active bond portfolio \nmanagement concepts and provides Landa with three \nalternative investment strategies, given spot and forward \nrate assumptions.  Bagby specifies that the investment \nhorizon is 1 year and the future spot rates in 1 year are \nequal to the no-arbitrage forward rates implied by spot rate \ncurve today, as presented in Exhibit 2.\n\nThe three alternative strategies are:\nStrategy 1\nBuy a 1-year zero-coupon bond\nStrategy 2:\nBuy a 3-year zero-coupon bond and sell it in \n1 year\nStrategy 3:\nBuy a 4-year zero-coupon bond and sell it in \n1 year\nNote that all spot and forward rates have been rounded.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c24_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c24_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c24_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c24_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 25, "vignette": "Marigold Darling, CFA, a fixed-income analyst at an asset \nmanagement firm, is evaluating three trading strategies \nbeing considered for Country A zero-coupon government \nbonds:\nStrategy 1:\nBuy a 3-year bond and hold to maturity.\nStrategy 2:\nBuy a 1-year bond and simultaneously buy a \n1-year forward contract on a 2-year bond.  When the 1-year \nbond matures, buy the underlying 2-year bond at the \nforward contract expiration, and hold that bond until it \nmatures.\nStrategy 3:\nBuy a 5-year bond and sell after 3 years.\nExhibit 1 contains the rates on the securities used for the \nthree strategies.\nDemetrio Burly, a portfolio strategist at the firm, is assigned \nto evaluate Country B's economy.  He is focusing on the \ncentral bank's expected actions and the effect of its policies \non interest rates.  Exhibit 2 contains information on Country \nB government bond rates.\n\nThe report Burly submits to his manager states \"Country B \nis in recession and its central bank is expected to reduce \nshort-term interest rates by 1.50% over next year.  \nFurthermore, the central bank has committed to buying \ngovernment bonds to help stimulate economic recovery \nand these bond purchases are expected to reduce \nintermediate term government bond yields by 1.00%.\"\nOne year later, Burly gathers the following information:\nInterest rates provided in all exhibits are based on annual \ncompounding.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c25_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c25_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c25_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c25_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c25_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c25_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 26, "vignette": "Jemmi Tauran, CFA, works on the proprietary trading desk \nof a private bank, dealing in fixed-income securities and \nrelated derivatives.  Tauran evaluates three bonds and the \nrelated single-name credit default swaps (CDS), which the \nfirm currently has no positions in.  Exhibit 1 contains \ninformation on the bonds and related CDS.  The market \nreference interest rate is 0.50%.\nIn search of other trading opportunities, Tauran performs a \ncredit analysis of Axit, Inc.  After completing the evaluation, \nshe buys 10-year Axit bonds and simultaneously buys credit \nprotection using a 10-year single-name CDS on Axit.  Exhibit \n2 contains information on the bonds and CDS.\nBased on her evaluation of economic conditions, Tauran\nconcludes there is likely to be a substantial weakening of US \nGDP growth near term, though her long-term outlook on \nthe economy is positive.  Near-term economic weakness \nwould create financial stress in the corporate sector and \nlikely result in a substantial increase in short-term credit \nspreads.  However, longer-term credit spreads are likely to \nremain stable or increase by a small amount.  Tauran\ndecides to implement a curve trade using the US high-yield \nindex CDS (ie, HY CDX) to profit from the expected change \nin the credit curve.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c26_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c26_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c26_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c26_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c26_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c26_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 27, "vignette": "Amanda Neal is a fixed-income strategist at a sell-side firm.  \nNeal has a meeting scheduled with Geetika Patel, a buy-\nside fixed-income portfolio manager.  Patel manages a \ngovernment bond fund with a weighted average maturity of \n10 years.  The two plan to discuss Neal's economic outlook \nand recommended positioning for the fixed-income \nportfolio.\nThe economy has experienced modest growth over the past \nseveral years, and inflation has generally been benign.  The \ncentral bank policy rate has been unchanged over this time, \nbut now there are some developing concerns regarding \ninflation pressures.  There is widespread expectation that \nthe central bank will raise interest rates several times over \nthe next 12 months.  The yield curve is currently upward \nsloping and would be considered normally shaped.\nAt the meeting, Neal suggests that the economy is likely to \navoid a recession as long as the central bank does not \ntighten monetary policy too much.  Neal's current forecast \nis for the central bank to raise the policy rate by a total of \n100 basis points over the next 12 months.\nPatel responds by stating that, given the forecasting \nuncertainty over the next 12 months, \"My belief is that \nexpected returns for all bonds over a short-term horizon \nwill match the risk-free rate due to the no-arbitrage \ncondition in the markets.  Therefore, I should allocate as \nmuch of the portfolio as possible to the 1-year maturity \nsegment of the curve and shorten the fund's duration.\"\nBased on Patel's portfolio reallocation comment, Neal \npresents her three-factor term structure model, which \nforms the basis of her yield curve forecast.  Neal is \npredicting no change in the level factor, but a 2increase in \nboth the slope and curvature factors over the next year.  \nNeal's model is presented in Exhibit 1:\n\nNeal then sums up her portfolio positioning views based on \nher economic forecast, yield curve assumptions, and term \nstructure model.  She notes that, over the next year, either \nof the following strategies would be preferable to a 10-year \nbullet strategy, provided the future spot rates stay below \nthe current forward rates:\nStrategy 1:\nRiding the yield curve using 11-year default-\nfree government bonds\nStrategy 2:\nA 1-year and 20-year barbell strategy\nPatel is intrigued by Neal's term structure factor model and \nits implication for the portfolio, and she makes the \nfollowing statements about the portfolio's yield curve risks \nand related duration measures:\nStatement 1: A portfolio's effective duration adequately \naccounts for shaping risk.\nStatement 2: Key rate duration is useful for individual \nbonds, but not portfolios.\nStatement 3: Key rate duration is appropriately only for \nbonds without embedded options.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c27_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c27_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c27_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c27_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_fixed_income", "source_label": "UWorld – Fixed Income", "topic": "Fixed Income", "case_no": 28, "vignette": "Ali Laschi manages the fixed-income trading desk for an \ninternational bank, where Jennifer Beaumont is an analyst.  \nLaschi asks Beaumont to assess the relative value of several \ngovernment bonds.  Exhibit 1 contains selected data on the \noff-the-run government bonds Laschi wants Beaumont to \nevaluate:\nExhibit 2 contains information on government par bond \nyields and spot rates that Beaumont will use in analyzing \nthe bonds:\nLaschi and Beaumont discuss bond valuation using spot \nrates versus using a binomial tree.  During the discussion, \nBeaumont makes the following comments regarding a \nbinomial interest rate framework with a tree constructed \nusing a lognormal interest rate model:\nComment 1:\nWhereas spot rate curve valuations discount \neach cash flow once by the appropriate term spot rate, \nbinomial valuations discount cash flows period by period, \neach time using the 1-period forward rates along every \npossible interest rate path from Time 0 to maturity.\nComment 2:\nUsing a volatility of 0% for tree construction \nresults in a tree with a single interest rate path that consists \nof the 1-period spot rate and a series of 1-period forward \nrates derived from the spot rate curve.\n\nLaschi asks Beaumont to explain in detail how backward \ninduction is used to calculate a coupon bond's value at each \nnode on a binomial tree.  In response, Beaumont makes the \nfollowing statements:\nStatement 1: The value at any node is any coupon due, \nadded to the average of the PVs of the two nodes along the \ninterest rate paths to the succeeding period.\nStatement 2: Before the addition of that period's coupon, \nthe value at any node is the average PV of subsequent \nperiods' cash flows for all the interest rate paths from that \nnode to maturity.\nLaschi then asks Beaumont to perform a pathwise valuation \nof Bond B.  Exhibit 3 contains a binomial tree constructed \nfrom government par bonds:\nExhibit 4 describes the possible interest rate paths on the \ntree to be used for a pathwise valuation of the bond:\nAssume all bonds pay coupons once per annum and all \ninterest rates are expressed as annual compound rates.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c28_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c28_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c28_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_fixed_income_c28_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Nathaniel Viklund, CFA, is a research analyst at Brinson Brokers \n(BB).  He covers several companies in the retail industry, \nincluding Guadiana Stores.  In the previous month, after \nextensive research and analysis, Viklund wrote a report with a \nbuy recommendation of Guadiana stock.  He distributed the \nreport to all BB's clients through email, text messaging, and \ninstant messaging apps, strictly following BB's written \nprocedures, which were revised by BB's compliance officer two \nyears ago.\nHowever, just after the report was released, one of Viklund's\nclients informed him that, six months earlier, a new regulation \nhad prohibited the use of instant messaging to distribute \ninvestment opinions or recommendations.  Viklund instantly \ncontacted the compliance officer, who was also unaware of the \nnew regulation and immediately updated BB's written \nprocedures.\nLater, Guadiana announces the closing of several stores, \nprompting Viklund to update his research.  He concludes that \nhis recommendation should be changed to \"hold.\"  In a call \nwith some of BB's clients, he explains the new outlook and the \nchange in recommendation.  These \"premium\" clients pay an \nadditional fee for this personalized service, which is offered to \nall BB's existing and prospective clients.  Viklund includes his \nupdated report with the new recommendation in BB's weekly \nnewsletter, which is released and distributed to all clients two \ndays later.\nViklund receives a call from Victoria Mwape, CFA, an \nindependent financial adviser who is a client of BB.  Mwape \nrecommended the stock of Tagus Corp. to her clients, based on \na report written by Viklund.  In the report, after diligent \nanalysis and with reasonable basis, Viklund recommended \nbuying Tagus, but the stock has declined sharply since then.  \nMwape's clients have had difficulty selling the stock due to its \nlow liquidity.  She contends the report should have warned that \nthe stock was highly illiquid, and Viklund explains that Tagus's \nlow average trading volume was public information.\nTagus recently announced a substantial impairment loss on an \ninvestment in an online retailer.  When Mwape complains that \nViklund's report did not include a warning about this, Viklund\nreplies that his research did not indicate a risk of impairment.  \nMwape also believes that the report was misleading since it \nstated, \"Given an estimated increase in operating margin from \n11% to 16% in the next three years, our recommendation for \nTagus stock is a strong buy.\"  After the report\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nwas written, Tagus released its quarterly financial results, \ndisclosing that its operating margin decreased to 9.5%.\nMwape gathers information on the financial objectives and \nconstraints of all her new clients.  She uses that information \nto develop individual investment policy statements (IPS), \nwhich she updates at least annually.  After determining \neach client's risk profile, Mwape usually recommends\nallocating a portion of the client's assets to the same index-\nbased ETF.  For the most risk-averse clients, she also \ntypically recommends buying index put options to limit \npotential losses from a decrease in the ETF's price.  She \nmakes the recommendations according to each client's IPS \nand explains to each client the potential outcomes and the \nrisks involved.\nMwape sends a monthly newsletter to her clients and \nprospective clients.  In the latest newsletter, she presents \nthe performance of Ticino Asset Management's funds, \nwhich she has been recommending to her clients.  Ticino \nhired an equity portfolio manager who had a remarkable \n10-year track record at a previous company.  Mwape \npresents this track record in the newsletter, disclosing both \nthe portfolio manager's specific role in achieving it and the \nfact that it occurred at a different firm.  The performance is \npresented gross of fees, which is also disclosed.\nOne of Mwape's clients is Mind the Future (MTF), a \nnongovernmental organization (NGO) that assists college \nstudents.  In a recent meeting, MTF's CEO told Mwape the \norganization had launched a fundraising campaign to create \na network of libraries that would lend textbooks to low-\nincome students.  The CEO also mentioned that the board \nof directors was defining the budget and funding to \nrenovate MTF's headquarters.  Later, Mwape meets with \nanother client, the dean of a university, who tells her that \nthe university recently awarded an honorary degree to one \nof MTF's board members.  Mwape tries to get the CEO's \nopinion on this, but she cannot reach the CEO by phone.  In \nthe monthly newsletter, she includes a section on MTF, \ndisclosing the public fundraising, the headquarters \nrenovation, and the honorary degree awarded to its board \nmember.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Salma Ahmad, CFA, works in fixed income sales for Bank \nGibraltar (BG), a large international investment bank.  \nAhmad lives and works in a country in the Middle East.  \nAhmad's sales coverage universe includes the entire Middle \nEast region, serving many countries and regulatory \njurisdictions, and she is required by BG to be familiar with \nthe rules and regulations of the countries in which she does \nbusiness.\nThe investment bank is finalizing work on an underwriting \nfor a new green bond issue for an industrial company based \nin the same country as Ahmad.  Ahmad emails a \npreliminary prospectus for the bond issue to all her \ninstitutional clients.  Ahmad also emails her largest \ninstitutional client, Ishmael Khan, portfolio manager at \nWahiba Capital, for feedback on the specific terms, \nstructure, and pricing.  Khan operates in a different country \nthan Ahmad.\nKhan manages three fixed-income funds for Wahiba:\nAn ESG-focused fund that invests exclusively in green bonds\nA diversified investment-grade corporate bond fund that \ninvests only in the Middle East region\nA broadly diversified global bond fund\nThe next day, after not receiving a response to her email, \nAhmad asks Khan for feedback and potential interest in the \nnew issue through an instant messaging (IM) app.  Khan \nresponds through IM, stating that he is agreeable to the \nproposed terms, and he places an order for the securities.  \nAhmad conveys the order information to BG's trading desk.  \nThe policies, laws, and regulations on the use of IM are \ndetailed in Exhibit 1:\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nKhan's order included an equal number of bonds for each \nof the three funds since Khan deemed the bonds were \nappropriate for all funds.  The demand for the green bonds \nis very high, and the offering is many times oversubscribed.  \nWahiba's allocation from BG is only a third of what Khan \nrequested.  Khan decides to place the entire allocation with \nthe ESG-focused fund since this was the original amount \nrequested for that fund, the fund has the least investment \nflexibility, and there are other attractive investment options \nfor the other funds.\nA few weeks ago, BG also underwrote a large negotiated \ncorporate bond offering.  BG still has a sizeable position in \nthese bonds, having been unable to place the entire issue \nwith institutional clients.  The market has deteriorated \nconsiderably since the original issue date as interest rates \nhave moved sharply higher.  The bonds are now trading well \nbelow the original par issue price.  Quarter end is \napproaching, and the bank is anxious to get these bonds off \nits balance sheet but does not want to take a loss on the \nposition.\nIn an attempt to sell the bonds at par, the bank's head of \nfixed income sales organizes a conference call with some of \nthe firm's largest high-net-worth investors who have \npurchased similar bonds in the past.  Ahmad is asked to \nlead the sales presentation but is told not to mention the \ncurrent market price of the bonds.  Ahmad complies with \nthe request because she has experienced similar situations \nin the past and believes it is in the best interest of the bank \nto participate.  Ahmad also knows that if the trading desk \ntakes a large loss on these bonds, it will negatively affect \nher bonus.\nA few months later, Bob Smith, CFA, the investor relations \nhead for the bond issuer, organizes a conference call with \ncompany management, institutional buy-side investors, and \nsell-side analysts.  During the call, material nonpublic \ninformation is inadvertently disclosed.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Tom Johnson, CFA, is a portfolio manager at Universal Advisors, \na US-based wealth management firm that serves high-net-\nworth individuals and families.  Universal's equity portfolio \noffering includes separately managed accounts (SMAs) in which \nclients own individual securities.  Johnson manages the \nportfolio with an aggressive high-risk, high-reward strategy, \ninvesting mostly in small-cap growth stocks.  To reduce \ntransaction costs and to simplify trading and settlement, \nUniversal buys only US-listed securities for client accounts.\nJohnson instructs Francois Martin, a CFA Level II candidate and \nnew equity analyst at the firm, to conduct research on Vent \nIndustries, a French wind turbine manufacturer.  Vent is dually \nlisted on exchanges in France and in the US.  Martin, a French \nnational who recently moved to the US, is already familiar with \nthe company since he has been following it personally for the \npast two years.\nAfter completing due diligence on Vent, Martin is thoroughly \nimpressed by the investment prospects and suggests that \nJohnson add the US-listed shares of Vent to client portfolios.  \nJohnson, as the sole decision-maker, reviews Martin's research \nand financial models but wants to think about the suggestion \nbefore reaching a conclusion.\nMartin is so impressed with Vent's investment prospects that \nhe wants to buy it for his personal account.  For guidance, \nMartin references Universal's publicly available personal \ntransaction disclosure, which states only the following:\n\"Investment personnel are subject to policies and procedures \nregarding their personal trading.\"\nNeeding more detail, Martin checks with the firm's compliance \nofficer, who informs him that the firm does have policies and \nprocedures designed to prevent potential conflicts of interest \nrelated to personal trading.  Universal does not require that \nemployees obtain preclearance before trading, but the firm's \npolicies do require:\na two-day blackout period before and after client trades, and\na quarterly report by its investment decision-making personnel \non transactions and holdings.\nBased on this information, Martin immediately places an order \nto buy Vent shares listed in France through his personal French \nbrokerage account, which he established prior to joining \nUniversal.  \nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nThree days later, Johnson decides to invest for clients in the \nUS-listed shares of Vent and places the order through \nUniversal's trading desk.\nJohnson is a board member for a local hospital endowment; \nfor this, he receives modest compensation.  Universal has \napproved Johnson's board participation and compensation.  \nJohnson is considered to be a thoughtful and successful \ninvestor.  The other board members, unhappy with the fees \nand performance of the endowment's existing income-\noriented large-cap equity manager, asked Johnson a year \nago if he would be willing to manage the equity portion of \nthe endowment.  Johnson responded by stating:\n\"The hospital provides so much to this community, I would \nbe happy to manage the endowment's equity portfolio.  It \nwon't even take much time; I will manage the endowment \nportfolio as an exact replica of Universal's equity portfolio.\"\nThe next month, without informing Universal, Johnson \nbegan managing the endowment portfolio as a mirror \nimage of Universal's equity strategy.\nRecently, Johnson evaluated the investment prospects of an \nupcoming IPO for a small-cap biotech firm.  The company \ndoes innovative cancer research, but it is still years away \nfrom profitability.  After conducting thorough due diligence, \nJohnson has concluded this is a great investment \nopportunity; he would like the hospital endowment to \nparticipate, believing the other board members would be \nexcited about the company's cancer research.\nJohnson speaks to Universal's lead underwriter and \nrequests that 5% of the firm's IPO allotment be allocated to \nthe endowment.  A few days later, when the IPO is priced \nand allocated, Universal receives 95,000 shares and the \nendowment receives 5,000 shares.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Gregory Tullos, CFA, is a sell-side equity analyst at Trailblazer \nFinancial, a multinational financial services company.  \nTrailblazer is approached by Insight Technologies, a small \ntechnology company that recently went public through an IPO, \nto write a research report to increase awareness among \npotential investors.  Trailblazer assigns the report to Tullos.  As \ncompensation, Insight offers to pay Trailblazer a flat fee and to \npay Tullos a bonus based on the number of new investors in \nInsight over the next year.  Tullos plans to disclose all \ncompensation in the report.\nTullos agrees to the offer and begins research on Insight.  \nDuring the research process, Tullos uses a model created by a \nformer employee of Trailblazer who developed the model while \nworking there.  When the report is completed, the former \nemployee is not credited for her work.\nBefore publishing the report, Tullos talks to Rania El-Hachem, \nCFA, about IPOs.  El-Hachem is a portfolio manager of \nseparately managed accounts at Trailblazer.  She recently \nresearched the upcoming IPO of Dome Pharmaceuticals and \nhas deemed it an appropriate investment for some of her \nclients.  She also likes the stock for her own portfolio, but she \nsubmits a request for only enough shares for her clients.\nDemand for the IPO ends up being less than anticipated, and \nthe underwriter asks if El-Hachem would be interested in \nadditional shares if the IPO comes in at a slightly lower price.  \nEl-Hachem confirms that she could purchase the full allocation \nfor her clients and then purchase some shares for herself.  She \npurchases the shares for her clients with Trailblazer's funds, \nallocating those shares to suitable client portfolios.  Then, using \npersonal funds, El-Hachem purchases the additional shares for \nherself, a practice allowed by Trailblazer.\nOne of El-Hachem's clients requests that El-Hachem add a \ncertain stock to his portfolio.  The stock has increased 2% or \nmore in each of the last four trading days, and the client wants \nto buy the stock before it increases further.  After researching \nthe company, El-Hachem does not believe that the stock aligns \nwith the risk and return objectives outlined in the client's \ninvestment policy statement.  However, El-Hachem believes \nthat the trade would have only a minimal impact on the \nportfolio's overall risk profile.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nOne of the analysts on Tullos's team recently left Trailblazer \nfor another firm.  This former employee manages two social \nmedia accounts: one personal account and one professional \naccount that has been sponsored by Trailblazer and used \nfor client engagement.  Trailblazer requires employees to \ndelete their professional social media accounts when \nleaving the firm.  Upon leaving Trailblazer, the former \nemployee posted that the professional account would \nremain active but that he would no longer be updating it; \ninstead, he provided a link to his professional account at his \nnew firm.\nChris Arendse, another portfolio manager at Trailblazer, is \nbecoming a father and plans to reduce his full-time role \nwith the company to help care for his child.  Trailblazer and \nArendse agree to a scaled-back role for him with fewer \nclients.  El-Hachem will assume some of Arendse's clients.  \nAfter this occurs, one client who was transferred to El-\nHachem asks Arendse to review his portfolio quarterly.  In \nreturn, the client offers to let Arendse use the client's beach \nhouse one week each year.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Chaiken Partners is an investment advisory firm in Country \nA.  The firm services institutions and high-net-worth \nindividuals from around the world but has no offices or \nadvisers outside of Country A.  Chaiken's investment \nstrategy is to invest based on outputs from complex \nalgorithm-driven computer models that are developed \ninternally by a team led by Charlene Laight, CFA.\nWhen creating an algorithm, Laight's team meticulously \nresearches the inputs that will be used and performs \nextensive backtesting on existing market data to forecast \nthe algorithm's performance.  Once Laight approves an \nalgorithm, she thoroughly explains the algorithm's strategy \nto Chaiken's advisers and provides a summary of the \nbacktest results, but she does not provide technical details \non how the algorithms work.\nLaight and her team recently developed and approved a \nnew model; when backtested over a 15-year period, this \nmodel generated average hypothetical returns of 9% with a \nstandard deviation of 13%.  However, the model has not yet \nproduced actual results.\nOne of Chaiken's advisers, Hitomi Tanaka, has been \ncontacted by a potential client, Bryson Petersen, who \nresides in Country A but is a citizen of Country B.  Petersen \nis considering an initial investment of USD 25 million and \nindicates that this amount represents about 50% of his net \nworth.  Petersen tells Tanaka that he understands in general \nthe tradeoff between investment risk and return but has \nlittle investing experience.  Tanaka discusses Petersen's \nbackground with Chaiken's compliance department, which \nthen authorizes her to take Petersen on as a client.\nTanaka explains to Petersen that Chaiken uses algorithms to \ngenerate investment options and, although the options' \nperformance has been good over long periods, there is \nalways a risk of loss.  Tanaka invests Petersen's assets using \nLaight's new model, which generates a 15% loss in the first \nquarter.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nShortly after Petersen becomes a client, Tanaka hears a \nrumor that Petersen is being investigated for money \nlaundering in Country B.  She is concerned that money \nlaundering might be the source of Petersen's investment \nwith Chaiken, but there is no definitive evidence of this.  \nTanaka gathers information about the applicable laws of \nCountries A and B that apply to this situation, and how \nthose laws compare with the Code and Standards.  This \ninformation is shown in Exhibit 1:\nRoss Farley, CFA, is head of compliance for Chaiken.  He has \nprovided all employees with Chaiken's code of ethics and a \npolicies and procedures manual.  Each year, Farley regularly \nconducts educational training, sets expectations for \nbehavior, and requires employees to sign a document \nattesting that they have received the code and manual and \nunderstand the contents of both.  In addition, employees \nare required to submit a quarterly transaction report \ndetailing their trading of individual securities.\nTanaka posts on an online chat site where participants offer \nadvice to Candidates studying for the CFA exams.  After \nreading a CFA Institute article that advocates for increased \ncorporate environmental, social, and governance (ESG) \nawareness, she posts the following on the site: \"CFA \nInstitute should think carefully about posting advocacy \narticles on key issues, such as ESG, that I and others \ndisagree with.  These articles can give potential employers \nthe impression that all investment professionals agree with \nCFA Institute positions when that is rarely the case.  In \naddition, CFA Institute's procedures are too strict.  For \ninstance, why are we allowed to use only two types of \ncalculators when there are others that are better?  Get with \nthe times!\"\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nTanaka's biography on Chaiken's website mentions that she \nis a licensed medical doctor, and her business cards read \n\"Hitomi Tanaka, MD, CFA.\"  In the biography, she states that \n\"completing the CFA Program greatly improved my \nknowledge of portfolio management and investing \nstrategies based on Big Data applications.\"\nChaiken permits employees to maintain investment \naccounts with outside firms but requires the employees to \nsend Farley duplicate confirmations for all trades.  This rule \napplies for accounts that an employee establishes in his or \nher own name, as well as accounts for immediate family \nmembers such as a spouse, domestic partner, child, or \nparent.  About half of the employees have outside \naccounts.  Farley does not regularly check the confirmations \nbut believes that employees are complying with the rule.\nGreg Smith, a Chaiken employee, recently circumvented the \nrule by having his neighbor open an account at another \nfirm, with Smith directing the trades made in the account.  \nFarley was not aware of this activity until the neighbor who \n\"owned\" the account was charged with insider trading.  The \nneighbor then told law enforcement authorities that Smith \nwas directing the trades.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Alita Wong, CFA, is the chief compliance officer at Lantau \nCapital, a sell-side brokerage firm.  Wong is updating the firm's \npolicies and procedures related to preventing general \nmisconduct, and she develops the following procedures:\nProcedure 1:\nCheck references of potential new employees \nto ensure they are of good character and for violations of \ninvestment-related laws or regulations.\nProcedure 2:\nDisseminate to all employees a list of potential \nviolations along with related disciplinary sanctions, including \nactions that lead to termination.\nProcedure 3:\nSecure an acknowledgment from every \nemployee that they have read, understand, and will abide by \nLantau's code of ethics.\nAs part of her duties, Wong has compliance supervision \nresponsibilities regarding the research Lantau produces.  Wong \ndelegates much of the review process to others, but she \nregularly conducts random reviews of new research that is \nscheduled to be released to clients.\nOne report in particular caught her attention; it was completed \nby Jared Devonshire, CFA, a quantitative analyst at the firm.  In \nthe report, Devonshire unveils a new quantitative stock \nselection model based on a new and innovative approach.  \nDevonshire credits the quantitative team at Lantau for coming \nup with the new model.\nIntrigued and genuinely curious about this new approach, \nWong stops by Devonshire's office to ask how he and the team \ncame up with the idea.  Devonshire responds by stating that he \nhad read about this new approach in a recently released paper \npublished in an academic journal.  Wong responds by stating \nshe did not see a reference to the original research.\nDevonshire replies that while the team maintained the core \nconcepts of the original research, the team also made some \nmechanical adjustments to the model to make it more \napplicable in the real world.  He believes these adjustments \nwere sufficient in magnitude that sourcing the original research \nis not necessary.  Wong agrees with Devonshire's assessment \nand approves the publication of the report.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nBack in her office, Wong is reviewing the firm's trading \nrecords and notices an exceptionally large series of trades \nthat were done by the firm's proprietary trading desk (ie, \nprop trading desk), which trades for the firm's benefit with \nthe firm's capital.  The trades involved homebuilder stocks: \nall were very profitable, and open positions were closed out \nwithin 24 hours after they were established.  The trades \nwere executed by the firm's most celebrated trader, Bill \nCampbell, who has generated considerable profits for the \nfirm over his career.  Wong then reviews trading records for \nthe past several months and identifies a pattern of trading \nthese same stocks at about the same time each month.\nWong asks Campbell about the trades.  Campbell replies \nthat Lantau's economic research team has been very good \nat correctly forecasting data on government housing starts, \nwhich are released monthly.  Lantau publishes each \nmonth's report before the government releases this data.  \nCampbell explains that he has been trading in advance of \nthe government's releases based on the firm's published \nresearch.\nWong consults with the economic research team to verify \nCampbell's story.  The head of economic research advises \nWong that the team recently hired a junior analyst, \nSolomon Timur, CFA.  Timur previously worked at the \ngovernment Department of Housing, and his monthly \nforecasts have been exceptional.\nWong then visits Timur's office to introduce herself.  Timur \nis visibly nervous when questioned about his forecasts.  \nDuring the conversation, Timur admits he has a contact \ninside the Department of Housing who provides him with \ninformation that will be included in each government press \nrelease on housing starts.  Timur has been using this \ninformation in his published research reports.  Timur states \nthat he has disclosed this information on the government \nemployee to Wong to achieve the protections afforded a \n\"whistleblower.\"\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nPlease do 3rd question here\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Transcend Capital is an asset management firm that specializes \nin domestic investments and hires third-party managers to \nmanage foreign investments.  Jericho Freeman, CFA, is one of \nthe portfolio managers at Transcend.  He plans to attend a large \nmedical industry conference to talk to companies' \nmanagement teams, third-party fund managers, and potential \nclients.\nTranscend has been reviewing third-party managers and plans \nto add another.  Borys Sokolowski, a manager at Fortune \nPartners, one of the third-party funds in consideration, is also \nplanning to attend the industry conference.  Although \ncommercial travel options are available, Sokolowski offers to \npay for Freeman's travel and accommodations for the \nconference since it is considered a business meeting.  \nSokolowski also offers to pay for Freeman's tickets to a sporting \nevent before their scheduled business dinner.\nFreeman plans to bring a marketing brochure he put together \non Transcend to present to prospective clients at the \nconference.  The brochure has the following summary:\n\"Transcend was able to generate returns of:\n25% last year,\n18% annualized over the past three years, and\n15% annualized over the past five years.\"\nThe returns are accurate but exclude several portfolios that \nTranscend closed last year due to underperformance.  The \nportfolios were excluded since Transcend does not claim GIPS \ncompliance.  The summary indicates that accounts were \nexcluded in the past but does not mention when.  The \nsummary also notes that it is limited in nature and that a \ndetailed presentation is available upon request.\nOn the plane to the conference, Freeman sits behind Sayuri \nNomura, CFA, a well-respected senior medical device analyst, \nand one of her associates, Amelia Parker, CFA.  Freeman \noverhears part of their conversation:\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nNomura:  I will finalize my research on Cardinal Medical \nTechnologies at the conference after confirming a few things \nwith their CEO.  The report will update the rating from hold to \nbuy, and the updated valuation will be 20% higher than my \ncurrent valuation and 30% higher than the company's stock \nprice.  I will distribute the note after my meeting with the CEO.\nParker:  Per your request, so you can use them in your \nresearch, I am keeping track of any:\ntrending Twitter posts about companies that we cover,\nprice movements of companies' stocks before and after big \nannouncements, and\npress releases issued by companies during the conference.\nAt the conference, Freeman meets with Ranjit Kapoor, CFA, a \nnewly hired pension manager at BTK Manufacturing (BTKM).  \nKapoor is considering investing a portion of the pension fund \nassets with Transcend.  Kapoor tells Freeman that the pension \nfund is heavily invested in BTKM stock, and BTKM's \nmanagement has pressured Kapoor to allocate more of the \nfund's assets to BTKM stock.  Kapoor believes this would help \nsupport the stock's price but would make the plan less \ndiversified.  Instead, Kapoor believes an investment in \nTranscend would provide much-needed diversification and \nreduce the plan's risk for participants.  BTKM's management \nbelieves third-party investment managers are too risky and that \nBTKM's stock is the safest investment.  They note that most of \ntheir compensation comes from BTKM stock options and \nwarrants.\nFreeman also meets with Xue Chin, who tells Freeman that she \nis planning to move her investments from her current asset \nmanager, Element Financial.  Chin says that the manager of \nElement, Fredrick Love, CFA, has done things that she is not \ncomfortable with:\nAction 1:\nBeing arrested during a protest of a newly \nelected government official\nAction 2:\nRefusing to testify against a colleague that he \nknew was trading on inside information despite being \nsubpoenaed\nAction 3:\nDeclaring bankruptcy after being fined for \naccepting undisclosed payments from brokers for \nrecommending their services to clients\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Kamala Mani, CFA, is a portfolio manager at an investment \nmanagement firm.  Mani has two clients, a municipal pension \nfund with assets under management (AUM) of $1.5 billion and \na college endowment fund with AUM of $250 million.\nMani is researching Upstream Technologies and believes it \nwould be an appropriate investment for both of her clients.  \nMani gives the Upstream stock buy order to Insight Brokers, \nwho executes the order in a single transaction as a block trade.  \nInsight Brokers is not the lowest-cost provider, but it also \nprovides research to Mani's firm through a soft dollar \narrangement.  The agreement is disclosed to Mani's two \nclients, and she uses Insight Brokers' research to benefit both \nclients.  The next day, Mani receives a research report from \nInsight Brokers, which she uses in her research to update \ncertain stocks' valuations.  After review, she provides the \nresearch report to another portfolio manager at the company, \nwho uses this report in his own research.\nMani receives another report from Insight Brokers later in the \nweek.  Typically, Mani reviews Insight Brokers' reports herself, \nbut she is getting ready for several meetings later that day and \ndoes not have time to do so.  She asks Binita Singh, an analyst \nat the firm, to review the report for accuracy and soundness, \nstating that Insight Brokers' previous reports have all been \naccurate with valid and compelling reasoning.  After review, \nSingh confirms that the research report is sound and free from \ninaccuracy.\nLater that morning, Mani decides to purchase Agile Logistics, \nwhich she recently researched and believes would also be an \nappropriate investment for both clients.  Mani plans to submit \na block trade to Basic Brokers, a low-priced broker, and allocate \nshares worth $500,000 to the pension fund and $100,000 to \nthe endowment fund.  However, to satisfy her soft dollar \ncommitment, she instead submits the $500,000 order to \nInsight Brokers and the $100,000 order to Basic Brokers.  When \nallocating the shares, she allocates the higher price paid to \nInsight Brokers to the pension fund and the lower price paid to \nBasic Brokers to the endowment fund since that is where the \norders were placed, which is allowed based on the firm's \npolicies.\nMani then attends a lunch meeting with Shalini Kohli, a \nrepresentative of a broker that is seeking Mani's business.  \nKohli brings an example of research conducted by the broker's \nresearch team.  Mani is not impressed by the research, but \nsuggests that a donation to the local food bank, where Mani \noften volunteers, would inspire her to look more deeply into \nthe broker's research.  After returning to the office and before \nhearing about whether Kohli accepts her offer, Mani reviews\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nmore of the broker's research and decides not to use the \nbroker's services.\nLater that day, Mani meets with Mihir Datta, a \nrepresentative from her pension fund client, for dinner.  \nThe pension fund has outperformed its benchmark over the \npast year and the municipality wishes to show its gratitude.  \nDatta gives Mani two front-row seats to the upcoming \nprofessional football playoff game.  Since her firm's office is \nclosed for the night, Mani accepts the invitation and \ndiscloses the gift to the firm's compliance department the \nnext morning.\nAt the dinner, Datta indicates that he is looking for \ninheritance tax planning services.  Mani's firm does not \nprovide these services, but it partners with CPA Tax \nAdvisors, a tax consultancy firm.  For every client that Mani \nrefers to CPA Tax Advisors, her firm receives a referral bonus \nbased on a percentage of the total value of the service \nreferred.  Mani suggests Datta contact CPA Tax Advisors and \nstates that her firm does receive a referral bonus, but all \nreferred clients have praised these services.  Mani does not \nspecify the terms of the referral bonus.\nOn her free time, Mani is a CFA Institute volunteer who is \nhelping to create material for the upcoming Level I CFA \nexam.  Mani is assigned to write a series of questions about \nmonetary and fiscal policy.  Mani invites another volunteer, \nDaichi Fujita, CFA, who is developing another set of \neconomics questions, to dinner to discuss their processes.  \nMani and Fujita discuss each of their questions under \nconsideration and provide feedback to each other.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c8_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c8_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Ionna Sideris, CFA, a financial adviser at Dauerhaft\nInvestments, attends an investment conference where she \nparticipates in a panel discussion.  She presents the \nperformance of funds managed by Dauerhaft, including DH \nEquities, a fund based on a proprietary stock selection \nmethodology.  The presentation includes:\nback-tested returns from 2010 to 2015 using the \nproprietary methodology,\nactual returns from 2016 to 2018, when DH fund was \nrestricted to Dauerhaft partners,\nthe fund's actual performance beginning in 2019, when it \nbecame widely available to investors, and\ndetailed explanations that fully disclose all aspects of the \nperformance being reported.\nAt the cocktail reception after the conference, Sideris meets \nwith potential clients and industry practitioners.  She is \nintroduced to Ludovic Tremblay, CFA, a sell-side equity \nanalyst at Regen Securities who is interested in having \nDauerhaft as a client and gives her copies of Regen's equity \nresearch reports.  The next day, Sideris realizes that, with \nthe reports, Tremblay mistakenly gave her a list of names \nand email addresses of Regen's clients.  She decides not to \ninform Tremblay and adds the email addresses to her \nmailing list of prospective clients.\nSideris holds a conference call with Dauerhaft's clients in \nwhich she details the characteristics of the DH Equities \nfund.  She describes the fund's returns, historical \ncomposition, performance attribution, fee structure, and \nmanagement team.  Since DH's proprietary methodology is \nconfidential, she does not disclose any aspect of the stock \nselection process or the risks involved.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nBack at his office at Regen, Tremblay finalizes a research \nreport on 4Auto, a small-cap auto parts manufacturer.  \nBased on an extensive analysis, he believes the company \nwill lose a third of its largest clients to a new competitor \nwith a groundbreaking manufacturing technology.  As a \nresult, expecting the stock to decrease 15%, he distributes a \nsell recommendation to his clients.  He documents \ninformation that supports his report, including financial \nmodels and notes from meetings.  He also uses an outside \nresearch report from an independent research firm, but he \ndoes not include it in the documentation file since it is \npublicly available.\nIn the following days, 4Auto stock decreases 28%.  Tremblay \ndiligently revises each step of his analysis, confirms that the \nfundamentals remain the same, and changes his \nrecommendation to a buy.  He explains to Regen's clients \nthat his assumptions and target price did not change, but \nthat the stock became undervalued due to its recent sharp \ndecline.  One month later, 4Auto stock recovers part of its \nlosses and generates substantial profits to the clients who \nfollowed Tremblay's recommendations.\nTremblay finishes writing a research report on another \ncompany, Aeolian Industries, which manufactures wind \nturbines.  After a diligent analysis, he recommends selling \nthe stock given worsening prospects for the wind turbine \nindustry.  Before distributing the report, he is informed that \nRegen's CEO has just been appointed to Aeolian's board of \ndirectors.  Tremblay is not sure if he should distribute the \nreport given this potential conflict of interest.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Susan Gobert, CFA, is a senior portfolio manager at Draco Asset \nManagement.  She has frequent meetings with her clients to \nexplain the performance of Draco's funds, including the Orion \nBalanced Fund.  In these meetings, she usually distributes \nmaterials that include fact sheets on the investment funds and \nDraco's annual report.\nGiven the current environment of lower interest rates, Draco's \ninvestment committee recently approved a temporary increase \nin the Orion Balanced Fund's allocation to equities, as well as \nthe inclusion of riskier high-yield corporate bonds.  Gobert \nknows that Orion's fact sheet has not yet been updated with \nthat information.  She is also aware that Draco's annual report \noverstates her years of investment experience by including a \nperiod of four years when she worked at a fintech firm focused \non digital payments.\nDraco's marketing department is responsible for creating both \nthe fact sheets and the annual reports.  Since these are the \nmost recent materials produced by the firm, Gobert distributes \nthem to clients without additional clarification.\nThe investment committee has decided to reduce the positions \nseveral of Draco's funds hold in WTech, a small data services \ncompany.  Gobert worries that this will lead to a substantial \ndecrease in the stock's price, given its low trading volume, and \nharm the return for the funds' shareholders.  She decides to \nengage in a series of trades between Draco's funds to increase \nWTech's liquidity and visibility and thus attract the interest of \nother market participants.  As a result, WTech's liquidity \nincreases, giving the funds a better selling opportunity and \nbenefitting the shareholders.  Gobert does not own WTech\nstock and does not personally profit from the trades.\nThe trading desk at Draco uses several brokers to execute \ntrades for the firm's funds.  Gobert has asked the desk to direct \na substantial portion of the WTech trades to XBrokers and \nZTrading, two small brokers with whom she has negotiated \nreferral arrangements: in exchange for receiving a significant \nportion of Draco's business, XBrokers recommends the firm's \nadvisory services to its wealthiest clients, while ZTrading\nprovides research reports to the firm.  Gobert and her team \nconsider ZTrading's reports extremely valuable and use them \nconsistently to select stocks for the funds.  The trading desk \nbelieves that trade execution through XBrokers is reliable but \nexpensive and that ZTrading has competitive prices and high-\nquality service.  Gobert did not disclose the arrangements to \nher superiors at Draco or her clients.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nGobert, who passed the three CFA exams between 19X6 \nand 19X8, encouraged Terence Lakatos, a portfolio manager \nwho reports to her, to obtain the CFA designation.  Lakatos \nis currently registered and enrolled to sit for the Level II CFA \nexam.  He is also responsible for the monthly market \noutlook newsletter Draco distributes to its clients.  The \nnewsletter includes bios of the portfolio management team \nand presents the following statements:\nStatement 1: Susan Gobert, CFA, has passed the three CFA \nexams in three consecutive years.  She believes that the CFA \ndesignation enhanced her credibility and improved her \nknowledge in the field of investment management.\nStatement 2: Terence Lakatos has passed Level I of the CFA \nProgram.  He enrolled in the CFA Program to gain the most \nrespected credential in the worldwide investment \nprofession.\nGobert recently became aware that Draco's written \ncompliance procedures restrict certain trades for its mutual \nfunds but not for its separately managed accounts (SMAs).  \nShe warned the firm's compliance officer, senior managers, \nand her team about the deficiency.\nDraco's internal research team is finishing a detailed \nanalysis of Hypersales, an e-commerce company that \nspecializes in discount clothing.  Gobert tells Lakatos that \nthe stock seems to be a good opportunity, but they should \nwait for the analysis to be completed before deciding \nwhether to acquire the stock.  Consulting the compliance \nmanual, Lakatos notices that it has not been updated by the \ncompliance officer, restricting the trade of Hypersales for \nmutual funds but not for SMAs; immediately, he buys \nHypersales stocks for some of the SMAs.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c10_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c10_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Paul Wentworth, CFA, is an independent investment adviser on \ninvestment strategies, but he does not sell any investment products.  \nWentworth also hosts a live, widely viewed weekly television show.  \nDuring each episode, he offers insights on investing and markets, and \nusually makes investment recommendations.  At the end of each \nepisode, a disclaimer is shown, stating: \"Any recommendations made \nduring this show are the host's own opinions.  Viewers should do their \nown research or consult with professional investment advisers before \nacting on any recommendation.\"\nSources that Wentworth uses to compile his research include:\nNotes written when interviewing company personnel\nTwitter posts\nText messages between himself and other advisers and analysts\nHistorical price feeds\nA popular segment of the show is \"Pick of the Week,\" during which \nWentworth highlights one stock, discusses relevant details, and \nrecommends whether to buy or sell it.  Many of Wentworth's advisory \nclients tell him they regularly watch his show and often follow his \"Pick \nof the Week\" recommendations.  Frequently, stocks that he highlights in \nthis segment experience significant short-term price movements based \non his recommendations.\nRecently, Wentworth recommended selling Clydesdale Tires in the \"Pick \nof the Week\" segment.  Wentworth does not presently own Clydesdale \nstock and would like to buy some shares, but he believes Clydesdale is \novervalued at its current price of $55 per share.\nWentworth based his Clydesdale recommendation on the following:\nAn interview with an employee in Clydesdale's purchasing department, \nwho told Wentworth that Clydesdale is producing fewer tires this year \nand buying smaller quantities of raw materials\nInterviews with some of Clydesdale's suppliers, which confirmed the \nreduced purchases\nResearch on industry trends indicating that demand for tires is lower \nthan in previous years\nAn individual in a restaurant saying that Clydesdale's earnings will be \nmuch lower than expected.  Wentworth overheard this, but he does not \nknow the individual and cannot confirm the statement with sources \nwithin Clydesdale.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nBased on his research, Wentworth thought Clydesdale's \nyear-end earnings would be significantly below market \nconsensus.  During the segment, Wentworth included \ndetails of the research, adding, \"I think earnings will be \nweak.\"  He also disclosed that he did not own any \nClydesdale stock, without mentioning that he would buy \nsome of that stock if the price were lower.  Based on his \nrecommendation, many viewers sell Clydesdale, causing the \nprice to fall.  Two days later, Wentworth buys 1,000 shares \nat a price of $40 per share.  A few weeks later, Clydesdale \nreleases its earnings report.  While Wentworth was correct \nthat earnings were weaker than expected, Clydesdale’s \nstock price remained around $40 per share and began to \nrise.\nCarson Puig, CFA, and Leandra Canas, CFA, are comanagers \nof the Extra Alpha Fund, which owns a sizeable position in \nClydesdale.  They had a target price of $60 for Clydesdale.  \nPuig saw Wentworth's show that included the Clydesdale \n\"sell\" recommendation.\nThe morning after the show, Puig contacted a source in \nClydesdale's investor relations department, who told him \nthat the company's year-end earnings would be a \n\"surprise.\"  Puig then made plans to sell the fund's \nClydesdale position and wrote a brief report that would \nadvise the fund's investors as to the reason for this \ndecision.  The report included Wentworth's rationale for \nthe \"sell\" recommendation, as well as what Puig's source \ntold him, but with no acknowledgment that Wentworth's \nshow was the basis for much of the report's content.  \nBefore releasing the report, Puig showed it to Canas, who \nhad not seen Wentworth's program.\nCanas concurred with Puig's decision to sell and added to \nPuig's report a portion of a government study indicating \nreduced demand for tires.  This information was not subject \nto copyright protection, but Canas did not indicate the \ngovernment source in the report.  Puig and Canas sold the \nfund's Clydesdale stock and distributed the report in an \nemail to the fund's investors.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Henry Bullard, CFA, is a well-respected sell-side analyst \ncovering the metals and mining industry at Denali Capital \nMarkets, a publicly traded investment bank.  He is currently \nreviewing his model and recommendation for GoldTech Mining, \na precious metals miner, with his associate, Marjorie Congdon, \nCFA.  GoldTech recently reported Q2 earnings and provided \nguidance that was below Bullard's estimates, causing its stock \nto decline 10%.\nBullard sets up a call with Emilia Schiavo, CFA, the CFO of \nGoldTech, with whom Bullard has developed a close \nrelationship over the years.  On the call, he asks questions \nabout the company's health and its competitive positioning.  At \nthe end of the call, Schiavo says \"I hope you're planning to \nmaintain our buy rating.  I would hate for you to lose your \nability to ask questions on our future earnings calls.\"\nAfter completing the call and updating their research on \nGoldTech, Bullard and Congdon determine that GoldTech is \novervalued based on its current price and believe a sell rating is \nwarranted.  However, Bullard believes the relationship with \nSchiavo provides valuable insights to his research, which he \nbelieves is in the best long-term interest of Denali's clients.  He \npublishes a new research report, downgrading the stock to a \nhold rating to maintain his relationship with Schiavo, but also \nincludes discussion of the additional risk factors that could \naffect the company.\nAfter Bullard publishes the report, GoldTech's stock increases \n3%.  Congdon has noticed that when Bullard issues reports on \ncertain companies, it can cause those companies' stocks to \nmove.  Congdon asks Bullard if Denali's policy to send the \nresearch only to clients and not the general public causes an \nissue with material nonpublic information (MNI) since the \nreport itself is material but its dissemination is nonpublic.  \nBullard notes that the dissemination is based on Denali's \npolicies and that all clients receive the research report at the \nsame time.\nBullard is currently the program chair for the local CFA society.  \nHe often arranges for speakers to present at the society's \nmonthly meetings.  Schiavo asks if Bullard would consider \nhosting her as a speaker for an event to increase GoldTech's\nexposure to potential investors.  In exchange for being allowed \nto speak to the society, Schiavo tells Bullard that she will attend \ngroup meetings with Denali's clients, which she has not done in \nthe past.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nDenali recently hired Eric Kim, CFA, as the new compliance \nofficer for the research group.  After reviewing the \ncompany's current procedures, Kim makes the following \nsuggestions to decrease the chances of analysts acting on \nMNI:\nSuggestion 1: Allowing investment banking analysts to \nreview the research team's reports before dissemination to \nensure MNI is not being used.\nSuggestion 2: Physically separating the research and \ninvestment banking teams on different floors of the \nbuilding to reduce the chances of unintended \ncommunication.\nSuggestion 3: Eliminating the watch list and more \nfrequently updating the restricted list, which will be more \neffective to stop trading on MNI.\nIn discussing the new rules with Denali's analysts, Kim \nspeaks broadly about ethical activity, stating:\nStatement 1: Since different regions have different views \non ethical behavior, when meeting with clients, analysts \nshould consider how their processes might affect this \ncountry's financial market only in the context of their \nethical obligations.\nStatement 2: At the end of the day, Denali is in business to \nmaximize value to shareholders at all costs, so we should be \nrevamping the compensation structure to reflect that while \npromising to continue acting ethically.\nKim oversees the monitoring of personal trading activity for \nDenali's research analysts.  He has confirmed that Denali's \ncompliance procedures are adequate to catch wrongdoing.  \nAfter two months at Denali, Kim discovers that one of the \ncompany's analysts made a trade that violated company \npolicy.  After learning of the wrongdoing, Kim does an in-\ndepth review of the analyst's trades to discover the extent \nof the wrongdoing and warns the analyst not to do this \nagain; the analyst agrees.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c12_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c12_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Grace Jones and David Arcos are junior analysts at Chestnut \nWealth Management, a retail client advisory firm.  Jones and \nArcos are both Level I candidates in the CFA Program and have \nbeen studying together; they were even able to schedule their \nexams for the same time, day, and location.\nDuring the exam, a software glitch resulted in answer choices \nnot displaying correctly for Arcos.  The other candidates were \nnot affected.  After Arcos notified a proctor, the proctor paused \nArcos's exam.  Arcos then became extremely frustrated and \nbegan shouting at the proctor to cancel the exam so that he \ncould reschedule.  Arcos's outburst was unsettling and \ndistracting to the other exam takers.  After a 30-minute delay, \nthe issue was corrected, and Arcos resumed the exam.\nAfter the exam, Arcos posted on an internet forum that caters \nspecifically to CFA Program Candidates:\n\"I wanted to warn the CFA Candidate community about a very \nfrustrating software glitch that occurred when I was taking the \nLevel I CFA exam this morning.  Just be prepared.\"\nOn another social media forum that focuses on stock investing, \nArcos touts the investment prospects of Orogeny Mining Corp. \n(OMC), a micro-cap stock.  Until recently, OMC was a gold \nmining exploration company.  A short time ago, OMC \nabandoned its conventional mineral-exploration program and \nstarted mining cryptocurrencies.  Arcos owns 100 shares of \nOMC, which are currently trading at $1.00 per share.  He has \nnot reported his ownership of OMC to Chestnut because his \nposition is small, and the stock is so speculative that the firm \nwould never buy it for clients.\nArcos knows little about the company and has done no due \ndiligence, but states on the social media platform:\n\"The word on the street is OMC is in discussions to be acquired \nby another cryptocurrency mining firm!  I can't say which one, \nbut it's a household name and one of the biggest in the \nindustry!  OMC is going higher: be long or be wrong!!!\"\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nThe next morning in the office break room, Jones, Arcos, \nand Shelia Smith discuss how stressful the exam experience \nwas.  Smith is one of the firm's advisers and not a Member \nor Candidate.  During the conversation, Jones asks Arcos\nwhat he thought of the accrued interest calculation \nquestion in the second session.  Arcos states that he was so \nupset by the whole testing experience that he does not \neven remember that question.\nLater that day, Smith is running late for a meeting with a \nnew client.  Smith had initially met the client two weeks \nbefore and this follow-up meeting is to present an \ninvestment policy statement and recommended \ninvestments to the client.  At the last minute, Smith asks \nJones to fill in for her for just a few minutes to not keep the \nclient waiting.  As an analyst, Jones does not normally meet \nwith clients and has not been briefed on the details of the \nprior meeting.\nJones immediately begins the conversation by suggesting \nthe client consider the firm's option overlay strategy, which \nis a covered call strategy on a popular equity index.  Jones \nstates that the strategy has generated better risk-adjusted \nreturns than the index over the past three years.  Jones \ncontinues by stating the strategy is very popular with clients \nas an alternative to their conventional equity allocation.  \nSmith arrives at this point in the conversation and Jones \nleaves the meeting.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Abdul Salim, CFA, is the head of institutional fixed-income \ntrading at Gold Capital Group (GCG), a sell-side brokerage firm.  \nSalim oversees a team of twelve traders; several of whom are \nCFA charterholders.  The trading desk facilitates orders for large \ninstitutional clients such as mutual funds, hedge funds, and \nother professional investors.\nThe macroeconomic environment has deteriorated in recent \nmonths as the central bank has raised its policy rate several \ntimes, the economy has shown signs of slowing, and the \ninflation rate has increased.  Real estate activity has also \nslowed significantly, with residential home sales down 10% \nyear-over-year.\nSalim speaks to one of his regular buy-side clients, who \nmentions that he has just moved into a new home but has not \nyet sold his old house.  The client is concerned that the \ndeteriorating market conditions may result in a lower listing \nprice for the old house.  In passing, Salim suggests the client \ncall his cousin, Shira, a real estate agent, saying that Shira could \nhelp the client get his house listed before the market \ndeteriorates further.\nGiven the macroeconomic conditions, equity markets have also \nfallen sharply.  Salim was recently subject to a margin call in his \npersonal account that he could not meet.  Salim was able to \nsecure a personal loan of €100,000 from a longtime client to \ncover the margin call.  Although borrowing from a client \nviolates local law, it is rarely enforced if the loan is repaid \nquickly.  Salim repaid the loan less than one month later, and \nno one else was even aware the loan took place.\nGCG's offices are currently undergoing renovations, \nnecessitating that some groups share office space.  In \nparticular, GCG's proprietary trading desk, which trades \nexclusively for the benefit of the firm, has temporarily \nrelocated to the main trading floor, where client transactions \nare carried out.  The groups are now in close proximity to one \nanother and can often hear each other's conversations and \ninadvertently see each other's screens.\nFirm management has decided that this temporary trading \nsituation is acceptable since the firm has a policy of not \nallowing the proprietary traders to act on any client \ninformation.  Both groups of traders agree that the situation is \nnot ideal but understand that it is temporary, so they go along \nwith the arrangement.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nSalim takes a call from a client who wants to buy a large \nblock of distressed B-rated corporate bonds that is owned \nby another broker-dealer, Apex Brokerage.  The client does \nnot have a trading relationship with Apex and needs Salim \nto facilitate the trade.  Even though Salim's client is not \nnormally a buyer of below-investment-grade securities, \nSalim executes the order for the client, buying at the offer \nprice and charging the minimum commission to execute the \ntrade.\nSalim purchased a large block of AA-rated corporate bonds \na few days ago and has been unable to find buyers at his \nquoted price.  Salim provides copies of the most recent \ncredit rating agency (CRA) research on this bond issue to all \nthe fixed income sales staff.  The CRA research confirms the \nstrong financial position of the firm and the excellent credit \nprotections specific to this issue.  Salim instructs the sales \nstaff to aggressively promote these bonds to clients \nbecause the firm needs to free up capital for other \npurposes.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Carlos Aguirre, CFA, is a sell-side analyst at Strike Brokers, a US-\nbased brokerage firm.  Aguirre covers the technology industry \nand provides recommendations on stocks for Strike's clients.  \nAguirre is currently analyzing the prospects of two US \ncompanies: Blue Point Technology (BPT) and New Edge \nSemiconductors (NES).\nAguirre expects BPT to announce an expansion of its operations \nto Latin America, which would give a substantial boost to the \ncompany's earnings given BPT's proprietary, state-of-the-art \ntechnology.  After analyzing the company's public filings and \ndoing his own extensive research, Aguirre is confident that this \nannouncement will occur soon.  He estimates the stock price \ntarget using his financial models and quantitative skills, and he \nwrites in his research report: \"In the next weeks, BPT will \nannounce the expansion of its operations to Latin America.  \nThis will have substantial positive effects on the company's \nfuture earnings.  We recommend buying BPT stock with a \ntarget price of USD 115.\"\nAfter studying NES and modeling information compiled from \nmultiple sources, Aguirre is preparing a report with a \"buy\" \nrecommendation.  However, just before finishing the report, he \nnotices a post from a respected financial analyst on an analyst \ninternet forum open only to subscribers.  The post says, \"NES \nmay look like a solid company, but I expect its stock to suffer a \nlot in the next quarter.\"  Aguirre knows that the analyst is very \ndiligent in his research, so he decides to change his \nrecommendation to a \"hold\" and releases the report to clients.\nAs a result of his strong track record, Aguirre receives—and \naccepts—a job offer from Whitaker Asset Management (WAM) \nto cover the technology sector as a buy-side research analyst.  \nBefore leaving Strike, he downloads a large database containing \npublic data from all equity offerings in the last twenty years in \nthe US.  Aguirre created and updated this database during his \ntenure with Strike, without the assistance of any other \nemployee, so he does not ask for his employer's consent.\nAt WAM, Aguirre analyzes another stock, Intrepid Software.  \nAfter thorough research, he prepares a presentation for the \nweekly meeting of the investment committee.  Aguirre \nrecommends buying the stock, and the committee decides in \nfavor of allocating Intrepid to the accounts for which the stock \nis suitable.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nLeticia Flores, CFA, a portfolio manager at WAM who was \npresent at the meeting, believes this would be a good \nopportunity for some of her clients.  After carefully \nreviewing Aguirre's analysis and her clients' investment \npolicy statements, Flores concludes that the stock would be \nsuitable to 20 of her 50 clients.  Since she does not have \ndiscretionary authority over her clients' accounts, she sends \nan email to the 20 clients, explaining the investment \nrecommendation.  Then, she conducts individual \nvideoconferences with her five major institutional clients to \nfurther discuss the recommendation.\nOne of these videoconferences is with Ben Gafford, a \nmember of the board of trustees of the Solstice Hospital \nEndowment Fund.  This fund has been a client for ten years, \nand the board of trustees has provided Flores with \nconfidential information from the fund, including planned \ninvestments in its main charitable projects.  Gafford is very \nsatisfied with Flores' recent recommendations.  During the \nvideoconference, on behalf of the board of trustees, he \nproposes to Flores a one-week trip to a ski resort with all \nexpenses paid for every year that the fund's portfolio beats \nits benchmark by at least 200 basis points.  Immediately, \nFlores calls her employer—who agrees with the proposal—\nand then Flores tells Gafford she accepts the offer.\nA few days later, Flores receives a call from James Conley, \none of her private wealth clients.  Conley tells Flores, \"I am \nconsidering donating to the Solstice Hospital Endowment \nFund.  Gafford told me recently that you also manage the \nfund's portfolio and that you were aware of the fund's \nplanned investments in charitable projects.  I have been \ncalling Gafford to get details on these investments, but I \nwasn't able to contact him.  I would like to decide as soon \nas possible, so I need you to send that information to me.\"  \nFlores knows that any donation would be greatly beneficial \nto the endowment fund; she sends Conley the requested \ninformation and immediately notifies Gafford.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c15_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c15_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Larry Fitzpatrick, CFA, is the chief investment officer at \nFoursquare Capital Management.  Foursquare primarily \noffers investment services to pensions, endowments, and \nother large institutional accounts.\nNicole Rappaport, CFA, is the chief investment officer for \nthe endowment of a large children's hospital.  Rappaport is \nevaluating the investment merits of the Foursquare \nBalanced Fund (FSBF), which invests 60% in stocks and 40% \nin bonds.  Foursquare also offers the same strategy as a \nseparately managed account (SMA) with the same level of \nservice but a higher fee.  Rappaport is primarily interested \nin the SMA option since the endowment could retain \nbeneficial ownership of individual securities and benefit \nfrom greater transparency with regard to specific \ninvestments.\nFitzpatrick and Rappaport are scheduled to meet in a few \ndays to discuss the possibility of an investment in the FSBF \nstrategy.  Rappaport is preparing for the meeting and is \nreviewing investment and legal documentation provided by \nFoursquare.\nRappaport identifies three specific policy areas that require \nfurther clarification from Fitzpatrick, as presented in Exhibit \n1:\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nAs Fitzpatrick prepares for the meeting, he is concerned \nabout the FSBF's most recent quarterly performance.  \nFoursquare has a reputation as a bottom-up manager, and \nduring the most recent quarter, one of the portfolio's \nlargest technology stock positions suffered a 40% decline.  \nNormally, fund performance attribution is presented at the \nsector level, but the loss was so large in this one position \nthat it negatively skewed the stock selection attribution for \nnot only the technology sector, but the entire fund.\nIn an effort to explain what occurred during the quarter, \nFitzpatrick omits the sector level performance attribution \nfrom the standardized client report and instead uses an \nindustry level attribution to better isolate the problem \nposition.  Instead of 10 sectors, the report now identifies 88 \nindustry groups.  Fitzpatrick believes this added level of \ndisclosure will help clarify that the occurrence was an \noutlier event and therefore should not overshadow the \notherwise strong stock selection in the fund.  He presents \nthis information to Rappaport in their meeting.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_ethics", "source_label": "UWorld – Ethics", "topic": "Ethics", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Sienna Rossi, CFA, is a portfolio manager at Mela Advisors, a \nbuy-side investment manager.  Rossi is the portfolio manager of \nthe Mela European Equity Fund, which invests exclusively in \nlarge-cap European equities.\nThe fund has two separate share classes: the A-class shares, \nwhich are marketed to retail investors; and the ADV-class \nshares, which are marketed to financial advisers.  The funds are \nidentical except for the minimum investment required and the \nfees that are charged; the A-class shares charge a higher fee.\nRossi is invited to be the keynote speaker at a popular \ninvestment conference.  During Rossi's presentation, she makes \nthe following comments:\nComment 1:\nI believe the Italian markets are undervalued by \n20%; Italian equities thus represent the largest country \noverweight in the fund. \nComment 2:\nMy favorite idea right now is Banco Pompeii; I \nthink it is a buy at the current price.\nComment 3:\nI believe everyone in this room should consider \nowning the Mela European Equity Fund.\nMela is in the process of hiring a new equity analyst.  Today, \nRossi is interviewing Stephen Brewer, a candidate for the \nposition.  At their meeting, Rossi asks Brewer about his \nqualifications.  Brewer responds by stating that he was recently \na summer intern at a large investment bank and that:\nStatement 1:\nI passed Level I of the CFA Program on the first \ntry, and\nStatement 2:\nI am a CFA, Level II Candidate.\nRossi asks Brewer when he is taking the next CFA exam.  Brewer \nresponds by stating that he passed Level I two years ago and \nhas been very busy since.  As such, he has not yet registered for \nLevel II but plans to do so in the near future.\nAfter the interview, Rossi meets with Wei Tan, CFA, a third-\nparty investment consultant conducting due diligence on the \nMela European Equity Fund.  Tan's firm is very influential in \nsecuring investment allocations to pensions and endowments.  \nIf the fund is approved and placed in the consultant's model \nportfolio, assets under management for the Mela European \nEquity Fund would likely double.\nTan notes that he has already completed a detailed review of \nthe fund's published return information and has been \nimpressed by the amount and quality of the information.  Tan \nhas also reviewed the fund's publicly stated objectives and \nstrategies but asks Rossi to provide more in-depth information \nregarding specifics about the adviser's investment process \nduring their discussion.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet\n\nAfter more than an hour-long discussion, Rossi is not \nconfident that Tan is sufficiently impressed.  Rossi needs to \nleave for a client meeting and suggests they finish the \ndiscussion over dinner at the Prestige Club, an exclusive \ncountry club where she is a member.  Tan eagerly accepts, \nas he has never been to the Prestige Club; he believes \nbecoming a member would be a great way to meet new \nclients.\nBack at Tan's office that afternoon, Guo Ming, CFA, an \nanalyst who works with Tan, asks Tan for additional \ndocumentation related to the Mela due diligence, including \nMela's:\nItem 1:Code of ethics,\nItem 2:Compliance policies and procedures, and\nItem 3:Internal control procedures.\nTan responds by stating that he did not review those items, \nbut that Rossi is a CFA charterholder and he (Tan) is \nconfident that she will hold the Mela organization to the \nhigh standard of care required by the Code and Standards.\nRossi and Tan continue their discussion over dinner at the \nPrestige Club.  As they finish, Rossi senses Tan's interest in \njoining the club and suggests that she could recommend \nTan to the membership committee.  As Rossi is about to pay \nfor the meal, Tan insists they split the cost, citing his firm's \npolicy.\nThe next day, Rossi sends Tan the Prestige Club membership \napplication, which includes her letter of recommendation \nto the club's membership committee.  Rossi expenses the \ncost of the dinner and pays for it with soft dollars.  A few \nweeks later, Tan informs Rossi that the Mela European \nEquity Fund has been added to Tan's firm's model \nportfolios.  A month later Tan is accepted as a new member \nof the Prestige Club.\nEthics And Professional Standard –Question Sheet", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c17_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_ethics_c17_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Sergio Reyes is an economist at Cuadrado Capital studying \nhow international parity conditions have held in certain \ncountries.  He is speaking to Andrea Gómez, a senior \ncurrency trader at Cuadrado.  Reyes tells Gómez:\nStatement 1: Forward rate parity states that the forward \nexchange rate will be an accurate forecast of the future \nspot exchange rate.\nStatement 2\nThe international Fisher effect states that the \nnominal yield spread between two markets is determined \nsolely by their expected inflation differential.\nStatement 3\nRelative purchasing power parity states that \nthe notional exchange rate between two markets is the \nratio of their broad price indexes.\nGómez asks Reyes what international parity conditions \nmean for expected spot exchange rates.  Reyes explains \nthat if all key international parity conditions held, the \nexpected spot exchange rate would track several measures \nbetween two countries.  He continues that if certain parity \nconditions held, there would be no difference between the \nreal interest rates of the two countries.\nReyes notes that uncovered interest rate parity between \nMexico and the United States has held in recent years.  He \nprovides Gómez with the following information:\nReyes mentions that international parity conditions do not \nalways hold, especially over the short term.  This allows \nCuadrado to exploit certain trades, such as the carry trade.  \nHe notes that a good carry trade opportunity is to borrow \nwith EUR to purchase BRL.  Gómez compiles the following \ninformation and expectations on the currencies:\n\nGómez asks Reyes if there is anything she should know \nabout the distribution of returns for carry trades.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Rana Al-Qahtani, a senior economist for Tigris Bank, is \ndiscussing the prospects for several foreign markets with \nAmin Saleem, a newly hired junior economist.  Al-Qahtani \nexplains to Saleem the difference between capital \ndeepening and technological advances in an economy, in \nthe context of neoclassical theory.  Saleem asks how \ntechnological advances affect the per capita production \nfunction, which leads to a discussion of economic growth \nmodels.\nAl-Qahtani and Saleem then discuss Country 1, a developed \ncountry with a large amount of capital per worker \ncompared with its neighbors.  Country 1 plans to continue \ninvesting in capital projects to improve its growth.  It has \nidentified three areas of investment focus:\nInvestment 1: Highways and transportation equipment\nInvestment 2: Machinery and nonresidential construction\nInvestment 3: Computers and telecommunications \nequipment\nCountry 2 is a developing country whose growth rate has \nslowed during the last two years.  Al-Qahtani asks Saleem \nto calculate the potential GDP growth rate for Country 2.  \nSaleem compiles the following information:\nSaleem notes that Country 2's GDP is growing faster than \nCountry 1's GDP, a trend that is consistent with \nconvergence theory.  However, he asks Al-Qahtani why \nCountry 3, another developing country, is growing slower\n\nthan Country 1 although it has characteristics similar to \nCountry 2.  Al-Qahtani replies that Country 3, although \nsimilar overall to Country 2, does not enforce property \nrights and will catch up with richer countries only when it \ndoes so.\nSaleem then asks Al-Qahtani about how different economic \nmodels affect convergence and other topics.  Al-Qahtani \nmakes the following statements:\nStatement 1: The endogenous theory asserts that all \ncountries' per capita GDPs should converge, but the \nneoclassical model suggests that divergences can persist \nindefinitely.\nStatement 2: Unlike the other models, the classical model \npredicts that an increase in a country's savings rate will \nresult in a greater permanent rate of per capita GDP \ngrowth.\nStatement 3: The neoclassical model assumes diminishing \nreturns on capital; the endogenous growth model assumes \na straight-line production function.\nAl-Qahtani also notes that two main criticisms of the \nneoclassical model by economists have resulted in \nexpansions of the model, as well as new theories.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Aulia Suryani is the head of the domestic equity research \nteam at FKY Capital.  She is especially interested in \nunderstanding the impact of regulation in three industries: \nchemical, financial, and software.\nThe chemical industry is dominated by a small number of \ncompanies.  The capital expenditure requirements pose \nhigh barriers to entry, allowing incumbents to maintain high \nprices and margins.  Chemical manufacturing processes \nresult in significant amounts of toxic waste.  Regulatory \nauthorities are considering new rules for the chemical \nindustry, and Suryani is evaluating the potential impact on \nthe companies.\nThe financial industry is heavily regulated.  Domestic banks \nand other financial institutions are required to diversify \nassets, prepare recovery plans, and keep funds in reserve as \ninsurance against losses.  In addition, these institutions \ncomply with the rules and standards of the industry's self-\nregulating organization (SRO).\nThe software industry is currently undergoing a significant \ntransformation:\nTwo major competitors recently announced their intention \nto merge, and the federal antitrust agency analyzed the \ncase.  The agency concluded that the merger may \nsubstantially reduce competition and suggested some \nremedies to resolve this issue.\nA neighboring country recently reduced its financial \ndisclosure requirements and employees' statutory benefits, \naiming to attract foreign companies to its jurisdiction.  As a \nresult, some software companies are considering making \nthis country their corporate domicile.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Vadim Sederko and Isabel Moray are trainees with Forex \nGlobal Partners.  The company actively participates in the \ninterbank foreign exchange market and also trades with \nindividuals and corporate clients.  Exhibit 1 shows the \nintermarket bid-offer spreads for selected currency pairs:\nSederko and Moray are discussing factors that may lead to \nchanges in the bid-offer spreads.  Sederko offers the \nfollowing as reasons why the JPY/USD spread would \nnarrow:\nReason 1:\n\"The JPY/USD spread will narrow when the \nTokyo market is open, since the New York and London \nmarkets will be closed and the yen is Japan's domestic \ncurrency.\"\nReason 2:\n\"For large orders where investors want to \nreceive JPY in exchange for USD, the spread will narrow \nsince the large orders demonstrate a lot of demand for JPY.\"\nForex Global also trades in the forward currency markets.  \nThirty days ago, Sederko bought USD 5 million in exchange \nfor SGD with delivery in 90 days at the 90-day forward rate \nof SGD/USD 1.3250–1.3350.  He observes that today's \nSGD/USD spot rate is 1.3450–1.3650, and that the forward \npoints and risk-free rates for both currencies are as follows:\n\nSederko believes that covered interest rate parity exists \nbetween the USD and GBP.  He compiles the following \ninformation:\nSederko and Moray then discuss aspects of interest rate \nparity:\nSederko: \"For uncovered interest rate parity to hold, \ninvestors are assumed to be risk neutral.\"\nMoray:\"If uncovered interest rate parity holds, the current \nspot rate is the best predictor of the future spot rate.“\nRemember to put first question", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Botan Saito is a portfolio manager for Spire Financial.  \nSpire's investments are largely domestic, and Saito plans to \ndiversify Spire's holdings by adding investments in \ninternational markets.  Saito identifies three countries that \nhe will research further.  He collects the following economic \ndata for each country, using average year-on-year growth \nrates:\nDuring his research, Saito realizes that numbers are not \nfully explaining the economic differences in these countries.  \nHe further researches institutional economic information, \nshown in Exhibit 2:\n\nCountry 2 is planning to implement new policies that create \na more open trade policy.  Saito expects that this will result \nin a higher permanent steady-state GDP growth rate.\nSaito is interested in how economic factors will ultimately \nimpact each country's stock market.  He notes that Country \n3's:\nGDP growth is slightly above the country's steady-state GDP \ngrowth rate in 20X4;\nearnings-to-GDP ratio was above the country's historical \naverage in 20X4; and\nprice-to-earnings ratio of the country's publicly traded \nstocks was below its historical average in 20X4.\nSaito is also interested in how potential GDP will affect each \ncountry's bonds.  He arrives at the following conclusions:\nConclusion 1\nActual GDP growth relative to a country's \npotential GDP growth rate is an important factor in central \nbank decision-making and the likelihood of changes to the \ncentral bank's policies.\nConclusion 2\nA decrease in a country's potential GDP \ngrowth rate increases the likelihood that the credit ratings \non its sovereign debt will be downgraded.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Ishita Rani is a currency trader at Inspire Bank discussing \nexchange rates with a newly hired junior trader, Raj Pawar.  \nRani asks Pawar about the balance of payment accounts and its \nimpact on exchange rates.  Pawar replies that the accounts can \nhave different effects in the short term and long term.  He \nelaborates by stating that over the long term, the current \naccount affects exchange rates through three channels:\nChannel 1:A current account deficit will result in decreased \nrelative demand for that country's currency, leading to \ncurrency depreciation.\nChannel 2:Countries with current account surpluses may \nrebalance their unbalanced foreign currency portfolios, \npositively impacting the foreign currencies.\nChannel 3:Investors in countries running persistent current \naccount deficits will revise their exchange rate expectations \nupward until the country stabilizes its debt-to-GDP ratio.\nRani also wants to gauge Pawar's knowledge of how monetary \nand fiscal policies can affect exchange rates.  She tells Pawar \nthat Country X, an emerging market, has recently instituted \nmore expansionary monetary and fiscal policies.  She adds that \nCountry X has had high capital mobility in recent years.\nPawar remembers reading about a currency crisis that \nhappened several years ago in Country X.  Rani states that \nalthough not all currency crises show all the expected warning \nsigns, the crisis in Country X did in fact show some warning \nsigns that investors ignored at the time.\nPawar notes that some governments have taken steps to \ncontrol their exchange rates.  He asks Rani if countries can \nactually influence their exchange rates, and if so, whether \ncountries with developed or developing economies would be \nmore effective in doing so.\nRani also wants to test Pawar's skills at valuing currency-based \ntransactions.  She tells Pawar that 3 months ago, Inspire \ninitiated a forward contract to purchase NOK 7.5 million in 6 \nmonths.  At the initiation of the contract, the 6-month forward \nexchange rate was INR/NOK 8.0000.  She gives Pawar the \ncurrent exchange rate information for the two currencies and \nasks him to mark the contract to market:\n\nRani notes that the INR risk-free rate is 2.5% and that \nInspire uses a 360-day count convention.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "The chief investment officer (CIO) of New Horizon Global \nInvestments (New Horizon) wants to understand how \nexisting and new regulations may affect potential \ninvestments in a country covered by New Horizon's equity \nresearch team.  The CIO is particularly interested in the \nmining and financial sectors and meets with Olivier Bodin\nand Sofia Esposito, equity analysts at New Horizon.\nBodin explains that several international mining companies \nintend to invest in the country but have expressed concern \nregarding the legal protection of the substantial long-term \ninvestments required.  Local government is planning new \nlaws and enforcement procedures to improve its legal \nenvironment, encouraging companies to commit to such \ninvestments.\nBodin adds that the local regulator, a government agency, is \nconcerned about the pollution generated by iron ore \nprocessing activities.  The agency proposed a new \nregulation, imposing a limit on industry pollution, \ndistributing tradable emission rights to mining companies.  \nThe agency is currently investigating the costs and possible \nunintended consequences of the proposed regulation as \npart of its cost-benefit analysis.  The CIO requests more \ninformation about this process, and Bodin explains that:\nStatement 1: Implementation costs relate to changes in \neconomic decisions and behaviors.\nStatement 2: Unintended consequences may influence \nboth implementation costs and indirect costs.\nStatement 3: The cost-benefit analysis is limited to the \nfirms and industries directly affected by the proposed \nregulation.\n\nAfterward, Esposito speaks about the country's financial \nsector.  She explains that its main regulatory body, the \nFinancial Oversight Authority (FOA), was created by statute \nas an autonomous agency with rulemaking and \nenforcement powers but is not funded by the government.  \nIn addition, the Association of Commercial Banks (ACB), a \nprivate organization, establishes and enforces rules and \nstandards in the financial industry.  ACB does not derive its \nauthority from legislation or from a government body, but \nexclusively from its members, who include most of the \ncountry's financial institutions.\nShe adds that the financial industry is highly concentrated, \nwith three major banks holding more than 70% of the \nassets of the financial system.  In the previous year, a small \nbank suffered a bank run after considerable trading losses, \nand FOA intervened by forcing creditors and depositors to \ncancel their claims in exchange for shares of the bank.\nAs a result, FOA is proposing stricter regulation of short \nsales and leveraged trading.  FOA's regulators must decide \nthe best approach to conduct a cost-benefit analysis: an \nafter-the-fact analysis or a regulatory sandbox approach.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Jerczy Walinski is a senior economist working for an \ninternational bank.  He is responsible for analyzing currency \nmarkets to support the bank's proprietary currency trading \nand the advisory services provided to bank clients.  Sonya \nSanchez is a newly hired junior economist assigned to assist \nWalinski.\nWalinski asks Sanchez to assess the forward value of JPY \ncompared with EUR using the exchange rate and interest \nrate data presented in Exhibit 1.\nThe two economists discuss the implications of the \ninternational parity relationships and their use in \nforecasting future exchange rates.  Walinski asks Sanchez to \nsummarize the key aspects of the international Fisher \nrelationship.  In response, Sanchez notes two important \nimplications of the international Fisher effect regarding the \nbehavior of key economic variables across countries:\nImplication 1: Real interest rates should converge on the \nsame level.\nImplication 2: Nominal interest rates should converge with \nexpected inflation rates.\nWalinski then asks Sanchez to evaluate the usefulness of \nthe interest rate parity relationships regarding exchange \nrates.  Sanchez makes two observations regarding currency \nmarket participants' views on the theories:\nObservation 1:Covered interest rate parity accurately \ndescribes the relationship of the spot and forward \nexchange rates between two currencies and the interest\n\nrates in those currencies.  Its validity is proven by the \narbitrage trading that occurs anytime the spot/forward \ndifference is inconsistent with interest rate difference.\nObservation 2:Uncovered interest rate parity does not \naccurately capture the relationship of interest rate \ndifferences between currencies and expected changes in \nspot exchange rates.  This is borne out by the frequent use \nof carry trades to earn higher returns by taking unhedged \npositions in high interest rate currencies.\nWalinski and Sanchez analyze the economic outlook for two \ncountries and their currency values relative to a global \nreserve currency.  One of the countries is a large developed \nmarket (DM), the other a small emerging market (EM).  \nBoth countries have recently begun pursuing expansionary \nmonetary policies to spur economic recoveries during a \nperiod of stagnant growth following a recent global \nslowdown.  Both countries are also raising taxes and cutting \ngovernment spending to prevent expansion of budget \ndeficits.  However, Walinski notes that capital mobility and \nother economic policies differ between the countries:\nDM has well-developed financial markets and places no \nrestriction on foreign ownership of domestic assets nor any \nrestrictions on capital inflows or outflows.  The country's \ntrade balance is usually at a small deficit relative to GDP.  \nDM uses a free-floating-rate currency regime, its currency is \nfully convertible, and the central bank generally does not \nintervene in currency markets.\nEM has much less developed financial markets, places \nrestrictions on foreign ownership of domestic assets, and \nrestricts both inflows and outflows of capital.  The country \nusually has a sizeable trade surplus, though the size of the \nsurplus has been very sensitive to the exchange value of its \ncurrency.  DM is both the largest market for EM's exports \nandthe largest source of its imports.  Until recently, EM had \na fixed-exchange-rate regime, maintaining a specified parity\n\nto DM's currency.  EM has recently introduced a crawling \npeg intending to manage the value of its currency relative \nto DM's in line with any difference in inflation between the \ntwo currencies.  DM is also considering easing restrictions \non capital inflows to enable increased foreign investment as \na tool to spur domestic growth.\nWalinski asks Sanchez to identify factors that may indicate \nan increased possibility of a currency crisis for EM.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c8_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c8_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Sylvia Yang manages a team of currency analysts for an \ninternational bank.  Abe Kim is applying for a position as a \njunior analyst on Yang's team.  During an interview, Yang \nasks Kim to discuss arbitrage pricing relationships and the \ninternational parity relationships that influence currency \nvalues.\nTo assess Kim's familiarity with the relationship between \nspot and forward exchange rates, Yang asks Kim to \ndetermine the forward discount or premium of MXN \nrelative to INR based on the information in Exhibit 1:\nYang then asks Kim to interpret the implications reflected in \nthe spot and forward rate quotes for CHF/EUR shown in \nExhibit 2:\nYang follows up by asking Kim to describe and evaluate \ninternational parity relationships.  During the discussion, \nKim makes the following statements regarding different \nversions of purchasing power parity (PPP):\nStatement 1: In the long run, currency exchange rates \ntend to move toward values implied by absolute PPP.\n\nStatement 2: Under relative PPP, spot exchange rate \nchanges are completely determined by differences in actual \ninflation, whereas under ex-ante PPP, expected spot \nexchange rate changes are based on differences in expected \ninflation.\nYang wants to assess Kim's understanding of arbitrage \npricing.  She shows him Currency Dealer Z's quotes (Exhibit \n3) and asks him to determine the potential arbitrage profit \nfrom investing EUR 1 million if Dealer X quotes NOK/EUR at \n9.6200/9.6530.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_economics", "source_label": "UWorld – Economics", "topic": "Economics", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Lucas Evans is an independent economic consultant.  Two \neconomic ministers from different developing countries \nhave asked to meet with him to discuss their countries' \neconomic growth prospects.  Evans first meets with Lena \nKowalczyk of Country 1.\nCountry 1 has significant natural resources.  Despite an \nincrease in exports of its natural resources and the \ninstallation of economic institutions necessary for growth, \nCountry 1's GDP growth has been sluggish.  After reviewing \ndata about Country 1's economy, Evans suggests the slow \ngrowth stems from the Dutch disease, since that would \nexplain the following factors demonstrated by Country 1's \neconomy:\nFactor 1:\nPersistently strong currency growth leading \nto a weakening manufacturing sector\nFactor 2:\nAdditional regulations to disincentivize the \nentrance of new domestic competitors\nFactor 3:\nAccelerating population growth as per capita \nincome rises above subsistence income, causing labor \nproductivity to fall\nKowalczyk explains that Country 1's government is planning \nadditional reforms to improve growth and asks Evans to \nreview the options:\nOption 1:\nEncourage lenders to improve screening of \nthose seeking investment funding\nOption 2:\nLimit foreign investment so domestic \ninvestors receive capital gains\nOption 3:\nPromote greater domestic consumption in \nlieu of saving\nAfter reviewing the options, Evans points out that Country \n1's R&D spending is below that of its peers.  He suggests \nproviding subsidies and tax breaks for private companies to \nfund R&D investment.\n\nEvans then meets with Alejandro Guerrero of Country 2, \nwhich is a developing country with a low capital-per-worker \nratio.  Guerrero presents the following data about Country \n2:\nCountry 2 is planning to close its borders and restrict \nimmigration to improve the employment rate for its \ncitizens.  The country also plans to subsidize part-time labor \nfor companies to encourage the hiring of more workers \nwho do not have time for full-time work.\nEvans notes that Country 2 can also improve growth by \nadjusting its trade policies with other countries.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_economics_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c10_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_economics_c10_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Wang Chen is a swap trader for a large hedge fund.  Petra \nFreund manages a global macro fund for the same firm.  \nFreund wants to speculate on relative interest rate \nmovements in Japan and Australia and asks Chen to price a \n3-year, at-market, fixed-for-fixed, annual-payment, pay-JPY, \nreceive-AUD currency swap.  Selected data for JPY and AUD \ninterest rates, as well as discount factors for pricing the \nswap, are contained in Exhibit 1:\nFreund wants the swap structured so that the notional \namounts are exchanged at both initiation and expiration.  \nExhibit 2 contains selected data on the swap and JPY/AUD \nexchange rates at the swap's initiation:\nOne year ago, Freund entered into a pay-fixed, receive-\nfloating USD interest rate swap to speculate on \nexpectations of rising USD interest rates.  US interest rates \nhave increased, so she is considering taking profits on the \ntrade.  Freund asks Chen to estimate the value of the swap \nbased on the information contained in Exhibit 3:\n\nExhibit 4 shows USD interest rates for valuing the swap:\nFreund's fund also holds a quarterly-payment, receive-\nequity, pay-fixed equity swap based on the S&P 500 Index \ntotal return.  The swap had a maturity of 1 year when it was \ninitiated 3 months ago, and the first quarterly payment has \njust occurred.  Freund is uncertain about the method for \nvaluing equity swaps and proposes three valuation methods \nfor this swap:\nMethod 1:\nCalculate the difference between the total \nreturn over the last 3 months on the S&P index and the \ninterest accrued on the fixed-rate leg over the same period.\nMethod 2:\nCalculate the difference between the PVs of \nthe fixed-rate leg on the existing swap and the fixed-rate leg \nof an otherwise equivalent, current at-market, S&P 500-\nreturn-for-fixed-rate equity swap.\nMethod 3:\nCalculate the difference between the PV of \nthe equity leg, based on the expected total return on the \nS&P 500 Index, and the PV of the swap's fixed-rate leg, \nbased on the coupon set at initiation.\nAssume all financial instruments use the 30/360 day-count \nconvention.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Herman Schmidt is a fund manager working at Rosige\nZukunft LLC (RZ), a derivatives trading firm.  RZ uses the \ncarry arbitrage model to assess the value of bond forward \ncontracts.  Exhibit 1 contains information on several \nGerman government bonds that pay coupons once per \nannum and have five years remaining until maturity.  \nSchmidt directs Hedwig Meyer, an RZ analyst, to price \nforward contracts on Bond A, Bond B, and Bond C.  The 6-\nmonth risk-free rate is 1.50% and the 1-year risk-free rate is \n2.00%.\nSchmidt and Meyer discuss different methods for valuing \ninterest rate and currency swaps.  Exhibit 2 contains \ninformation on at-market EUR and CHF interest rate swaps, \nand the CHF/EUR exchange rate.\nSchmidt has RZ initiate a fixed-for-fixed EUR/CHF currency \nswap, agreeing to pay 1.20% in CHF and receive 1.80% in \nEUR.  RZ exchanges the swap notional with the \ncounterparty at contract initiation, paying EUR 10 million \nand receiving CHF 10.5 million.  Six months later, at-market \n2.5-year fixed-for-fixed EUR/CHF currency swaps are quoted \nat 1.60% EUR for 1.40% CHF and the spot CHF/EUR \nexchange rate is 1.1000.\nSchmidt anticipates that the equity of Dash Haber Ltd., a UK \nclothing retailer, will outperform versus expectations.  He \ndecides to use a 1-year, quarterly settled, equity-return-for-\nfixed-interest rate swap to gain long exposure to Dash \nHaber equity.  Exhibit 3 contains information related to the \nswap:\n\nAll interest rates are annual compound rates and are based \non a 360-day year.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Johan Marten and Gisele Thierry are equity derivatives \ntraders for an international bank.  They discuss the factors \nunderlying no-arbitrage pricing of forward commitment \nderivatives, focusing on how these factors apply to pricing a \n2-year forward contract on the Euro Stoxx 50 Index.  The \ndetails of this forward contract are:\nThe current market value of the Euro Stoxx 50 Index is \n3900.\nThe continuously compounded dividend yield on the index \nis 2.5%.\nThe continuously compounded risk-free interest rate is 3%.\nThierry's previous experience has mostly involved trading \nequity options, futures, and forwards.  She asks Marten to \ndescribe the structure and pricing of equity swaps.  In \nresponse, he makes the following statements:\nStatement 1: Equity swaps are always structured to have:\none equity leg, with a value based on the return of the \nunderlying equity, and\none interest rate leg, with a value based on either a fixed or \nfloating rate.\nStatement 2: The return of the equity leg can be valued on \neither a total return basis or a price-change-only basis.\nStatement 3: The fixed-rate leg in an equity return-for-\nfixed interest rate swap is a weighted average of the \ninterest rates used in valuing the equity forward contracts \nthat would replicate the swap's cash flows.\nThierry is unsure that she fully understands the relationship \nbetween the value of an equity swap and the price of the \nunderlying equity.  She asks Marten to explain that \nrelationship, using the receive-equity, pay-fixed interest \nrate swap on Marticart common stock shown in Exhibit 1.  \nThe EUR term structure is flat at 1.5%.\n\nThe discount factors relevant for valuing the swap, based on \nthe current term structure, are shown in Exhibit 2:\nThe bank currently holds a position in a Gamboling \nCorporation common stock pay-equity, receive-fixed \ninterest rate swap.  Exhibit 3 contains select information \nregarding the swap.  The swap was initiated 6 months \nearlier, and the settlement payment for the quarter ending \nyesterday is due by the close of business today.  Thierry \nasks Marten how the settlement payment is calculated.\nAssume all swap periodic settlement payments use a \n30/360 day-count convention.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Sondra Schute manages an interest rate and fixed-income \ntrading desk for an international bank.  Rick Patere is a \nrecently hired junior analyst supporting the desk.  Schute\nwants to evaluate Patere's understanding of forward \ncommitment contracts and asks him to describe the \nvaluation of bond forward and futures contracts.  Patere\nsays that forward and futures prices are similar \nconceptually, but there is a fundamental difference in the \nvaluation of the two types of derivatives:\nStatement 1: The value of a forward contract after \ninitiation is the future value of the difference between the \nagreed price in the existing contract and the no-arbitrage \nforward price of a contract initiated today, assuming both \ncontracts are written on the same underlying bond and \nhave the same expiration date.\nStatement 2: The value of a futures contract resets to zero \nafter every trading session since there is a mark-to-market \ncash settlement equal to the change in the future's price \nfrom the previous day's settlement.\nSchute asks Patere to assess the value of a bond futures \ncontract relative to the underlying bond, using a risk-free \nrate of 1.00% and given the current data shown in Exhibit 1:\nA commercial loan officer has requested a quote for an at-\nmarket, pay-fixed, receive-floating 3 × 9 forward rate \nagreement (FRA) to hedge the interest rate on a future loan \nto a client.  Schute directs Patere to calculate the fixed rate \non an at-market FRA and then to describe how the value of\n\nthe trading desk's FRA position will be affected by shifts in \nthe yield curve, as the receive-fixed, pay-floating party.  \nCurrent reference index interest rate data is shown in \nExhibit 2:\nSchute asks Patere to describe how he would value a pay-\nfixed, receive-floating, interest rate swap initiated 1 year \nago.  The swap has 2 years remaining until expiration and a \nfixed-leg coupon of 3%.  Patere identifies two methods to \nvalue the swap.  The value of the swap is equal to:\nMethod 1:\nThe present value of the difference between \n3% and the coupon of the fixed-rate leg of a new at-market, \nannual payment, 2-year swap, assuming the same notional \namount and floating-leg reference interest rate as the swap \nbeing valued.\nMethod 2:\nThe difference between the value of two 2-\nyear annual-pay bonds with par values equal to the swap \nnotional amount, assuming one bond has a 3% fixed-\ncoupon rate and the other has a floating-rate coupon based \non the same reference interest rate as the floating leg of \nthe swap.\nSchute directs Patere to price a 4-year, at-market interest \nrate swap based on the information in Exhibit 3:\n\nUnless stated otherwise, assume all instruments have \nannual payment frequencies and use the 30/360 day-count \nconvention, and that all interest rates are stated on an \nannual compound basis.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Alexa Rohm, an option trader at a large hedge fund, is \nassisted by Brian Bosnik, a derivatives analyst that works at \nthe firm.  Rohm directs Bosnik to construct a binomial \ninterest rate tree to value a $1 million notional 2.75% 2-\nyear interest rate call.  The binomial tree, based on a 50% \nprobability of an up move, is shown in Exhibit 1:\nRohm is considering the use of various option-pricing \nmodels to evaluate equity options.  She asks Bosnik to \nprovide a general description of how binomial models \nanalyze European option values.  In response Bosnik\ncomments on the value of the option from a binomial tree:\nComment 1:\nThe value of an option is the PV of the \nexpected payoffs.\nComment 2:\nThe payoffs reflect the cash flows at \nexpiration from the exercise of the option if it is in the \nmoney, discounted at the investor's required rate of return.\nComment 3:\nThe PVs of the expected payoffs are \nweighted by risk-neutral probabilities.\nRohm is long 5,000 shares of Positron Corporation's \ncommon stock.  Near the end of the day's trading, she \nhedges the Positron stock holding by selling Positron equity \ncalls to create a delta-neutral position.  Exhibit 2 contains \ninformation on Positron stock and options:\n\nThe day after Rohm establishes the delta-neutral position in \nPositron, its stock opens at $65 per share at the beginning \nof the trading session.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Parker Brookes, CFA, works for a portfolio management firm \nthat invests in options to hedge its equity positions.  \nBrookes is analyzing equity options for Alphatron, Inc. and \nOmegasys Corp.  The relevant information for each \ncompany is provided in Exhibits 1 and 2:\nExhibit 3 shows the information for Alphatron options.  All \nthe options are European options that expire in one year, \nand each option allows the holder to purchase or sell one \nshare of Alphatron common stock:\nNeither Alphatron nor Omegasys currently pays a dividend.\nWhile talking with a colleague, Brookes makes the following \nstatements about the expectations approach for valuing \noptions:\nStatement 1: The expectations approach relies on the \ninvestor's subjective belief as to how the underlying asset \nwill move.\nStatement 2: The discount rate used for the expectations \napproach is not adjusted for the investor's risk preferences.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Yannes Bolger is a newly hired junior analyst at a derivatives \ntrading firm, working on an interest rate option trading desk \nmanaged by Hidemi Okura.  To assess Bolger's \nunderstanding of derivatives, Okura first asks him which \ncombination of interest rate puts and calls is most similar to \na pay-fixed, receive-floating forward rate agreement (FRA).\nShe then asks Bolger to identify which of the strategies \nbelow is equivalent to being short an interest rate cap and \nlong an interest rate floor, assuming both options are 1-year \ncontracts based on the 3-month market reference rate \n(MRR), with quarterly settlement payments, 3% exercise \nrates, and $1 million notional amounts.\nStrategy 1:\nPositions in two $1 million par value 1-year \nbonds that pay interest quarterly: long a floating-rate bond \nwith a coupon rate based on the 3-month MRR, and short a \n3% fixed-coupon bond.\nStrategy 2:\nA 1-year, $1 million notional, quarterly \nsettled, receive-fixed, pay-floating interest rate swap with a \n3% fixed-rate leg and a floating-rate leg based on the 3-\nmonth MRR.\nStrategy 3:\nLong a payer swaption on a 1-year, quarterly \nsettled, $1 million notional swap with a 3% fixed-rate leg \nand a floating-rate leg based on the 3-month MRR.\nA corporate client contacts Okura for advice on structuring \na debt offering.  The client wants to borrow at a fixed cost \nfor a specified maturity but expects interest rates to rise \nover the next few years.  The client believes the bond \nmarket is underpricing interest rate options relative to the \nderivatives market.  Therefore, the client asks how interest \nrate options might be used to achieve the preferred fixed-\nrate funding for the specified maturity while also reducing \nfinancing costs by taking advantage of the mispricing of \nembedded calls in the bond market.\nAnother client has requested a quote on a €10 million \nnotional amount, 2-year, 4% European interest rate put.  \nOkura asks Bolger to value the put option using the \nbinomial interest rate tree shown in Exhibit 1.\n\nAssume all instruments use a 30/360 day-count convention.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Axeron Financial Corporation\nAxeron is a proprietary trading firm that invests in securities \nand derivatives markets around the world.  The firm uses \nthe carry arbitrage model to identify when forward \ncommitment derivatives appear to be mispriced relative to \nthe underlying asset.\nNichi Tano, CFA, who manages a derivatives-trading desk for \nAxeron, is overseeing the training of Ralle Mittan, a new \nresearch associate.  To assess Mittan's familiarity with \nequity derivatives, Tano asks her to evaluate the accuracy of \nthe following statements regarding otherwise equivalent \n(ie, same underlying asset, same expiration date) equity \nfutures and forward contracts:\nStatement 1: Long futures and forward positions exhibit \nthe same pattern of cash flows over the time to expiration.\nStatement 2: The no-arbitrage value of futures and \nforward contracts is the same given the same time to \nexpiration.\nStatement 3: Futures and forward prices decrease by \nsimilar amounts if the expected dividends from holding the \nunderlying increase.\nAfter discussing these statements, Tano asks Mittan to \ndescribe the elements of the arbitrage carry model and \naddress what effect, if any, an increase in the risk-free rate \nhas on equity forward prices.\nAxeron is the pay-fixed/receive-floating party in a 3 × 6 \nforward rate agreement (FRA) initiated 30 days earlier.  \nInformation on this existing FRA and current index rates are \npresented in Exhibits 1 and 2.\n\nGahbi Faddal, an Axeron swap trader, gathers the exchange \nrate and risk-free rate data to evaluate a fixed-for-fixed EUR \n/ GBP currency swap.  The terms of the swap and related \ninformation are given in Exhibit 3.\nAxeron is the fixed-rate payer/floating-rate receiver in an \nannual reset USD interest rate swap.  The USD 25,000,000 \nnotional amount contract was created at-market with a \n2.50% fixed rate and 4 years to maturity when initiated 1 \nyear ago.  Exhibit 4 contains current data for valuing USD \nfixed-for-floating interest rate swaps.\nAxeron is the equity-return receiver/fixed-rate payer in the \nswap described in Exhibit 5.\n\nUnless otherwise noted, assume a 30/360 day-count \nconvention applies to all pricing and valuation questions.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c8_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c8_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Antoine Nim manages a bond portfolio for a small hedge \nfund.  He contacts Julia Trussock, a derivatives trader at a \nbank.  Nim intends to use bond forward and futures \ncontracts to adjust portfolio risks and asks Trussock about \nthe carry arbitrage model for pricing derivatives.\nTrussock makes two observations regarding no-arbitrage \nbond forward prices:\nObservation 1:Zero-coupon bond forward prices are always \ngreater than their spot prices.\nObservation 2:Forward prices for coupon bonds may be \neither greater or less than their spot prices, depending on \nthe bond's current yield relative to the risk-free rate. \nNim expects the European Central Bank (ECB) to raise its \npolicy rate.  He goes long a forward contract on a German \ngovernment bond to profit from the market reaction he \nanticipates.  Information on the contract is contained in \nExhibit 1.  At the time of the purchase, the government par \nbond yield curve is flat at 3%.\nLater that day, the ECB increases its policy rate as Nim\nexpected.  The ECB rate move has resulted in the German \ngovernment bond yield curve inverting.  Exhibit 2 contains \ninformation on the current government bond yield curve.\n\nNim's portfolio contains an off-the-run German government \nbond that is deliverable against the standardized futures \ncontract.  Since the bond's coupon rate differs from the \nfutures contract coupon, Nim is uncertain if the futures \ncontract is fairly valued relative to his bond.  Nim asks \nTrussock to explain how to assess the relative fair value of \nthe two instruments.  Exhibit 3 contains information on the \nbond and the standardized futures contract.  The 0.75-year \nrisk-free rate is 3.50%.\nAnother client of Trussock's wants to lock in the rate on a 3-\nmonth loan to be taken 6 months in the future.  Exhibit 4 \ncontains selected data on the market reference rates \n(MRRs) used for pricing forward rate agreements (FRAs).\nAll interest rates are expressed as annual rates, and all \ninstruments use the 30/360 day-count conventions.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Samir Madeq, an experienced equity derivatives trader at a \nlarge bank, has been mentoring Alana Hernan, who was \nrecently hired as a trainee after earning a degree in \nquantitative finance.  Madeq wants to assess Hernan's \nfamiliarity with the Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) model and \noption pricing theory.  Madeq asks Hernan to describe how \na call option on Voltrin Corporation common stock can be \nreplicated with positions in the underlying shares and risk-\nfree bonds, using the inputs and selected outputs from a \nBSM valuation of the Voltrin options, shown in Exhibit 1:\nMadeq then turns the conversation to option Greeks.  He \nasks Hernan to interpret (rho) relative to put options.  She \nresponds that put option values are inversely related to \ninterest rate changes and provides two reasons:\nReason 1:\nIn the context of BSM, interest rates affect \noption values through their impact on the PV of the \nexercise price.\nReason 2:\nBSM implies that a put option is equivalent \nto having a delta-weighted short position in the underlying \nshares.  A higher interest rate implies a greater opportunity \ncost of not earning interest on short-sale proceeds.\nMadeq then asks Hernan to describe the meaning of \nimplied volatility and how it is used in assessing option \nvalues.  In response, Hernan makes three statements \nregarding implied volatility:\n\nStatement 1: Implied volatility can be used as an estimate \nof future asset price volatility and therefore may be more \ninsightful than historical volatility regarding market \nexpectations.\nStatement 2: When investors perceive increased \nuncertainty about the future, the demand for options \nincreases, resulting in higher option prices and increases in \nobserved implied volatility.\nStatement 3: No-arbitrage pricing ensures that all options \non the same underlying will have the same implied volatility \nregardless of strike price or time to expiration.\nMadeq is long 2,500 call options with a 50 exercise price on \nDemino Corporation common stock.  Madeq intends to \ncreate a delta-neutral call spread by selling calls with a 40 \nexercise price.  Information concerning Demino common \nstock and call options is shown in Exhibit 2:\nAssume each stock option contract is for one share of the \nunderlying stock.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Parasail Trading Corp. (Parasail) is a broker-dealer firm with \nseveral operating subsidiaries.  One Parasail division is a \nmarket maker in over-the-counter stocks.  Another division \nconducts proprietary trading in equities and equity options.\nParasail's market-making unit operates under tight risk-\nmanagement policies and requires traders to minimize \nmarket exposure by using options to hedge their overnight \npositions.  Kerry Young is the trader responsible for making \nmarkets in High-Grade Gold Ltd. (High-Grade) and Pure \nPlatinum Plus Inc. (Pure Platinum).\nYoung uses the Black-Scholes-Merton (BSM) model to \nevaluate options on High-Grade and Pure Platinum stock.  \nNeither company pays dividends.  All options on both \nstocks are trading at fair value according to the BSM model.\nOptions on High-Grade\nExhibit 1 contains the BSM model inputs for the stock price \n(S), strike price (X), risk-free rate (r), volatility (σ), time to \nexpiration in years (T) and dividend yield (ẟ).  The exhibit \nalso contains selected BSM-model outputs.\nOptions on Pure Platinum\nYoung has a significant long position in Pure Platinum \nshares, which she intends to delta hedge with call options.  \nExhibit 2 contains information on Pure Platinum shares and \noptions.  These options have two months until expiration.\n\nOptions on Code Shift\nLee Philips is a managing director in Parasail's proprietary \ntrading business.  Parasail currently holds 200,000 shares of \nCode Shift Programming (Code Shift) common stock.  \nAlthough Code Shift is now a publicly traded company, the \nshares were acquired through Parasail's participation in a \nround of venture funding before Code Shift's initial public \noffering.  The Code Shift shares are subject to a lockup \nagreement that expires in six months.  Exhibit 3 contains \ninformation on Code Shift stock and options.\nPhilips intends to sell the Code Shift shares as soon as the \nlockup period expires.  In the interim, Philips decides to sell \ncall options against the shares to generate income.  Two \ndifferent call-writing strategies are being considered:\nStrategy A:  Sell 200,000 at-the-money (ATM) Code Shift \ncalls having six months until expiration.\n\nStrategy B:  Sell 200,000 ATM Code Shift calls that have two \nmonths until expiration.  When those options expire, sell \nanother 200,000 ATM options that have two months until \nexpiration.  When the second group of options expires, sell\nanother 200,000 ATM calls that have two months until \nexpiration.\nPhilips also has a 50,000-share short position in Nano Node \nSemiconductor Inc. (Nano Node).  Philips decides to use \nNano Node options to construct a delta-neutral hedge that \nhas a positive gamma.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Shanna Zephron is a risk management specialist at an \ninternational bank.  She advises the bank's corporate clients \non the use of derivatives to manage interest rate and \ncurrency risk.  Raymundo Mattis is responsible for \noverseeing the funding operations for Tazenda Group, a \nGermany-based multinational.  Tazenda entered a EUR pay-\nfixed, receive-floating forward rate agreement (FRA) 1 \nmonth ago.  Mattis wants Zephron to calculate the FRA's \ncurrent value.  Information on the FRA is contained in \nExhibit 1:\nExhibit 2 contains information on current EUR market \nreference rates (MRRs):\nMattis is also interested in entering a EUR interest rate \nswap to manage the interest rate risk of a floating-rate \nbond issued several years earlier.  Exhibit 3 contains \nselected data on the bond:\nExhibit 4 shows the EUR spot interest rates used in valuing \nthe swap:\n\nMattis wants to move some of Tazenda's interest rate \nexposure between CHF and EUR.  He asks Zephron for a \nquote on an at-market receive-fixed-EUR, pay-floating-CHF \ncurrency swap.  The terms of the swap are shown in Exhibit \n5:\nMattis wants to make sure he understands the pricing and \nvaluation of the currency swap described in Exhibit 5.  \nMattis states his understanding of those issues and asks \nZephron whether his statements are correct.\nStatement 1: Currency swaps are effectively interest rate \nswaps with legs denominated in different currencies.  \nTherefore, a fixed-rate leg in an at-market currency swap is \nthe same as the fixed-rate leg of an otherwise equivalent \nfixed-for-floating interest swap in the same currency.\nStatement 2: The swap's value is based on the difference \nbetween the value of two 3-year, semiannual-pay bonds \nthat replicate the swap's cash flows:\nshort a EUR 55 million par-value bond with 1.80% fixed \ncoupon, and\nlong a CHF 50 million par-value bond with a floating-rate \ncoupon that resets at the 6-month CHF MRR.\n\nTazenda needs to raise debt funding for a UK-based \nsubsidiary.  Mattis views GBP fixed-rate borrowing costs as \nrelatively attractive.  However, the subsidiary primarily \nholds floating-rate assets.  Mattis asks Zephron if a swap \ncan be used to take advantage of attractive fixed-rate \nfunding in GBP while better matching the effective \nfinancing rate to the subsidiary's asset returns.\nAll the financial instruments in this scenario use the 30/360 \nday-count convention.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Mitoshi & Santer Options Advisory for Clients\nMitoshi and Santer LLC (M&S) is a firm that offers advisory \nservices for high-net-worth clients and corporations.  Javi\nJalli is a senior derivatives consultant at M&S.\nSeveral M&S clients have positions in Elephantine Airlines \ncommon stock, which does not pay a dividend.  There are \nno exchange-traded options on Elephantine.  Several clients \nhave asked M&S to evaluate over-the-counter (OTC) \noptions on Elephantine shares.  M&S uses the Black-\nScholes-Merton (BSM) model to evaluate equity options.\nOptions on Elephantine\nExhibit 1 contains the BSM model inputs and selected BSM \nmodel outputs.\nTim Smith, an employee of an M&S client, contacts Jalli for \nadvice on taking a leveraged position in Elephantine using \nOTC options.  To confirm that his interpretations of BSM are \ncorrect, Smith makes several statements regarding volatility \nand asks Jalli if they are accurate.\nStatement 1:  The BSM model implies the volatility surface \nis flat and fixed over time, whereas implied volatility varies \nacross strike prices and expirations and changes over time.\nStatement 2:  Implied volatility provides a means for \ncomparing the relative value of different options on the \nsame underlying.\n\nAnother M&S client, Comper Halle, has a well-diversified \nequity portfolio in which Elephantine is the largest position.  \nHalle meets with Jalli to discuss M&S's use of pricing \nmodels to value options and design trading strategies.  To \nverify his understanding of the BSM approach to estimating \noption values, Halle states several of the assumptions upon \nwhich the BSM model is based.\nAssumption 1:  Options are continuously exercisable from \npurchase until expiration.\nAssumption 2:  Traders have full use of the proceeds from \nshort sale of the underlying asset.\nAssumption 3:  Asset prices exhibit geometric Brownian \nmotion and normally distributed returns.\nHalle had requested that M&S get a quote for an \nElephantine 50 put to protect the value of his position in \nthe stock.  However, Halle is concerned that the put is too \nexpensive and asks Jalli to recommend a less costly strategy \nto achieve his objective.\nHalle asks Jalli about which types of derivatives could be \nused to manage the market risk of his equity portfolio.  \nThey discuss the relative benefits of options in equity \nindexes, index futures options, and equity swaptions.  Jalli\ndescribes the models M&S uses to assess the values of the \nvarious options.\nSwaptions\nM&S has been retained by Peak Performance \nManufacturing (Peak), an AA-rated company, for advice on \ndebt financing alternatives.  Peak is considering the \nissuance of a five-year fixed-rate bond.  M&S suggests that \nPeak can reduce its effective funding rate by using synthetic \nnoncallable debt that is created by issuing a callable bond \nand selling a receiver swaption.  M&S advisors use the Black \nmodel to establish the terms of the swaption.\nExhibit 2 contains relevant interest rate information.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_derivatives", "source_label": "UWorld – Derivatives", "topic": "Derivatives", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Aki Ming is a derivatives trader at a global hedge fund.  \nMing is seeking profitable trading opportunities with the \nassistance of Tara Sinn, an equity derivatives analyst with \nthe firm.  Ming wants to evaluate the potential for index-\narbitrage trades using a S&P 500 futures contract that has 6 \nmonths until expiration.  Exhibit 1 contains the information \nneeded to price the futures contract:\nMing and Sinn discuss using the carry arbitrage model for \npricing equity derivatives and how changes in the carry \naffect no-arbitrage forward prices.  Ming has a position in a \nforward contract in Vector Transport common stock that \nhad one year to expiration when it was initiated.  Ming asks \nSinn to value the contract based on current market \nconditions.  The information gathered by Sinn is shown in \nExhibit 2:\nMing and Sinn discuss equity swap pricing and valuation.  \nAfterward, Ming has Sinn evaluate a position the hedge \nfund currently holds in an annual-pay, receive-equity, pay-\nfixed swap on Hitashi Corporation common stock.  \nInformation on the swap is shown in Exhibit 3:\n\nThe PV factors for valuing annually settled swaps is shown \nin Exhibit 4:\nThe hedge fund borrows opportunistically in a variety of \ncurrencies to reduce its cost of debt.  Ming wants to use a \ncurrency swap to move interest exposure from USD to EUR.  \nExhibit 4 contains selected data related to the pricing and \nvaluation of quarterly settled USD/EUR currency swaps.  \nThe spot exchange rate between the currencies is currently \n$1.20 per €.\nUnless stated otherwise, assume all instruments use the \n30/360 day-count convention and that all interest rates are \nstated on an annual compound basis.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_derivatives_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Ranbir Chauhan is a commodities fund manager working for \nNavigator Capital.  His fund is currently invested in several \ncommodities and commodity indexes using different \nstrategies.  Chauhan invests in copper through futures \ncontracts by purchasing the contracts 3 months out, then \nrolling them over before expiration.  The spot price for \ncopper has remained relatively stable over time.\nChauhan wants to reduce Navigator's exposure to the broad \ncommodities market to focus on certain sectors.  When \npicking an index for this strategy, he considers the following \nfactors:\nFactor 1:  Since commodities are relatively diversified, there \nis not much difference between production value-weighted \nand equal-weighted indexes.\nFactor 2:  Indexes that invest using exchanges around the \nworld make it more expensive for investors to replicate \nrelative to indexes that focus on regional exchanges.\nFactor 3:  All major indexes use nearby or front-month \ncontracts, so their roll return will be similar for a given \ncommodity sector.\nTo reduce Navigator's exposure, Chauhan takes a short \nposition in a commodities index total return swap with \nGenesis Financial.  After two months, he collects the \nfollowing swap data:\n\nChauhan expects infrastructure investment in developed \nmarkets to increase over the next couple years.  He plans to \nshift his portfolio's exposure to the commodity sector that \nis most likely to benefit from increased infrastructure \nspending.  The portfolio's exposure to cotton will be \nreduced to free up funds to pursue this strategy.\nCotton has been part of Chauhan's portfolio for six months \nsince the January 20X4 purchase of the June 20X4 \ncontracts.  Chauhan just rolled over his June 20X4 contracts \nto August 20X4 contracts, and collects the following \ninformation:\nChauhan is looking to expand into new commodities to \ndiversify his portfolio.  The commodities that he is \nresearching are:\nGrains\nCoffee\nLivestock\nHowever, he is concerned about how seasonality affects the \nsupply of these commodities and how that will impact his \ninvestment strategy.\n\nMarius Ervand is an associate at Normandy Capital, a \nprivate equity (PE) firm focused on leveraged buyouts \n(LBOs).  Ervand is scheduled to meet with Marie Camille, an \ninvestment analyst at Bordeaux Pension Fund, a \nprospective investor.  Camille is unfamiliar with PE \ninvestments, so Ervand has given her a brief explanation:\n\"PE funds use debt to magnify returns for investors.  As \ndebt is gradually retired over time, more operating cash \nflows become available to investors.  PE funds generally \ncharge higher fees than publicly traded funds such as \nmutual funds; the difference is partially attributable to high \nperformance fees.  PE investments have little to no market \nrisk since portfolio companies are not publicly traded.\"\nNext, Camille tells Ervand that she wants to assess \nNormandy's historical fund performance, so Ervand\nselectively presents data on two funds with an original 10-\nyear life (Exhibit 1).\nAfter viewing the data, Camille makes the following \ncomments:\nComment 1: \"Fund 1 has distributed €1.39 to investors for \nevery euro invested.\"\nComment 2: \"If I were a fund investor, gross IRR would be \nmore relevant to me than net IRR.\"\nComment 3: \"Fund 2's low returns can be explained by the \nJ-curve effect.\"\n\nA few days later, Ervand meets with Leyonce Cyrille, a \nNormandy Capital managing partner, to discuss the year-\nend performance for Normandy Fund 3.  This fund has not \nyet incurred any carried interest, which is paid only when \nNAV before distributions exceeds committed capital.  \nCarried interest is calculated as 20% of the difference \nbetween NAV before distributions and committed capital.  \nCyrille wants to approximate NAV after distributions net of \ncarried interest, based on the following data (in €):\nInvested capital:  95 million\nNAV before distributions:  150 million\nAnnual distributions:  20 million\nCommitted capital:  120 million\nThis year, Normandy Capital will exit its current stake in \nBigTrailers Industries, a portfolio company that \nmanufactures industrial-grade trailers.  Ervand states that \nmarket conditions are strong.  Cyrille responds, \"I prefer the \nhighest exit valuation.  We should prepare to sell BigTrailers\nto WideRoad Inc., the largest company in this space.  This \ntransaction would most likely yield the highest premium.\"\nBigTrailers was purchased 5 years ago for €200 million.  The \npurchase was financed as follows (in €):\nDebt:  140 million\nPreferred equity:  10 million\nPE fund equity:  45 million\nManagement equity:  5 million\nCyrille expects to sell BigTrailers for €300 million.  \nNormandy Capital will have retired all of BigTrailers' debt \nprior to the sale.  Preferred equity shareholders are \npromised a 15% annual return.  After accounting for \npayments to debtholders and preferred equity investors, \n95% of the remaining proceeds will go to the PE fund's \nequity and 5% will go to management.\nIn the future, Cyrille wants to start a fund with a target size \nof €1 billion but will likely require a placement agent to \nreach that goal.  Ervand states:\n\"Management fees will be 1.5%, transaction fees will be \nsplit 50/50 between the general partner and the limited \npartners, and carried interest will be paid on a deal-by-deal \nbasis.\"", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Ichika Suzuki is a REIT analyst at a sell-side firm.  Her \nsupervisor, Haruki Kimura, has asked her to write a report \non Fuji Residential Investment Corporation (Fuji RIC), an \napartment REIT.  Kimura notes that apartment REITs are a \ntype of multi-family rental property.  Suzuki plans to begin \nthe report with an overview of the economic environment, \nincluding factors such as retail sales growth, business \ninvestment, and consumer confidence.  Based on her \nanalysis, the economic environment is favorable for Fuji RIC.  \nSuzuki then collects the financial data for Fuji RIC shown in \nExhibit 1:\nKimura mentions that using NAV to value REITs can have \ndrawbacks and suggests doing a discounted cash flow \nanalysis.  He provides Suzuki the information in Exhibit 2:\nKimura also notes that Fuji RIC has just purchased a large \nproperty and asks Suzuki to value the property.  Kimura \nexpects Fuji RIC to sell the property in 3 years.  He indicates \nthat there are three methods to value the property, and \neach method can provide a justified valuation.  Suzuki \ncollects information on the property:\n\nSuzuki suggests adding a section to the report about why \ninvestors should invest in REITs instead of investing directly \nin private real estate.  Kimura notes several advantages and \ndisadvantages to REIT investments.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Sakchai Mali, CFA, is chief investment officer (CIO) of the \nKulap University endowment and wants to allocate some \ncapital to private equity (PE) funds.  She speaks with two of \nher analysts, Hulka Sukohn and Wattana Kamon, to learn \nmore about PE investments.  Sukohn first discusses how PE \nfirms adopt mechanisms that align their interests with the \ninterests of the portfolio company's management team.  \nSukohn states:\nStatement 1: A noncompete clause prohibits founders \nfrom competing against the firm for an indefinite time \nperiod.\nStatement 2: Earn-outs enable a PE firm to pay less to \nacquire a company if the company reaches certain \nmilestones over time.\nStatement 3: PE firms often exert control over the \nportfolio company through corporate board seats.\nMali is curious about how target companies are valued.  \nKamon says:\nAnswer 1:\nThe replacement cost method is typically \nused to value mature companies.\nAnswer 2:\nThe discounted cash flow approach is \ntypically used to value companies with a long history of \nprofitable operations.\nAnswer 3:\nThe relative value approach is often used to \nvalue seed-stage companies.\nNext, Sukohn discusses the various exit routes and sources \nof returns for PE investments.  Sukohn says that exit routes \ninclude initial public offering (IPO), secondary market sale, \nand management buyout (MBO).  He further makes the \nfollowing comments:\nComment 1:\nIt is very common for venture capital \ninvestments to exit via a leveraged buyout from another PE \nfirm.\nComment 2:\nDecreasing the amount of debt outstanding \ngradually is crucial to enhancing shareholder returns.\nComment 3:\nAn IPO can yield the highest valuation but \ncan also incur the highest cost.\n\nSoon after these discussions, Kamon leaves the endowment \nand starts a PE firm, Raintree Partners.  The Raintree Fund \nhas:\nCommitted capital of $200 million\nCarried interest of 20%, computed on a deal-by-deal basis\nHurdle rate of 9%\nCalled 55% of its committed capital at the beginning of the \nfirst year, of which\n$100 million was invested in Company A\n$10 million was invested in Company B\nA clawback provision that includes annual true-up \n(accounting reconciliation)\nAt the end of the second year, Raintree made $20 million in \nprofit from the exit of Company A.  Raintree retained \nownership of Company B, which has not changed in value.  \nAt the end of the third year, Raintree had a $10 million loss \nfrom the exit of Company B.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Punam Khan is an individual investor considering adding \npublicly traded real estate instruments to her investment \nportfolio.  After starting her research, she discovers that \ncommercial properties are typically classified into many \nsegments.  Khan prefers:\nthe types of properties that are the most management \nintensive since she believes management skill is not always \nproperly accounted for in a property's value.  This allows \nher to pick undervalued properties with strong \nmanagement.\ntaking on riskier investments as she believes the economy \nwill be stable for the foreseeable future.\nKhan decides to call Dipak Sahu, another individual investor, \nwho has more experience in real estate.  Sahu tells Khan \nthat he trades in three main types of publicly traded real \nestate securities, a recommended strategy for smaller \ninvestments.  Of those types, Sahu prefers to invest in \nassets that:\nappreciate when the properties' value appreciates, and\nallow for greater flexibility in how funds are invested.\nSahu also suggests that instruments based on real estate \nindexes are a viable option for introductory real estate \ninvestments.  He warns Khan to consider whether a \nbenchmark index is appraisal- or transaction-based since \nthis will affect the index's values and returns.\nAfter getting Sahu's advice, Khan decides to research Brick \nand Mortar Financial (BMF), a diversified REIT.  Khan \ncollects the following data from BMF's financial statements \nand the industry:\n\nKhan observes that BMF has a greater amount of land held \nfor development than its peers and wonders how that \nwould affect its funds-from-operations (FFO) ratios.  In \naddition, she notices that FFO multiples vary over time and \nalso between different companies.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Marcus Doppler is the chief information officer (CIO) of \nPittman University's endowment fund.  After analyzing the \nfund's recent performance, he believes that the fund \nshould diversify its investments and allocate a portion of its \nassets to private equity (PE).  He hires Sigrid Marek, a senior \nanalyst with much experience in the PE industry.\nDoppler questions Marek about characteristics of PE \ninvestments, starting with the rate of return on investments \nthat could be expected for the endowment fund.  Marek \nexplains that this return depends on several factors, \nincluding the chosen exit route.  Typically, she adds, the \nhighest valuation multiples are achieved when the \ninvestment is sold in an IPO, but market downturns and \nother external events may shut down the IPO markets.  In \nthose situations, other exit routes are available.\nDoppler asks about the costs of investing in PE funds.  \nMarek warns that the costs are higher than the costs of \ninvesting in public equities and makes the following \ncomments:\nComment 1:\nFund setup costs include the costs of \nfinancing and structuring an acquisition.\nComment 2:\nPlacement fees are charged as a percentage \nof the money raised by the PE fund.\nComment 3:\nAdministrative costs are fixed annual fees \nthat include custodian and transfer-agent costs.\nDoppler explains to Marek that the endowment fund \nrecently had the opportunity to invest in Accelana Capital 3 \n(AC3), an early-stage venture capital (VC) fund managed by \nAccelana Partners.  AC3 invests in start-ups that develop \nnew technologies for the automotive industry.  Doppler \nadds that AC3's portfolio companies have short or \nnonexistent operating histories, but most of them have \nsigned long-term contracts with large car manufacturers.\n\nThis will substantially increase revenues in the following \nquarters, although cash flows will still be difficult to predict.  \nDoppler asks Marek what approach she recommends for \nvaluing AC3.\nLastly, Doppler tells Marek that Accelana Partners provided \ninformation regarding the firm's two previous PE funds as \nevidence of the firm's superior performance.  Information \nabout the returns earned by Accelana Capital 1 (AC1) and \nAccelana Capital 2 (AC2) is presented in Exhibit 1:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Maria Valdez is a commodity specialist covering global \nnatural resources at Pecos Mutual Fund Inc., a buy-side \nfirm.  Valdez's commodity price forecasts are used by other \nanalysts at the firm and incorporated into their bottom-up \nfinancial models.\nHector Ortiz covers the global automotive industry group, \nwhich is significantly impacted by the costs of various \ncommodities used in auto manufacturing.  Ortiz is \nspecifically concerned with the price of lithium, a key \nindustrial metal used in the production of lithium-ion \nbattery packs for electric vehicles.  Over the past year, \nlithium prices have increased significantly due to tight \nsupplies.\nOrtiz asks Valdez to comment on recent developments \nimpacting the lithium market.  Valdez states that it is \nimportant to consider how stocks and flows of a commodity \nimpact the market price of that commodity.  She then \nmakes the following comments:\nComment 1:\nThe country that is the world's largest \nsupplier of battery-grade lithium recently increased its \nrevenue royalty tax on lithium from 1% to 2%.\nComment 2:\nThe largest global producer of lithium \nannounced a capacity expansion of an existing mine, which \nwill increase lithium output by 20%.\nComment 3:\nSeasonal rains in one of the large lithium-\nproducing countries have been unusually heavy this year.\nLater that day, Valdez receives a call from one of the firm's \nquantitative analysts, Pablo Fernandez, who is building a \nquantitative model to value commodities.  As the firm will \nuse commodity futures to implement an investment \nstrategy, Fernandez asks about the underlying drivers of \ncommodities futures prices.  Valdez responds that she \nbelieves that most commodity producers consistently sell \nforward to lock in prices, causing futures prices to decrease, \nresulting in a \"normal backwardation\" of the futures curve.\n\nValdez then proposes the following factors as potential \ninputs for valuing a commodity derivative such as a \ncommodity futures contract:\nFactor 1:\nFutures roll yield\nFactor 2:\nExpected volatility of future prices\nFactor 3:\nSupply and demand fundamentals of the \nunderlying commodity", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Conrad Green is the chief investment officer at the \nMunicipal Pension Fund.  The fund currently holds no \ncommodities, and Green has now decided to allocate 10% \nof the fund to commodities.  The rationale for Green's \ndecision is based on the following forecasts:\nCommodities' historically low return correlation with other \nmajor asset classes will continue.\nIn a change from the recent past, commodity prices will \nsteadily rise over the next decade due to persistently higher \ninflation.\nGreen recently hired Robert White as the pension fund's \ncommodity strategist.  White and Green are discussing the \ninitial implementation strategy, and White suggests an \ninvestment in a broad-based commodity index using swaps \nto gain exposure to the asset class.  Green cautions that \nindex selection is critical in establishing the proper \nexposure for the fund and makes the following statements:\nStatement 1: Due to my commodity price expectations, an \nindex with less frequent rebalancing is preferred.\nStatement 2: Production-weighted indexes provide greater \ndiversification benefits relative to fixed-weighted indexes.\nStatement 3: The index's methodology for rolling \nindividual futures contracts is critical since it is a key \ndeterminant in capturing price return.\nWhite suggests adding a fully collateralized lumber futures \nposition to aid diversification since lumber is not included \nin any of the indexes that are being considered.  The \nlumber futures data is presented in Exhibit 1:\n\nGreen has concerns about buying individual futures \ncontracts for the fund and prefers an index approach.  He \nasks White to explain the expected payoff of a fully \ncollateralized strategy, assuming the collateral earns 3%:\nBuy a July 20X3 lumber futures contract.\nAt expiration, roll that contract into a new six-month \ncontract.\nGreen provides a lumber futures price forecast in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Lucas Moreau is a real estate investment manager for La Vie \nSolide (LVS), an insurance company.  He has prepared a \npresentation for LVS's administrative personnel, who are less \nfamiliar with the types of investments the company makes.  \nThe presentation begins with a breakdown of the real estate \nsector into a matrix, using the categories of public, private, \nequity, and debt investments.\nMoreau explains that LVS invests in all four categories, but the \nmajority of its real estate holdings are illiquid and typically held \nfor many years.  The company also invests for value \nappreciation of the assets in addition to cash flows.\nThe presentation shifts to the roles of real estate in the \ninvestment portfolio.  Moreau includes the following \nstatements:\nStatement 1:\nCurrent income has historically been the largest \npart of real estate investment returns.\nStatement 2:\nThe ability to adjust rents and realize capital \nappreciation means that the nominal rate of return for real \nestate is often less volatile than the real return.\nStatement 3:\nIn general, real estate performance is highly \ncorrelated with stocks and bonds, so the investment portfolio \ndoes well when the overall market does well.\nFinally, Moreau mentions that certain characteristics of real \nestate investments distinguish them from equity and bond \ninvestments, which can make them more difficult to properly \nvalue.\nAfter completing the presentation, Moreau meets with Anna \nDupont, CFA, the senior portfolio manager at LVS.  Moreau tells \nDupont about a potential new investment opportunity in a \nproperty with a NOI of €800,000 per year.  Moreau expects \nthat:\nThe property's NOI will be flat for the next 4 years due to \nexisting leases.\nThe NOI will increase to €900,000 per year in Year 5 and grow \n2% per year thereafter.\nLVS will hold the property for 4 years and has a 10% required \nrate of return.\n\nLa Tour Financial provides interest-only loans for LVS's real \nestate investments, with requirements for two key ratios: \nthe loan-to-value ratio (LTV) ratio must be no greater than \n60% and the debt service coverage ratio (DSCR) must be at \nleast 1.4.  La Tour's own estimates and information about \nthe property purchase are shown in Exhibit 1:\nLVS may purchase the property with 100% cash or using a \n€4 million, interest-only loan.  Dupont wants to know the \nIRR of each alternative, assuming LVS holds the investment \nfor 4 years and then sells it for €12 million.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Hu Lao is a fund manager at an asset management \ncompany.  The firm has recently modified its investment \nstrategy to include real estate.  Lao meets with John Tian, a \nsenior analyst at the firm, to discuss implementing the new \nstrategy.\nLao states, \"The local market is very attractive right now \ndue to impressive job growth in our city; we should add \ncommercial real estate exposure.\"  Tian agrees and \npresents options for several REITs and limited partnerships.  \nLao asks about the differences between the two investment \nvehicles.  Tian states that one major difference concerns tax \ntreatment and makes the following comments:\nComment 1:\nREITs are unable to defer taxes on capital \ngains since profits must be distributed to investors.\nComment 2:\nUnlike REITs, limited partnerships are taxed \nat both the company level and investor level.\nLao next turns her attention to several REITs found in an \ninvestment brochure.  Lao is curious as to why market \nprices are trading at a discount to their net asset values per \nshare (NAVPS).  Tian replies with the following explanations \nas to why REIT share prices are trading at a discount to \nNAVPS:\nExplanation 1: Market prices have more quickly captured \nrecent near-term deterioration in market conditions.\nExplanation 2: Public market liquidity allows shares to trade \nat a discount to NAVPS.\nExplanation 3: Corporate income tax rates have dramatically \nincreased recently.\n\nAfter viewing the brochure, Lao becomes interested in \nLakesphere REIT and speaks with Vanessa Vuong, \nLakesphere's investor relations manager.  Vuong tells Lao \nthat Lakesphere's NAVPS will be reappraised soon, based \non a next-12-month NOI growth rate of 2.5% and a cap rate \nof 5.0%.  After the call, Vuong's team finalizes the inputs for \nupdating the NAVPS, shown in Exhibit 1:\nLater, Lao reviews several hotel REITs with Tian.  Tian \nnotices that one REIT has a much lower P/AFFO multiple \nrelative to its peers.  Tian asks Lao to investigate this \ndiscrepanc", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Keiko Takeuchi is a commodity trader with Relic Financial.  \nShe is reviewing the futures curve for the wheat market and \nnotices that the curve is more-or-less flat over the next 12 \nmonths.  Several of her clients have mentioned that wheat \nconsumers are increasingly looking to lock in future delivery \nprices during the next month, while producers' demand for \nprice protection will remain constant.\nA new client contacts Takeuchi to ask about the differences \nbetween spot prices and futures prices.  Takeuchi responds:\nResponse 1:\nBoth spot prices and futures prices are \nstandardized across regions and therefore are universal.\nResponse 2:\nAlthough standardized, futures prices can be \nadjusted to reflect potential quality differences across \nregions.\nResponse 3:\nCash settlement of a futures contract upon \nexpiration ensures price convergence between the futures \nand spot prices.\nAsahi Hara, another client of Relic, wants to roll over a \nfutures position in silver.  The futures contract expires in \nMarch, and Hara wishes to roll over half of the position to a \nMay contract.  Takeuchi collects the following data on the \nposition and the relevant contracts:\nHara also has a $100,000 position in April expiry crude oil \nfutures that he wishes to roll over until July while \nmaintaining the same exposure.  Takeuchi collects the \nfollowing data on the relevant contracts:\n\nFinally, Hara is considering adding an investment in \nlivestock futures and asks Takeuchi for investing alternatives \nand information on the market.  Takeuchi tells Hara that \nlivestock in the US is exported less than other commodities.\nTakeuchi then talks to Setsuko Inoue, a fixed income trader \nwith Relic.  She and Takeuchi discuss valuations of their \nrespective asset classes and make the following statements:\nInoue: Fixed income valuation is fundamentally different \nfrom commodity valuation.  Although fixed income \nvaluation is based on profitability and cash flow \nexpectations, commodity valuation is based on \nexpectations for supply and demand.\nTakeuchi:\nCommodities are always physical assets and, \ntherefore, considerations like storage must be accounted \nfor, adding to the complexity of forecasting.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Thalia Beltran is a recently hired associate at Galant Equity \nPartners, a private equity (PE) firm specializing in leveraged \nbuyouts (LBO).  She is assigned to work with Horace Curtis, \na managing director in charge of Galant's investment in \nFagioli Restaurants, a casual dining restaurant chain.\nCurtis wants to assess Beltran's knowledge and asks about \nthe sources of value creation in PE investments.  Beltran \nmakes the following statements:\nStatement 1: PE firms create value by reengineering \nacquired companies, whereas public companies do not \nhave that capability.\nStatement 2: PE firms execute buyouts using large \namounts of debt with restrictive covenants, reducing the \ncost of capital.\nStatement 3: Managers from acquired companies typically \nbecome more efficient due to PE firms' focus on short-term \nresults.\nCurtis explains to Beltran that Fagioli Restaurants is well \npositioned in the casual dining industry and has been \ngenerating consistent amounts of cash flow in recent years.  \nHe adds that Galant, as an LBO firm, seeks investments with \na similar profile, whereas venture capital (VC) firms typically \ninvest in early-stage companies, which have less predictable \ncash flows.  He asks Beltran if she knows other \ncharacteristics of VC firms.\nCurtis says that he is currently discussing the sale of Fagioli \nRestaurants to a strategic buyer.  He asks Beltran to \nestimate Galant's multiple of expected proceeds from the \ninvestment and provides the following information:\nGalant acquired 100% of Fagioli four years ago for $200 \nmillion.\n\nThe initial investment was 65% financed from payment-in-\nkind (PIK) coupon bonds, with annual interest of 10%.\nGalant's equity investment consisted of $40 million in \npreferred stock with a 15% annual return (paid at exit) and \n$30 million of common stock.\nThe strategic buyer is offering $317 million for 100% of \nFagioli.\nCurtis tells Beltran that Galant originally expected to sell \nFagioli for a greater exit multiple.  However, he believes \nthat Fagioli did not perform as well as expected due to a \nmisalignment of interests between the PE firm and Fagioli's \nmanagement team.  Beltran asks Curtis for examples of \nmeasures that a PE firm could implement to provide the \nright incentives to the management team of a portfolio \ncompany.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Chao Li is a vice president at Tailwind Capital, a private \nequity (PE) firm.  He is currently raising £100 million for \nTailwind Fund A.  Li meets with An Heng, an associate at \nInsular Insurance Co., a potential institutional investor.  In \ntheir meeting, Li explains the general structure of PE funds \nto Heng:\nStatement 1: The limited partners (LPs) and the general \npartner (GP) in a private equity fund participate in \nmanaging the fund.\nStatement 2: The GP is entitled to carried interest, which \nis typically a percentage of the fund's profits after \nmanagement fees.\nStatement 3: PE funds are closed-end funds, so LPs can \nredeem their investments only at specified times.\nHeng says, \"I understand there are costs associated with \ninvesting in PE, such as significant performance fees, \ndilution costs whenever the PE firm starts new funds, and \nannual audit costs.  And I know that there are some agency \nrisks involved with investing in a PE fund.  Is there a \ngovernance provision that would address GP gross \nnegligence?\"\nSubsequently, Li raises the full £100 million for Fund A.  One \nof the fund's investments is ModernWare, an online \nservices company.  The initial investment is £20 million: £13 \nmillion in debt, £2 million in preferred equity, and £5 \nmillion in the PE fund's equity.  Tailwind plans to sell \nModernWare 5 years from now and plans to reduce the \ndebt balance by £10 million by the time of exit.  The \npromised return for the preferred equity holders is 15%.\nSeveral years after the fund's inception, Tailwind exits two \ninvestments: StoneWare and BronzeWare (Exhibit 1).  \nCarried interest to the GP is accrued on a deal-by-deal basis \nand equals 20% of the profits from each exit.  The hurdle \nrate is 10%.  When a deal's IRR is above the hurdle rate, \ncarried interest is accrued; when a deal's IRR is below the \nhurdle rate, a clawback penalty amount is accrued if there \nis a loss.\n\nTwelve years after the fund's inception, Li meets with Heng \nagain to review Fund A's performance, shown in Exhibit 2.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Eduardo Herrera, a private wealth manager, is discussing \nportfolio diversification with a client, Ana Castillo.  Castillo's \ninvestment portfolio consists currently of only stocks and \nbonds (ie, traditional assets), and Herrera suggests adding \nreal estate to the portfolio since real estate is less than \nperfectly correlated with such assets.  Castillo asks about \nthe risks of real estate, and Herrera responds:\nRisk 1: Although rents often remain steady throughout the \nbusiness cycle, excess supply can affect occupancy rates.\nRisk 2: Short-term leases increase the risk of declines in \nreal rental income during periods of higher-than-expected \ninflation.\nRisk 3: Real estate competes with other assets for debt and \nequity capital, and this competition can lower prices when \nthere are shortages of capital.\nHerrera tells Castillo that REITs offer an easy way to gain \nexposure to real estate.  He says that NAV is an optimal \nstarting point to estimate a REIT's value.\nAfter reviewing her options, Castillo is interested in direct \nreal estate investments.  Herrera tells Castillo about \nProperty 1, a retail property where all the leases are \nstructured so that the tenants' rents increase once their \nsales reach a certain level.  He thinks a property with this \ntype of lease is well positioned to take advantage of an \nexpected rise in consumer spending.\nHerrera also notes that he prefers to use the income \nmethod to value real estate properties.  Castillo asks why \nthe values obtained using the direct capitalization and \ndiscounted cash flow (DCF) methods differ.\n\nIn response, Herrera explains that although both methods \nare based on a property's income, they apply income \ndifferently, and that he will use both the direct \ncapitalization and DCF valuation methods.  He provides \nCastillo with the following information about Property 1:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Barry Bohana is a new analyst at Turtlerock Partners \n(Turtlerock) a private equity (PE) firm.  Bohana is tasked to \nmeet Malia Mochi, a wealthy client who wants to learn \nabout investing in private equity.  During the meeting, \nBohana makes the following statements:\nStatement 1: Private equity firms create value by \nreengineering portfolio companies, borrowing debt at \nfavorable terms, and reducing conflicts of interest by \nseparating ownership and control.\nStatement 2: Private equity investments can be broadly \ncategorized into venture capital (VC) and buyout (LBO) \nfunds.  VC investments are primarily funded with debt, have \nlow cash predictability, and have a well-defined route-to-\nmarket strategy.\nNext, Bohana presents information (Exhibit 1) for a \nleveraged buyout opportunity of Baluga Corp. (Baluga), a \njewelry manufacturer.\nIn addition, Bohana makes the following stipulations related \nto Baluga at exit, after an expected holding period of five \nyears:\ndebt is expected to have been reduced by $45 million,\npreference shareholders are promised a 15% annual return,\nPE fund equity is promised 95% of the firm's residual value \nafter creditors and preference shareholders are paid, and\nmanagement is promised the remaining 5% of the firm's \nresidual value.\n\nMochi responds by asking to see Turtlerock's funds' \nperformance history.  Bohana addresses Mochi's request by \npresenting a historical performance for Orca Fund, \nTurtlerock's primary fund.  Bohana clarifies that carried \ninterest is initially calculated from the difference between \nNAV before distributions and committed capital, and \nsubsequently calculated from the difference between NAV \nbefore distributions in any given year and NAV before \ndistributions in the prior year.\nMochi wants to interpret the schedule and asks for the \nrealized return metric for Orca Fund.  Bohana responds by \nsaying, \"the appropriate measure of PE realized returns is \ntotal value to paid-in capital (TVPI).  The TVPI for Orca is \n2.79x.\"", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c15_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c15_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Rylan Jerold is the chief investment officer at the Crest \nfamily office and oversees their large investment portfolio.  \nThe Crest family wants to add real estate to their traditional \nstock and bond portfolio.  Jerold is new to real estate \ninvesting and schedules several meetings to learn about \npotential opportunities.\nFirst, Jerold meets with his friend Richard Redmond, a vice \npresident at Hightower Realty Capital.  Redmond describes \nHightower's primary investment approach:\n\"Hightower typically takes passive ownership stakes in \nproperties with large minimum capital requirements and \nlong capital lockup periods.  We benefit primarily from \nincome and capital appreciation.\"\nJerold mentions the Crest family's interest in investing in \nhotels and wants to understand the major factors that \nimpact returns for that sector.  Redmond explains to Jerold \nthe main characteristics of the hotel sector and how returns \nare affected by various factors.\nJerold then meets with Leroy Langston, a commercial real \nestate broker, to better understand the different categories \nof commercial real estate.  Langston explains that the \nprimary commercial property types are office, industrial, \nretail, and multifamily, and makes the following statements:\nStatement 1: A percentage lease is a feature unique to \nretail properties for which the rent is based on a constant \npercentage of the total sales.\nStatement 2: In a gross lease, the property owner is \nresponsible for operating expenses, which increases the risk \nfor the owner.\n\nStatement 3: The demand for multifamily rental properties \nwill decrease if the cost of owning a home is more than the \ncost of renting.\nLangston markets a 100-unit apartment complex selling for \n$30 million to Jerold, who meets with the property's \nmanagement team to gather information for due diligence.  \nDuring the meeting, Jerold reviews the costs and incentives \nthat are provided to the renewing tenants and new tenants.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Diana Dragomir is the chief investment officer at \nWestPacific Regional Bank.  WestPacific's board recently \ndecided to diversify the bank's assets by investing in real \nestate.  Dragomir meets with Vivek Varghese, the \ninvestment committee chair, to discuss possibly including \npublicly traded real estate securities in the investment \nstrategy.\nDragomir tells Varghese that \"compared with directly \ninvesting in real estate, there are many advantages to \ninvesting in publicly traded REITs, such as superior liquidity, \nlower investment minimums, and greater transparency.\"  \nVarghese agrees with those benefits but also wants to know \nthe drawbacks to investing in REITs.\nVarghese then asks how REITs are valued.  Dragomir replies \nthat REITs can be valued through methods such as net asset \nvalue per share (NAVPS), price multiples, and discounted \ncash flow.  She further explains that NAVPS is based on a \nREIT's net assets, and she makes the following statements:\nStatement 1: NAVPS also includes non-real estate asset \ncomponents.\nStatement 2: NAVPS is based on book value per share of \nassets.\nStatement 3: All else equal, a higher cap rate results in a \nhigher NAVPS.\nDragomir then explains how REITs are valued using price \nmultiples.  Dragomir demonstrates an estimated valuation \nfor Oceanwave REIT and explains that this valuation \nincludes Oceanwave's noncash rent.  Oceanwave has 108 \nmillion shares outstanding, and the price per share is $50.  \nOther data for Oceanwave is shown in Exhibit 1:\n\nIn the afternoon, Dragomir spots a post on social media for \nAVTD Holding Company.  The post explains that AVTD \nprimarily develops and sells properties, reinvests significant \nportions of its profits, and offers brokerage and property \nmanagement services.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 18, "vignette": "Marcel Jaga is a high-net-worth investor who has \ntraditionally invested in stocks and bonds.  Jaga wants to \ndiversify his portfolio by investing in real estate.  Jaga meets \nwith his friend Lea Daniela, an experienced real estate \ninvestor, to learn more and potentially partner together on \nfuture opportunities.  Daniela explains that there are three \ncommon approaches to valuation:\nApproach 1:\nThe cost approach is based on the cost of the \nland and the building's current replacement cost net of any \ndepreciation or obsolescence.\nApproach 2:\nThe sales comparison approach is based on \ncomparisons to historical sales transactions over long \nperiods.\nApproach 3:\nThe gross income multiplier is an income \napproach that factors in vacancy rates and operating \nexpenses when estimating property values.\nDaniela states that under the income approach there are \ntwo methods: the direct capitalization (direct cap) method \nand the discounted cash flow (DCF) method.  Daniela \nelaborates with the following statements:\nStatement 1: Under the direct cap method, a lower \ngrowth rate results in a higher cap rate, which results in a \nlower valuation. \nStatement 2: Under the DCF method, a cap rate is not \nneeded since the value of a property is derived by \ndiscounting projected cash flows.\nStatement 3: The cap rate used in the direct cap method is \nthe residual cap rate.\nNext Daniela informs Jaga that she is currently assessing a \nmulti-family opportunity; if she purchases the property, she \nplans on selling it at the end of Year 4.  She plans to use \nboth the direct cap method and the DCF method for \nvaluation, averaging the values from both approaches.  She \ncollects the following data, shown in Exhibit 1:\n\nA few weeks later, a real estate broker approaches Jaga with \na different investment opportunity.  Jaga accepts the deal \nand then visits a bank to obtain an interest-only loan.  The \nloan officer gathers relevant information, shown in Exhibit \n2, to assess the maximum loan amount available.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c18_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c18_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c18_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c18_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_alt_inv", "source_label": "UWorld – Alternative Investments", "topic": "Alternative Investments", "case_no": 19, "vignette": "Chris Ward is the Chief Financial Officer of Pillar Energy, an \nintegrated oil and gas company.  Pillar regularly sells \nportions of its inventory during different stages of the \nrefining process.  Ward is exploring alternatives to hedge \nthe price of Pillar's crude oil and natural gas sales to reduce \nprice risk.\nThe January contract for Brent crude oil, which is the \nnearest contract to expiration, is currently trading at \n$73/barrel.  The January–March calendar spread is \n−$4.00/barrel.  Based on the type of contract, Ward knows \nthat the convergence of Brent futures and spot rate prices \nis ensured.\nWard decides to enter an excess return swap to hedge \nPillar's crude oil risk.  The swap will cost Pillar $50 per \ncontract and Pillar will receive the amount exceeding \n$72/barrel each month over the next year.  Ward creates a \nforecast of the expected crude oil futures prices at delivery \nover the next 12 months:\n\nWorking for an integrated oil and gas company, Ward \nbelieves that he has more information about the future \nsupply and demand dynamics of oil and gas than the \naverage investor.  However, he is focused solely on hedging \nPillar's future production.  He realizes that other companies \nin the industry tend to speculate more.\nWard wishes to estimate what the futures price of natural \ngas should be in June based on the theory of storage.  He \ngathers the following current data:\nWard plans to hedge Pillar's natural gas production market \nto market.  Pillar produces some of its natural gas in smaller \nmarkets that are more thinly traded, but Ward wants to peg \nthe price of this production to the more liquid Henry Hub \nnatural gas.  He plans to enter a swap to pay the difference \nin price between the illiquid markets and the more \ncommonly traded natural gas prices.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c19_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c19_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c19_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c19_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c19_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_alt_inv_c19_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Mila Cote, an environmental, social, and governance (ESG) \nconsultant, is hired by Conductor Mining to review its \npolicies and recommend improvements.  Conductor is a \nmetals mining company that was founded by a member of \nthe Bouchard family, who together are the largest \nshareholder and also hold executive management \npositions.  Cote meets with Oliver Bouchard, CEO of \nConductor, and suggests starting with governance policies.  \nBouchard provides Cote the following information about \nConductor and its peers:\nBouchard adds the following information:\nConductor's audit, compensation, and nomination \ncommittees are made up of independent directors.  The risk \nand investment committees are headed by family directors.\nConductor has cross-ownership agreements with one of its \nsuppliers and one of its distributors.  Conductor has \npurchased stock in those other companies, and the \nmanagement teams of those companies have similarly \npurchased positions in Conductor, with an agreement to \nhold the stock for the foreseeable future.\n\nBouchard states that he has been receiving more ESG-\nrelated questions from investors.  He asks Cote how \ninvestors typically use disclosed ESG-related information \nand what Conductor can do to attract more ESG-focused \ninvestors.  Cote states:\nStatement 1:  Most countries have mandated ESG-related \ndisclosures, which allows analysts to view a consistent set \nof metrics across companies.\nStatement 2:  Equity and credit analysts often integrate ESG \nfactors in similar ways, such as through adjusting forecasted \nfinancial metrics and ratios.\nStatement 3:  Green bonds, which can help fund green \nprojects, will have lower credit ratings since they can be \nrepaid only by funds from environmentally friendly \nprojects.\nAfter reviewing Conductor's operations and the industry in \nmore detail, Cote notes that the country where Conductor \noperates is discussing new environmental regulations.  One \nset of regulations that is likely to become law would require \nmining companies to use environmentally friendly mining \ntechniques in 50% of their operations within 5 years.  Cote \nnotes that only 25% of Conductor's operations meet the \nstandard currently and that upgrades to existing equipment \nare available but costly.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Amelia Nowicki, chief financial officer of Skyline Wireless, \ndiscusses ways to attract new equity investors with the \ncompany's chief executive officer, Elias Keller.  Skyline \noperates in a country with a dividend imputation tax \nsystem.\nNowicki believes the initiation of a dividend next year \n(20X8) will be most effective in attracting new investors.  \nOn average, she found that competitors' share prices \noutperform the market the year after initiating a dividend.  \nKeller agrees that many investors view dividend initiations \npositively, though he also expects that some may view it \nnegatively.\nNowicki provides three factors that she expects will affect \nSkyline's dividend policy in the coming years if a dividend is \ninitiated:\nFactor 1: Skyline has fewer profitable investment \nopportunities than it did in prior years.\nFactor 2: Skyline's sales are dependent on large contracts \nwith a small number of customers.\nFactor 3: As a relatively small company, Skyline would have \nhigh flotation costs.\nNowicki indicates a good target for a dividend coverage \nratio is 1.5, but she is concerned about the company's other \ncompeting uses of cash and wants a measure that factors \nthis into the coverage ratio.  Nowicki compiles the following \nfinancial forecast for 20X8:\n\nNowicki indicates that she intends to use cash on hand to \npay the dividend.  Keller is concerned about maintaining \ncompliance with bond covenants on Skyline's debt.  He asks \nNowicki what impact a dividend would have on the \ncompany's leverage ratios.\nAs an alternative to initiating a dividend, Keller suggests \nthat Skyline split its stock two-for-one.  He believes Skyline's \nstock price is too high, which limits the stock's potential \ninvestor base.  He also expects the company to have a more \nfavorable P/E ratio after the split since the company will \nhave more shares outstanding, lowering its EPS.  Skyline has \nthe following market and financial data:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Nigel Thompson is an equity analyst covering the global \nauto parts industry at a US-based buy-side firm.  Thompson \nis in the initial stages of analyzing two public companies: \nUS-based Acme Auto and a direct competitor, Universal \nAutomóvel, which is based in the emerging market country \nof Brazil.  Thompson begins the analysis by compiling 20 \nyears of monthly data to estimate Acme's cost of equity, \nshown in Exhibit 1:\nThompson decides to estimate Acme's cost of equity using \nthe bond yield plus risk premium (BYPRP) approach.  He \nalso wants to compare those results with the Fama-French \nfive-factor model (FF5), using the past 60 months of data, \npresented in Exhibit 2:\nThompson turns his attention to Universal; he believes the \nCAPM equity risk premium presented in Exhibit 1 is a good \nstarting point for his analysis, but also recognizes the need \nto incorporate a country risk premium (CRP) into the \nanalysis.  He considers the following options:\n\nMethod 1:\nCountry spread model\nMethod 2:\nGlobal CAPM\nMethod 3:\nInternational CAPM\nThompson selects the Damodaran cost of equity model for \nhis analysis of Universal.  Relevant information is presented \nin Exhibit 3:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Ganesh Kaur, CFA, is an investor researching two companies as \npotential new investments.  National Gas & Electric (NG&E) is a \nlarge, publicly traded utility that has been a consistent dividend \npayer for several years.  Revive Inc. is a food and beverage \ncompany that has sustained above-average revenue growth \nand has been paying a dividend for several years using different \npayout policies.\nNG&E\nKaur expects NG&E to increase its target dividend payout ratio \nthis year and collects the following data:\nKaur expects NG&E to use a stable dividend policy and to reach \nits target dividend payout ratio over a four-year period.  The \ncountry in which NG&E operates uses a dividend imputation \ntax system.  The corporate tax rate for NG&E is 20%; investors \nin the country have a 15% tax rate on dividends.\nKaur wants to determine whether NG&E's higher dividend \npayout ratio is sustainable in the coming years.  He considers \nthe following:\nFactor 1:  Cutting capital expenditures to fund dividends \nprovides more cash for dividends and is a positive indicator of \nsustainability.\nFactor 2:  Coverage ratios that measure dividend safety should \nconsider dividend payments but not share repurchases.\nFactor 3:  Above-average financial leverage can foretell more \nrestricted use of new debt to cover future dividend payments.\n\nRevive Inc.\nKaur collects Revive's dividend history:\nRevive's management has also indicated plans to pay a one-\ntime 5% stock dividend if earnings reach a certain threshold \nnext year.  The stock dividend would supplement any cash \ndividends it may initiate.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Armando Stanton is the CFO of Solstice Auto Parts, a traditional \nautomotive valve manufacturer that started producing car \nsensors in recent years.  New sensor technologies developed by \nSolstice improved the company's market share and the \nprospects of its sensors segment.  As a result, Solstice \nincreasingly shifted its focus to that segment and diversified its \nproduction to include home security and industrial sensors.\nStanton asks Flora Mays, Solstice's preferred investment \nbanker, about strategic alternatives to maximize Solstice's \nvalue.  He summarizes information from Solstice's segments in \nExhibit 1:\nMays explains that Solstice has a conglomerate discount based \non the information provided and recent market data:\nSolstice has 25 million shares outstanding at a market price of \n$250.00 per share.\nSolstice's debt has a market value of $2.5 billion.\nPeers in the valves segment have a median EV/EBITDA multiple \nof 9.0.\nRecent transactions in the valves segment resulted in an \naverage takeover premium of 20.0%.\nPeers in the sensors segment have a median EV-to-sales (EV/S) \nmultiple of 10.0.\nMays tells Stanton that she was contacted by a strategic \ninvestor interested in purchasing 100% of Solstice's valves \nsegment for $6.5 billion, not assuming any debt.  She adds that \nthe offer is greater than the price the bank estimates for the \nvalves segment and advises Stanton to accept.\nStanton tells Mays that other auto parts companies recently \ndecided to spin off certain segments instead of selling them.  \nMays explains that spin-offs tend to provide less liquidity to the \nparent company, but they also have some advantages when \ncompared with a sale.\n\nStanton tells Mays that he is not sure this is the best time to \ndivest.  He acknowledges that Solstice's management has \nneglected the valves segment in recent years and needs to \nimprove the segment's margins; however, if the company \ncould restructure the segment and improve its EBITDA \nmargin, he believes it could be sold for a greater multiple in \nthe future.  He asks for Mays's advice, and she lists the \nfollowing alternatives:\nAlternative 1: Outsource part of the valves segment \nactivities to a low-cost supplier.\nAlternative 2: Form a joint venture with a foreign low-cost \ncompetitor.\nAlternative 3: Sell and lease back manufacturing plants and \nvehicles.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Erica Pearce is a portfolio manager for Tenor Financial.  Pearce \nasks Young-Jae Cho, an analyst at Tenor, to review several of \nthe company's positions for environmental, social, and \ngovernance (ESG) factors.  Pearce's preference is to use \nframeworks backed by nonprofit organizations when \nidentifying companies' ESG factors.\nCho and Pearce first look at Galaxy Technologies.  Galaxy's \nlargest and most influential shareholder is the Gallagher family.  \nPearce collects governance-related information for Galaxy and \none of its peers that has no influential shareholder:\nPearce then analyzes the debt securities for Wellness Insurance \nheld by Tenor.  Tenor currently holds recently issued 3-year and \n8-year bonds for Wellness.  Pearce learns that a new regulation \nwill force Wellness to hold a greater amount of cash as \nreserves, limiting the cash available to reinvest in its business \nand pay its capital providers.\nThe regulation is expected to go into effect 4 years from now, \nbut Wellness will begin building up its cash reserves 6 quarters \nbefore the regulation goes into effect (or 2.5 years from now).  \nPearce suggests that Cho adjust each bond's credit spread used \nfor the valuation to update the bonds' estimated values based \non the regulation's effects.\nWellness just announced plans to issue a green bond, the \nproceeds of which will be used for environmentally friendly \nprojects.  Pearce is reviewing pricing expectations for the green \nbond against the other Wellness bonds that Tenor holds.  Cho \nnotes that overall demand for green bonds is strong and they \nare rarely issued from this sector.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Compass Engineering's board of directors is deciding \nbetween cash dividends or a share repurchase program as a \nmethod to begin returning cash to shareholders.  Liam \nFischer, a member of the board, states that both cash \ndividends and share repurchases have certain advantages \nthat should be considered:\nAdvantage 1: Share repurchases tend to be more flexible. \nAlthough dividends can be raised, lowered, or suspended, \nthey appear to create an expectation among investors that \nthe distribution will continue in the future. Share \nrepurchases do not seem to create the same expectation.\nAdvantage 2: If the tax rates for capital gains and dividends \nare the same, and the information content is the same, \nthen shareholders' wealth will be greater with cash \ndividends since all shareholders receive cash.\nAlicia Wu, the chairman of the board, indicates that \nCompass should review the long-term trends in the country \n(the United Kingdom) before deciding.\nAfter deliberation, the board favors a share repurchase \nprogram.  Jessica King, another member of the board, is \nconcerned about the effect of share repurchases on \nCompass' EPS.  She collects the following data for 20X2:\n\nIn addition, King wonders what effect share repurchases \nhave on companies' book values.  She gathers data from \nthree of Compass' peers who have made a share \nrepurchase:\nThe board members then discuss the best approach for \nCompass to repurchase shares.  King has identified two \npossible scenarios, both using the same amount to \nrepurchase shares:\nScenario 1:\nPurchase shares using all cash on hand.\nScenario 2:\nPurchase shares with funds from an issuance \nof debt.\nWu states that her preference is to choose a method by \nwhich the company can control the process and execute it \nquickly.  She also prefers a process that would allow \nCompass to discover the minimum price at which it can \nrepurchase the desired number of shares.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Edoardo Tedesco is a junior analyst covering the consumer \nstaples sector at an Italian investment bank.  Tedesco \nbelieves that the number of corporate transactions in the \nsector will increase significantly and calls his colleague \nTatiana Galasso, a senior analyst.  He asks her about the \nmost common forms of corporate restructurings, and she \nmakes the following statements:\nStatement 1: A joint venture is created by two or more \ncompanies, which remain independent from each other.\nStatement 2: A divestiture usually raises less liquidity than \na spin-off.\nStatement 3: A balance sheet restructuring always reduces \nthe size of the firm or the scope of the business.\nGalasso explains to Tedesco that companies have different \nmotivations to engage in investments, divestments, and \nrestructurings.  She lists some common issuer-specific \nmotivations and the actions associated with them.\nTedesco turns his attention to Raganello Food, a company \nthat is seeking to acquire SottoZero, a smaller player in the \nfrozen food business.  He summarizes both companies' \nexpected revenues and operating expenses for the next \nthree years in Exhibit 1:\n\nTedesco believes that the acquisition can bring long-term \nbenefits for Raganello in the form of cost synergies but \nwould also require cash costs in the first three years.  To \nassess the outcome of a potential acquisition occurring at \nthe beginning of Year 1, he estimates the following \nsynergies and costs for Raganello, shown in Exhibit 2:\nTedesco notices that Raganello's beverage manufacturing \nplant, located in Italy, has been hurting the company's \nresults for the last few years.  The plant is outdated, with \nlow productivity and high operating expenses.  Tedesco \nbelieves that investing in the existing plant is not \neconomically viable and asks Galasso what actions the \ncompany could take.  She replies that Raganello could \nimplement a cost restructuring action by closing the \nexisting plant and offshoring its beverage manufacturing \nbusiness.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Iordanis Zervou is a portfolio manager at an asset \nmanagement firm.  Zervou is considering an increase in the \nfirm's exposure to ElatiTech Computers, a large \nmanufacturer of laptop and desktop computers.  Recently, \nElatiTech's CEO stated that the consistent growth of the \neconomy had increased management's confidence.  Soon \nafterward, ElatiTech announced its interest in acquiring \nGiona Tech, a small company that develops and sells \ninnovative cloud computing software.\nZervou calls Antonia Chalaris, his colleague and a senior \nanalyst covering the technology industry.  He asks Chalaris if \nshe believes that the current strong-growth economic \nenvironment is favorable for acquisitions.  Chalaris\nresponds by comparing acquisitions made during \nexpansionary periods with acquisitions made when the \neconomy is weak.\nChalaris conducts an initial evaluation of the deal, based on \nElatiTech's announcement, and reaches the following \nconclusions:\nThe transaction would cost approximately €600 million in \ncash, 100% financed with new debt, plus the assumption of \nGiona's debt, estimated at €250 million.\nElatiTech's current enterprise value is approximately €29 \nbillion.\nElatiTech is expected to shift to a new strategy that \nemphasizes the cloud computing software industry, which \nhas been growing significantly faster than ElatiTech's\ncomputer manufacturing business.\nChalaris says that her team conducted a preliminary \nvaluation of Giona using comparable company analysis.  \nZervou asks her to provide more details, and she lists \nadvantages and disadvantages of using the comparable \ncompany method to estimate a fair acquisition price.\nChalaris adds that her team has already identified three \nrecent acquisitions involving other companies in which the \ntargets were considered similar to Giona, and her team has \nmade a comparative transaction analysis with the following \nassumptions:\n\nThe analysis is based on the average P/E and average price-\nto-book (P/B) multiples.\nThe price estimate is based on 60% P/E and 40% P/B.\nChalaris presents selected data from Giona and from the \ncomparable acquisitions in Exhibit 1:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Nyasha Thabethe is an equity analyst at Mountain View \nAsset Management.  Thabethe is researching Revolution \nMedical Devices Inc., a small-cap publicly traded company.  \nRevolution's capital structure and the information needed \nto determine the company's WACC are presented in Exhibit \n1:\nTo estimate the cost of debt, Thabethe gathers information \non similar actively traded BBB-rated bonds, shown in \nExhibit 2:\nThabethe uses the discount dividend model (DDM) \napproach to estimate the equity risk premium (ERP) and \nRevolution's cost of equity.  She gathers relevant \ninformation, shown in Exhibit 3:\n\nTo check her assumptions, Thabethe also calculates the cost \nof equity using three other methods.  The methods and \nresults are presented in Exhibit 4:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Rodrigo Neves is a credit analyst for Horizonte Capital \nPartners.  He is reviewing his analysis of three companies \nand plans to update each company's model to include \nenvironmental, social, and governance (ESG) factors.  He \nbegins by analyzing how consistently investors consider \neach of the individual ESG factors on a regional basis.\nTo begin his research, Neves collects ESG information on \neach of the companies:\nWhile Neves is doing the research, Company A announces \nthat a hedge fund took a 6% stake in the company and has \npresented a list of suggestions for management to improve \nshareholder value, including adding two of the fund's \npartners to the company's board.\nCompany B currently has a single-share class structure but \nis planning to issue a new class of shares through an IPO.  \nThe company is considering several structures.\nHorizonte owns Company C's 3-year bond.  Recently, a new \nregulation was enacted and, as a result, in five years one of \nCompany C's chemical production plants will no longer be \nfinancially viable.  Neves expects Company C to begin \noperation of a replacement production plant next year, \nwhich will increase forecasted capex by 30% each year.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_corp", "source_label": "UWorld – Corporate Issuers", "topic": "Corporate Issuers", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "During the summer of 20X2, Stanislaw Grajek, a junior \nanalyst covering the automotive sector for an investment \nbank, is investigating potential acquisitions in the sector.  \nHe asks his supervisor at the bank, Klementyna Lisowska, \nhow an acquisition may affect the acquirer's earnings and \ncost of capital.  She replies that projecting these effects \nrequires building pro forma financial statements of the \ncombined operations of the acquirer and target and \ncomparing them with the acquirer's metrics before the \nacquisition.  She then observes that, all else equal:\nObservation 1:The acquirer's cost of capital may change \ndue to factors such as profitability and volatility of \nrevenues.\nObservation 2:A leveraged acquisition increases the \nacquirer's cost of equity and WACC.\nObservation 3:A leveraged acquisition of a profitable target \nincreases the acquirer's EPS.\nLisowska tells Grajek that Resistenza Motors has just \nannounced its interest in acquiring a smaller competitor, \nMuronka Autoparts.  She asks him to analyze the proposed \nacquisition and the potential changes to the acquirer's \ncapital structure.  Grajek presents current information \nabout Resistenza Motors in Exhibit 1:\nGrajek then summarizes Resistenza's offer for Muronka in \nExhibit 2:\n\nLisowska asks Grajek to construct the 20X3 pro forma \nfinancial statements for the combined entity assuming the \nacquisition occurs at the end of 20X2.  In addition to the \ncompanies' forecasted standalone financial statements, \npresented in Exhibit 3, she asks him to use the following \nassumptions for 20X3:\nCost synergies:  €650 million\nInterest expense:  €1,940 million on total debt, including \nassumed debt and new debt\nAmortization of acquired intangible assets not recognized in \nMuronka's balance sheet:  €410 million\nEffective income tax rate:  25%\nLater, in a conference call, Resistenza Motors informs \ninvestors that the company is considering a change in the \noriginal offer, substituting the €4.5 billion of new debt with \ncash since the company currently holds a comfortable cash \nposition of €8.0 billion.  The company adds that the total \nconsideration and equity financing would remain \nunchanged.  Grajek asks Lisowska how that change would \nimpact Resistenza's pro forma financial statements, \nparticularly net debt-to-EBITDA and EPS, when compared \nwith the original offer.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_corp_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_corp_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Continental Inc. is a full-service brokerage firm catering to \ninstitutional buy-side investors. During the normal course \nof business, Continental continuously buys and sells all \ntypes of commonly traded bonds and regularly maintains a \nlarge inventory of bonds to facilitate client transactions.\nRoger Smith, chief risk officer at Continental, is responsible \nfor monitoring the risk of the firm's bond inventory.\nSmith is evaluating Continental's risk policies and discusses \nthe firm's policies with Edgar Monroe, a newly hired risk \nanalyst. Smith asks Monroe how familiar he is with value at \nrisk (VaR). Monroe replies that he is generally familiar with \nthree methods of VaR estimation:\nMethod 1: Parametric\nMethod 2: Historical simulation \nMethod 3: Monte Carlo simulation\nSmith then states that Continental uses a 5% VaR model, \nwhich estimates expected returns, volatilities, and \ncorrelations for each risk factor, assuming a normal return \ndistribution. Portfolio details are presented in Exhibit 1:\nSmith then asks Monroe to provide a historical simulation \nVaR for comparative purposes. Monroe provides Smith \nwith the following data and states that the historical \nanalysis is based on a lookback period of two years.\n\nMonroe discusses the findings with Smith and makes the \nfollowing observations:\nObservation 1: This analysis is a more accurate estimate of \nrisk since it uses historical data.\nObservation 2: £3,355,647 is 1.65 standard deviations from \nthe mean.\nObservation 3: These calculations are derived from the\ncurrent portfolio constituents only.\nMonroe then comments that this specific portfolio is \nsubject to the following limitations of VaR:\nLimitation 1: Correlation and liquidity risk \nLimitation 2: Subjectivity and extreme event risk\nLimitation 3: Oversimplification and trading regime risk", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Elizabeth Ngobeni is a newly hired performance attribution \nanalyst at an investment consulting firm. Maria Mabaso is a \nsenior research analyst at the firm and Ngobeni's \nsupervisor. Mabaso quizzes Ngobeni on her fundamental \nknowledge of factor models and asks her to describe the \narbitrage pricing theory (APT). Ngobeni responds by stating \nthe key underlying assumptions of APT:\nAssumption 1: No arbitrage opportunities exist among well- \ndiversified portfolios.\nAssumption 2: Investors are able to form portfolios that are \nfree of factor risk but still subject to asset-specific risk.\nAssumption 3: APT describes asset returns through the \nthree factors of portfolio beta, value, and market \ncapitalization.\nNgobeni continues by comparing the Carhart model to \nCAPM and APT:\nComparison 1: The Carhart model is an APT model.\nComparison 2: The Carhart model is a multifactor extension \nof CAPM.\nComparison 3: Persistent market anomalies that are difficult \nto explain in a CAPM framework are systematic risk factors \nin the Carhart model.\nMabaso then provides a statistical one-factor model, shown \nin Exhibit 1, that describes the expected return for three \nequal dollar-weighted portfolios, along with their \nassociated expected returns and factor sensitivity. She asks \nNgobeni to test for an arbitrage opportunity with these \nthree portfolios.\n\nMabaso shifts the discussion to the pros and cons of \nstatistical fixed-income factor models, and then to the \npotential benefits of factor models in strategic portfolio \ndecision-making. Ngobeni adds that in strategic portfolio \ndecisions, multifactor models help investors to:\nComment 1: \nportfolios\nComment 2:\nDevelop more efficient, better diversified\nAllocate to risks in which investors have a\ncomparative advantage\nComment 3: Develop market timing models between the \nmarket portfolio and a risk-free asset to adjust risk \nexposure", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Elif Yildiz is an equity portfolio manager at a corporate \npension plan. The plan invests in multiple equity funds, \neach managed by a different manager. Yildiz is conducting \na search for a new equity manager who would diversify the \nexisting mix of purely fundamental, bottom-up managers.\nYildiz instructs Anushka Patel, a research analyst at the plan, \nto prepare information on three new managers who invest \npredominately with a top-down macro approach, \nminimizing stock selection risk. This information is \npresented in Exhibit 1:\nYildiz then reviews last year's factor sensitivity and return \ninformation for the Value Fund, one of the current equity \nfunds used by the plan. Yildiz notes the benchmark for the \nValue Fund is appropriate. This information is presented in \nExhibit 2:\nYildiz expects the equity momentum factor to outperform \nover the near term and is concerned about the pension \nplan's active exposure to this factor. She asks Patel to \nidentify three factor portfolios that could be added to the \nplan to hedge the momentum risk exposure. These \nportfolios are presented in Exhibit 3:\n\nYildiz then considers the plan's asset allocation mix.  The \nplan uses a two-factor macroeconomic model to assist in \nasset allocation weighting decisions. Based on the plan's \ndiversified asset allocation approach, the weighted average \nfactor risk premium for the portfolio is 2.5%. The portion of \nthe return not explained by the two-factor macroeconomic \nmodel is 0.5%. Information for the last year is presented in \nExhibit 4:\nPatel then reminds Yildiz of the multiple risk dimensions \nthat must be considered when arriving at an asset \nallocation policy. Patel makes the following statement \nregarding the pension plan:\n\"The plan is sponsored by a highly leveraged cyclical \ncompany whose revenue and profitability are very sensitive \nto economic fluctuations. The plan's participants are \nrelatively young compared with many other plans in the \nsame industry.\"", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Tatiana Rybakova is a trader at Super-Fast Inc., a proprietary \nelectronic trading firm. Rybakova recently hired Igor \nZhukov as a trading assistant. Rybakova explains to Zhukov \nthe three primary trading strategies of the firm by providing \nexamples of recent trades:\nTrade 1: \nA trade involving an equity short sell and an \nalmost simultaneous purchase to close the position. The \ntrade was small, only a few hundred shares on an actively \ntraded name, and was completed in a fraction of a second, \nearning a small profit. The firm often completes hundreds \nof these trades per day.\nTrade 2: \nThe firm's proprietary trading algorithm, at \nits own discretion, bought shares of a stock immediately \nafter earnings results were released, initially pushing the \nprice higher, and closed out the position an hour later at \neven higher prices.\nTrade 3: \nUsing data-mining software, Super-Fast is \noften able to identify and predict the algorithmic trading \npattern of brokerage firms. In this case, the software \nindicated that more selling of a stock's shares was \nimminent, so Super-Fast shorted shares of the stock and \nthen closed the position by the end of the day.\nRybakova then provides a hypothetical example of another \ntrading strategy that is used only occasionally.\nTrade 4: \nUsing a pegged limit order below the best \nask (ie, Super-Fast is the buyer) on Exchange A, the order is \nfilled when a large seller takes out numerous bids in the \nlimit order book at one time (ie, large market order).\nSuper-Fast's electronic trading system immediately sells the \nposition using a market order on another exchange before \nother participants can change their orders, generating a\nsmall profit.\n\nRybakova explains to Zhukov that each strategy is \nthoroughly tested and validated before it is used in live \nmarkets. Even with thorough processes and procedures, \nmistakes have happened.\nFor example, due to a programming error, one of Super- \nFast's high-frequency trading strategies erroneously began \nbuying at the ask and selling at the bid. This resulted in \nsubstantial losses: hundreds of executions occurred before \nit could be shut down.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Evergreen Investment Management (Evergreen) is the fund \nsponsor for the New Growth Technology (NGT) ETF. NGT's \nasset base has grown substantially in recent years due to a \nlow fee structure, low tracking error, and, in general, a \nrelative outperformance over technology stocks.\nNGT is now one of the largest and most actively traded ETFs \nin the market. Dragon Trading (Dragon) is an authorized \nparticipant (AP) for NGT and one of its most active market \nmakers.\nSelected data for NGT is presented below:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "A client has requested that Wolfgang Schmidt, an analyst at \nBacktesting Inc., run a multifactor portfolio backtest to \nassess historical performance and then run several \nsimulations using different assumptions. The client has \nidentified six value-oriented fundamental factors to \nbacktest and has selected a widely available value index as \nthe investable universe. Schmidt will use 30 years of data \nwith monthly rebalancing and a rolling-window procedure.\nSchmidt's data provider has point-in-time constituent data \nbut no other point-in-time information. Companies in the \nindex report financial results on a quarterly basis. To help \ncalibrate the model, Schmidt conducts an initial backtest \nwith Factor 1, using numerous monthly reporting lags to \nfine-tune the analysis for the other factors. Schmidt then \nreviews the results:\nSchmidt then designs a benchmark portfolio (BM) and a risk \nparity portfolio (RP) and runs a backtest and three \nsimulations to generate investment performance based on \nthe underlying factor portfolios. Each simulation is \nconstructed from 1,000 trial runs. The three simulation \napproaches are as follows:\n\nApproach 1: Historical simulation\nApproach 2: Monte Carlo simulation (multivariate normal \ndistribution)\nApproach 3: Monte Carlo simulation (skewed Student's t- \ndistribution)\nSchmidt then reviews the results, comparing the downside\nrisk characteristics of each approach.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Yannick Radkov is a fund manager for Fund One, a multi- \nasset fund for Destiny Financial. Radkov is reviewing the \neconomic landscape with Inge Holland, an analyst at \nDestiny, and how changes to the landscape may affect the \nfund's holdings.\nHolland states that the country's economy is showing signs \nof worsening. Holland notes that the yield curve has \ninverted and states that this often happens before a \nrecession. Radkov is curious how a worsening of the \neconomy will affect Fund One and collects information on a \nselection of the fund's holdings:\nHolland notes that it will be difficult to compare all of the \nassets in the fund since they have different hedging \nproperties against bad economic outcomes. She uses three \nexamples of assets held in the fund:\nA 3-month T-bill\nA 10-year government bond\nA noncyclical, dividend-paying stock\nAnother Fund One investment is a commercial real property \nthat it rents to a developed-economy government tenant \nfor a fixed amount not indexed to inflation. Radkov \nquestions the discount rate used to value this property. He \nprovides data on the risk factors relevant for that tenant \nand asks Holland to calculate the correct discount rate:\n\nFinally, Radkov considers Fund One's equity holdings. He is \ncurious what might happen to P/E values when the \neconomy improves. Radkov decides to look at the most \nrecent recession and notices that P/E values for the stock \nmarket were below their historical average after the \nrecession ended. He considers three potential reasons:\nReal interest rates\nUncertainty about future inflation \nExpectation for future real earnings growth\nHolland adds that the changes to the economy are likely to \naffect cyclical companies and noncyclical companies \ndifferently, stating:\nStatement 1: Poorly managed cyclical companies are more \nlikely to be exposed during an economic boom than during \nweaker economic periods.\nStatement 2: Noncyclical companies' earnings growth is \nunaffected by the business cycle.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Sri Patel, CFA, is a registered investment adviser for high- \nnet-worth US retail clients. To construct client portfolios, \nPatel mainly uses exchange-traded funds (ETFs), preferring \ntheir low cost and the ease of using them to implement \nboth core strategic asset class exposure and tactical trading \nideas.\nDuring the last equity bear market, which occurred one \nyear ago, volatility futures spiked to the highest level in \nrecent years, with the volatility index (VIX) of the Chicago \nBoard Options Exchange (CBOE) reaching 40. Viewing this \nas an opportunity to position client portfolios for a \nnormalization in volatility, Patel purchased an inverse \nvolatility futures (IVIX) exchange-traded note (ETN), which \nappreciates as volatility declines. Patel also positioned \nclients in a momentum factor (MO) ETF, believing it would \noutperform the broad market during the early stages of the \nnext bull market.\nOver the past year, financial markets have rebounded \nsignificantly and volatility has declined. The IVIX has \nperformed as expected since the VIX has fallen to 10 and is \nnear a record low. Patel is concerned that another stock \nmarket sell-off is likely, due to high valuations, steadily \nrising bond yields, and a series of credit losses at several \nlarge European banks.  Therefore, he believes now is the \ntime to exit both the MO and IVIX and allocate that money \nto a low-volatility (LOWV) equity ETF.\nThe next day—before Patel can act on his views—global \nequities sell off considerably, falling 4%. In the US, the VIX \nsurges to 19, almost doubling overnight, and the stock \nmarket opens significantly lower, falling 3%. Patel is \nshocked to see the value of IVIX plunge by more than 95% \nat the open, wiping out not only the gains of the last three \nyears but virtually all his client's original investment.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c8_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c8_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Pilot Advisors (Pilot) is considering replacing Fund A in the \nfirm's model equity portfolios based on recent historical \nperformance.\nPilot believes that Fund A is a closet index fund based on\nthe following factors:\nFactor 1: Active return \nFactor 2: Active risk \nFactor 3: Sharpe ratio\nPilot has identified three potential replacement funds for \nFund A and has analyzed the performance of the funds' \nmanagers:\nPilot is also examining the management of fixed-income in \nthe firm's model portfolio, which is constructed in house \nand includes a combination of passive exchange-traded \nfunds (ETFs) and a proprietary active strategy. The fixed- \nincome portfolio is benchmarked 60% to a broad-based \ndomestic fixed-income index and 40% to a broad-based \ninternational fixed-income index.\n\nThe domestic fixed-income benchmark index includes four \nETFs: one each for government, mortgage-backed, \ninvestment grade corporate, and high-yield bonds; these \nbonds are all equally weighted (25% in the index, 15% in \nthe portfolio). The international fixed-income benchmark \nincludes two ETFs: an equal 50/50 split (ie, 20% in the \nportfolio) between developed markets and emerging \nmarkets.\nThe five-year historical performance of the in-house fund is \nlisted below:\nIn an effort to add value, Pilot uses active strategies within \nthe active portion of the fixed-income portfolio. Two of the \nstrategies are listed below:\nStrategy 1: On an annual basis, the four benchmark \ndomestic fixed-income ETFs are over- or underweighted \nbased on Pilot's forecasted returns. The risk model for this \nstrategy is based on the 4 × 4 active return correlation \nmatrix:\n\nStrategy 2: Each quarter, a market timing call is made \nbased on the forecasted performance of the two \ninternational ETFs. The ETF expected to relatively \noutperform is scored \"1\" and receives 100% allocation; the \nother is scored \"−1\" and receives no allocation. Over the \npast 20 quarters, the market timing call has been right 12 \ntimes. The annualized active risk of this strategy is 5.0%.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Marta Nowak is the research director at Eagle Investments, \nan ETF manager. Eagle is developing a new long-only \nmultifactor equity ETF, and Nowak is responsible for the \ninitial backtesting and strategy development.\nNowak instructs Zibi Turek, research analyst at Eagle, to \ncreate and backtest two factor-weighted allocation \nportfolios: a benchmark (BM) portfolio and a risk parity (RP) \nportfolio.\nThe historical investment simulation will use the ETF's \nexpected BM securities as the investment universe and \ninclude five fundamental factors, the last 10 years of point- \nin-time data, a rolling-window approach, and monthly \nrebalancing.\nTurek reviews the results as presented in Exhibit 1 with \nNowak and makes the following comment:\n\"The returns of factor portfolios 1 and 5 are accurately \ndescribed by a normal distribution.\"\nTurek reviews Exhibit 2 with Nowak:\n\nBased on his review of Exhibit 2, Turek makes the following \nobservations:\nObservation 1:95% of the time, monthly losses for the BM \nportfolio will be less than 4.1%.\nObservation 2:Once monthly losses for the RP portfolio \nreach at least 2.4%, 5% of the time losses will be as great as \n3.6%.\nObservation 3:The RP portfolio's single worst monthly \nreturn was 11.4%.\nTo confirm the results of the backtest, Nowak instructs \nTurek to cross-validate the results.\nFinally, Nowak requests that a scenario analysis be \nperformed to determine how the two strategies perform in \ndifferent market regimes. The results are presented below:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c10_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c10_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Makore Investments is an asset management firm \nspecializing in US equities. Hugo Dawson, a junior trader at \nMakore, executes a market buy order for 5,000 shares of \nBalsa Chem, a thinly traded stock. He fills the order in four \ntrades; details are presented in Exhibit 1. He then \ncalculates the market impact of the trades and their \naverage effective spreads.\nDawson presents the information to Rose Preston, a senior \ntrader and his supervisor at Makore. Dawson explains he \nused a trading strategy that consisted of automatically \nbreaking the order into smaller, random-sized orders, which \nwere sent to different exchanges at random times, and that \nthe trades were executed using an electronic broker. He \nadds that electronic trading systems are faster and cheaper \nthan floor-based trading systems and lists other \nadvantages:\nAdvantage 1: Electronic trading systems have more \nliquidity since they are used in most equity and bond \ntrades.\nAdvantage 2: Electronic trading systems keep audit trails \nthat prevent parasitic traders from manipulating the \nmarket.\nAdvantage 3: When properly programmed, electronic \nexchange systems are capable of executing orders without \nany errors.\n\nPreston tells Dawson to sell the stocks of three \nsemiconductor manufacturers since Makore's investment \ncommittee decided to underweight the industry. She \nexplains that Makore's position in the stocks is substantial, \nand she is concerned about a large decrease in prices \nbefore Dawson can complete the sale.\nDawson suggests executing several trades at the same price \nbetween different mutual funds managed by Makore. He \nbelieves that these trades would increase the liquidity \nperceived by the market and allow the firm to sell the \nstocks at better prices. Preston explains that Makore \ncannot carry out the activity suggested by Dawson since it \nis an abusive trading practice that manipulates the market.\nDawson then warns that competitors are trading at \nincreasing speeds, which is reducing the effectiveness of \nthe trades. Preston explains that Makore is currently \nupgrading its computers and optimizing programming \ncodes—two measures expected to reduce latency and \nincrease the firm's competitiveness.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c11_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c11_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Vanessa Wells is a senior portfolio manager at Mohaka \nAsset Management, a firm that manages funds and \nportfolios of international bonds and equities. She is \nreviewing the firm's latest risk report with Duncan Boyd, a \nrisk analyst at Mohaka. Wells wants to understand the \ncurrent risk exposure of Waitaki Equities, a large cap fund \nmanaged by Mohaka, and Boyd presents the following \ndetails:\nBoyd calculates Waitaki's daily and annual parametric value \nat risk (VaR), assuming that the statistical properties of the \nreturn distribution remain constant across the year. He says \nthat the concept of VaR has several advantages and can be \nestimated using three methods: parametric, historical \nsimulation, and Monte Carlo. Then he makes the following \nobservations:\nObservation 1:The parametric method allows giving a \ngreater weight to more recent observations.\nObservation 2:The historical simulation method is not well \nsuited for assessing the risk of options.\nObservation 3:The Monte Carlo method can accommodate \nvirtually any type of distribution.\nWells tells Boyd that since the Waitaki fund is measured \nagainst the market index, she is particularly interested in a \nrisk measure that takes the benchmark into consideration. \nBoyd proposes applying a risk measure to a theoretical \nportfolio that consists of a long position in the total value of \nthe Waitaki Equities Fund and a short position in the \nbenchmark's holdings, proportional to the fund value.\n\nBoyd explains that there are other risk metrics that can \nimprove the risk assessment of Mohaka's products, like \nsensitivity risk and scenario risk measures. He says, \n\"Sensitivity risk measures—such as beta, convexity, and \nvega—measure the portfolio's exposure to changes in \nunderlying risk factors.\"\nBoyd adds that scenario risk measures such as historical \nscenarios and hypothetical scenarios allow for testing the \neffect of past or unprecedented market movements on the \nvalue of a portfolio. However, he explains to Wells, such \nmeasures also have important limitations:\nLimitation 1: Scenario risk measures do not allow taking\nliquidity into account.\nLimitation 2: \nscenario.\nLimitation 3:\nIt is difficult to attribute probabilities to each\nScenario risk measures focus exclusively on\nextreme losses, without considering gains.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c12_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c12_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Bruce Harris, CFA, is a buy-side equity trader at Chesterfield \nAsset Management (Chesterfield). Sarah Dickinson, CFA, is \na large-cap equity portfolio manager at Chesterfield.\nDickinson enters a sell order into the firm's order \nmanagement system for 20,000 shares of Warp Drive\nTechnologies (WDT). Harris sees the order and assesses the \ncurrent trading details on WDT.\nDue to the size of the order and the intraday price \nweakness of WDT, Harris decides to immediately sell 2,000 \nshares. Instead of routing the order to a specific broker, \nHarris enters the order into Chesterfield's trade execution \nsystem at the midpoint price of $20.13. The 2,000 shares \nare immediately filled at $20.13, as the trading software \nfound hidden liquidity in a dark pool. Harris then enters an \norder to sell an additional 1,000 share order at $20.13, but \nthis order goes unfilled and the stock trades lower.\nHarris holds the remaining 18,000 shares, anticipating that \nmore liquidity will develop at better prices. Unfortunately, \nthe stock continues to trade lower as the day progresses, \nfalling to $19.95. Harris and Dickinson discuss the situation \nand decide to wait until the next day to fill the remainder of \nthe order in hopes of selling at $20 or better.\nThe next morning, indications are that WDT will open at\n$19.65. Harris electronically instructs Broker A to use a \nvolume-weighted average price (VWAP) algorithm for the \nremaining 18,000 shares over the course of the trading day. \nFor the day, 180,000 shares of WDT traded, the benchmark \nVWAP was $19.89, and Harris's order was filled at $19.87, \nresulting in an average price for the entire 20,000 shares of \napproximately $19.90.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Alberto Aguiar is a risk analyst at a long-only asset \nmanagement firm. Horizonte Balanced Fund, one of the \nfunds managed by the firm, has a diversified portfolio that \ninvests in an equities subportfolio and in a fixed income \nsubportfolio. Aguiar uses the parametric method to \nestimate Horizonte's value at risk (VaR) based on the \ninformation in Exhibit 1:\nMarcela Velasco, the firm's chief risk officer, explains to \nAguiar that the fixed income subportfolio may also include \ncallable and putable bonds. Therefore, Velasco believes \nthat the parametric method may not be the most \nappropriate VaR method for the Horizonte Balanced Fund.\nVelasco is concerned about the allocation of risk to each of \nthe firm's trading units and asks Aguiar about firm-wide risk \nmanagement. Aguiar makes the following observations:\nObservation 1:In an optimal risk allocation, the sum of each \ntrading unit's individual risk budget should exceed the \nfirm's overall risk budget.\nObservation 2:Restrictive risk limits may result in \nconsiderable losses.\nVelasco tells Aguiar that one limitation of VaR is its \nvulnerability to trending markets. A portfolio can have \nseveral consecutive losses that individually do not exceed \nthe VaR amount but that collectively result in a substantial \nloss. She asks Aguiar what he would suggest to address this \nlimitation of VaR.\n\nVelasco is preparing a presentation about fund risk for the \nfirm's management committee. All funds managed by the \nfirm are long-only, unleveraged, and open-ended. She \ndivides her presentation into two parts:\nPart 1: Velasco explains how the risk exposure of a fund is \nestimated. Initially, she calculates the portfolio's reaction \nto small changes in risk factors, such as the equity risk \npremium and interest rates. Then she constructs extremely \nadverse scenarios and assesses their impact on the \nportfolio.\nPart 2: Velasco addresses how risk measures can be used to \nimprove the firm's capital allocation decisions since the firm \ninvests most of its own capital in the funds it manages.\nVelasco assesses the risk of each fund using appropriate risk\nmeasures. Then she suggests to the committee the most \nrisk-efficient allocation of the firm's capital into each fund.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Devin Garner is a senior sales executive at Finaxen \nInvestments, a wealth management firm. Garner's clients \nhave begun to ask him about investing in ETFs, so he asks \nJody Rosales, a portfolio manager at Finaxen, to help him \nbetter understand the mechanics of ETFs. Rosales explains \nthe creation/redemption process and the role of authorized \nparticipants (APs). She also discusses the arbitrage \nopportunities for APs when an ETF is trading at a premium \nor discount to the intraday indicated NAV (iNAV) of its \nbasket of securities.\nRosales tells Garner that a fundamental feature of ETFs is \ntheir ability to track the underlying index, and similar ETFs \nwith the same benchmark may have different tracking \nerrors. For example, she continues, if ETF 1 and ETF 2 track \nthe same global equities index and ETF 1 has a higher \ntracking error than ETF 2, this could result from differences \nin the ETFs' characteristics. She lists three differences \nbetween ETF 1 and ETF 2:\nDifference 1: ETF 1 holds American depositary receipts \n(ADRs) if local markets have low liquidity, whereas ETF 2 \nholds shares in each local market.\nDifference 2: ETF 1 holds all constituent shares of the \nindex, whereas ETF 2 optimizes its portfolio by holding a \nrepresentative sample.\nDifference 3: ETF 1 has a lower expense ratio than ETF 2.\nGarner asks about the costs of investing in ETFs. Rosales \nexplains that although ETFs typically have lower expense \nratios than mutual funds, the bid-ask spread is an important \ncost component of ETFs. She explains that there are \nsignificant differences in bid-ask spreads among ETF \ncategories and presents the spreads of ETF 3 and ETF 4 in \nExhibit 1. Garner asks Rosales to explain possible reasons \nfor that difference.\n\nFinally, as Garner also sells European securities to his \nclients, he asks Rosales to discuss the main characteristics \nof ETF trading in Europe.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Richard LeBlanc, CFA, is evaluating the investment merits of \nthe Universal Emerging Market Bond (UEMB) ETF and the \nAcme Emerging Market Bond (AEMB) ETF. Based on his \nshort-to-intermediate term market views, LeBlanc is \nconsidering replacing a portion of a US government bond \nmutual fund held in client portfolios with one of these two \nETFs. He hopes to achieve two objectives: generating \nadditional returns while also providing diversification \nbenefits.\nBoth ETFs trade on a US exchange and invest in USD- \ndenominated sovereign emerging market bonds. They also \nshare the same benchmark, the Total Emerging Market \nBond Index, which consists of 2,035 underlying securities.\nInformation on the ETFs and their performance is listed in \nExhibits 1 and 2:\nLate in the US trading day, the US Federal Reserve (Fed) \nunexpectedly raises interest rates, sending US bond yields \nhigher and reversing gains from earlier in the day. After the \nFed's announcement, LeBlanc observes the trading activity \nshown in Exhibits 3 and 4, which compare the ETFs with \nboth the benchmark and historical tracking data:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c16_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c16_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Bob Smith, research director at Innovation Capital, is \nconsidering refining the investment process for the firm's \nglobal small-cap equity (GSCE) strategy. Innovation uses a \ntraditional long-only portfolio construction process for \nGSCE, which prohibits shorting and leverage. Innovation's \ninvestment process currently screens for estimated free \ncash flow yield to identify suitable investment candidates, \nbut this strategy has not been statistically backtested.\nSmith believes a backtest based on the most recent 30 \nyears of data and that uses the historical earnings yield as \nthe sole factor could lead to better investment results.\nSmith will conduct a rolling window backtest with a \nmonthly rebalance, using the current constituents of the\nRussell 3000 Index (a cap-weighted index that tracks the \n3,000 largest US public corporations) as the investment \nuniverse. Smith's data vendor does not provide point-in- \ntime data, but transaction costs will be included in the \nanalysis.\nRachel Wilmer, a small-cap research analyst at Innovation, \nquestions the need for this analysis and makes the \nfollowing statements:\nStatement 1: Backtesting provides no value to traditional \nbottom-up managers since it relies on backward-looking \ndata for stock selection.\nStatement 2: A single-factor backtesting strategy is most \nappropriate for risk parity portfolios.\nStatement 3: Hedged long/short managers can more fully \nimplement the backtest's results.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c17_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c17_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 18, "vignette": "Emiko Hirata is a portfolio manager at an investment \nmanagement firm. Hirata is talking to Caleb Dawson, an \neconomist at the firm, about the current state of the \neconomy. Dawson begins by talking about economic theory \nand how changes in consumer behavior can affect asset \nprices.\nDawson states that investors' marginal utility from current \nconsumption is declining, which can affect the price of \nbonds. Dawson also observes that wealthier individuals are \ninvesting more and therefore their ownership percentage of \nthe investment assets is increasing, which may affect asset \nprices, returns, and savings rates.\nHirata and Dawson turn their attention to the central bank's \nmonetary policy. The central bank has maintained a low \npolicy rate of 0.5% through the current recession and has \nnot indicated plans to change it. Dawson collects current \ninformation on the economy:\nDawson notes that factors other than the central bank's \npolicy rate can affect the real interest rate; specifically, \nfactors related to real GDP are among the primary factors \nfor determining real interest rates.\nHirata holds a 2-year, zero-coupon bond for Champion \nManufacturing in her portfolio and plans to sell it in one \nyear. She asks Dawson what the price should be today if \nthe price of the bond in one year is estimated to be:\n$0.97 per $1.00 of par in a good economy or\n$0.87 per $1.00 of par in a bad economy.\nDawson provides estimates for probabilities of each state \nand investors' intertemporal rate of substitution:\n\nHirata and Dawson then discuss equity investments and the \nequity risk premium and make the following statements:\nHirata: The risk premium for investing in corporate debt \nover government debt is not sufficient to justify discounting \nequities.\nDawson: \nThe equity risk premium is driven in part by \nits poor hedging properties against bad consumption \noutcomes.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c18_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c18_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c18_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c18_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c18_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c18_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 19, "vignette": "Cyclops Partners is a hedge fund manager. Ramesh Singh, \nthe chief risk officer at Cyclops, is responsible for \nmonitoring and reporting both portfolio and firm-wide risks \nto senior management.\nOne of the firm's funds operates as a long/short, market- \nneutral equity strategy and has €100,000,000 in assets. The \nfund is benchmarked to:\nthe risk-free rate, with\ngross equity exposure of 200%, and \nnet equity exposure of zero.\nThe fund is sub-advised by four separate internal teams, \neach with a risk budget based on the expected tail risk of \nthe team's sub-portfolio. The resulting overall portfolio is \nbroadly diversified and, when necessary, the firm's \nmanagers use an option overlay strategy consisting of index \noptions to fine-tune the fund's net market exposure.\nThe market values in the portfolio are within an acceptable \nrisk tolerance; however, the portfolio's net beta position is \ncurrently outside acceptable levels. The fund's current \npositions are shown in Exhibit 1:\nSingh asks Ahana Agarwal, a newly hired junior risk analyst, \nto review the results of a value-at-risk (VaR) analysis on the \nfund, the results of which are presented in Exhibit 2:\n\nSingh then states there are three standard VaR approaches \nused at Cyclops:\nApproach 1: \nOne-day 5% historical simulation VaR with a \nlookback period of five years\nApproach 2:\nApproach 3:\nMonthly 1% parametric VaR\nAnnual 16% Monte Carlo simulation VaR\nSingh states that he prefers Approach 1 for this portfolio. \nHowever, Agarwal interjects and recommends that, in this \ninstance, either Approach 2 or Approach 3 would be more \nappropriate.\nFinally, Agarwal suggests that additional risk measures \nshould be included in the risk assessment and that the \nportfolio should be stress-tested.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c19_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c19_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c19_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c19_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c19_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c19_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 20, "vignette": "Ching Na, CFA, is a performance attribution analyst for a \nlarge buy-side firm. Na is tasked with analyzing the \nperformance of the firm's active managers. Na has \ncompiled the following information for the firm's Large-Cap \nStock Fund and Global Balanced Fund for 20X2.\nNa has also compiled the following historical data for the \nLarge-Cap Stock Fund (LCSF) and Global Balanced Fund for \nthe past 10 years. Over this time, the average risk-free rate \nwas 5%.\nSome of the firm's clients have expressed concern regarding \nthe volatility of the LCSF. Na has been instructed to create \nthe New Portfolio, consisting of cash and the LCSF that has \nthe same level of risk as the benchmark.\n\nThe portfolio management team is considering adjusting \nthe Global Balanced Fund's investment strategy but will \ncontinue to benchmark to the same index. The portfolio \nteam provides pro forma data based on the new strategy \nand asks Na to calculate portfolio statistics based on this \ndata.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c20_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c20_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c20_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c20_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c20_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c20_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 21, "vignette": "John Jones, a fixed income analyst at a large investment \nmanagement firm, is evaluating domestic monetary policy \nand the overall interest rate environment within the context \nof the business cycle. Jones observes that the last \nrecession was six years ago, and over the past few years the \ndomestic economy has experienced consistent growth of \nabout 3.0%. Jones forecasts GDP growth will peak this year \nat 3.5% and inflation will accelerate.\nThe current central bank policy rate is 1.50% and has been \nunchanged for several years. Using the Taylor Rule, Jones \ncalculates a policy rate of 4.50%. The yield curve is \npositively sloped with short-term bond yields anchored by \nthe policy rate while the long-term government bond is \nyielding 4.00%.\nJones then suggests that consumption hedging properties \nembedded in government bond risk premiums are due to \nthe following conditions:\nCondition 1 \ninvestors\nCondition 2:\nthe lack of currency risk for domestic\nbond returns being negatively correlated\nwith GDP growth\nCondition 3: \nthe fact that government bond risk \npremiums are static\nJones then compiles data on several five-year corporate \nbonds that are held by the firm.\n\nJones shifts his attention to the firm's high-yield bond fund \nand notes that the fund holds more than 400 positions and \nhas not experienced a default during the past five years.\nJones then observes:\nObservation 1:The default risk for this portfolio has been\ndiversified away.\nObservation 2:The business cycle obviously impacts the \ncredit spreads on individual securities, but at the fund level, \ncredit spreads are stable.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c21_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c21_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c21_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c21_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c21_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c21_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 22, "vignette": "Park Ji-Won is an analyst at an investment firm analyzing \nthe domestic economy and interest rates. Park's long-term \neconomic outlook for the country is for relatively high but \nvolatile GDP growth, even though the economy has been in \na recession for the past six months. Just prior to the onset \nof the recession, the yield curve inverted. The most recent \nmonthly economic data has shown some signs of economic \nstabilization and the stock market has rallied 10% since its \nlow two months ago.\nPark is analyzing the one-year nominal default-free \ndomestic government zero-coupon bond, which is currently \npriced at 97.36 per 100 par value. Park is forecasting 1.50% \ninflation over the next year. A one-year inflation-adjusted \ndefault-free domestic government zero-coupon bond is \ncurrently priced at 0.85%, which Park estimates to be the \nreal one-year rate.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c22_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c22_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c22_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c22_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c22_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c22_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 23, "vignette": "New Age Management (\"New Age\") is the fund sponsor of \nthe Cutting-Edge Technology Fund (CETF), an exchange- \ntraded fund (ETF). In its investment strategy, CETF invests in \nthe 10 largest US technology stocks based on market \ncapitalization. Broad Street Brokers (\"Broad Street\") acts as \nan authorized participant (AP) for CETF. CETF trades on a \nUS exchange and rebalances on the last trading day of every \ncalendar quarter.\nCETF's current creation and redemption baskets are \ndisplayed below.\nIntraday on March 31, CETF last trades at $20.00, a \npremium to the current intraday NAV (ie, iNAV) of $19.98. \nThe current bid-ask spread is $19.96–$20.02. Another \nbroker/dealer, Zebra Trading (ZT)—which is not an AP— \ncomes to Broad Street with a large order to buy 500,000 \nshares of CETF. Broad Street immediately fills the entire \n500,000-share order at $20.00, although it has only 100,000 \nshares in inventory.\nDuring the day, Broad Street also trades blocks of CETF with \na third broker/dealer, Alpha Trading. The net trading \nactivity at the end of the day is as follows:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c23_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c23_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c23_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c23_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c23_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c23_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 24, "vignette": "Bob Jones is a senior research analyst for a United States \nuniversity endowment. Jones is considering hiring a new \nUS equity manager for a portion of the endowment's equity \nallocation. The new manager describes his investing style \nas a \"multi-cap, go anywhere\" manager.\nJones analyzes the manager's performance over the past \nyear using the Carhart four-factor model relative to a \nmarket-capitalization weighted benchmark index based on \nthe 500 largest US-listed stocks. The manager's security \nselection contributed −0.10% to his overall return for this\nperiod. The factor sensitivities and returns are presented in \nExhibit 1:\nJones then compares the new manager's historical \nperformance with the endowment's existing active equity \nmanagers using the same benchmark. This information is \npresented in Exhibit 2:\nJones discusses the results presented in Exhibit 1 and \nExhibit 2 with Jennifer Williams, a junior research analyst at \nthe endowment. Williams makes the following comments:\n\nComment 1: By squaring active risk, the various sources of \na portfolio's active risk components can be separated.\nComment 2: The components of active risk are systematic \nrisk and residual risk.\nComment 3: The benchmark selection for the new\nmanager may not be appropriate.\nWilliams believes that instead of hiring the new manager, \nthe endowment would be better off investing in an \nalternative index product. The bases for her belief are that \nalternative indexes:\nBasis 1:\nBasis 2:\nBasis 3:\ncapture systematic alpha exposures\ncost less than traditional active managers\nuse a mechanical, rules-based process", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c24_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c24_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c24_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c24_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c24_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c24_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 25, "vignette": "Amy Gomez, CFA, a portfolio manager at Peak Global Inc. \n(Peak), has just hired Elizabeth Martinez as a junior analyst. \nDuring orientation, Gomez asks Martinez to describe the \nfundamental law of active management. Martinez states \nthat, assuming optimal active weights and forecasted \nsecurity returns, the basic fundamental law of active \nmanagement includes factors for skill, breadth, and \naggressiveness.\nMartinez then elaborates on the full fundamental law of \nactive management by stating the following:\nStatement 1: The information coefficient measures the \ncorrelation between the active weights and the forecasted \nactive returns.\nStatement 2: The transfer coefficient measures the \ncorrelation between the forecasted active returns and the \nrealized active returns.\nStatement 3: The value added, or skill of the manager, \nmeasures the correlation between the active weights and \nthe realized active returns.\nNext, Gomez begins a discussion of the Peak Global Fund \n(PGF). Gomez states that the portfolio is broadly diversified \nand that her annual return projections for each holding are \nuncorrelated; the portfolio is subject to the following active \nconstraints: long only, no leverage, and an investment \nuniverse that is limited to the 50 benchmark securities.\nGomez then provides Martinez with five years of selected \nhistorical data and asks for observations.\n\nMartinez observes the following:\nObservation 1: The information coefficient implies little \nforecasting skill.\nObservation 2: The transfer coefficient looks artificially low\nand should be very close to 1.00.\nObservation 3: The information ratio will likely be \noverstated due to difficulty in accurately measuring \nbreadth.\nGomez responds to Observation 1 by stating, \"My \ninvestment process leads to a portfolio with a value tilt and \na median market capitalization less than the benchmark.\nAlso, to be fair, it is only 60 months of data.\"", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c25_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c25_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c25_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c25_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c25_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c25_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 26, "vignette": "Paul Durham, senior performance attribution analyst at a \npension fund, is reviewing the fund's conventional asset \nallocation portfolio data for year-end 20X9. The \ninformation is presented in Exhibit 1:\nDurham is considering adding a new equity manager and is \nevaluating the All-Weather Equity Fund, a 130/30 long- \nshort fund. Fund data is presented in Exhibit 2:\nAs part of his evaluation, Durham compares the All- \nWeather Equity Fund with the Rainy-Day Fund, a fund with \nan unconstrained long-short strategy that also uses the \nsame benchmark. The Rainy-Day Fund has a forecasted \ninformation ratio (IR) of 0.30.\nDurham discusses the All-Weather Equity Fund with Joanna \nBaran, a peer and coworker. Baran suggests looking at the \nfund using the fundamental law of active management to \nbetter understand performance drivers. Based on this \nsuggestion, Durham requests historical data on the All- \nWeather Equity Fund's transfer coefficient (TC), information \ncoefficient (IC), and breadth (BR) from the fund's manager.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c26_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c26_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c26_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c26_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c26_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c26_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 27, "vignette": "Ronald Jones, CFA, is a junior analyst at a buy-side firm. \nJones has been tasked with evaluating the three domestic \nequity funds that are used to construct the firm's model \nclient equity portfolio. Jones compares the funds to the \nmodel portfolio's broadly diversified domestic equity \nbenchmark.\nThe model portfolio equally weights each fund, and Jones \nhas developed the following fundamental single-factor \nmodel based on arbitrage pricing theory (APT).\nJones then constructs a four-factor APT model for Fund A \nusing a 2% risk-free rate, which is presented in Exhibit 2.\nJones also creates a new three-factor model for the firm's \ndomestic bond fund (DBF) based on industrial production, \ninflation, and corporate credit spreads. Corporate credit \nspreads are calculated by comparing the yields on a broad- \nbased corporate bond index to the five-year government \nbenchmark on a monthly basis. The portion of the return \nnot explained by the model was 0.5%. The details of the \nDBF model are presented in Exhibit 3, and the previous \nmonth's model inputs are shown in Exhibit 4.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c27_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c27_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c27_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c27_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c27_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c27_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_portfolio", "source_label": "UWorld – Portfolio Management", "topic": "Portfolio Management", "case_no": 28, "vignette": "Karsten Knapp and Inna Schilling are financial advisors at \nBloxx Investments, a wealth management firm. They are \nassessing the suitability of ETFs for existing clients who \ncurrently invest in mutual funds.  Knapp is concerned that \nETFs trading at a premium or discount to their NAV may \naffect clients' returns. He asks Schilling's opinion, and she \nmakes the following statements:\nStatement 1: Fixed income ETFs typically have lower \npremiums and discounts than equity ETFs.\nStatement 2: An index with an active futures market \ncontributes to increased premiums and discounts of ETFs \nbenchmarked to that index.\nStatement 3: The percentage of foreign securities held and \nthe frequency of trading are factors that influence ETF \npremiums and discounts.\nKnapp and Schilling discuss the total costs of owning an ETF. \nSchilling explains that trading costs can be a significant \nportion of total costs, depending on the holding period.\nShe gives Knapp an example based on PJW, an ETF that \ntracks the S&P 500 Index:\nManagement fee: 0.30% per year \nCommission: 0.08% per trade\nBid-offer spread of 0.14% on purchase and sale \nHolding period: 15 months\nSchilling then says that ETFs have specific types of risk, and \nKnapp asks for more details. Schilling gives examples of \nevents related to ETF risks, including \"soft\" closures.\nLastly, Knapp and Schilling discuss ETF applications and how \nETFs can be used to increase the overall efficiency of a \nportfolio. Schilling tells Knapp that ETFs are often \nemployed, for example, when an active manager moves the\n\nportfolio out of a certain asset class or factor that Bloxx's \nclients want to remain exposed to. This temporary gap can \nbe offset by purchasing liquid ETFs and maintaining \nexposure to that asset class or factor.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c28_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c28_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c28_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_portfolio_c28_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 1, "vignette": "Kenji Tanaka is a junior portfolio manager at Warusawa\nAsset Management (WAM).  He is building a machine \nlearning (ML) model to improve WAM's investment process.  \nAfter collecting data from recent corporate debt offerings, \nTanaka constructed a data set where each row represents a \nbond, and each column represents a feature (or variable) of \nthese bonds.  Exhibit 1 provides a sample of the data set:\nSubsequently, after cleansing and preprocessing the data, \nTanaka explores the data and creates new variables—by \ntransforming, combining, and decomposing existing \ncolumns—to optimize the performance of the ML model.\nTanaka believes it is also important to use unstructured \ndata in his analysis.  He explains to his manager, Noriko \nSaito, that WAM should leverage the availability of multiple \nsources such as text communications and online news \nservices.  He explains the process of building a ML model \nbased on unstructured (text) data and tells Saito the \nfollowing:\nStatement 1: The model may have a structured output, \nlike a sentiment score.\nStatement 2: Web spidering programs are capable of \nextracting and cleansing raw data.\nStatement 3: It is not possible to create text visualizations \nsince text data are not structured.\nTanaka adds that, after cleansing, the text data are split into \nseparate words (tokens), which are further processed to \nmake data exploration more efficient.  Saito asks how this\n\nworks, so Tanaka creates two examples of token groups, \nshown in Exhibit 2.  Tanaka explains that the first group \nrepresents tokens before the process, while the second \ngroup has already been processed.\nTanaka adds that the exclusion of the word \"not\" in Token \nGroup 2 would prevent the ML model from capturing the \ncorrect meaning (ie, \"not significant\").  Saito asks if this \nwould affect the efficiency of the model.  Tanaka explains \nthat he can employ a technique to overcome this problem.\nFinally, Saito asks if Tanaka is ready to start building the text \nML model.  Tanaka answers that he still has a concern \nabout noisy features that could affect the results of the \nmodel, especially regarding sparse (rare) terms that could \nbe present in just a few of the texts.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c1_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c1_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c1_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c1_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c1_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c1_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 2, "vignette": "Lana Marek, CFA, a pricing analyst at a European auto \nmanufacturer, is analyzing competitor pricing data.  She is \nworking with Micah Hould, CFA, a quantitative analyst at \nthe same company, to create multiple regression models \nthat help identify factors affecting vehicle pricing.\nAs part of her analysis, she has chosen three independent \nvariables to regress against vehicle price: engine power \noutput (HP), vehicle seating capacity (SC), and size of the \ncar's interior (CI).  Marek assumes that all three variables \nare positively related to price.  To test this, she has \ncompiled data for 30 different vehicle models from last year \nand developed a regression equation and statistics at a 5% \nlevel of significance.\nMarek believes that there is another variable that affects \nvehicle price: whether the car belongs to a luxury vehicle \nbrand.  She adds a dummy variable and assigns a value of 1 \nfor a luxury vehicle and a value of 0 for a nonluxury vehicle.  \nMarek expects that there will be a positive relationship \nbetween the brand variable and price.  The updated \nregression statistics and equation are as follows:\n\nMarek notices that R2 increased after she added the \ndummy variable to her analysis, but adjusted R2 (or R2\ndecreased.\nFinally, Marek is concerned about multicollinearity's effect \non her model.  She suspects the seating capacity and \ninterior size variables are highly correlated, which may \ninfluence the regression results.  She shares her concern \nwith Hould, who responds as follows:\nStatement 1:  \"The most reliable way of detecting \nmulticollinearity is to assess the magnitude of correlations \nbetween independent variables.\"\nStatement 2:  \"A key issue with multicollinearity is that t-\ntests on regression coefficients will result in instances of \nrejecting a true null hypothesis.\"\nStatement 3:  \"The most straightforward way to correct \nmulticollinearity is to remove one or more correlated \nvariables from the regression.\"", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c2_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c2_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c2_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c2_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c2_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c2_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 3, "vignette": "Dahlia Bakker, CFA, is a strategist who focuses on the airline \nindustry.  At the start of this year, many of the airlines that \nBakker covers warned that they would report lower \nearnings than expected, and most blamed rising fuel costs \nfor their poor performance.\nFirst, Bakker analyzes the stock price of GlobeMax, a large \nairline.  She assesses a model that would predict the stock \nprice of GlobeMax given a change in the price of jet fuel.  \nBakker tried using a linear trend model but found that the \nregression followed a nonlinear trend.  Bakker also found \nthat the time series did not exhibit signs of seasonality, nor \ndid the model's residuals show signs of serial correlation.\nBakker is performing a more detailed analysis on the stock \nprice trend for AFX, one of GlobeMax's competitors.  To \nreduce the effect of short-term price fluctuations, Bakker \ndecides to use a moving average.  AFX's stock price for the \nprevious 12 months is shown in Exhibit 1, with December \nbeing the most recent month.\nSince a historical moving average calculation gives equal \nweight to all prior periods, Bakker decides she would prefer \na more complex moving average time series model to \nforecast the stock price of AFX.  Based on her evaluation of \nthe data, Bakker determines that an MA(2) model is most \nappropriate for her analysis of AFX's stock.\n\nNext, Bakker decides to build a regression on the average \nprice of jet fuel over time.  She starts by using a linear trend \nmodel of the monthly spot price over the last five years, as \nshown in Exhibit 2.\nBakker correctly decides the linear trend model is not \nsuitable for predicting jet fuel prices, so she tries instead to \nuse a log-linear model.  She correctly determines this is also \nunsuitable, so she switches to an autoregressive (AR) \nmodel, as shown in Exhibit 3.\n\nBakker determines from the data in Exhibit 3 that the AR \nmodel is not properly specified.\nFinally, Bakker creates a first-differenced autoregression for \nthe stock price of another airline, TerraAir, based on the \ncompany's monthly stock price for the last five years.  \nBakker determines the regression is properly specified and \ngenerates the following regression data.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c3_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c3_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c3_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c3_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c3_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c3_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 4, "vignette": "Meiying Chan is a quantitative analyst at Karlsson \nManagement, a global macro hedge fund.  She has recently \ndeveloped a classification and regression tree (CART) model \nto guide Karlsson's allocation to certain investments.  \nChan's manager, Patrick O'Leary, asks her to classify the \nstock of Diamond Head Industries (DHI).  DHI recently \nreported the following information:\nP/E-to-growth (PEG) ratio of 1.03\nPrice-to-sales (P/S) ratio of 1.42\nPrice-to-book (P/B) ratio of 0.99\nP/E ratio of 16.15\nFCFE year-over-year growth of 4.8%\nChan runs DHI's information in the CART model:\n\nAfter comparing the results from the model with his own \nanalysis, O'Leary is concerned that the CART model might \nbe overfitted and unable to generalize.  O'Leary asks Chan \nwhy she used CART instead of k-nearest neighbor (KNN) for \nthis exercise and states that KNN models have certain \nbenefits, such as:\nBenefit 1:\nThey can be used directly for multiclass \nclassification.\nBenefit 2:\nThey are parametric, and assumptions about \nthe distribution of the data can be adjusted.\nBenefit 3:\nThey automatically create a distance metric \nto model similarity based on the features selected.\nO'Leary asks Chan to review a set of models received from \nother analysts that predict price upsides in a set of stocks.  \nThe model set contains support vector machine (SVM), k-\nnearest neighbor (KNN), and CART.  Karlsson uses each of \nthe models to evaluate the same five stocks and decides \nwhich stock has the greatest potential upside:\nChan is also asked to categorize these stocks in the \nEuronext 100 index based on two factors: capital intensity \nand revenue growth.  Categorizing the stocks will help her \nanalyze each company's relative value versus its peers.  \nChan does not yet know the most appropriate number of \ncategories, so she would prefer a model that provides \nmultiple possibilities.  In addition, given Karlsson's top-\ndown approach to investment research, she would prefer a \nmodel that uses a top-down approach to categorization.\n\nFinally, O'Leary and Chan discuss neural networks, which is \nan area of ML that Karlsson has not yet explored.  Chan \nstates that:\nStatement 1: Within neural networks, the magnitude by \nwhich weights are reassigned during the learning process \ncan be adjusted by changing the learning rate.\nStatement 2: Deep neural networks improve on regular \nneural networks since they do all the learning on their own \nand therefore do not require any hyperparameter selection.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c4_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c4_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c4_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c4_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c4_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c4_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 5, "vignette": "Jae Park, CFA, is a manager of a hedge fund that bases its \nsecurity selection on advanced quantitative analysis.  For \nseveral open job positions with the fund, Park is looking to \nhire people with scientific and research backgrounds.  Using \nmultiple regression, she would like to evaluate the \nrelationship between the expected salary of the candidates \nbased on their years of experience (EXP), number of \npublished research papers (PRP), and amount of grant \nfunding received in their career (GF).  The results of that \nregression are shown below, along with sample critical \nvalues.  Park wishes to test the results at a 5% significance \nlevel (α = 0.05).\n\nPark also notices that each candidate attended one of five \nuniversities.  She is considering how to add a variable for \nuniversity attended to the regression model and believes \ndummy variables are the best way to capture this.\nFinally, Park suspects that her regression in its current form \nmay violate regression assumptions.  Her concern is that \nher model might have an artificially large R2 and t-statistics \nthat are understated.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c5_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c5_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c5_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c5_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c5_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c5_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 6, "vignette": "Soojin Han, CFA, a fixed income portfolio manager at K2 \nFinancial, is exploring how machine learning (ML) can \nimprove several of the firm's investing and operational \nfunctions.  After researching on her own and asking \ncolleagues for advice, Han decides that she is most \ncomfortable labeling factors and targets to train an \nalgorithm, then allowing the algorithm to infer a pattern \nand predict output values.  Based on this process, she plans \nto begin by implementing simple algorithms to infer \nrelationships within the functions.\nHan identifies several processes at K2 that she considers \ngood candidates for ML.  They include:\nProcess 1:\nWhen a company announces a new bond \nissue, K2's analysts will classify that issue in one of five \ncategories based on existing bonds outstanding from other \ncompanies with similar characteristics.\nProcess 2:\nOn a quarterly basis, K2 will analyze the \nissuers of the bonds it holds to determine whether they are \nlikely to default within the next 12 months.\nHan reads about classification and regression tree (CART) \nalgorithms and the risk of overfitting if the algorithms are \nleft unconstrained.  To counter this risk, Han considers \nadding regularization parameters to any CART models she \ndecides to use.\nHan soon realizes that in some of K2's processes, multiple \nalgorithms could provide applicable outputs.  She asks the \ncompany's chief technology officer, Minjae Cho, for advice \non which algorithm to select in such cases.  Cho tells Han \nabout the ensemble method:\nStatement 1: When using ensemble learning with \nheterogeneous learners, the first ML model used is \nvalidated by each additional model.  Therefore, a single \noutput should be agreed upon for the models; otherwise, \nthe results will be unreliable.\n\nStatement 2: When using ensemble learning with \nhomogeneous learners, bootstrap aggregating is used to \ngenerate multiple sets of training data from the same data \nset.\nStatement 3: The random forest classifier is a type of \nensemble learning that uses a large number of decision \ntrees so that errors in a single tree tend to cancel out.\nStatement 4: When using random forest classifiers, an \nanalyst should ensure that the number of features in each \ndecision tree is the same.\nStatement 5: One benefit of random forest classifiers is \nthat they are based on models such as CART and thus have \nhigh levels of interpretability.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c6_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c6_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c6_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c6_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c6_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c6_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 7, "vignette": "Jing Shu, an investment analyst at a brokerage firm, is \ndeveloping a text data machine learning (ML) model based \non social media posts and online news services.  Fang Chen, \nhis supervisor, requests more details about the text \nexploration step, and Shu replies:\nStatement 1: Document frequency (DF) can be used to \nremove noise features from the data set.\nStatement 2: Name entity recognition (NER) and parts of \nspeech (POS) are common techniques of feature selection.\nStatement 3: Mutual information (MI) measures the \nindependence between the occurrences of a token and the \noccurrence of a class.\nShu adds that the next step in the ML process is fitting a \nsystem of rules to the data set.  If the model is a good fit, it \ncan be generalized to new (out-of-sample) data; however, \nhe is concerned that the model may be overfitted.  Shu also \ninforms Chen that he has already selected an appropriate \nmethod for model training since the data set contains \nground truth.\nLater, Shu divides the data set into a training set, a cross-\nvalidation set (CV), and a test set.  Shu runs a model using \nthe training set, aiming to anticipate trends in the stock \nmarket.  The model classifies each text as Class \"1\" (positive \nsentiment) or Class \"0\" (negative sentiment).  Shu uses an \nerror analysis to measure model performance, creating the \nconfusion matrix in Exhibit 1:\n\nAfter the performance evaluation of the model, Shu \ncompares the model's prediction error using different data \nsets (training and CV).  He concludes that the prediction \nerror on the training set is low, but the prediction error on \nthe CV set is significantly higher.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c7_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c7_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c7_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c7_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c7_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c7_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 8, "vignette": "Frederick Butler is a senior quantitative analyst for a macro \nhedge fund.  He is testing models using various \nmacroeconomic data sets and their relation to companies' \nfundamentals.  Butler begins modeling by deciding how to \nforecast each of three data sets.  He wishes to use \nautoregressive (AR) models for forecasting, but first plans to \ncheck to determine if the data sets are covariance \nstationary.  The data sets are for monthly:\nnatural gas consumption in the US.\ninflation rates for a developed market.\nrevenues of an expanding restaurant chain.\nStephanie Hsieh, a junior quantitative analyst for the hedge \nfund, is forecasting US retail sales and has adjusted her \nmodel several times to make it covariance stationary.  She \npresents the model's most recent outputs to Butler in \nExhibit 1:\nButler explains that the requirements for expected value, \nvariance, and covariance are what allow a covariance \nstationary time series to be regressed using AR.  These\n\nrequirements differ from those of a random walk.  He notes \nthat the dependent variable of a random walk is observed \nover several periods.\nButler runs an AR(1) regression on US household financial \nobligations as a percent of disposable personal income:\nThe most recent data points for HFO%DPI are:\nv\nButler asks Hsieh to review the regression.  Hsieh plots the \ndata points in a graph and notices that the data appear to \nexhibit multiple regimes.  She asks Butler what the best \ncourse of action would be to fix the issue.\nHsieh asks Butler whether she should use an autoregressive \nmoving-average (ARMA) model instead of an AR model.  \nButler states that:\nStatement 1: ARMA models have more stable parameters \nand therefore produce better forecasts than AR models.\nStatement 2: Unlike AR models, ARMA models do not \nhave to be tested for autoregressive conditional \nheteroskedasticity (ARCH).", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c8_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c8_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c8_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c8_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c8_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c8_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 9, "vignette": "Jonas Bauer plans to start an independent sell-side firm and \nuse machine learning (ML) techniques for some of the firm's \nprocesses.  He plans to cover 28 healthcare and pharmaceutical \nstocks with a team of analysts.  Unfamiliar with many popular \nML techniques, Bauer decides to read articles from popular \ninvestment professionals:\nArticle 1:  As an alternative to simply classifying your \ninvestments by industry, I found that it is more helpful to \nclassify them by similar financial characteristics.  My model \nuses 4 primary financial metrics to classify companies.  After \nseveral trials with different hyperparameters, I found that \nsplitting companies into 7 groups brought the most useful \nclassification system.\nArticle 2:  I have found three advantages to hierarchical \nclustering when compared with K-means clustering.  These are:\nBenefit 1:  Hierarchical clustering will produce the same results \nwhether using agglomerative or divisive methods.\nBenefit 2:  With hierarchical clustering, analysts can see the \nalternative segmentations at different levels of granularity.\nBenefit 3:  Unlike K-means clustering, hierarchical clustering \nallows you to predetermine the number of clusters.\nArticle 3:  ML is a powerful tool and, if set up right, allows the \nmachine to do the hard work for you.  I prefer to use \nalgorithms that discover the structure of the data themselves, \nso training is not required.  Just provide it the inputs without a \ntarget or reward and they can help with dimension reduction \nand clustering problems.\nBauer, through a former colleague, learns about another hedge \nfund, Ancient Oak Investments (AOI), that is exploring the use \nof ML algorithms to make investment decisions.  Bauer \ncontacts the chief investment officer of AOI, Hanna Krause, to \nget more information.  Krause tells Bauer that AOI is planning \nto integrate two types of ML algorithms.\n\nThe first ML algorithm that AOI is planning to use learns \nthrough a trial-and-error process, testing new trading \nstrategies to decide which will get the best returns.  Krause \nhas programmed the algorithm with certain trading rules it \nmust follow and specified that its goal is to maximize \nreturns.\nThe second ML algorithm that AOI is planning to use will \nsimplify complex economic models.  These economic \nmodels will sometimes have 15 or more variables.  Krause \nwants to use as few variables as possible, preferably 2 or 3, \neven if the original variables are combined.  Krause does \nnot want to train the model and would prefer the model to \ndiscover the relationships on its own.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c9_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c9_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c9_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c9_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c9_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c9_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 10, "vignette": "Oscar Bass, a junior data analyst at Great Price Electronics \n(GPE), is building a machine learning (ML) model to predict \nsales growth for each of GPE's stores.  The model is based \non structured data collected from GPE's enterprise resource \nplanning (ERP) system.  After performing some initial steps, \nBass built a data set in which each row represents a GPE \nstore, and each column represents a feature (or variable) of \nthese stores.  Exhibit 1 presents a sample of the raw data \nset before cleansing:\nAfter cleansing the data, Bass\ncreates an additional variable to the data set: annual sales \nper square feet\nremoves the third column (State) since the state can be \nidentified by the first two characters of the second column \n(Store).\nBass then decides to normalize the annual sales per store.  \nOver the full data set, the variable \"Annual Sales\" ranges \nfrom USD 21.2 million to 81.2 million, with an average of \nUSD 30.2 million and a standard deviation of USD 10.0 \nmillion.\nBass presents his analysis to Pamela Walker, GPE's chief \ninformation officer (CIO).  Walker believes that GPE's ERP \nsystem provides a substantial amount of data and is an \nadequate source for the model.  However, she is concerned \nabout relying on a single data source and believes that \nexternal sources could produce insights about market\n\ntrends and competitors' strategies.  Walker asks Bass to add \nother sources of information to his analysis, including \ncompetitors' annual reports, social media posts, and online \nnews services.  Walker reminds Bass that unstructured data \nrequires specific operations in the cleansing process of a \ntext ML model.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c10_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c10_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c10_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c10_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c10_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c10_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 11, "vignette": "Henrik Lippert is a junior quantitative analyst at Regnitz.  \nLippert is running a multiple regression to predict revenue \ngrowth for an energy stock he covers and presents his \nfindings to Tanja Vogl, his supervisor at Regnitz.  Vogl\nanalyzes the residual plot and notices a pattern: the \nvariance of residuals changes across observations and is \ncorrelated with one of the independent variables.  She tells \nLippert that he needs to adjust the model since it appears \nto violate a multiple linear regression assumption.\nLippert uses 72 observations to recalculate the regression \nof the dependent variable against four factors, and he plots \nthe three graphs presented in Exhibit 1:\nVogl analyzes the graphs and says that Graph 1 suggests a \npotential violation of another multiple linear regression \nassumption.  She adds that scatterplots can also be used to \nidentify the violation of other assumptions, such as \nlinearity.\n\nFinally, Vogl asks Lippert to create a Q–Q plot of regression \nresiduals; he presents the graph in Exhibit 2:", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c11_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c11_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c11_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c11_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 12, "vignette": "Hector Castillo and Rosa Espinosa are fixed-income analysts \nrunning regressions on companies they cover.  They start by \nregressing revenue for Sol Banco, a regional bank.  Castillo \nchooses two independent variables: the central bank's \ninterest rate and the country's aggregate savings rate.  \nEspinosa believes that the interest rate spread is an \nimportant third independent factor for an accurate \nregression.\nCastillo and Espinosa then discuss violations of regression \nassumptions they should be aware of.  Castillo states that \nwhen using multiple regressions to forecast asset prices:\nStatement 1: Positive serial correlation affects the \nconsistency of regression coefficients and statistical tests.\nStatement 2: Negative serial correlation means a negative \nresidual for one observation increases the chance of a \nnegative residual for the next observation.\nStatement 3: Patterns in serially correlated residuals may \ncontain information that has the potential to be exploited.\nEspinosa then discusses heteroskedasticity, saying that one \nof her models was displaying signs of conditional \nheteroskedasticity.  Knowing this, she questions the \naccuracy of the t-tests for the individual coefficients.  She \nadds that running a Breusch–Pagan (BP) test can diagnose \nfor potential conditional heteroskedasticity.  She shows \nCastillo a BP test that she ran on an earlier regression with a \ntest statistic of 37.3 and p-value of 0.005.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c12_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c12_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c12_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c12_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 13, "vignette": "Pauline Vaillant, a data analyst at a hedge fund, built a \nmachine learning (ML) model using text data from social \nmedia posts, internet discussion forums, and online news \nservices.  The objective of the model is to predict the \nsentiments of the texts in the data set.  Vaillant explains the \nmodel-building process to Pierre Frossard, an equity analyst \nat the hedge fund:  \"After cleansing and normalizing the \ndata, I used common techniques to gain insights about the \ndata.\"  Frossard asks about these techniques, and Vaillant \nstates:\nStatement 1: A bag-of-words model presents the tokens in \nthe sequence they appear in the data set.\nStatement 2: A document term matrix is a table displaying \nthe occurrences of each token in each text or sentence of \nthe data set.\nStatement 3: A word cloud is a visualization in which the \ntokens with the largest font size are the most relevant for \nmodel training.\nVaillant explains to Frossard that she built a table with the \nterm frequency (TF) and inverse document frequency (IDF) \nof the tokens in the data set.  She then analyzed the TF–IDF \nvalues and selected the most relevant tokens for model \ntraining.\nVaillant tells Frossard that she split the data set into \ntraining, cross-validation (CV), and test data sets.  She built \na confusion matrix to understand the performance metrics, \nusing the training data set and classifying the texts as 1 \n(positive sentiment) or 0 (negative sentiment).  Frossard\nsays, \"I am very concerned about the model incorrectly \npredicting a positive sentiment.\"\nVaillant decides to evaluate different models using a logistic \nregression.  She applies this method to the training data \nset, predicting positive or negative sentiments based on \neach sentence's probability value (p-value).  She then uses \nthe receiver operating characteristic (ROC) technique to \ncompare the results of four different models with both \ntraining and CV data sets.  Exhibit 1 displays the area under \nthe curve (AUC) for each model's ROC curve:\n\nFinally, Vaillant decides to apply the least absolute \nshrinkage and selection operator (LASSO) regularization to \nModel 4 in Exhibit 1.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c13_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c13_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c13_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c13_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c13_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c13_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "A"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 14, "vignette": "Camila Soares, CFA, is a portfolio manager at Down River \nCapital (DRC).  She reads about how machine learning (ML) \ncan improve her portfolio's performance and asks three of \nher colleagues, Luciana Dias, Gabriel Batista, and Felipe \nAraujo, for advice.\nDias is building an ML algorithm that will automatically \ntrade overbought or oversold stocks based on multiple \ntechnical factors.  She states that although the model \ncaptures the relationships well in the test sample, it is not \ntranslating this into useful results once she uses validation \nand test samples.\nBatista wants to improve on an existing model.  He tells \nSoares that the model has shown low in-sample error but \nhigh out-of-sample error.  To correct this, Batista plans to:\nEmploy a least absolute shrinkage and selection operator \n(LASSO) model with λ = 0.\nAdd features to help explain outliers.\nUse cross-validation to reduce holdout samples.\nBatista adds, \"More than one ML algorithm can be used in \nmost cases, but not every ML algorithm is useful for every \npurpose.  In this case, I wanted to use a supervised ML \nalgorithm to simplify some fixed income models by cutting \ndown on the number of features I had identified using an \nordinary least squares regression.  As you know, having \nmultiple correlated parameters can result in incorrect \nresults.\"\nAraujo has been working with advanced ML algorithms \nbased on neural networks.  One such algorithm that Araujo \ndiscovered involves speech recognition and natural \nlanguage processing.  Araujo has begun looking into how \nthis algorithm could be used to help sift through large \namounts of news articles, press releases, and company \npresentations to filter for information that would be \npertinent for investment decisions.\n\nSoares tells Araujo that she has heard about neural \nnetworks but is not sure how they work.  Araujo says:\nComment 1:  One difference between neural networks and \nmultiple regression is that neural networks add a hidden \nlayer that adds multiple steps of linear functionality \nbetween the input and output layers.\nComment 2:  Each node in a neural network typically has a \nsummation operator and activation function that transform \nan input from the previous layer into an output for the next \nlayer.\nComment 3:  Deep learning nets, which are neural \nnetworks with 100 or more hidden layers, can help solve \neven more complex problems.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c14_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c14_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c14_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c14_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c14_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c14_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 15, "vignette": "Deepak Patel, CFA, is analyzing the gross revenues of \nMillard, a large online retailer.  He compiled quarterly \nrevenues (in $ billions) from Q1 20X3 through the most \nrecent quarter-end of Q4 20Y8 (totaling 64 quarters), then \ncreated regressions in the form of both linear and log-linear \ntrend models.  The revenue data for both models is plotted \nin the graph in Exhibit 1, while regression results for both \nare shown in Exhibit 2.\n\nPatel determines that neither of these regressions \nappropriately models Millard's revenues over time.  \nInstead, he creates a model by first-differencing the log-\nlinear time series, in which Rev equals quarterly gross \nrevenue.  He then calculates a new regression, as \nsummarized in Exhibit 3.\nAfter reviewing the data in Exhibit 3, Patel determines that \nhe must adjust for seasonality by adding two seasonal lags \nto the model.  He then updates the regression, as shown in \nExhibit 4.\n\nPatel would like to use the latest model (Exhibit 4) and \nMillard's revenue data to forecast revenue growth.  He \nincludes the historical growth that Millard has experienced \nin the four preceding quarters:\nPatel's manager asks if Patel could improve on his models \nby adding more time periods to his analysis.  In response, \nPatel states that:\nStatement 1: Adding time periods may reveal that the \ntime series has multiple regimes.\nStatement 2: Deriving the regression with different sample \nperiods can result in different forecasted values.\nStatement 3: If model data show multiple regimes, this \nrequires adjusting the model in the same way as needed to \naccommodate for seasonality.\nPatel would also like to determine whether there is a \nrelationship between Millard's revenues and stock price.  To \ntest this, he models a time series for both the revenues and \nthe stock price.  He then must perform tests on each for \nunit roots and cointegration.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c15_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c15_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c15_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c15_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c15_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c15_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "B"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 16, "vignette": "Anna Meccini, CFA, is interested in the effects of rising \ninflation on client portfolios.  Meccini analyzes monthly US \ninflation levels for the past 8 years and performs a \nregression using an AR(1) specification.  The results of the \nautoregression are shown in Exhibit 1:\nMeccini must next determine if this model is properly \nspecified.  She plans to test for nonstationarity and is \nassessing options for how to perform that test.  She decides \nto first-difference the AR(1) model data and then runs a \nsecond regression.  That regression's results are shown in \nExhibit 2:\n\nMeccini believes that the first-differenced AR(1) model is \nstationary and mean-reverting.  She would like to \ndetermine the mean-reverting level and compare it against \nthe most recently observed month-over-month inflation \nchange (Δi).\nAfter estimating the current period's error to be 2%, \nMeccini believes that the first-differenced model can be \nused to estimate the variance of the errors in subsequent \nperiods.  Using the residuals from the first-differenced \nmodel, she performs a regression to test whether the \nmodel has ARCH(1) errors.  Exhibit 3 summarizes those \nresults:\nFinally, Meccini uses the first-differenced AR(1) model to \nforecast inflation for the next three months.  She is \ninterested in the out-of-sample forecasting performance of \nher model, so she compares the actual inflation for those \nmonths against the forecast.  For the three months, the \nsum of square errors of that sample is 0.0019.  An earlier \nversion of her model (Model 1) had a RMSE of 3%, and she \nwould like to verify her current model (Model 2) using the \nsame method.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c16_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "C"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c16_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c16_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c16_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c16_q5", "q_num": 5, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c16_q6", "q_num": 6, "correct": "C"}]}, {"source_id": "uworld_quant", "source_label": "UWorld – Quantitative Methods", "topic": "Quantitative Methods", "case_no": 17, "vignette": "Ursula Beijer is the portfolio manager of Scheldt Equities \nFund.  The fund holds stocks from emerging markets, and \nBeijer is concerned about the risk of government debt \ndefault.  She asks Roland Drenth, a quantitative analyst at \nthe firm, to build a multiple regression model based on \nmacroeconomic factors to predict whether or not a country \nwill default.  Drenth explains that the objective of the \nmodel is to predict a discrete variable that takes the value \nof:\n1 when the model predicts a debt default or\n0 when a default is not expected.\nBeijer also wants to investigate how the returns of the \nScheldt fund are influenced by distinct market factors.  She \nasks Drenth to build a regression model of the fund's excess \nreturns against the three factors in the Fama-French model.  \nDrenth presents the following multiple regression equation:\nSEFi = b0 + b1MKTRFi + b2SMBi + b3HMLi + εi, where:\nSEF = Excess return of the Scheldt Equities Fund\nMKTRF = Market excess return over the risk-free rate\nSMB = Small minus big (size effect)\nHML = High minus low (value premium)\nBeijer asks Drenth how to interpret the regression variables \nand estimated coefficients.  Drenth makes the following \nstatements:\nStatement 1: The intercept b0 is the fund's expected \nexcess return if the error term εi is zero.\nStatement 2: The slope b1 describes how SEF is affected \nby a unit change in MKTRF while holding SMB and HML \nconstant.\nStatement 3: MKTRF, SMB, and HML constitute the \nstochastic part of the model.\n\nDrenth then runs the regression with 60 monthly return \nobservations, stated in whole percentages (ie, 1 = 1%), and \narrives at the following equation with the estimated \ncoefficients:\nSEF = 0.06 + 0.51MKTRF − 0.72SMB + 0.30HML\nBeijer asks for a numeric example, and Drenth estimates \nScheldt's excess return in a hypothetical month, given the \nfollowing assumptions:\nMKTRF = 1.00%,\nSMB = 1.00%, and\nHML = 0.50%.\nBeijer wants to use the regression model to predict the \nfund's performance.  However, Drenth advises Beijer that \nseveral additional steps must be taken before deeming the \nmodel acceptable for making predictions and performing \nfurther analysis.", "questions": [{"q_id": "uworld_quant_c17_q1", "q_num": 1, "correct": "A"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c17_q2", "q_num": 2, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c17_q3", "q_num": 3, "correct": "B"}, {"q_id": "uworld_quant_c17_q4", "q_num": 4, "correct": "A"}]}]};